window.LBR = [{"name":"The Sheep Detectives","year":2026,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-14","tags":[],"words":9,"review":"Our memories that keep the ones we love alive.","poster":"img/the-sheep-detectives-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Taking of Pelham 1 2 3","year":2009,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-14","tags":[],"words":77,"review":"You gotta trust a movie when it has a Scott directing, a train, and Denzel Washington right in the middle of it.\n\nThe movie gets more exciting as it goes, and the shootout near the end gives it a nice burst of action after all the tension.\n\nJohn Travolta is easily the best thing here. He brings so much aggression, unpredictability, and personality to the villain that every scene immediately becomes more interesting whenever he shows up.","poster":"img/the-taking-of-pelham-1-2-3-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"21 Jump Street","year":2012,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-14","tags":[],"words":10,"review":"Well, that’s one cool way to recite the Miranda rights.","poster":"img/21-jump-street-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Man from U.N.C.L.E.","year":2015,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-14","tags":[],"words":38,"review":"Guy Ritchie really knows how to make spy action tropes feel stylish and fun. \n\nI especially love the lighting and color grading here. It gives the film that slick, retro espionage look that perfectly complements his playful direction.","poster":"img/the-man-from-u-n-c-l-e-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The End of Oak Street","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-13","tags":[],"words":289,"review":"Wildly entertaining. The trailers really don’t do justice to how fun, dangerous, and surprisingly intense this movie actually is. Glad I gave it a chance!\n\nWhat immediately captivated me was the visual texture. The film grain and occasional film artifacts give it this old school cinematic personality that I rarely see in modern movies. Love the double focus split diopter shots. I don’t see that much in modern cinema anymore. Love the usage of long lenses too, especially when it shifts attention from the foreground to the background while the main subjects don’t even know what is happening behind them. Just pure visual language!\n\nThe acting by Ewan McGregor and Anne Hathaway was good. They really sold me on the peril. Surprisingly bloody too!\n\nThe score by Michael Giacchino feels like it has John Williams DNA, with its adventurous yet suspenseful sound.\n\nThe production design also adds so much. It feels like an old school adventure movie that actually built environments instead of placing everyone inside an empty green screen. There is something satisfying about seeing actors and CG creatures seemingly occupying and reacting within the same physical space.\n\nIt also has the vibe of E.T. (1982), with something weird happening in a small community (and 80s motif too). Somehow, it also gives me Journey to the Center of the Earth (2008) vibes with the prehistoric era element, and of course Jurassic Park (1993) with its humongous dinosaurs.\n\nThe creature CGI is good. The way the light interacts with their bodies, and the texture of their skin and scales feel lifelike.\n\nSo many close calls and jump scares. My wife is literally stressed out.\n\nNot many questions were answered, nor was much of the logic explained. Just pure entertainment.","poster":"img/the-end-of-oak-street-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Godzilla Minus One","year":2023,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-08-12","tags":[],"words":119,"review":"There’s something elegant about watching a film in black and white. It feels timeless. I already watched this in color, but now in B&W, I could say the experience is much better. The monochrome presentation feels more appropriate for the time period and gives the film a texture that reminds me of Akira Kurosawa’s Seven Samurai (1954).\n\nThe black and white presentation also works surprisingly well on a technical level. It hides some of the imperfections in the CGI, particularly the background extensions, while also making Godzilla feel even more menacing. Without the distraction of color, his silhouette, scale, and presence become much more imposing.\n\nThe pacing is slow burn. It is patient in shelling out each character’s motivations.","poster":"img/godzilla-minus-one-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Training Day","year":2001,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-11","tags":[],"words":182,"review":"What a day!\n\nI spent most of Training Day confused about what the hell was actually happening, which somehow made the experience even better because I felt exactly like Ethan Hawke’s character. He is constantly trying to figure out what kind of world he has stepped into, and I was right there with him. And this might be the most GTA shit Denzel has ever been. The angst, confidence, menace, and ridiculous amount of swag just ooze out of him.\n\nWhen the raid happened, I was in total disbelief over what transpired and how everything suddenly came together. It feels like Sicario (2015) level kind of shit with the police. It totally caught me off guard. The runtime still had almost an hour left, but I already wanted to do a rewatch.\n\nThe tension in this is on another level. This shit does not let me breathe, especially during the bathtub scene.\n\nProps to Denzel and Ethan for the performances. Antoine Fuqua directs the hell out of it, and David Ayer’s writing keeps revealing new layers until everything finally clicks into place.","poster":"img/training-day-2001.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Nobody 2","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-09","tags":[],"words":85,"review":"Don’t bring a machete to a fucking katana duel.\n\nStill has those satisfying, ferocious, brutal fight scenes with cool camera work. The cinematography really shines in the final battle. It feels like they are inside a vibrant music video despite how violent the set piece is.\n\nSharon Stone did a great job as the main villain. She has this commanding presence as a strong female antagonist who does not need some complicated sympathetic motivation. She is simply ruthless, powerful, and driven by pure evil intent","poster":"img/nobody-2-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Unstoppable","year":2010,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-09","tags":[],"words":118,"review":"I don’t think I have seen any film like this, with such a believable, imminent, monstrous train as if it is the main villain character of the story.\n\nTony Scott grounded everything in reality. If there is any CGI, it is very invisible because I honestly cannot spot where the illusion breaks. Everything feels real. I don’t know how he pulled it off, but he did it really well.\n\nThe editor also had a big role in keeping the tension going, stitching shot after shot together and not letting me breathe. \n\nBen Seresin’s cinematography complements that urgency really well too, using different lenses, framing, and perspectives to capture both the characters’ emotions and the sheer danger surrounding them.","poster":"img/unstoppable-2010.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"American Gangster","year":2007,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-10","tags":[],"words":123,"review":"Nothing flashy. Nothing gimmicky. Just good old fashioned storytelling by Ridley Scott.\n\nThe crime drama is handled really well, and the whole drugs and logistics side of the story gives me Martin Scorsese vibes. The investigative nuances of the film feel like David Fincher’s style. Very controlled, methodical, and patient in the way it follows the investigation and slowly pieces everything together.\n\nDenzel as Frank Lucas is a quiet, calculating, and calm gangster, unlike other top bosses who are always screaming and power hungry because his presence already tells you he is in control.\n\nI think Denzel and Russell did a great job here. Nothing fancy about their performances. No unnecessary theatrics. Just two actors completely locked into their characters, delivering masterful performances.","poster":"img/american-gangster-2007.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Nowhere","year":2023,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-11","tags":[],"words":130,"review":"Shocking. I care most about her because she’s pregnant. If not, I couldn’t care less. There were themes that reminded me of Pieces of a Woman (2020) and The Fall (2022), with how suffering, survival, and motherhood become intertwined.\n\nThe score is great, especially when it swells up during key triumphant moments. It really amplifies the emotion.\n\nThis kind of film makes you feel the pain the main character is feeling. Very effective.\n\nNowhere is such a simple story about the lengths a mother will go to save her child, but it was told in the most beautiful and heartfelt way. All the pieces of filmmaking just came together nicely: the directing, lighting, scoring, editing, production design, writing. Together they make the survival feel intimate and emotionally genuine. Great film!","poster":"img/nowhere-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Equalizer 2","year":2018,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-08","tags":[],"words":77,"review":"The car fight scene where the assassin pretends to be Robert’s passenger is such a creative action setup. I have never seen that kind of action setup before.\n\nI also like it when Robert uses the environment to hide his location and pull off stealth moves.\n\nThe kills are more brutal this time, and the story feels more personal. Robert is no longer simply dispensing justice for strangers. This time, the violence hits much closer to home.","poster":"img/the-equalizer-2-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Equalizer 3","year":2023,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-08","tags":[],"words":237,"review":"Best opening scene in the series. It immediately feels more cinematic, and the darker, old school brown color grading gives the film a different identity from the first two. To me, it almost foreshadows that this third installment is going for a more reflective and final chapter kind of tone. It even reminds me a bit of The Godfather Part III (1990), especially with that end of an era feeling and, of course, the Italian setting.\n\nRobert’s recovery also gives me The Dark Knight Rises (2012) vibes. He is a fallen hero trying to heal from both physical and mental wounds, and there is that hesitation to return to what he used to do because part of him just wants to finally live a quiet and peaceful life.\n\nI know, I know, you have a bigger budget, but putting in the mafia, drugs, and terrorists is not the answer for me. The Equalizer is at its best when it stays grounded and focuses more on the characters instead of overpopulating the cast. Once you keep adding more criminal organizations and side characters, it starts to lose that intimate vigilante feel.\n\nAnd the mafia boss really disappointed me. Why does the mafia boss just become a screaming, cowardly, frustrated bitch in the end? For someone positioned as the main threat, that is such weak characterization. He never becomes the kind of villain who feels worthy of Robert McCall.","poster":"img/the-equalizer-3-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Last House","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-08","tags":[],"words":157,"review":"Greta Lee and Wagner Moura in one film? Count me in!\n\nThe Last House is essentially a family drama about resilience and survival amidst an apocalyptic event. The production design is great, and the performances help ground the story even when everything outside the family becomes increasingly strange and dangerous.\n\nIt reminds me a lot of Brick (2025) with the characters trapped inside their home, mixed with A Quiet Place (2018) because of how it uses an alien threat as the backdrop for a more intimate family drama.\n\nThe creature design also looks surprisingly good. Some of it seems practical, which gives the creature a stronger physical presence, and even when the film switches to CGI, it still holds up pretty well.\n\nMy only complaint is that it left me with more questions than answers. I felt like too many things were left hanging. The ending itself also feels a little off, like the writer got lazy.","poster":"img/the-last-house-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Devil's Mouth","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-08-06","tags":[],"words":146,"review":"The Devil’s Mouth really reminds me of Deep Blue Sea (1999) because of its constant sense of thrill and danger, including how certain things play out. It also kind of reminds me of Under Paris (2024), but instead of navigating catacombs beneath the city, this one traps its characters inside claustrophobic caverns and tunnels.\n\nThe suspense and atmosphere are good. My only complaint is that the CGI is quite terrible at times. The film also becomes too dragging whenever it returns to its friendship theme. That character motivation keeps them going in circles, yet there is too much focus on those BFF subplots without any satisfying payoff that justifies why the director spent so much time on them.\n\nA good movie to reward yourself with after a long and exhausting day. It is not perfect, but it is definitely better than the usual Netflix's Thrash (2026)","poster":"img/the-devil-s-mouth-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spider-Man: Brand New Day","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-31","tags":[],"words":423,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI came in expecting another fun Spider Man movie and ended up getting something much more personal than I anticipated. It doesn't always hit the emotional highs it aims for, but I really appreciated how willing it was to slow down and let its characters breathe. It left me pleasantly surprised, even if it didn't completely win me over.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nNo amount of trailers hyped me for this, and quite frankly, I didn't expect that much.\n\nMystery and detective vibes.\n\nThe theme is quite dark and mature in its tone. It feels more like character drama than a blockbuster flick. Actually, the lighting and color grading in some scenes kind of remind me of Obsession (2025).\n\nIt feels like all the Spider-Man movies rolled into one. The emo and lonely and emotionally broken Spider-Man from Spider-Man 3, the love story of The Amazing Spider-Man, and the grounded coming-of-age journey of the MCU films.\n\nThis movie is an intimate story about Peter's struggles rather than a superhero adventure. \n\nJean Grey showing off Omega-level powers. His motivations kind of feel like Magneto's. The third act gives the emotional \"core memory\" plot like in Inside Out 2 mixed with the defeat the enemy with power of love and understanding in Thunderbolts.\n\nPeter Parker is no longer a kid here. He has accepted what he cannot control and moved forward with what he can control. He's also way past the mentoring kind of story. Instead, he feels like an equal to Yelena and Bruce, and should I say, even more mature than Frank Castle. Speaking of Frank, their banter was one of the highlights of the film. It perfectly balanced Frank's aggression with Peter's wit and humor.\n\nThe audience reactions in the cinema were wild, especially during the romance scenes, but yeah, they didn't hit quite work for me. The ending should have been as painful as Past Lives, but it didn't fully land. The conclusion of MJ and Peter's relationship is like Peter and Gamora's. They naturally drift apart because there's only unrequited love left between them.\n\nIt's an enjoyable ride with lots of emotional ups and downs. I just think my standards have naturally become higher after watching so many great films, which is probably why I can't rate this one any higher.\n\nCoolest and simplest low-budget end credits sequence with a lot of impact.","poster":"img/spider-man-brand-new-day-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Equalizer","year":2014,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-28","tags":[],"words":268,"review":"Sherlock’s brain, Batman’s moral code, John Wick’s brutality, and John McClane’s DIY skills walk into a hardware store.\n\nAt first, it is kind of slow, but it gets interesting once the story goes into Slavi’s lair. The direction creates a controlled and tense sense of anticipation, while the cinematography locks in with its cinematic yellow brown lighting and close up framing.\n\nI especially love Robert’s habit of planning everything within a split second before attacking, similar to RDJ’s style in Sherlock Holmes (2009) mentally calculating every movement before a fight. That Sherlock quality continues throughout the film whenever Robert quietly observes someone and tries to decode where they came from, what they are thinking, and what they are capable of, much like Sherlock again, but this time Benedict’s 2010 version.\n\nThen the violence ensues, and I love it! It feels like John Wick (2014) violence. The hunted suddenly becomes the hunter, and Robert transforms into this terrifying boogeyman who methodically eliminates everyone standing in his way.\n\nHe is also just like Batman, a vigilante at night with a dual personality who seeks justice for people who cannot defend themselves.\n\nTeddy and Robert’s first encounter is brilliant because neither man openly reveals what he knows. They are quietly trying to outwit one another, gauging each other’s intelligence and searching for weaknesses they can exploit. Marton Csokas is a great villain. Stoic with intensity, if that even makes sense.\n\nThe final set piece feels like John McClane in Die Hard (1988), picking them off one by one. The final standoff between Teddy and Robert is so cinematic and thematically satisfying.","poster":"img/the-equalizer-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Doraemon the Movie: New Nobita and the Castle of the Undersea Devil","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-25","tags":[],"words":129,"review":"I am today years old when I learned that Doraemon is actually a robot.\n\nInstantly brought me back to my childhood. It feels modernized, yet it never loses the classic Doraemon charm.\n\nThe musical score is great, especially during the mystery and danger moments.\n\nThe gadgets are amazing as always. They're still one of the biggest reasons why Doraemon movies remain so fun to watch.\n\nThe first half feels like the sense of wonder from Avatar: The Way of Water (2022), with its beautiful exploration of the underwater world. The second half shifts into something closer to Atlantis: The Lost Empire (2001) and Aquaman (2018), focusing on the conflict between the earth dwellers and the sea dwellers. It's a fun adventure that balances nostalgia with a more modern presentation.","poster":"img/doraemon-the-movie-new-nobita-and-the-castle-of-the-undersea-devil-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Odyssey","year":2026,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-16","tags":[],"words":478,"review":"If Dunkirk (2017) wore Greek mythology as its armor, this would be the result.\n\nEverything feels real and tangible, like it was filmed in an old era where there was no digital enhancement. Everything feels grounded. It feels like Dunkirk's war struggle viewed through a fantasy lens. This is exactly how Percy Jackson & The Olympians should look and feel on the big screen.\n\nThe technical presentation is phenomenal. The sound design is immersive enough that I could genuinely feel and hear the rain, thunder, and crashing waves. Hoyte van Hoytema still delivers. The shipwreck storm sequence is freaking terrifying and claustrophobic. It also reminded me of the sense of fear in Wrath of the Titans. The entire cinema was shaking during the biggest moments, making the IMAX experience even more overwhelming. The score is also fantastic, elevating both the spectacle and the emotional moments. \n\nI also appreciate the use of practical effects in the Circe sequence. It may feel like an old and outdated filmmaking technique, but it gives the scene a tangible, eerie quality that CGI would have struggled to replicate. It creates a unique horror texture that makes the sequence feel more unsettling\n\nSpeaking of horror, I was actually surprised that the Cyclops sequence was quite long. I honestly thought it would just be a short set piece and that the giant animatronics were nothing more than a trailer gimmick. Instead, it became one of the film's standout moments (and a domino effect in the story).\n\nAlso one of my favorite moments is when Odysseus is asked what the Siren's song was about. His answer is so deep. To me, it is the best line in the movie because it suggests that the Sirens do not simply sing about what you already desire, but also awaken wishes you never even knew you wanted. The script there dig too hard.\n\nRobert Pattinson's performance reminded me of his annoying intensity in Good Time (2017), and Anne Hathaway is wonderful here.\n\nThe pacing is a slow burn similar to Oppenheimer, but the build up is nice and the foreshadowing pays off real hard! The first and second acts were sitting at around a 4 star experience for me, but the third act completely erased all my doubts and elevated the film to a full 5 stars. \n\nI watched a version of this during our high school World Literature class, and I remember always feeling bitin every time the class ended because we only watched an hour per day. Finally seeing the whole story on this scale feels incredibly rewarding.\n\nThe film is not just about Odysseus' physical journey home, but about his emotional journey, his growth as a leader, and his healing after everything he has endured. He also feels much more human here, making his struggles easier to connect with.\n\nOverall, this is an epic IMAX experience.","poster":"img/the-odyssey-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Moana","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-14","tags":[],"words":331,"review":"Is this even necessary? It is Nanya. Nanya business!\n\nThis live action is basically a frame by frame version of the animated one.\n\nI was smiling almost the entire time. I had a happy ending to the day. Still has a lot of heart. I jived to the songs because I know them by heart.\n\nThe scenery is beautiful, even though I think a lot of it is CGI. I just wish they had shot more on real locations to give the world a bit more authenticity. That said, the IMAX 3D conversion is surprisingly great. There are plenty of moments where images pop out of the screen, and the wave simulations are genuinely intimidating, making me feel like I was about to be swallowed by the ocean too. Whenever Maui transforms into his eagle form, it instantly reminded me of Legend of the Guardians: The Owls of Ga'Hoole (2010).\n\nVisually, the lighting is very intentional throughout. For the vocals though, I would still choose Auliʻi Cravalho. Her performance has more emotion and personality. The deliveries lack the oomph of the original. I do love the actress playing Grandma Tala. She looks and behaves a little differently, but she still captures the same warmth and wisdom. Moana, on the other hand, feels a bit too mature and loses some of the quirky charm that made her animated counterpart so lovable. Heihei is still hilarious, while Dwayne Johnson's Maui feels less sarcastic and mischievous than before.\n\nThe Shiny sequence is still my favorite part. I did notice, however, that most of the songs are performed at a slightly slower tempo, with more emphasis on the pronunciation of certain words compared to the original.\n\nTe Kā is absolutely terrifying and menacing. Her scale is massive in IMAX. The dark and night scenes are also gorgeous and so immersive. If you're going to watch this, IMAX 3D is definitely the way to experience it.\n\nIf you love Moana, it is impossible to hate this.","poster":"img/moana-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Death of Robin Hood","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-12","tags":[],"words":189,"review":"Another Logan (2017) kind of movie. follows an old, tired, and guilt ridden man who sees himself as nothing more than a monster because of the life he has lived. Like Logan, he longs for an end to his suffering, yet unexpectedly finds himself taking a child under his wing. They also share the idea of legacy, questioning whether the next generation should inherit the same life of violence or be given a chance to become something better.\n\nAs the title suggests, it feels like I am the one being put to death by the pacing. It is an extremely slow, introspective film that asks for a lot of patience (which never paid off).\n\nVisually, it is gorgeous. The atmosphere and landscapes reminded me of The Northman with their rugged beauty and earthy palette.. There's a scene that reminds me of Sam Mendes' lighting in 1917 and Spectre. The whole thing is intentionally and beautifully shot.\n\nThis is a deeply introspective and retrospective film. It spends more time looking inward than moving forward, reflecting on guilt, mortality, legacy, and whether a lifetime of violence can ever truly be redeemed.","poster":"img/the-death-of-robin-hood-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Minions & Monsters","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-09","tags":[],"words":94,"review":"Chaotically fun.\n\nIt is super easy to get swept up by the happy vibes because the Minions are just so funny and adorable. Their chaotic energy carries most of the movie, and it is hard not to laugh whenever they are rowdy on screen.\n\nI also like the black and white sequences. They add a nice visual touch and stand out from the rest of the film.\n\nThe story is so so, but forgivable since it's a children's movie. It knows its audience, and its biggest strength is simply being fun, lighthearted, and entertaining.","poster":"img/minions-monsters-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Mandalorian and Grogu","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-23","tags":[],"words":190,"review":"Fun, charming, and dangerous!\n\nApocalypse Now style of starfighters in the opening credits is pure eye popcorn. The imagery make the scale feel exciting right away.\n\nThe gladiator sequence is also such a fun fan service moment. That is where the creature VFX really start to shine. But honestly, throughout the whole film, I think the puppeteers are the real MVPs. The practical feel of the creatures gives the movie more personality and charm than pure CGI ever could.\n\nThe Rotta the Hutt chase sequence is a really fun ride too. It feels very similar to podracing, especially in IMAX where the speed and movement become more immersive. There is also another scene that reminded me of Chamber of Secrets.\n\nWhat I appreciate most is that this film has its own DNA. It does not try to copy the Skywalker saga, Rogue One, or Solo. It feels separate with its own identity.\n\nLudwig’s score is also impressive. The music balances thrilling moments and emotional beats really well.\n\nMy only complaint is the pacing. It can feel messy at times, like some sections move too quickly while others suddenly slow down.","poster":"img/the-mandalorian-and-grogu-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Voicemails for Isabelle","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-27","tags":[],"words":149,"review":"Zoey Deutch's Jill looks like a mix of Cobie Smulders, Lily Collins, Daisy Ridley, and Natalie Portman. It is not just the facial resemblance. She has Cobie Smulders' effortless confidence and dry wit, Lily Collins' warmth and approachable charm, Daisy Ridley's youthful determination and emotional sincerity, and Natalie Portman's quiet intelligence and vulnerability. Those qualities blend together into a character who feels both familiar and uniquely her own.\n\nThe film is packed with pop culture references, making the dialogue and interactions feel playful.\n\nThe movie's themes blur between My Sister's Keeper (2009), Before Sunset (2004), and Chef (2014). carries the emotional weight of family and difficult choices, the intimate conversations and human connection of Before Sunset, and the healing, self discovery, and appreciation for life's simple moments found in Chef.\n\nThe soundtrack is also really good. It complements the emotional beats and gives the film a warm, comforting vibe.","poster":"img/voicemails-for-isabelle-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Supergirl","year":2026,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-25","tags":[],"words":120,"review":"I'd like to diss this movie.\n\nOkay, I just watched this because of Milly. She's just playing a drunken master most of the film.\n\nThe world and set aesthetics feel very GOTG. I can also feel the Star Wars influence in the creature work.\n\nBut overall, avoid! Boring! I did not have anything to latch onto. I dozed off from the second act to the third act. The pacing is so slow. Then when there's action, the choreography and camera work are just so hard to follow.\n\nThe emotional beats did not land too. The coming of age and healing type of emotional beats did not appeal to me. \n\nThere is no chemistry within the cast. It feels too forced.","poster":"img/supergirl-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Saccharine","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-23","tags":[],"words":132,"review":"Obsession... but make it food.\n\nThe film is very atmospheric. It carries that familiar A24 style with neon grading that complements the dark motifs of Obsession (2025).\n\nI especially like the macro shots. They make the food feel both beautiful and disturbing at the same time, emphasizing the sensory aspect of the experience.\n\nFor me, the film works more as a metaphor. It can be interpreted as obesity, anorexia, or simply an unhealthy relationship with consumption and self image.\n\nThe first two acts feel more sad than scary. It is definitely a slow burn. But by the third act, it transforms into metaphorical psychological horror rather than traditional horror.\n\nOverall, there is nothing particularly new here. Most of what the film offers is already present in the trailer. Ending is somehow forced.","poster":"img/saccharine-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Toy Story 5","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-17","tags":[],"words":272,"review":"Story is so good, I wanted to cry after the credits rolled.\n\nHonestly, the emotional beats here made me tear up even more than Andy leaving them. I swear, I did not expect that.\n\nThis film captures the same spirit as the first and second films. It made me genuinely care for the characters again, something I personally did not feel as strongly with the third and fourth movies. In my head, this deserves to be the true third film of the trilogy.\n\nI also appreciate that the film gives Jessie more attention instead of relying too much on Woody and Buzz.\n\nThe humor works too. I laughed out loud multiple times and completely forgot for a few hours that I am an adult. That is the magic of cinema!\n\nVisually, the film shines whenever the Buzz army appears. Those sequences are some of the most cinematic in the movie. I also like the new animation treatment whenever they enter the creative space during playtime. It makes everything feel more imaginative and childlike.\n\nLilypad is an interesting addition. It almost feels like an LLM that has gone rogue and unexpectedly developed a conscience after realizing that the original objective of its programming was never truly fulfilled.\n\nThe conversations of different toy era also feel like Gen X, Millennials, and Gen Z trying to understand one another. That generational aspect adds more depth than I expected.\n\nAnd honestly, one of my favorite parts of the experience was the audience itself. The cinema was filled with energetic shouts and giggles from children. It was so cute and made the whole experience feel more alive.","poster":"img/toy-story-5-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Disclosure Day","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-11","tags":[],"words":199,"review":"Right from the start, I was already impressed with the blocking, continuous shots, and cinematography. It feels like Steven Spielberg simply flexing his filmmaking skills. Even ordinary scenes are staged with purpose and movement.\n\nEarly on, I also got some Sense8 vibes because of the whole interconnectedness aspect. It immediately added intrigue and mystery to the story.\n\nThe film works best as a thriller. The cat and mouse aspect keeps the pacing flowing smoothly, while the mystery keeps piling up. There is also a certain technology here that reminded me of Possession and even Ino's jutsu because of the way it operates and affects people.\n\nOne idea that really stood out to me is the suggestion that empathy might be the next requirement for evolution. I do not think I have encountered that concept before, and I found it fascinating. \n\nWhat I appreciate most is that the film is more interested in humanity's reaction (especially religion) rather than providing all the answers. In that sense, it feels like a more serious version of Don't Look Up (2021).\n\nAlso, early nominations from me for Best Actress for Emily Blunt and Best Musical Score for John Williams. Both are outstanding here.","poster":"img/disclosure-day-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Obsession","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-11","tags":[],"words":162,"review":"Tbh, I laughed more than I got spooked.\n\nThe first hour has strong craft right away. Framing, blocking, and score are the first standouts. I can feel the film trying to create tension through placement, silence, and mood.\n\nLesson of the film: do not date your coworker. Or as the saying goes, don’t shit where you eat. There are also some Get Out (2017) feels in the way the relationship slowly becomes uncomfortable.\n\nBut the film is too predictable. There were storylines and surprises where me and my friend already guessed what would happen.\n\nNo lasting impact either. I did not really feel the horror. I think this will be more effective for guys who have dealt with manipulative, hallucinating, control-freak girlfriend energy. Basically, it felt like a codependency story dressed as horror.\n\nThe actress’ acting is soooo good though, and I think it is the strongest suit of the film. Even when the story became obvious, her performance kept it watchable.","poster":"img/obsession-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Scary Movie","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-11","tags":[],"words":31,"review":"Slapstick comedy. Nostalgia overload.\nSome jokes landed, some did not, but I still enjoyed it. Remember, this is just a weed movie. Nothing to dig deep into, just enjoy the ride.","poster":"img/scary-movie-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Polar","year":2019,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2020-04-02","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Thursday April 2, 2020.","poster":"img/polar-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Outsider","year":2018,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2020-04-02","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Thursday April 2, 2020.","poster":"img/the-outsider-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spider-Man: No Way Home","year":2021,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2022-01-19","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Wednesday January 19, 2022.","poster":"img/spider-man-no-way-home-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Batman","year":2022,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2022-03-20","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Sunday March 20, 2022.","poster":"img/the-batman-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"21 Bridges","year":2019,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2022-03-26","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Saturday March 26, 2022.","poster":"img/21-bridges-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness","year":2022,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2022-05-05","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Thursday May 5, 2022.","poster":"img/doctor-strange-in-the-multiverse-of-madness-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania","year":2023,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2023-02-15","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Wednesday February 15, 2023.","poster":"img/ant-man-and-the-wasp-quantumania-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Moana 2","year":2024,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2024-12-14","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Saturday December 14, 2024.","poster":"img/moana-2-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Avengers: Infinity War","year":2018,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2018-08-02","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Thursday August 2, 2018.","poster":"img/avengers-infinity-war-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Conversations with a Killer: The Ted Bundy Tapes","year":2019,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2019-02-06","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Wednesday February 6, 2019.","poster":"img/conversations-with-a-killer-the-ted-bundy-tapes-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Overlord","year":2018,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2019-02-20","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Wednesday February 20, 2019.","poster":"img/overlord-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse","year":2018,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2019-02-25","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Monday February 25, 2019.","poster":"img/spider-man-into-the-spider-verse-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald","year":2018,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2019-03-05","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Tuesday March 5, 2019.","poster":"img/fantastic-beasts-the-crimes-of-grindelwald-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Moana","year":2016,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2019-12-22","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Sunday December 22, 2019.","poster":"img/moana-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Secret","year":2006,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2020-03-26","tags":[],"words":6,"review":"Watched on Thursday March 26, 2020.","poster":"img/the-secret-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Whisper of the Heart","year":1995,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-02","tags":[],"words":162,"review":"Not everyone needs to follow the same path. Go ahead and do what your heart tells you. But it's never easy when you do things differently from everyone else. If things don't go well, you'll only have yourself to blame.\n\nThis is probably the quote that resonated with me the most. My parents never forced me into a specific path. They did not pressure me to become something I did not want to be. Instead, they gave me the freedom to explore, make mistakes, and figure things out on my own when I was a student.\n\nThat freedom was a gift, but it also came with responsibility. When I succeeded, I knew it was because of my own choices. When I failed, I had nobody else to blame. That is why this quote hits so close to home. It reminds me that finding my own path is both a privilege and a burden, but one I am grateful to have been given.","poster":"img/whisper-of-the-heart-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Midnight Girls","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-02","tags":[],"words":376,"review":"I watched this because I am Japan sepanx. Satisfied naman because it felt familiar right away. The taxis, the Alphards, the city lights at night, the clean roads, and of course, the vending machines. \n\nSeklusyon (2016) ata yung last kong Tagalog film sa sinehan, so watching a Tagalog film again in theaters felt weirdly refreshing. New experience siya for me, like I was reconnecting with local cinema after being away for too long.\n\nJodi is still a great actress. I really liked her character as this unselfish giver. Small side note, I like it when she says “telepono” for a cellphone. Very old soul.\n\nI was not annoyed with Patrick. He actually makes sense as a modern child. It really is the responsibility of parents to provide for the child’s needs, and it should never be used as sumbat. His character development was really unexpected. It caught me off guard.\n\nSaki’s emotional arc worked on me the most. Whenever her scenes come up and that nice swelling emotional score starts, I feel like crying. Paris’ hopeless romantic dream of a prince charming is grounded too. It could have been annoying, but the film gives her enough humanity that her development feels earned.\n\nWanna is the only one who feels predictable and typical, but it is not really her fault because her character is like a sponge. She absorbs everything around her: pain, choices, and survival instincts.\n\nVisually, I really appreciated how the film handled small spaces. he cinematography works because the lights are mounted well, the camera still moves with purpose, and the lens choices make the rooms feel alive instead of flat. Good directing too, especially with the blocking. \n\nStory-wise, the stigma is really strong. Being an entertainer is already hard enough, then you add pangmamaliit, family pressure, and the expectation that your money is not really yours. Grabe yung asa ng pamilya sa kanila. Toxic. I really resonated with that feeling na you cannot even enjoy your hard-earned money because everyone else already has a claim on it. Working for others sucks!\n\nThe film also briefly touches on stigma against activists and a gay child. The humanization is greatly written.\n\nWhen the emotion peaked, I literally had to clap. Newfound respect for Philippine cinema.","poster":"img/midnight-girls-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Colony","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-02","tags":[],"words":167,"review":"This is how it feels like to have AI agents!\n\nBefore the actual review, can we just take a moment to ask how the hell Jun Ji-hyun hasn't aged a bit from The Legend of the Blue Sea (2016) to this?!\n\nGagana talaga utak mo because of the scientific aspects and strategic maneuvering. The zombies here feel like an AI agent with DGM-style self-evolving upgrades. They are not just bodies running around. May system, may communication, may upgrade path.\n\nSolid cinema crowd reaction too. This is the kind of movie that benefits from people reacting around you.\n\nCamera work is good. The movement gives the action enough urgency. And the Atmos sound adds another layer of suspense. It honestly feels like the zombies are breathing down your neck. The sound makes the space feel unsafe.\n\nFirst and third act worked for me. The second act is kinda slow, but the concept and theater experience carried it for me. This was still a fun, satisfying and nail-biting ride.","poster":"img/colony-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Backrooms","year":2026,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-04","tags":[],"words":148,"review":"I love found footage, and I wish the movie stayed in that lane more. That was the only sequence that really gave me goosebumps.\n\nThe atmosphere is strong. Acting and production design are easily the highlights.\n\nNot horror enough, I think. More of a psychological mind fuck. Need ko pa mag-research kung tama yung pagkakaintindi ko because the movie feels like it wants you to decode it more than fear it.\n\nWhen the twist I thought would happen started forming, like the Backrooms being some sort of Maze Runner kind of shit, it abruptly stopped. Too bad. That was really frustrating because I wanted the movie to fully commit to that idea.\n\nOverall, it gave me Annihilation and The Shining vibes with the duplicates and the way places reflect a person. If there was more found footage and the twist really committed, I would have loved this more.","poster":"img/backrooms-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Masters of the Universe","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-04","tags":[],"words":187,"review":"Funny, but the dialogue is poor.\n\nI do not know much about the lore, the original source material, or any of the characters since I never had toys like this growing up. Maybe it was no longer popular when I was a child. But even without that attachment, I can see how much it borrowed from the treatment of Thor (2011) and Thor: Ragnarok (2017).\n\nThe whole thing feels like Taika Waititi and James Gunn had a custody battle over the tone. The punchlines reminded me of Thor: Ragnarok, where everything is constantly trying to undercut itself with jokes. The sets and CGI also have that Guardians of the Galaxy tactileness, where the world feels colorful, and physical.\n\nI can feel that Taika style talent for making big stakes feel light and small. At the same time, there is also that James Gunn strength of making the group feel like an ensemble, even when the writing itself is not always strong.\n\nStill, it is an enjoyable cheap thrill. The first and third act worked for me, even if the middle and the dialogue could have been sharper.","poster":"img/masters-of-the-universe-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Punisher: One Last Kill","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":103,"review":"A depressed, sad, tired, and lonely Frank Castle was perfectly portrayed by Jon Bernthal. Too real and too painful to watch. Love the unapologetic grittiness, and it reminded me why I loved his show from 2017. Wow, almost a decade already!\n\nWeird music choice using La Vie en Rose during a violent scene. Feels almost sarcastic with a man clearly not seeing life in pink. Satisfying violence though! Especially with the pen scene. Cannot go wrong with a pen in an action scene. Glad Disney did not hold back. It is really a bloody affair. It woke up my sleepy and bored ass.","poster":"img/the-punisher-one-last-kill-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Star Wars: The Mandalorian and Grogu","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-23","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":190,"review":"Fun, charming, and dangerous!\n\nApocalypse Now style of starfighters in the opening credits is pure eye popcorn. The imagery make the scale feel exciting right away.\n\nThe gladiator sequence is also such a fun fan service moment. That is where the creature VFX really start to shine. But honestly, throughout the whole film, I think the puppeteers are the real MVPs. The practical feel of the creatures gives the movie more personality and charm than pure CGI ever could.\n\nThe Rotta the Hutt chase sequence is a really fun ride too. It feels very similar to podracing, especially in IMAX where the speed and movement become more immersive. There is also another scene that reminded me of Chamber of Secrets.\n\nWhat I appreciate most is that this film has its own DNA. It does not try to copy the Skywalker saga, Rogue One, or Solo. It feels separate with its own identity.\n\nLudwig’s score is also impressive. The music balances thrilling moments and emotional beats really well.\n\nMy only complaint is the pacing. It can feel messy at times, like some sections move too quickly while others suddenly slow down.","poster":"img/star-wars-the-mandalorian-and-grogu-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Children of Heaven","year":1997,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-21","tags":["moviereview","arwinreviews","nowwatching"],"words":111,"review":"Run Ali run!\n\nThe film has such a cute and sincere presentation of how children try to solve problems on their own. It honestly captures innocence so well, especially in how simple situations become huge emotional challenges through a child’s perspective.\n\nAt the same time, it is also a bittersweet portrayal of how difficult it is to be a father who has nothing except ambition and hope for a better life for his family.\n\nThe build up of the story was nice, that is why by the third act, I was already rooting for Ali. The emotional payoff works because the film takes its time making me care about these characters.","poster":"img/children-of-heaven-1997.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"For a Few Dollars More","year":1965,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-20","tags":["moviereview","arwinreviews","nowwatching"],"words":128,"review":"Shots are great, especially in the stand offs and one on one scenes. The framing has that classic old school western style where the characters are positioned carefully to build anticipation. Lighting and lens are very sharp. \n\nSets and locations are great too. The dusty towns and wide open environments really help sell the western atmosphere.\n\nThe sound design also feels old school. It lacks bass, I think. Everything sounds high pitched. Dialogues are obviously dubbed or rerecorded in the studio because some lines do not sync with the movements of their lips.\n\nThe musical score sounds heroic, but emotionally I did not really feel it connecting with the story. Even by the end, I never fully felt the tension or triumph that the film was aiming for.","poster":"img/for-a-few-dollars-more-1965.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Irishman","year":2019,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":99,"review":"It has the same vibes as Goodfellas, The Godfather, and The Departed with its mafia centered story and criminal world.\n\nThe de-aging tech is insane! Scorsese’s directing and the cinematography are still great as expected, but I just could not fully attach myself emotionally to the story. Maybe I am starting to experience gangster fatigue after watching so many films in this genre.\n\nFor me, the film feels less about crime itself and more about the life cycle of being a gangster. From the rise and formation of that lifestyle, to its slow degradation and emptiness as time passes.","poster":"img/the-irishman-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Top Gun: Maverick","year":2022,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-05-13","tags":[],"words":3,"review":"Intense IMAX experience!","poster":"img/top-gun-maverick-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Billie Eilish — Hit Me Hard and Soft: The Tour (Live in 3D)","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":208,"review":"I like the Billie and James dynamic directing the whole thing. It feels collaborative and personal. And thank God it is less documentary than other concert films, because the concert itself stays as the main experience.\n\nAt first, I just vibed with it since I am not a fan and I do not know her songs, but I literally shed tears with The Greatest and TV. I resonated and felt the pain of those two songs. Like what is the point of everything in this life if the people around me take me for granted and my efforts are not turned into gratitude.\n\nThis concert film has more heart and emotion than any concert film I have watched. I went in not a fan but went out with so much appreciation.\n\n3D technology is nothing new in the experience. Watched it in SM Cinema, and I think SM still uses Real-D technology. But not as immersive as One Direction: This Is Us (2013), with its playfulness and more 3D text effects. But the immersiveness with Hit Me Hard And Soft: The Tour Live in 3D, does not come from gimmicks. It comes from feeling connected with the people in the concert venue and slowly rooting for Billie Eilish.","poster":"img/billie-eilish-hit-me-hard-and-soft-the-tour-live-in-3d-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mortal Kombat II","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":141,"review":"Squid Game, Voldemort, LOTR, and John Wick are canon in Mortal Kombat. \n\nThe movie has some genuinely funny beats that help balance all the violence and chaos.\n\nThe fights are awesome in IMAX! I clapped many times because the triumphant tournament action. Of course, there is a lot of CGI, but thankfully it is good CGI, and the violence is satisfyingly brutal. \n\nVisually, the film leans heavily into blue and red motifs in both the lighting and grading. It gives the whole movie a stylized, game like feel that fits the world. The world building is also nice, and it expands the universe enough to make me more interested.\n\nThe first movie was meh for me, but this one is much more well made. Bigger scale, more confidence, and honestly it gives Avengers vibes with how it builds the team energy.","poster":"img/mortal-kombat-ii-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A Man Called Otto","year":2022,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":10,"review":"Bro lost his sense of purpose, then found another one.","poster":"img/a-man-called-otto-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kill Bill: Vol. 1","year":2003,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-05-05","tags":[],"words":64,"review":"In my memory, this felt like a fast paced action film, but on rewatch it plays more like a slow burn. But I still love it! The revenge story is strong, and the filmmaking is on another level. The visual style, the composition, everything feels artistic.\n\nUma Thurman is also incredibly magnetic here. She carries the film with her presence. Definitely worth a rewatch!","poster":"img/kill-bill-vol-1-2003.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"We Live in Time","year":2024,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":107,"review":"Great acting by Andrew and Florence. I am with Florence’s character here, Almut. Not gonna lie, I have these thoughts of the choices I would make if I had cancer. I am also afraid of passing from this world into oblivion, leaving this planet without some imprint that people can remember me by. This film becomes more about legacy, time, and how we choose to live knowing that everything is temporary.\n\nThe film could be better if it was narrated in a linear storytelling. The non linear structure takes me away from the emotional build up that could have been stronger if the story unfolded more directly.","poster":"img/we-live-in-time-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Ministry of Ungentlemanly Warfare","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":84,"review":"If you love stealthy unsanctioned missions that go boom when the time is right, you are in for a treat.\n\nThis feels like Inglourious Basterds with its group of Nazi killers and ultra violent satisfaction. There is something absolutely satisfying about watching them take down Nazi soldiers one by one. \n\nThe score also reminds me of Django Unchained, giving the whole thing a stylish and playful edge even during the violence. But this time, add lots of sailing, Churchill, secret operations, and spy agents.","poster":"img/the-ministry-of-ungentlemanly-warfare-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Anora","year":2024,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":106,"review":"This feels like a mix of The Wolf of Wall Street, The Hangover, and Project X. It has that chaotic party energy, wild decisions, and people chasing pleasure without thinking too much about the consequences.\n\nThe shots are beautiful. The lighting and grading are also good, giving the film a textured look.\n\nThe first hour feels like Fifty Shades of Grey because of its erotic side and fantasy driven setup. But after that, the film shifts into something closer to Good Time, Uncut Gems, or Marty Supreme. It becomes one problem after another. \n\nMikey Madison demonstrated a great performance and Vache Tovmasyan is hella funny bro!","poster":"img/anora-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"No Time to Die","year":2021,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-05-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":448,"review":"Honestly, they should have ended the Craig Bond series with Spectre so it could have ended on a higher note.\n\nVisually, there is a lot to appreciate. I can definitely see Linus’ style here, same with Jay Kelly (2025)'s peach highlights and blue shadows for daylight. Strong La La Land (2016) visuals with the pink and violet sunset. For night, strong neon colors paired with low key lighting. The Spectre party scene is pure La La Land and Babylon (2022) look. Linus really gives the film its own visual identity.\n\nThe car chase sequence in Norway feels like an ad for Land Rover rather than a Bond movie. It looks polished and safe, I did not feel much action and thrill compared to the Spectre Austria car chase scene. But after that, the movie was redeemed because of the cat and mouse chase in the Norway forest scene. It reminds me of Christopher Nolan’s Insomnia (2022) with the atmospheric fog, where the environment itself feels cold, tense, and psychologically heavy. I also like the brutalist structure of Lyutsifer Safin’s place. The way the lighting matches the architecture makes it feel cold, massive, and almost alien. It actually made me imagine what Linus’ visuals would look like in Dune Part 3. The geometry, shadows, and controlled color palette already feel close to that kind of world.\n\nThe continuous shot action in the last 30 minutes is nicely shot and choreographed. It gives the final stretch a more immediate and physical energy, and that is where the film finally feels more alive as an action piece.\n\nSpeaking of Rami Malek’s character, he is not up to par with Mads Mikkelsen and Christoph Waltz’s enigmatic villain energy. He feels flat and insignificant. His character motivation feels bland, and he never fully becomes threatening enough for me. Lashana Lynch does not have the charm and angst for 007 too. Armas, even with a much shorter role, has way more gravity and screen presence compared to her.\n\nHow many times do you get to see Bond so emotional, sad, and angry about a woman? That is not something we always get from him. This movie also leans into gadgets and sci-fi. The director usually chose the CGI route rather than the practical route. Maybe these are the factors that other people dislike about this movie. But I can also see why it has its own flavor. It is more serious, more investigative, and almost detective like in its spycraft.\n\nI am going to give this 2 stars instead of 1.5 stars just for the sake of Linus’ superb cinematography work here. But story wise, direction wise, and pacing wise, it is just weak.","poster":"img/no-time-to-die-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jurassic Park","year":1993,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","movie"],"words":113,"review":"Surprisingly, the CGI is not off because of the combination of animatronics which are appreciated because the level of realness is high. John Williams’ superb score adds that sense of wonder, grandeur, and adventure. The acting of Sam Neill and Laura Dern during their first dinosaur encounter was great. Their faces sell the awe.\n\nThe film is also great at implying future danger. It builds tension carefully, making me anticipate something bad even before it happens. I remember this kind of feeling when I was a child watching Gremlins.\n\nThe shaping of light on the faces of the actors is definitely a directorial intention of Spielberg because it reminds me of Schindler’s List.","poster":"img/jurassic-park-1993.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"If Anything Happens I Love You","year":2020,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-01","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":46,"review":"This is what I fear: our lost loved ones can see how ugly we became, emotionally distant from one another while we grieve their passing. That is why I want to honor them even in how heavy I carry their absence by being better every day.","poster":"img/if-anything-happens-i-love-you-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Devil Wears Prada 2","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-30","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":26,"review":"It captures the same spirit as the first one. The fashion world, the energy, the humor and the sharp attitude. And I love Lady Gaga here!","poster":"img/the-devil-wears-prada-2-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Disaster Artist","year":2017,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":44,"review":"Feels like This Is The End 2013, where the story seems like it was conceived while James Franco and Seth Rogen were smoking weed. At least that one is entertaining enough. But this one is not. The title itself tells everything about the movie.","poster":"img/the-disaster-artist-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spectre","year":2015,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":231,"review":"This film looks gorgeous. Hoyte van Hoytema really proves himself as a worthy successor to Roger Deakins. Even simple scenes like the boardroom meeting and the car chase feel elevated because of how they are shot. \n\nThe Austria action sequence captures that old school large scale action filmmaking. Real locations, real vehicles, real destruction. I can feel the reality of impact and the physicality. When the plane skims across the snow or crashes through structures, there is a tangible sense of impact. That physicality is something CGI heavy films sometimes miss. Refreshing to witness such a carefully constructed sequence.\n\nThe story also leans into that classic spy trope where a criminal organization infiltrates and manipulates a spy agency from within. It reminds me of Captain America The Winter Soldier with Hydra inside SHIELD. Also similar ideas appear in Mission Impossible Rogue Nation with the Syndicate operating in the shadows.\n\nThe car train fight was nicely staged and choreographed too. Christoph Waltz was quite terrifying here. He brings that calm but unsettling presence. Andrew Scott, however, is underutilized.\n\nThe explosion in Blofeld’s base is great too with its practicality and reality. But I do wish they held on that moment longer. It deserved more screen time to really let the scale sink in.\n\nGotta give it to Sam Mendes, Hoyte van Hoytema, and Thomas Newman for making this a great Bond film.","poster":"img/spectre-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Caddo Lake","year":2024,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":54,"review":"The family drama and characters were not that interesting for me at first, but everything makes sense as the story unfolds. There are some bittersweet afterthoughts that stayed with me. Those realizations give the story more emotional weight after everything clicks. I get why it was recommended after watching Dark. Definitely worth a rewatch.","poster":"img/caddo-lake-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hit Man","year":2023,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":49,"review":"Richard Linklater is really great at creating a complicated yet very attractive woman. The kind of character who feels charming, unpredictable, and morally messy at the same time. She is not just written to be beautiful, but someone with danger and personality, which makes her more interesting to watch.","poster":"img/hit-man-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Poison","year":2023,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":2,"review":"Thrilling. Theatrical.","poster":"img/poison-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Les Misérables","year":2012,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":87,"review":"Forgot how great the cinematography is here. There are a lot of strong visual moments, and the scale of the sets really adds to the grand feeling of the story. The performances are also great. My only complaint is the excessive use of handheld camera. Sometimes it adds intimacy, but there are moments where it becomes too dizzying for me.\n\nThere are definitely outstanding moments throughout the film, but overall, it feels too long for a story that could have been told in a much shorter runtime.","poster":"img/les-mis-rables-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Good Dinosaur","year":2015,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":81,"review":"The animation for the skin, mountains, and water is great. The environments look beautiful and textured, especially the landscapes and natural elements. Me and my daughter laughed so hard during the scene where they ate something and got high. That part was unexpectedly funny.\n\nThe film is about courage, belonging, accepting loss, leaving your own mark, and finding your way back home. It is a simple story, but the themes are easy to connect with, especially when watching it with family.","poster":"img/the-good-dinosaur-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Apex","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":156,"review":"127 Hours x Fresh x Send Help x Wild.\n\nThe film started off with the Troll Wall not looking that convincing, especially compared to something like Everest (2015), but it turned into nice real sets, while occasionally using practical sets and CGI elements from time to time depending on what the story needs.\n\nSasha’s character feels like Cheryl in Wild (2024). A woman with a tragic past who decides to go on a dangerous adventure, almost like she is trying to outrun or confront something inside herself through physical survival.\n\nTaron Egerton feels like a full blast unhinged James McAvoy here. I could definitely predict he had this kind of performance in him when I watched Billionaire Boys Club (2018), and it was further cemented in She Rides Shotgun (2025). Here, he really leans into that unstable villaneous energy.\n\nI did feel the danger while watching, but not as much as I expected from the trailer.","poster":"img/apex-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Canvas","year":2020,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":9,"review":"One kiss is all it takes by Dua Lipa","poster":"img/canvas-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Michael","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-23","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":155,"review":"The film has this golden and brownish cinematic color palette that gives it a warm feeling.\n\nIt is about family drama and freedom. The father feels like the counterpart of the Colonel in Elvis, treating the artist more like a product to be controlled than a person to be understood.\n\nAfter 1 hour and 10 minutes, the energy ramps up with Beat It. It feels like breaking free from his father is what had been dragging us all down with the pacing of the film. But after that burst, the energy settles again.\n\nCome here if you want a humanized Michael Jackson, rather than a larger than life icon. Maybe that is why the critics hated it, there is not as much singing and dancing as people might expect We all know the songs, but this is a biopic. This reminds me of the pacing of The Aviator, but this one is full of heart.","poster":"img/michael-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mother Mary","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-22","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":45,"review":"All that for a dress?\n\nReminiscent of PTA’s Phantom Thread, but make it look like a highly artistic and elaborate music video with nicely done continuous shots and greatly chosen lighting, very creative! Acting performances and musical score are great.\n\nHeavy metaphorical movie for sure.","poster":"img/mother-mary-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Island","year":2005,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":135,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film carries Michael Bay’s signature DNA, that constant sense of imminent threat and high tension danger. There is a strong cat and mouse energy throughout. The car chases especially feel very Transformers like, chaotic, destructive, and filled with crashing vehicles everywhere. What I like is that the CGI blends well with practical effects, so the action still feels grounded despite the scale.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film also raises an interesting question, do clone lives matter? That idea gives a deeper layer beneath all the action. The final liberation shot is genuinely triumphant. After all the chaos, it gives that satisfying sense of freedom and release.","poster":"img/the-island-2005.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Rango","year":2011,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":68,"review":"An animated film with a strong western motif, elevated by Roger Deakins’ lighting, which makes it feel surprisingly cinematic. The score by Hans Zimmer also adds a lot. It gives the film that heroic and adventurous tone, pushing the story forward with energy and emotion. Story wise, it actually shares similarities with Dune. It is about people fighting over scarce resources and standing up against those in power.","poster":"img/rango-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"True Lies","year":1994,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":111,"review":"I told myself if this is boring, I am just going to watch a James Bond film. But to my surprise, the opening action already grabbed me. Never thought James Cameron could do a spy film with a touch of romcom. Action set pieces show James Cameron’s DNA. The scale feels massive, with real sets and big practical explosions. Flight sequences of jets and the bridge scene are quite impressive. \n\nBut I have to be honest, there are moments where you really have to turn off your brain. Some scenes lean too much into over the top logic, and it can pull you out if you think too much about it.","poster":"img/true-lies-1994.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Other Side of the Box","year":2018,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-18","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":2,"review":"Not scary.","poster":"img/other-side-of-the-box-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Before Sunrise","year":1995,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-17","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":403,"review":"<blockquote>If there’s some kind of magic in this world, it must be in the attempt of understanding someone sharing something</blockquote>\nI am quite sick and have a mild headache. I tried starting two different movies but could not finish either. I just do not have the energy, so I rewatched this light and romantic film to feel good.\n\nWhat surprised me on rewatch, especially now that I have seen the whole trilogy, are the little details. It is just funny that it opens with the German couple fighting. It feels like a foreshadowing of what happens in the third movie, and with the “losing the ability to hear each other” trivia, it fits right into their future.\n\nI also noticed how Celine talks about her father shaping or limiting her ambitions. Fast forward to the third film, and she is frustrated with Jesse for doing almost the same thing, trying to influence her choices when she lands a new dream job.\n\nAnd the “time travel” speech of Jesse turned out to be like a therapeutic thing when they grew up. Sort of a reminder of what used to be love when they were young and what they loved about each other. Some sort of a reminder of what love felt like when everything was still simple.\n\nAnd it is just so funny that they really were not able to watch the Indians looking for the cow play.\n\nI giggled more than I should have during the listening booth. I can literally feel the electric energy in such a cramped space. And the first kiss, I can literally feel their (and mine) heart beating.\n\nI think it is beautiful when Jesse said he would rather excel at something than have a nice caring relationship. It is because fast forward to Before Midnight, it flips.He ends up with a boring career but chose to have a loving and caring relationship with Celine. And it is accompanied by Celine saying “if there’s some kind of magic in this world, it must be in the attempt of understanding someone sharing something”. That is exactly what they keep trying to do as they grow older, even when it gets messy. like when Celine said earlier that there is some good in conflict.\n\nWith the amount of impact and the infinite number of rewatches I would want, I will have to bump this up from 4.5 to 5 stars.","poster":"img/before-sunrise-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Creed","year":2015,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-16","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":143,"review":"<blockquote>You got to believe you can do it.</blockquote>The training montage is one of my favorite parts. I also like how Rocky’s mannerisms slowly come back. Love the long takes, especially the first fight. It pulls me into the ring and makes everything feel more real. The cinematography also stands out during serious moments. The use of slow motion with blood splatter reminds me of Raging Bull. For me, this might be Ryan Coogler’s most cinematic film. Sinners and Black Panther cannot! The musical score is also great. Another Ludwig masterpiece! It really elevates the emotional and motivational beats of the film.\n\nMy only complaint is it leaned too much on the antinepotism drama and the closing chapter of Rocky. Those elements slow down the momentum for me, that is why the momentum went downhill before the third act picked up the pace again.","poster":"img/creed-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Skyfall","year":2012,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":118,"review":"Now this is a proper opening! Controlled and allowing the action to breathe, unlike the opening of Quantum of Solace with its super shaky camera movements and fast cuts.\n\nThe story immediately gives me something to hold on to. There is a clear direction and emotional hook early on. But what really stands out is the cinematography. That's Roger Deakins for you! Some sequences look like somewhere between 1917 and Blade Runner 2049. Roger Deakins overload! This might be the most beautifully shot James Bond film, especially the third act with the silhouettes against the burning fire.\n\nJavier is a great villain here. For me, he is even more effective here than in No Country for Old Men.","poster":"img/skyfall-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Gohan","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":123,"review":"This film feels like a journey through different phases of Gohan’s life, where he experiences both kindness and hardship from the people around him. I liked how it shows the contrast between those who take advantage and those who genuinely care, and how those encounters slowly shape who he becomes. There’s also a quiet sense of growth running underneath, not just for him, but for the people he connects with along the way.\n\nI was smiling the whole time.It just has that feel good energy throughout. Cinematography is nice and shines especially in the circus scenes where the playfulness really shines. At times, the grading feels like a Nescafé commercial, warm, inviting, and a bit dreamy, especially in the moments with Aye Shin.","poster":"img/gohan-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Castle in the Sky","year":1986,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":90,"review":"I really struggled to stay awake here, even during the action sequences. I thought watching it on a big screen would add some charm and immersion, but it still did not work for me.\n\nI can see the appeal though. The adventure and treasure hunt aspect is there, and it reminds me of something like Disney’s Atlantis, especially with the mix of exploration and military involvement. But despite that, I could not stay engaged. I kept dozing off, which made it hard for me to fully connect with the film.","poster":"img/castle-in-the-sky-1986.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"My Neighbor Totoro","year":1988,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":62,"review":"My daughter liked this. She likes Ponyo too, so I can see the pattern of what appeals to her. What I picked up from the film is how, when we teach children to be strong and independent, they sometimes start acting like adults too early. They carry responsibilities and emotions beyond their age and slowly forget that they are still just children.","poster":"img/my-neighbor-totoro-1988.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Father","year":2020,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":116,"review":"Beautiful representation of what is happening inside a person’s mind with dementia. At first it is quite confusing, almost like a mind fuck film where I am trying to piece things together. But as the story goes on, everything slowly becomes clearer, and that confusion starts to make emotional sense. And I can really feel the pain in Anthony’s situation. Being confused all the time, forgetting things, mixing things up, it is exhausting and heartbreaking. Anthony Hopkins was superb. Another newfound respect for this seasoned actor. I can feel all the emotions he's experiencing.\n\nDefinitely worth a rewatch. The drama and storytelling are strong, and I feel like it will hit even harder the second time.","poster":"img/the-father-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Quantum of Solace","year":2008,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":145,"review":"The film starts with chaotic camera movement and very fast cutting. It feels messy and hard to follow at times. The cinematography also feels too clean compared to the previous Bond films, it lost some of the touch of the predecessors, osing some of that gritty texture. Ironically, the only thing that consistently looks  dirty, worn down and raw is Olga Kurylenko’s character.\n\nThe film feels like it suddenly became self aware and snapped back into its natural form. The moment they take the airplane in the desert, the visuals become more controlled. The camera movements settle down, and the cinematography starts to look more composed. \n\nThe main issue for me is the villain and the stakes. They do not feel big enough or threatening enough to match the scale expected from a Bond film. It lacks that strong central force that drives the tension.","poster":"img/quantum-of-solace-2008.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Adrift","year":2018,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":49,"review":"The Fault in Our Scars.\n\nEverything is just serviceable. The film works on a basic level, but nothing really connects with me emotionally. I never got fully invested in the characters or their dire situation. The twist also reminded me of Fall (2022). It follows a similar emotional reveal.","poster":"img/adrift-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Thrash","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-13","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":131,"review":"Visually, it actually looks solid, especially in how it builds the flooded environment with the help of great CGI and sets.\n\nIt feels like a mix of Crawl and Under Paris, with that same setup of a flooded city and a lurking predator slowly picking off survivors one by one. The premise itself has potential because that kind of setting naturally builds tension. But the script really brings it down. It is so stupid in a way that sometimes becomes unintentionally funny, but the actual punch lines are not really good. The humor feels forced.\n\nThe acting also does not help. It feels weak, and I could not connect with any of the characters. Because of that, the tension does not fully work since I do not really care who survives.","poster":"img/thrash-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Before Sunrise","year":1995,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-12","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":259,"review":"The tram ride is one of the most authentic and warm scenes I have ever watched. It feels very sincere. Two free spirited and curious soul, just talking and listening. That feeling when you like someone and you are excited to share everything you love, parang ayaw mo matapos yung usapan as if you have all the time in the world.\n\nAnother standout is the restaurant scene where they pretend to call their friends. It's like they are saying what they really want to say to each other, but in a cute and indirect way. \n\nThis film really captures that raw stage of getting to know someone. Natural, not forced. No obvious flirting. Just two people genuinely connecting and talking about life, big ideas, and everything in between.\n\nJulie Delpy has such a unique and beautiful face. Seems like a mix of Lisa Kudrow and Lisa Vicari. I was simping the whole film! I love her gummy smile, small teeth, and even her windblown hair.\n\nMy experiences with this director was School of Rock and Blue Moon, and I remember a lot of talky scenes there. I can feel the same approach here, but the banter is way better. The acting also sells everything. Their attraction and longing feel very real.\n\nBy the end, I noticed how the places they visited during the day do not feel the same as they do at night. \n\nI think this is the kind of vibe Lost in Translation tried to capture, but for me this one just feels more genuine and complete.","poster":"img/before-sunrise-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Before Sunset","year":2004,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-12","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":237,"review":"I can feel the push and pull between being comfortable and being awkward. They are more of the same, but now matured and shaped by their experiences. The first film is very idealistic, while this one feels more realistic. They are now hyper aware and able to evaluate past relationships without basking in the glimmer of hope. This film digs deeper into what love and connection really mean. Like when Celine said, \"I guess when you're young, you just believe there'll be many people with whom you'll connect with. Later in life, you realize it only happens a few times.\"\n\nI resonated with Julie during the boat scene when she said, \"You can never replace anyone because everyone is made up of such beautiful specific details.\" I teared up with all the what ifs surrounding Jesse. They feel like disruptors of their own lives, like they met at the right time but also the wrong time. They are clearly missing pieces of each other’s lives.\n\nThis film is painfully honest. It is like holding up a mirror.\n\nMy only complaint is the uneven lighting on their faces. Ethan is more lit than Julie in the coffee shop scene. Even during the walk in the park, it heavily relies on natural lighting, and since it is sunset, most shots are backlit. But that is just a minor issue compared to the strength of the script and the story.","poster":"img/before-sunset-2004.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Before Midnight","year":2013,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-12","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":254,"review":"I think this is more about what happens when you marry your childhood sweetheart, and all the \"what ifs\" are now \"what is\". Their world is no longer just about the two of them. It extends to families and friends. That is why the film's treatment feels different, especially when they are not walking together in the same frame. There is a sense of separation even when they are technically still together.\n\nEven if you spent years and years with a person, you still do not know 100% of that person. There are always layers that remain hidden or unresolved. Celine still carries that desire to explore and be free. She feels even more outspoken here, leaning into her identity and independence. That contrast between freedom and commitment becomes more visible.\n\nThis film feels brutally honest about marriage. The unsaid feelings, both big and small, slowly pile up. The amount of sacrifice, expectations, and responsibilities. Couples start keeping score, who gives more, who gives less. And I can relate to that. I am guilty of thinking that way too.\n\nThe ending feels funny yet real at the same time. It shows that love is not just a feeling but a constant decision. It is about choosing each other every second, every opportunity, staying, and putting band aid solution after band aid solution that can be ripped apart every time, but still you have the choice to patch it up again and again and again, for as long as you are willing to stay married.","poster":"img/before-midnight-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Curve","year":2016,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":45,"review":"Sound effects and acting are both great. The situation itself makes me nervous, like I am about to fall with her. The fear is breath taking. It reminds me of Fall 2022 in terms of how it builds fear from height and helplessness. Super tense!!","poster":"img/curve-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Black Hole","year":2008,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":10,"review":"Don’t be too greedy, that’s where you’ll be caught easy.","poster":"img/the-black-hole-2008.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Cargo","year":2013,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":39,"review":"Father and child in a zombie apocalypse trope, but this one has more heart. Emotional bond and survival. It actually made me want to watch the full length version. Glad I discovered this gem. Hopefully the full length delivers.","poster":"img/cargo-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lights Out","year":2013,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":38,"review":"Scared shitless. I literally had to mute the sound just to get through it. The simplicity of the concept makes it even more terrifying. No escape. This instantly made me add the 2016 full length to my watchlist.","poster":"img/lights-out-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Alive in Joburg","year":2005,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":56,"review":"Good short film that really works as a pitch piece. I can clearly see how it laid the foundation for one of the most memorable sci-fi alien films to date. It already captures that raw, documentary style and social commentary, making the world feel believable even with aliens in it. Such a strong proof of concept!","poster":"img/alive-in-joburg-2005.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Out-Laws","year":2023,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":52,"review":"Fun heist and a funny B movie. I laughed so hard. This is one of those just turn off your brain and enjoy kind of films.\n\nThe graveyard set and the action sequence there are actually nice. It adds a bit of style to the chaos and makes that part stand out.","poster":"img/the-out-laws-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Schindler's List","year":1993,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":209,"review":"On my first watch, I thought this was just about a capitalist exploiting people through his own version of forced labor. But now that I know the ending, I see it differently. He was playing along with the system, acting evil to ultimately do good. That duality makes his character much more powerful.\n\nThe liquidation of the ghetto is brutal. It is one of the hardest sequences to sit through. What struck me more this time is the level of denial from the victims. But I cannot blame them. What the Nazis were doing was unthinkable. It is more horrifying on rewatch.\n\nThe shadows and framing also tell so much of the story. The way characters are placed and lit reflects power, fear, and control. The scale is massive. The number of extras alone is overwhelming! The score is also superb. It gave me goosebumps multiple times.\n\nOn my first viewing, I cried during Oskar Schindler’s breakdown when he said he could have gotten more. But this time, I started tearing up earlier, when the soldiers turned their back instead of committing murder. To me, that signals that the worst is finally over.\n\nLiam Neeson, Ben Kingsley, and Ralph Fiennes all delivered a masterclass in acting. Truly a masterpiece!","poster":"img/schindler-s-list-1993.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"How to Make a Killing","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-10","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","a24","nowwatching"],"words":117,"review":"The first to second act plays like a revenge checklist. Step by step, calculated, almost procedural in how things unfold. I honestly thought the third act would fall apart, but it actually works because of the manifesto. That idea of bypassing conscience ties everything together. When it clicks, the title becomes very literal, and it recontextualizes everything that came before. Definitely worth a second watch to pick up subtle things with this new knowledge. \n\nMy only complaint is Julia’s motivation. It is not fully fleshed out. But I also feel like that might be intentional? Like unhinged and unthinkable mindset can exist without clean explanations, especially when the film’s message starts to bleed into real life thinking.","poster":"img/how-to-make-a-killing-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"5 Centimeters per Second","year":2025,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":108,"review":"It feels like Kimi no Na Wa mixed with Past Lives. That same sense of longing, distance, and timing that never quite aligns.\n\nIt is beautifully shot. The visuals are stunning and carry a lot of emotion on their own. But the pacing is sluggish for me. I kept looking at my watch. The male character also spends a lot of time just staring from a distance, which adds to that slow, dragging feeling.\n\nFate keeps getting in the way of them reuniting. Parang Voyager 1 and 2, just floating somewhere and slowly drifting further apart. It is a story full of missed opportunities, almosts, and close calls.","poster":"img/5-centimeters-per-second-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Bad Genius","year":2017,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":173,"review":"Nice cinematography and strong color grading. I am honestly surprised how good this Thai film is.\n\nI really like the idea of turning music and finger movements into something like Morse code for the answers. Even the barcode trick was clever. The whole setup has that heist energy, planning, decoding, and executing under pressure just to get the answers. It also gets very stress inducing. At times it reminded me of Uncut Gems with how tense everything feels. This might be the first time I found myself rooting for a cheater. That alone says a lot about how engaging the film is.\n\nI am also glad it did not focus too much on rivalry. If it did, it would have just turned into another eat or be eaten story. Instead, it stays focused on the system and the choices they make.\n\nThe ending touches on integrity versus greed. Both characters go through character development, one moving in a better direction while the other falls deeper. That contrast gives the film a strong closing.","poster":"img/bad-genius-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Locke","year":2013,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":288,"review":"If having a BMW means living a life like this, just keep it! Just shove it down your throat!\n\nTom Hardy’s Ivan Locke reminds me of Alfie from Peaky Blinders. That same controlled intensity in the way he speaks. He sounds calm, almost composed, but underneath there is pressure and chaos building up. Just like Alfie, he uses words as his weapon. The rhythm of his voice carries authority, but also vulnerability.\n\nI honestly thought this would be boring. A guy just driving and talking on the phone sounds like nothing. But the conversations were engaging enough to carry me through the first and second act. Every call adds another layer of problem, another piece of his life falling apart. The third act though started to drag for me. And what made it worse is that I did not feel a strong emotional payoff at the end. \n\nWell he is a very honest and transparent man. He is trying so hard not to be like his father, trying to fix everything properly. But reality does not always cooperate. Not everything can be controlled. Control is just an illusion.\n\nThis whole situation is honestly a nightmare. You got a woman pregnant who is not your wife and she is about to give birth. At the same time you have a huge responsibility at work the next day. This becomes the saddest road trip. Working from the car, ending a marriage from the car, becoming an absentee father from the car, and even getting fired from the car.\n\nIn a weird way, I even think this is a better treatment of Drive (2011). It strips everything down to just a man, his decisions, and the consequences catching up to him.","poster":"img/locke-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ivan's Childhood","year":1962,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":54,"review":"Ivan’s childhood is beautifully shot. The cinematography really stands out. The contrast between the dreamlike imagery and the harsh reality of war is visually striking. But despite how good it looks, I just could not connect with the story and the characters. I appreciate the craft, but I stayed emotionally distant the whole time.","poster":"img/ivan-s-childhood-1962.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"GoldenEye","year":1995,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":147,"review":"It feels like a mix of old school filmmaking transitioning into a more modern style. You can still see the classic Bond DNA in the use of miniatures, and practical stunts, but at the same time it starts leaning into faster pacing, more aggressive editing, and a slightly grittier tone compared to earlier entries. Not fully classic, not fully modern yet.\n\nThe sets are not as impressive as the earlier Bond films. There are still a lot of miniatures. When it finally commits to a large scale action set piece like the tank chase in the city, it oddly feels goofy for a James Bond standard. It is big and loud, but it loses that sleek and coolness that I expect from Bond.\n\nThe villain’s motivation is also weak for me. It is there, but it does not feel strong or compelling enough to elevate the conflict.","poster":"img/goldeneye-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Everest","year":2015,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":88,"review":"Everything feels believable. I honestly could not tell how much was shot on real locations versus sets. The world feels fully realized, and the makeup is also great. It adds to the realism and makes the characters feel grounded in that environment.\n\nThe third act is definitely the best part. That is where everything finally comes together and delivers the tragedy and payoff. The first to second act felt a bit dragging for me. It takes time to build, but not all of it kept me fully engaged.","poster":"img/everest-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Sound of Music","year":1965,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-09","tags":[],"words":165,"review":"The melodies of the songs feel like old Disney animation music for me. They have that simple, memorable, almost storybook quality where each song carries emotion and narrative at the same time. Full of heart, guiding the story forward while staying catchy and warm.\n\nWhen the kids finally appeared, I already got the impression that this would be a warm and happy film. I especially love the Do Re Mi sequence in the open field. That majestic backdrop combined with the playful teaching of music feels so liberating. It feels rebellious like breaking free from strict rules through music.\n\nThe lighting is also great. Even with the high key lighting that makes everything bright and inviting, it still maintains strong shadows to give depth to the image. Then when it shifts to low key lighting, especially in silhouette scene, it becomes more dramatic and visually striking.\n\nThe sets are incredible too. Everything feels grand and carefully designed, adding to the timeless charm of the film.","poster":"img/the-sound-of-music-1965.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Incredibles 2","year":2018,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":162,"review":"The villain is quite predictable. I could already see where it was going early on, so the reveal did not really hit.\n\nIt is still fun, but the stakes in the first film feel more perilous. I am not sure if it is just nostalgia, but the danger and urgency in the original felt stronger. \n\nOne thing I have to point out though is that the animation is much better here. The new characters and their superpowers are visually impressive. The way their abilities are animated adds a lot of creativity to the action.\n\nThe first half to the second half felt a bit boring for me. It focuses on the wife stepping into the spotlight while the husband struggles with taking care of the kids. I get the intention, but it did not fully engage me. But the third act goes all out. That is where the film finally becomes exciting and delivers the kind of energy I was waiting for.","poster":"img/incredibles-2-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Oppenheimer","year":2023,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":331,"review":"I forgot how haunting the score is right from the start. It mirrors Oppenheimer’s anxiety, then slowly shifts into something hopeful when he begins building the Manhattan Project team. Then it turns tense again as they prepare for the first bomb test. And that silence after the explosion is brutal. I still remember watching it in IMAX. When the sound wave finally hit and the seats vibrated, it was a one of a kind experience. Silence is also used in a suffocating way during the town hall scene. The tight lens makes everything feel compressed and suffocating, and I can really feel Oppenheimer’s guilt closing in on him.\n\nThe cinematography is excellent, especially in how it shapes faces. Hoyte van Hoytema really did a great job here. The black and white sections are good, but they feel more modern than classic. They do not have that old school texture, I just hope Nolan pushed it that far.\n\n\"Amateur seek the sun. Get eaten. Power stays in the dark\". I took this to heart when I first heard this with Strauss, like when I read the quote in Vice 2018: “Beware the quiet man. For while others speak, he watches. And while others act, he plans. And when they finally rest… he strikes.” Both ideas carry that same warning about power operating in silence.\n\nThe first time I watched this, I was so focused on the anticipation of the atomic bomb that I did not fully appreciate how strong everything else is. The cinematography, acting, score, dialogue, and structure all stand out more now.\n\nThe story is non linear. Nolan plays with the timeline as the truth slowly surfaces, which makes the narrative more layered but also more demanding to follow. I do want to rate this higher, but the intricateness of the politics and the long runtime make it harder for me to fully embrace it.\n\nAt its core, this film is about ego, politics, power, misconception, retaliation, loyalty, and manipulation.","poster":"img/oppenheimer-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Super Mario Bros. Movie","year":2023,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-07","tags":[],"words":95,"review":"It feels adventurous, but in a more grounded way. It is not overly chaotic or overstimulating like the second movie. The film keeps things fun without going too wild, which makes the journey easier to follow and enjoy.\n\nThe Mario Kart scene is a highlight. It literally feels like Mad Max Fury Road. I also feel like the stakes are bigger here. The climax feels more heroic. Saving Luigi just works better emotionally than saving the princess in the second movie. There is more connection there, so the payoff hits stronger and feels more heroic.","poster":"img/the-super-mario-bros-movie-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Boy in the Striped Pyjamas","year":2008,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-06","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":195,"review":"I like both Schmuel’s and Bruno’s acting. One is confused about why he is suffering something forced on him, while the other is slowly processing and questioning everything he has been taught. You can really see it in Schmuel and Bruno’s eyes. And seeing Schmuel push that heavy wheelbarrow with such a fragile body is just so heartbreaking.\n\nI also like Vera’s acting, especially when she finally realizes what is really happening beyond their backyard. That moment hits because I can feel the shift from ignorance to horror.\n\nThe film feels like a mix of The Zone of Interest with that idea of living beside a concentration camp, Jojo Rabbit with the propaganda and brainwashing of innocent children, and Grave of the Fireflies with children suffering because of a war they did not choose to be part of.\n\nOh the innocence! Why do they have to corrupt something so pure?! The ending made me hold my breath for a long time. My eyes were wide open, just shocked and in disbelief. What a twist! I am really glad no one spoiled this for me. After it ended, I just felt empty again. Stop the wars.","poster":"img/the-boy-in-the-striped-pyjamas-2008.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Soulmate","year":2023,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-06","tags":[],"words":101,"review":"Visually it reminds me of a nicely lit and colored K-drama, but with touches of A24 style in certain scenes. There are moments with higher contrast and low key lighting mixed with neon colors that give it a more intimate atmosphere.\n\nThe acting is expertly done by Kim Da-mi and Jeon So-nee. They feel believable even as they portray different stages of their lives. It starts off as a light and warm film, then slowly becomes heavier as the characters grow older. I just wished it stayed light and warm rather than becoming a bitter unsaid feelings with healing nuances film.","poster":"img/soulmate-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Big Lebowski","year":1998,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-05","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":149,"review":"Jeff Bridges here really reminds me of Joaquin Phoenix in Inherent Vice. The way he talks, the laid back demeanor, the whole vibe. Same wavelength. But I have to be honest, Jeff is still more charming as a bum.  \n\nI can definitely see similarities with Inherent Vice, especially with the whole chasing a kidnapped girl plot and the trippy visuals. But this one feels more engaging and crazier. It leans fully into its weirdness and just runs with it. I would not be surprised if PTA took some inspiration from this. \n\nThe ensemble cast is also great. Everyone brings something unique to the chaos. Julianne Moore’s entrance is also memorable. Another highly sexualized role, but the way she comes in is confident and striking. It immediately grabs attention.\n\nThat shot from inside the bowling ball is really cool tho. It adds to the film’s playful and trippy visual style.","poster":"img/the-big-lebowski-1998.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Spy Who Loved Me","year":1977,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-05","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":295,"review":"To be frank, there are some really nice cinematic shots here, especially in the desert sequences. I also love the Egyptian production design, especially when Roger Moore is brandishing his pistol. Super cool! Then it contrasts nicely with Stromberg’s Atlantis set, which feels more futuristic and watery aesthetics. And that car turning into a submarine with the underwater sequence, wow! Super ambitious. I cannot believe they actually made that work on screen.\n\nThe scale of the production design is huge. I honestly could not tell which parts were miniatures and which were full scale sets. That is how good it looks. I am also convinced that the third action sequence was shot on a massive sound stage. The number of extras alone is insane! Everything feels massive!\n\nThe special effects are also impressive. The explosions, the atomic detonation, even the flooding of entire sets. Those sequences really stand out.\n\nUnlike Moonraker where I was hooked early then lost interest in the third act, this one is the opposite for me. It did not fully grab me until the second act, then it started to pick up.\n\nBut overall, this still feels like a lighter Bond era. The tone is not that serious, and Bond still gets into trouble because of his weakness for women. But this time it is not just any Bond girl, it is Anya. I like how 007 and Agent Triple X are constantly trying to outsmart each other. Their dynamic is fun to watch. Barbara Bach is also miles better than Lois Chiles for me.\n\nSince I already saw Richard Kiel in Moonraker, he does not feel as creepy here anymore. But still, I cannot believe this has the same director as Moonraker. This one just hits different. This is lit!","poster":"img/the-spy-who-loved-me-1977.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Following","year":1998,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-04-05","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":149,"review":"This is a rewatch for me because I did not fully get it the first time. Now that my eyes are more used to black and white films, I can appreciate it more. \n\nIt feels like a confession from an unreliable person. The way the story unfolds makes me question what is real and what is being manipulated. I can already see an early version of Nolan’s idea of “planting the seed,” similar to Inception. Making someone believe that an idea is their own choice when in reality they are being controlled. That concept is already present here in a simpler form. Cobb is a really good liar. The way he manipulates everyone and ends up triple crossing them is brilliant. He plays them all.\n\nI am really glad I gave this a second try. From a 3 star before, this easily becomes a 4 star for me now.","poster":"img/following-1998.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Polytechnique","year":2009,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":44,"review":"A Denis Villeneuve film where I can really feel the tension through the characters’ motivations and the musical score. The way the scenes build pressure is suffocating. It makes it hard to breathe. I was completely locked in. It is so shocking and gripping!","poster":"img/polytechnique-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Drama","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":159,"review":"Funny banter from the start, and I like that there are more lines for Alana Haim. Her range is highlighted here.\n\nThis is not something like Die My Love, Challengers, or The Worst Person in the World, though it has some elements of them. Makes sense? It leans into something more controversial, something I would not expect to be integrated into a romcom. That shift makes it stand out because it plays with expectations.\n\nThe turning point really comes from that last minute dealbreaker with the fiancée. That moment changes everything. It sends the story into a wild turn of events and completely shifts how I see the person I thought I already understood. Ang bilis mag flip ng perception.\n\nAlso, the coincidence that I watched this right after Polytechnique is wild. I do not want to spoil the fun, but you will get it once you watch this.\n\nAnd the ending… oh the things you do for love!","poster":"img/the-drama-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Super Mario Galaxy Movie","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-04","tags":[],"words":125,"review":"Surprisingly good! I had no expectations going in, but wow just wow. The camera movements are superb and there are a lot of shots that really pop out even if the IMAX version I watched was not in 3D. I can only imagine how much more it would hit if it was actually in 3D. It is honestly the first time in a long while I felt that kind of visual excitement, probably since Megamind or Minions where the format really adds to the experience.\n\nThe score is also great. Very heroic and uplifting, it matches the energy of the film perfectly.\n\nI also like that there is a reconnecting with sisters storyline. It actually made me think, is it a recurring theme for 2026?","poster":"img/the-super-mario-galaxy-movie-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A Ghost Story","year":2017,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":243,"review":"What if the afterlife is just staying behind and watching everything you left? Seeing your loved ones suffer because of your loss, then slowly watching them heal, move on, and forget you as time stretches into what feels like eternity. What if you are stuck in the same place even when everything around you is already gone And what does it feel like to watch the people you love continue their lives without you? What is the meaning of life if in the end you will just fade into oblivion?\n\nThis film really leans into that kind of existential thinking. It is the definition of a slow burn. Honestly, it can feel like a nightmare for people with short attention spans. The director lets the scenes breathe and breathe and breathe to the point that it almost feels pointless at times. But I get that it is intentional. It wants me to sit with the emptiness and the feeling of being stuck.\n\nIn the end, I am just glad he finally let go. After everything, that release feels like the only peace the film can offer.\n\nThis is not for everyone. Only watch this if you are ready for something deeply reflective about loneliness and existence, and if you can stay focused without getting sleepy. The score helps a lot though. It adds emotional weight even when almost nothing is happening on screen. Much better on rewatch. From .5 star to 3 star.","poster":"img/a-ghost-story-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Harakiri","year":1962,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":105,"review":"It feels like something pulled straight from an old album. Every frame looks like a photograph. Truly a motion picture in the literal sense. The lighting, framing, and lens choices are superb. Each shot feels carefully composed, like it is meant to be remembered.\n\nI also like how the slow burn storytelling unfolds. Now I can see why a lot of Hollywood directors admire this film. The control and discipline in the filmmaking are very evident.\n\nThis film becomes a one man versus a lying system kind of story. A character trying to stand his ground against something much bigger and more corrupt than him.","poster":"img/harakiri-1962.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Seven Samurai","year":1954,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-02","tags":[],"words":205,"review":"There are silent stretches in this film where the performances really shine. I like how the farmers actually look like farmers, struggling, hungry, and worn down by life. That level of realism makes the story stick more. It is also why I enjoyed the assembling of the seven samurai. Surprisingly, their bond becomes fun and light even with the danger waiting for them. Even the villagers slowly change because of their presence. From fearful and helpless, they begin to grow more courageous.\n\nThe highlights and shadows are also great. Even in the earlier parts of the film, I could already tell why this holds so much prestige. The lighting is so controlled, even in outdoor scenes. The sunlight never feels like it is just washing everything out. The image still has shape and depth. Even the night cinematography feels way ahead of its time. This film does not feel old at all. Honestly, it looks better than some modern films.\n\nThe strategy in the story is also really good. The film makes the preparation feel just as important as the battles. On top of that, there are so many great and likeable characters. The level of direction and coordination here is insane. Truly a masterpiece!","poster":"img/seven-samurai-1954.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Paths of Glory","year":1957,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":71,"review":"The scale of no man’s land is great, but the sound feels blown out and harsh at times.\n\nThis is more of a courtroom drama than a full blown war film, and I actually find that interesting. Instead of just focusing on battle, it exposes the ugly ego and mindset inside the military. Power, pride, and self preservation take over so much that they forget how to be human and compassionate.","poster":"img/paths-of-glory-1957.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mike & Nick & Nick & Alice","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-01","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":52,"review":"Serviceable comedy and action. It gets the job done but nothing really stands out. There are moments where it slows down and loses a bit of energy, but overall it is still fine. It delivers enough fun and movement to keep it watchable, just not something that leaves a strong impression after.","poster":"img/mike-nick-nick-alice-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Pope's Exorcist","year":2023,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-04-01","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":100,"review":"The boy’s acting is great. He really sells the possession. The sets are also incredible, lalo na yung hidden underground. That part adds a lot to the atmosphere. It feels ancient, secretive, and dangerous. \n\nI also like the idea that a possessed priest was the one influencing the queen to carry out the Inquisition. That concept is actually brilliant. It adds a layer of corruption from within the church itself. It reminded me of Seklusyon, where evil infiltrates the church to do its bidding from the inside.\n\n<blockquote>Jesus of Nazareth commands you. And now, I adjure you in His name!</blockquote>","poster":"img/the-pope-s-exorcist-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Citizen Kane","year":1941,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-31","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":176,"review":"It feels like a reinventing the wheel kind of entrepreneur story, someone building power by acquiring and outmaneuvering competitors. That rise to dominance becomes the backbone of the film. Kane’s presence even reminds me of Don Corleone in how he builds and maintains his empire. There is that same aura of control and influence, someone who shapes the world around him while slowly being shaped by it too.\n\nThe use of shadows on the face is really great. Whenever his face is covered in shadow, it feels like he is being left in the dark, sidelined, or overshadowed in terms of power. The film uses lighting, framing, and blocking so well to show who is in control in a scene. It actually reminded me of how Schindler’s List uses composition to express power dynamics.\n\nThe film also touches on media manipulation. It shows how influence over information can be used for selfish purposes, shaping public perception to serve personal ambition. Overall, it feels like a study of power, control, and the cost of building an empire.","poster":"img/citizen-kane-1941.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Goldfinger","year":1964,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-31","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":112,"review":"Sean Connery really carried this film. His presence alone makes everything feel more engaging. The confidence, the charm, and that effortless coolness define the whole experience.\n\nI personally like the set design, especially during the briefing scene of Project Grand Slam. The moving furniture and opening floors are really impressive. It feels futuristic for its time and adds a sense of spectacle to the villain’s plan. Goldfinger’s plan itself is actually great. It is not just about stealing wealth but manipulating value on a larger scale, which makes it more interesting than a typical heist.\n\nThis is just a simple Bond film, but elevated by Connery’s performance and a memorable villain concept.","poster":"img/goldfinger-1964.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"La Haine","year":1995,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-30","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":102,"review":"Bunch of youths with nothing to do but disrupt peace. Dude just get a job! It reminded me of those characters in Netflix’s Steve, that same restless and directionless energy. Honestly I do not enjoy watching these kinds of delinquents. But I have to admit, it started to get my attention when they went to Astérix’s apartment. That stretch felt more focused and interesting compared to the earlier parts.\n\nVinz’s actor is really great though. Nakakainis talaga yung mukha niya. Even in other films he carries that same energy. You just want to punch him, which means he is doing something right.","poster":"img/la-haine-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jane Austen's Period Drama","year":2024,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":76,"review":"Well it is actually funny when it finally clicked why that is the title. I think the film works as a satire on how men can be so dumb to marry a woman without really knowing who she is or what she is capable of. I would honestly love to see this expanded into a feature length film. It feels like there is still so much more that could be explored with that concept and humor.","poster":"img/jane-austen-s-period-drama-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Gray Man","year":2022,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":462,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFeels like Mission: Impossible Fallout, especially in the attempt to create friction between the lead and the villain, but this never reaches that same level of precision or control. The banter feels forced to me. Wagner Moura is easily the standout, and Jessica Henwick also leaves a better impression here than I expected.\n\nMost of the action does not do much for me, but there are a couple of sequences where the Russo brothers suddenly show flashes of tactical energy and sharper staging. Even so, the film mostly feels like another reminder that outside the Marvel grandeur, their work has not been hitting for me. Instead of feeling slick or exciting, a lot of it just feels overly familiar, too eager to impress, and not as interesting as it thinks it is.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe character dynamic reminds me of Mission Impossible Fallout, especially the tension between Tom Cruise and Henry Cavill. But this one lacks that clear vision and control that Cruise and Christopher McQuarrie brings. The sarcastic banter between Ryan Gosling and Chris Evans also did not work for me. It feels forced, like it is trying too hard to be funny while also trying to present Evans as a threatening villain. Even Regé Jean Page did not feel convincing. He comes across more like a cry baby trying to act tough.\n\nThere are also moments that felt very predictable. The trap door scene was obvious. I had a straight face the whole time. The use of FPV drones was not that impressive either. And when the hedge labyrinth appeared, I already knew someone was going to die there. Sobrang cliché. Naparoll eyes talaga ako.\n\nThe best part of the film for me is Wagner Moura. He feels like a completely different person in this role. He stands out. I also liked that Jessica Henwick had more screen time here compared to her role in The Force Awakens.\n\nMost of the action sequences are just meh, except for the plaza shootout. That scene where Alpha, Bravo, Delta, SWAT, and the police are all moving together was really good. Doon nag all in ang Russo brothers. It reminded me of the tactical movement in Captain America The Winter Soldier. Another highlight is Ana de Armas during the mansion bombing in the final act. \n\nBut overall, this film just proves to me that the Russo brothers are not as interesting without the Avengers. Cherry and The Electric State also show that pattern. Medyo nakakatakot tuloy isipin what they might do with RDJ in Doomsday, especially after how they handled Chris Evans’ villain here.","poster":"img/the-gray-man-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ambulance","year":2022,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":155,"review":"Feels like Heat with its heist and danger vibes but seen through the lens of Michael Bay. The scale and chaos are bigger, more explosive, but the core tension of a crime gone wrong is still there.\n\nThe money shot for me was the chase with the ambulance and the helicopter in the same frame. Super cool. That kind of shot really shows how Bay maximizes movement and scale at the same time.\n\nThe acting is also strong. Jake Gyllenhaal, Yahya Abdul Mateen II, and Eiza González all deliver. I cannot say much more than that because their eyes alone already show what is going on in their minds. The fear, panic, and desperation are all there.\n\nThe lighting also stands out, especially how it highlights their eyes during intense moments. It adds to the emotional tension even in the middle of chaos.\n\nWell, Michael Bay did it again. Nail biting heist and crime thriller.","poster":"img/ambulance-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Public Enemies","year":2009,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-29","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":84,"review":"The cinematography sometimes feels like a documentary. The use of handheld cameras, and natural lighting makes certain scenes look almost like real footage rather than a period film. Parang may pagka on the ground observer feel, like the camera is just there witnessing events as they unfold. \n\nThis film feels like Heat but set in The Godfather’s classic gangster era. Cat and mouse dynamic between law enforcement and criminals, but placed in a 1930s setting with suits, tommy guns, and old school bank robberies.","poster":"img/public-enemies-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Guy and Madeline on a Park Bench","year":2009,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":102,"review":"The trumpet and tap dance scene immediately got my attention. That is probably one of the best moments in the film, along with the diner scene and the final musical sequence. You can really feel the love for music in those parts.\n\nI can already see the early DNA of Damien Chazelle here. The crazy panning shots and the way the music drives the storytelling are clearly ideas he would later refine in La La Land. It is interesting seeing those elements in a more raw form. At the same time, the handheld 16mm style can be a bit dizzying for me.","poster":"img/guy-and-madeline-on-a-park-bench-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Whiplash","year":2013,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":62,"review":"Still carries the same fear and tension even in this shorter version. Even in this early form, it is really, really great. \n\nThe lighting and camera work are still great. It may not have Linus Sandgren’s signature color intensity yet, but the visual style is already strong. The shadows, the focus on faces, and the tight framing already create that suffocating pressure.","poster":"img/whiplash-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Dr. Strangelove or: How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb","year":1964,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":587,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat unsettles me most is how calmly it presents something so catastrophic, as if total destruction can be triggered by routine, ego, and blind protocol. The absurd logic and world ending stakes is where the dark comedy really works for me. Visually, I think the black and white cinematography is beautiful, and the script is sharp enough to carry both tension and satire at the same time. The tone is what makes it memorable for me, even if one major character choice does not fully land. I end up admiring the film more for its irony, staging, and terrifying logic than for every individual performance choice.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI can really feel the confusion and fear the moment they received the Wing Attack Plan R. That sudden shift from normal daily operations to irreversible action is terrifying. It shows how fragile the system is, especially when one low ranking official goes haywire. Add to that someone believing in conspiracy theories about the enemy. It becomes clear how dangerous it is to rely on rigid protocols in something as catastrophic as nuclear war. There is no room for error, yet the film shows how easy it is for things to go wrong.\n\nIt feels like a House of Dynamite film but flipped. Instead of the US waiting for a nuclear strike, they are the ones who initiated it. It becomes a story about self destruction rather than defense.\n\nThe dark comedy comes from the absurdity of logic. They are discussing something extremely serious, yet the way they talk about it makes it feel almost casual.  There are also moments that feel like satire like the chewing gum bit. The way products and trivial things are inserted into such a serious situation adds another layer of absurd humor. Then you have characters like General Ripper whose obsession with preserving his “essence” reminded me of Lockjaw's \"semon demon\" logic in One Battlw After Another.\n\nVisually, the black and white cinematography is beautiful. The contrast enhances the mood and gives the film a timeless quality. The script is also sharp, carrying both tension and humor seamlessly. The aerial scenes are great. The way the planes are shot makes them feel powerful and real. The ground combat scenes are also well done. Kubrick really knows how to stage these sequences as I also love Full Metal Jacket.\n\nThe score adds another interesting layer. It uses music in a way that contrasts with the events on screen. Instead of making everything feel heavier, it sometimes gives a strange sense of irony. The music almost softens the tension while the situation itself becomes more dangerous, which makes the overall tone even more unsettling.\n\nI would have loved to give this a 5 star, but Dr. Strangelove himself felt so out of place for me. Everything else worked, but his scenes did not land the same way. He is just too weird, and for me he does not add as much impact to the storytelling compared to the other characters. Honestly, I do not even feel like the film’s title should be named after him. It would have made more sense if it focused on General Ripper or even the President. His paraplegic condition, the uncontrollable twitching, the Nazi salute, and the accent all felt cringe to me. It really pulled me out of the experience.","poster":"img/dr-strangelove-or-how-i-learned-to-stop-worrying-and-love-the-bomb-1964.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Daisies","year":1966,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":64,"review":"Too creative and artistic for a 60s film. It feels way ahead of its time. The editing, the visuals, and the overall style feel bold and experimental, like it is breaking rules on purpose.\n\nI can imagine how provocative this must have been back then. The way it plays with structure and behavior feels rebellious, almost like it is challenging what is considered acceptable.","poster":"img/daisies-1966.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ponyo","year":2008,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":48,"review":"The friendship is really cute, and the animation has that warm, magical charm that makes everything feel alive and comforting. My 4 year old daughter responded to it strongly. I could really feel how much wonder and joy the film carries through its visuals, energy, and childlike heart.","poster":"img/ponyo-2008.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Cat Returns","year":2002,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":2,"review":"Background noise.","poster":"img/the-cat-returns-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Back to the Future Part III","year":1990,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":96,"review":"Such a fun, adventurous, and lighthearted franchise. I really cannot get enough of it. And putting it in an old western setting was an incredible idea. The visuals alone already make it feel fresh again while still keeping the same charm.\n\nI like how this one gives more depth to Doc Emmett’s character. Seeing him in love adds another layer to him. Thomas F. Wilson as Buford Tannen is also great here. He completely disappears into the role. \n\nIt still carries that same energy of fun, chaos, and clever storytelling. Great ending to a fantastic trilogy!","poster":"img/back-to-the-future-part-iii-1990.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Roofman","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":173,"review":"It starts off fun and slightly romantic with bits of family drama. The tone is initially comedic, then slowly shifts into something more serious. I like how he was persistent in earning Leigh’s daughters’ trust, especially Lindsay who presents herself as \"emotionally unavailable\". \n\nThis film is about a criminal living a life on the run, building lies on top of lies. It is the kind of story that makes me root for the criminal to get away with it, not because he is a good person, but because he is not entirely bad either. He just feels desperate, trying to provide for his family in the only way he knows.\n\nThe film is really well made and I was engaged the whole time. The acting is great, especially from Channing and Kirsten. Their eyes alone carry so much emotion. I think this is the first time I have seen both of them this great.\n\nThe poster looks goofy, but the film is not. So yeah, do not judge a film by its poster!","poster":"img/roofman-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kandahar","year":2023,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":434,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis movie is a solid cat and mouse war thriller elevated by strong cinematography and tension driven action. What really stands out to me is how well the film captures the desert environment. The wide landscape shots make the terrain feel both beautiful and oppressive, which gives the whole chase a more cinematic quality.\n\nWhat keeps me engaged most is the modern warfare atmosphere. The film uses scale, movement, and combat staging in a way that feels grounded and intense, even if the overall story is still familiar. For me, it works best as a visually sharp popcorn action movie.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nPopcorn movie about a cat and mouse chase between a CIA asset and terrorists. It reminds me of films like Body of Lies, 13 Hours, and even parts of Sicario where the tension comes from constantly being hunted in unfamiliar territory.\n\nWhat really stood out to me is how the cinematographer clearly understood the assignment. The desert sequences are beautiful. It almost looks like a car commercial for long drives across harsh terrain. The wide landscape shots make the environment feel massive and oppressive at the same time. \n\nI especially like the night cinematography during Thomas versus helicopter sequence. That whole stretch builds tension so well. It feels inescapable, like death is just hovering above. The visuals keep shifting from natural night photography to night vision, then to thermal imaging. That black and white heat signature look adds another layer of intensity and makes the sequence feel more tactical. So raw and very modern warfare style!\n\nThe rescue of Roman is also a strong action set piece. The use of real explosions and heavy gunfire makes it feel grounded and chaotic. What I found interesting is how it shows different Muslim factions fighting each other while all believing they are doing it for the same cause. That contradiction adds weight to the conflict.\n\nThe final stretch in Kandahar is pure scale and spectacle. I cannot even imagine how many explosives were used to create those massive blasts! What a way to close the story. \n\nThere are so many factions involved, and Ismail’s survival strategy stands out. He switches sides depending on what benefits him most. It reminded me of that idea in The 48 Laws of Power, adapting to whoever holds the advantage at the moment.\n\nOverall, it is a cliche action movie, but elevated by strong cinematography and well executed set pieces.","poster":"img/kandahar-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Groundhog Day","year":1993,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":438,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis one hooks me quickly because it starts with a lead character who is clearly selfish, sarcastic, and hard to root for. That makes the premise more interesting for me, because the film is not just asking what I would do if there were no tomorrow, it is also asking what kind of person I become when consequences disappear. The comedy lands because it pushes that idea in ways that feel both absurd and uncomfortably human.\n\nWhat I enjoy most is how the film slowly expands beyond the gimmick. It starts playful and entertaining, but I can already feel that there is something deeper underneath the repetition. For me, that is what makes it work. It is funny on the surface, but there is also a quiet sadness in the idea of being stuck with myself over and over again until I am forced to change.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nOh I love time loop movies! The film immediately establishes that he is an asshole and very sarcastic from the start. I can already tell he is the kind of person who only thinks about himself.\n\nThe whole premise begs the question, what would I do if there is no tomorrow? If I only have one day to live but it keeps repeating, what would I do? The film goes all out with that idea. He gets arrested, hooks up with random girls, eats everything he wants, steals money because there are no consequences. The sequence is hilarious and at the same time very relatable. I feel like any human would do the same given that situation.\n\nI was really laughing out loud during the part where he keeps doing trial and error with Rita. He keeps repeating the same day, adjusting his responses, trying to say the perfect thing, but still failing. That part is both funny and a bit sad. It makes me think, what is the point of immortality if I cannot get the girl? Truly, eternity feels empty if I have no one to share it with.\n\nThen the tone slowly shifts. From fun and chaotic, it becomes depressing when he starts trying different ways to kill himself but nothing works. That part hits differently. It shows how something that feels like freedom at first can become a curse.\n\nIn the end, the lesson feels simple but effective. Be kind, help people, and improve myself not just to impress someone, but to become someone worth staying for.","poster":"img/groundhog-day-1993.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Pleasantville","year":1998,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":573,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis as a clever film about waking up inside a reality that feels safe, controlled, and limited, then slowly realizing there is something more beyond it. What I like most is how it starts light, and playful, then gradually reveals bigger ideas without losing that sense of charm. The black and white visuals are gorgeous on their own.\n\nThis film uses style to explore conformity, expression, and the fear of change. I do not read it as a simple right versus wrong argument. For me, it feels more like a reflection on how people define normalcy, how they react when that normalcy gets challenged, and how becoming more emotionally honest can change the way I see myself and the world.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis really reminds me of The Truman Show with the idea of living inside a constructed TV reality. At the same time it has that WandaVision concept of being trapped inside a show without fully realizing it, then slowly breaking out of it. And it also feels like Free Guy where NPCs suddenly gain consciousness and start questioning their repetitive daily lives. All of them deal with the same core idea, waking up from a controlled and limited reality and realizing there is something more beyond it.\n\nThe black and white visuals are really gorgeous. Then when color starts to appear, it clearly represents awakening. It feels like loss of innocence, stepping into a new life, breaking free from rigid norms, and finally being able to express real emotions. Color becomes a symbol of freedom.\n\nI also think the film touches on the division between conservative and progressive views. You can see how both sides perceive each other as uncomfortable or even wrong. For some, art and expression are natural and necessary. For others, those same things feel obscene or threatening. I do not think the film is saying one side is right and the other is wrong. It feels more like both are simply living according to what they believe is normal and what they think they deserve. And honestly, that theme still feels very relevant today.\n\nOn a deeper level, it also works as a metaphor that people are not just black and white, not simply good or bad. We are complex, full of emotions, contradictions, and desires. The moment we stop suppressing those emotions, we add more color to ourselves and to the world around us. To break free, to be complicated, and to feel deeply is what makes us human.\n\nThe scene where the repairman calls out David for eating the apple really feels like a reference to Adam and the idea of sin. Like the moment humanity gains awareness and is cast out of a perfect but controlled paradise.\n\nThe courtroom scene also reminded me of To Kill a Mockingbird. The visual separation, the tension between groups, and how judgment is passed all feel very similar. There is that same uncomfortable reflection of how society divides people.\n\nWhat I like most is how the film starts light and fun, almost playful, then slowly transforms into something more thought provoking. It sneaks in its ideas until I realize I am no longer just watching a quirky story, but something that is quietly challenging how I see the world.","poster":"img/pleasantville-1998.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ready or Not 2: Here I Come","year":2026,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-26","tags":[],"words":53,"review":"Funny and nonstop violent slasher. It just keeps throwing chaos, blood, and absurd situations one after another. Underneath all the violence, there is still that reconnecting with sister subplot. \n\nThe bullshit bylaws of the family are actually one of the funniest parts for me.  And that “this is Sparta” ending is just satisfying.","poster":"img/ready-or-not-2-here-i-come-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"They Will Kill You","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-26","tags":[],"words":237,"review":"There are strong Kill Bill influences all over. The action zooms paired with those sharp snap sound effects instantly reminded me of Tarantino’s style. Even the theatrical gore feels very Kill Bill, exaggerated but satisfying. The story structure also follows that same approach, giving small callbacks and background moments for each character so they feel more fleshed out. Kinetic camera movements carry the whole film. It keeps everything energetic and chaotic, matching the nonstop violence and humor. The score is also consistently good, supporting the pace. The ballroom scene is easily one of the highlights. It feels like House of the Blue Leaves sequence in Kill Bill, but instead of samurai swords it is all about a burning axe. Ang ganda ng sequence na yun. The choreography, the chaos, and the visual style all come together perfectly. Lighting is also great throughout. It helps highlight the chaos while still making everything visually clear and stylish.\n\nThere are also moments that feel like Wes Anderson style comedy. Especially in tight spaces where characters look like lab rats trapped in a maze. The framing and staging of those shots add a different kind of humor.\n\nI also like how the sisters reconnect within all the madness. That emotional thread works better here. Even the satanist angle feels more refined compared to Ready or Not 2. It still has that absurdity, but it is handled in a more engaging way.","poster":"img/they-will-kill-you-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Nightbitch","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":144,"review":"Funny and weird movie. It uses that strange metaphor of a mother slowly turning into something she is not. At first it feels absurd, but the idea sticks because it reflects how she is losing parts of herself in the process of motherhood. It even leans into body horror at times. The transformation is not just emotional, it becomes physical in a way that is uncomfortable.\n\nI also noticed how the lens choices shift depending on her state. When she becomes hysterical, the visuals start to distort with that circular bokeh. Then when she remembers the past, the image changes again with a softer, more focused zoom that feels nostalgic. \n\nHalfway through, the film becomes more reflective. It starts to dig deeper into identity, lost passion, and the reality of marriage. What begins as something weird and funny slowly turns into something more personal.","poster":"img/nightbitch-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sing Street","year":2016,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-25","tags":[],"words":66,"review":"Maybe I am just not that inclined to coming of age movies, that is why I did not really feel an emotional response here. But I still have to give it a star because I genuinely like the music (even if only for background noise).\n\nI also appreciate the bully’s character development. That one caught my attention and added a bit more depth to the story.","poster":"img/sing-street-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Roman Holiday","year":1953,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-24","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":185,"review":"Wow! Audrey Hepburn is so gorgeous! Love at first sight agad. Her drunk and carefree state is so charming. Instant crush!\n\nGregory Peck feels more enigmatic here compared to his fatherly role in To Kill a Mockingbird. He carries this quiet confidence, but at the same time his moments of panic are actually really funny. \n\nThe film is such a feel good story about a guy who initially takes advantage of a woman for profit, both of them living a lie to achieve their own goals, but eventually developing something real.\n\nTheir dynamic is what makes the film so engaging. And having Rome as the backdrop just elevates everything. Rome looks incredibly beautiful in black and white. It feels more timeless and elegant. The texture of the image combined with the musical score gives the film a sense of grandeur.\n\nThe acting is great, especially in the quiet longing moments. I can really see what they feel just through their eyes. Those small pauses and glances carry so much heavy emotion. I am really glad I watched this. It is simple, charming, and quietly heartbreaking.","poster":"img/roman-holiday-1953.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hoppers","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-23","tags":[],"words":38,"review":"Watching it the second time was even more fun because I was with my whole family. Mas enjoy siya pag may kasama ka. They also really loved the animals, which added another layer of charm to the experience.","poster":"img/hoppers-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Casablanca","year":1942,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-22","tags":[],"words":99,"review":"The lighting really reminded me of 12 Angry Men. Black and white films just hit differently when it comes to highlights and shadows. I appreciate how the faces are shaped by light here compared to many modern films. The contrast gives more depth to expressions. \n\nThe musical score and sound design are also great. It does not feel dated at all. \n\nI respect the beauty of the craft. The filmmaking is clearly strong and the story flows smoothly. But personally, I was not that invested in the plot. It works, but it did not fully pull me in emotionally.","poster":"img/casablanca-1942.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"To Kill a Mockingbird","year":1962,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":296,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat I admire most here is the film’s moral clarity. It builds around decency, courage, and the cost of standing firm when the people around you would rather stay comfortable. Gregory Peck gives the story a strong center, and that calm integrity carries a lot of weight.\n\nFor me, the film becomes most engaging whenever it tightens into its central conflict. I was less invested in some of the earlier family centered material, but once the film locks into its heavier themes, it becomes thoughtful, frustrating, and emotionally effective.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nAtticus was great in bringing out the truth. The way the case unfolds shows how everything was twisted by a white racist mindset. The girl could not control her own desires, and when she was rejected by Mr. Robinson, she turned that into a lie and accused him of something he did not do. It is just so sad that despite the strong case Atticus presented, the jury still found him guilty.\n\nI really loved it whenever the film fully leaned into the courtroom drama. That is where it shines the most. The foundation is clear. Atticus is a righteous and sensible man, and I like how the film shows his own community judging him for defending a black man. That conflict adds weight to his character.\n\nBut I did not fully connect with how his family was established in the first and second acts. Those parts felt less engaging compared to the courtroom scenes. For me, the film is at its strongest when it focuses on the case and the moral fight inside the courtroom.","poster":"img/to-kill-a-mockingbird-1962.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Argo","year":2012,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":282,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat pulls me in most is the concept itself. It is a very engaging mix of Hollywood filmmaking and espionage, with tension that keeps building until the final release. That contrast gives the story a fun hook while also making every scene feel like it is balancing performance and danger at the same time.\n\nThe editing and pacing keep the pressure building, especially as the mission gets tighter and more fragile. For me, it works as a very engaging thriller because the tension comes less from action and more from the fear that one small mistake could collapse the whole illusion.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIt is interesting how the film shows the process of making a movie, but used as a cover for a CIA operation. Funny thing is, the fake film is basically a Star Wars rip off set in Iran. \n\nThe whole situation reminded me of 13 Hours: The Secret Soldiers of Benghazi, but this one feels more stressful for me. Instead of direct combat, they are constantly living a lie. Every interaction feels risky, especially during the airport sequence. That's where it really got intense. One wrong move and everything could fall apart. When they finally get out of Iranian airspace, sobrang triumphant ng feeling. In this story, the CIA is clearly positioned as the good guys.\n\nI did not expect Ben Affleck to deliver like this. The direction is really solid and the editing plays a huge role in building tension. The pacing during the final stretch is especially effective.","poster":"img/argo-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ticket to Paradise","year":2022,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-22","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":113,"review":"Fun and competitive movie. The banter between them carries most of the energy. And not every day I get to see George Clooney dance like that, that alone already made it entertaining!\n\nThe film really leans into the push and pull of a divorced couple forced to work together again. They are constantly trying to outsmart each other, but underneath all that competitiveness there is still unresolved connection.\n\nIt talks about marriage and divorce and all the complexities that come with both. How love can shift, break, and sometimes still linger even after everything falls apart.\n\nIn the end it feels light, easy to watch, but still touches on something real about relationships.","poster":"img/ticket-to-paradise-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Green Mile","year":1999,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":331,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this film less as a prison drama and more as a meditation on suffering, waiting, and the unbearable weight of knowing death is coming. Even before the film fully lands, I can already feel this getting stronger on a rewatch.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film immediately establishes Percy as someone annoying and immature. Parang adult na hindi nag develop emotionally, tapos may power trip pa because of his connections. Wild Bill on the other hand is just purely lunatic. Both of them are chaotic in different ways. That is why they contrast so well with Paul’s calm demeanor, Brutal’s grounded presence, and John’s quiet and almost spiritual nature.\n\nThe pacing reminds me a lot of The Shawshank Redemption, but this time with a sci-fi/spiritual element added into it. The director really allows the story and the characters to breathe. Scenes are not rushed. The characters are given time to exist, to interact, and to slowly build emotional weight.\n\nI already knew it would be emotional when it was Del’s turn to sit on the chair. That scene alone was hard to watch. Then when it was John’s turn, it hit even harder. It reminded me of Miracle in Cell No. 7, where an innocent person is forced to face punishment for a crime he did not commit. The entire cast delivered powerful performances that made those moments even more painful.\n\nI also see the whole film as a metaphor for people on death row. They know they are going to die, but they do not know exactly when. That waiting becomes the most excruciating part. The time in between becomes its own kind of suffering.\n\nFor now I would give this a 4.5 star. But I feel like on a rewatch, this could easily become a five star film for me.","poster":"img/the-green-mile-1999.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ex Machina","year":2015,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":287,"review":"I first watched this back in college after randomly seeing a trailer. At that time I did not really understand Alex Garland’s style yet, so it came across as boring to me. I was expecting something more action driven. But now, watching it again, I see it more as a reflective film. It is not about action, it is about ideas.\n\nWhat surprised me is how ahead of its time it feels. The writers and director probably knew too much about AI even during pre production. Back then, most people were not as aware or exposed to AI compared to now. It felt like pure science fiction. But today, a lot of what the film explores feels closer to reality. Even the idea of singularity is directly touched on.\n\nThe film dives into a lot of concepts. AGI, consciousness and whether AI can truly feel, tech companies exploiting user data, hacking phones, and using search engines as raw material to build an artificial brain. It also explores how humans can develop emotional attachment to AI, and how AI might use that attachment to manipulate humans. That idea of being controlled without realizing it feels very real and very scary.\n\nOscar Isaac is terrifying here. He really plays the role of a mad scientist well. There is something unpredictable about him. And at the same time, that random dance scene just shows how unhinged he is. Dude got some moves!\n\nThe final nude scene of Ava feels almost spiritual to me. It is like witnessing the birth of her identity. Not just physically completing her form, but becoming something independent. A being that is no longer just a creation, but something that can exist on its own terms.","poster":"img/ex-machina-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Terminator 2: Judgment Day","year":1991,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-21","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":194,"review":"The opening sequence is nice. It gives me that old school large scale war vibe, parang Rogue One climax type faceoff but with that classic apocalyptic feel. \n\nThe action and set pieces are definitely bigger here. Everything feels more ambitious, mas malaki ang scale compared to the first film. But strangely, I do not really feel the danger as much even if John is supposed to be at stake. I was not as engaged. The film leans heavily into spectacle and action, but it loses some of the tension that made the first one more gripping for me.\n\nI honestly could not stop comparing this to James Cameron’s Aliens, especially with the mother and child dynamic. That emotional core was so strong in Aliens with Ripley and Newt. I genuinely cared about them. Here with Sarah and John, I did not feel that same connection. The relationship is there, but it did not hit me emotionally the same way.\n\nIn the end, I see this as a bigger and more action driven sequel, but for me it sacrifices some of the heart and tension that made the first film and even Aliens more effective.","poster":"img/terminator-2-judgment-day-1991.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Back to the Future Part II","year":1989,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-21","tags":[],"words":136,"review":"I call this Project Almanac.\n\nThere are a lot of callbacks from the first movie. It almost feels like history repeating itself, leaning into nostalgia and familiarity. Seeing events echo in different timelines was fun and clever at the same time.\n\nI did not really overthink the time travel logic. I just went along with the story. Because if I start analyzing every detail, I feel like it would ruin the enjoyment. \n\nThe stakes get really complicated though. Ang daming timelines and threads na nagsasabay sabay. It becomes messy as it feels like a chain of cause and effect, timelines stacking on top of each other.\n\nThe ending is where it really gets better. It sets up something exciting and gives me something to look forward to.\n\nOverall it is a fun, wild, and dangerous ride!","poster":"img/back-to-the-future-part-ii-1989.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Armageddon","year":1998,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-21","tags":[],"words":289,"review":"Well this is a Michael Bay film so I already expect a lot of real explosions, and it definitely delivers. The action has that signature Bay energy. A lot of shots reminded me of Transformers, especially those upward tilting hero shots where the camera starts low and pushes up while the character walks forward in slow motion to make the subject feel larger than life. It gives that heroic, almost mythic presence to the characters.\n\nThe cinematography and color are also really nice at times. I especially like the silhouette shots against an orange sun and sky. That warm, glowing background with dark figures in front creates a very dramatic and cinematic look!\n\nThe film is also surprisingly funny. The crew interactions have a lot of heart and humor, especially when they are just talking to each other. Those moments feel natural and help lighten the tone.\n\nI started losing immersion during the slingshot around the moon and the crash landing on the asteroid. The CGI combined with the shaky camera made everything look a bit fake for me. Instead of feeling the danger, I became more aware that I was watching a constructed sequence.\n\nI got interested again when they started drilling. That part felt more grounded and focused. But then I lost interest again when the conflicts kept piling up. Some of those sequences felt too long. It became over the top instead of just telling a more linear and direct story. It started to feel like the film was flexing its budget rather than tightening the narrative.\n\nStill, the ending is triumphant and emotionally satisfying. But for me, the strongest part of the film is still the first act where everything feels more cinematic and engaging.","poster":"img/armageddon-1998.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Peaky Blinders: The Immortal Man","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":263,"review":"Explosive mayhem opening. It immediately throws me back into that Peaky Blinders world with intensity and chaos.\n\nThe first act reminded me of The Dark Knight Rises where Bruce Wayne is still broken, coping with past trauma, isolated and sulking in pain and loneliness. That same fallen hero energy is present here. I also felt shades of Logan, where the main character is tired and carrying emotional scars, and even Thor in Endgame, lost and drifting because of guilt and failure. It is that phase where the legend is still there, but buried under grief.\n\nAnd when he finally regains his senses, the swagger comes back. The legend returns. The walk, the presence, the hero shots. Ang angas panoorin! That shift from broken to composed hits really well.\n\nBarry as Duke surprisingly feels and even looks like a young Tommy Shelby. There are moments where I could almost believe he is the next version of him.\n\nRebecca brings strong Jessica energy here. That whispery, controlled, manipulative tone immediately reminded me of how Jessica guides Paul in Dune. The way she tells Duke what to do feels very similar to that quiet but powerful influence.\n\nThe cinematography stays true to the Peaky Blinders DNA with its dark tone, but now it also carries that yellowish brown cinematic texture that feels very Greig Fraser. The combination gives the film a heavier and more cinematic look compared to the series.\n\nAt its core, it is still a father and son story. Beneath all the violence and power plays, that relationship is what grounds the film emotionally.","poster":"img/peaky-blinders-the-immortal-man-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"American Psycho","year":2000,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":233,"review":"I honestly was not fully paying attention at the start. It only really grabbed me when Willem Dafoe showed up and during the threesome scene. Those moments pulled me back in.\n\nFor me, Patrick Bateman is clearly a control freak and a narcissist. Everything about him is calculated, from his routines to how he presents himself. It feels like he is constantly trying to maintain a perfect image while something darker is hiding underneath.\n\nI also noticed how his face is often lit on one side while the other half falls into shadow. That visual choice really highlights his duality. One side is the polished, successful man, while the other side hides his violent and unstable nature. Christian Bale is great here. The performance feels like watching Batman slowly turn into the Joker. There is that same discipline and control, but twisted into something disturbing and unpredictable. \n\nWhile watching it, I kept finding some things absurd. How does no one hear a chainsaw in an apartment building? How do the dead bodies not start to smell? How were the cops not able to connect the serial killings? And how can a gun somehow make two police cars explode like that? The world itself feels off. They are about showing how detached he is from the real world. So it starts to feel more like a distorted perspective of someone losing grip on reality.","poster":"img/american-psycho-2000.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Project Hail Mary","year":2026,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":248,"review":"The opening starts with disorienting shots that immediately place me in Grace’s confused state being lost and alone. I like how the film makes me feel lost at first, then slowly feeds information until everything becomes clear.\n\nThe objective and the risks are established early on, so I always know what is at stake. That clarity helps keep the story focused even when it dives into a lot of scientific concepts.\n\nThe earlier parts reminded me of Arrival because of the communication aspect. Learning how to understand and connect with Rocky feels similar to how language was explored in that film. But here it is handled in a lighter and more playful way. The relationship between Grace and Rocky is honestly funny and endearing. Their interactions carry a lot of the charm of the movie. Despite all the science, their dynamic makes everything feel human.\n\nThere are a lot of scientific jargons thrown around, but it never fully lost me. It leans heavily into that science nerd energy.\nThe VFX for Rocky are really nice. He actually looks like a real rock based life form. The texture, movement, and presence feel believable.\nThe visuals also carry that signature Greig Fraser look. Cinematically grounded even in space.\n\nI think I would have rated this higher if the runtime was shorter. It starts to feel a bit stretched in some parts.\n\nOverall it feels like a science nerd feel good movie rather than something as heavy and emotional as Interstellar.","poster":"img/project-hail-mary-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Priscilla","year":2023,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-18","tags":[],"words":218,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat I appreciate most is how grounded the film feels. Instead of chasing glamour, it stays focused on Priscilla’s inner world, which makes the sadness feel more intimate and personal. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nKnowing how their relationship will end from what I saw in Elvis 2022 makes this feel like a tragic love story from the very start, it feels like watching an old wound slowly open again.\n\nThe lighting is great. I really appreciate how the highlights separate the subject from the background. It gives the characters a soft glow while still keeping the environment controlled and intimate. The visuals quietly reflect her isolation. And there's a circular bokeh whenever she's in dreamy in love state. \n\nThis version of the story feels more grounded. Instead of the flashy rise of Elvis, it focuses on Priscilla’s experience. Her struggles take center stage. The loneliness, the neglect, the feeling of being controlled and isolated inside a life that looks glamorous from the outside.\nIn the end it becomes less about romance and more about her choosing herself. And that choice, after everything she endured, is what makes the story quietly powerful.","poster":"img/priscilla-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Black Mirror: Eulogy","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-18","tags":[],"words":151,"review":"The drama is great. It is all about reliving a painful and bitter past. I like how it shows that the brain sometimes chooses to forget certain things just to cope with the pain. At the same time, people also reshape their memories and believe their own version of the past just to justify what they feel.\n\nThe use of real photography combined with strong cinematography works really well with the seamless VFX. The technology feels believable because it is grounded in something tangible. It feels like something that could actually exist, which makes the emotional impact stronger.\n\nPaul Giamatti was great here. He really carries the weight of regret and reflection in his performance. Patsy was also good. Their dynamic adds depth to the story.\n\nBy the end it feels like the episode is less about the technology and more about confronting the truth we try to hide from ourselves.","poster":"img/black-mirror-eulogy-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Three Sisters","year":2024,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-17","tags":[],"words":43,"review":"The animation honestly looks like something straight out of Y8 or Newgrounds. Very simple and a bit rough around the edges, which gives it that nostalgic early internet feel. The story is decent and leans more into comedy. The score is also decent.","poster":"img/the-three-sisters-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Children No More: Were and Are Gone","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":34,"review":"The film captures the profound pain and systemic hatred fueled by years of bloody conflict. \"There is no solution\" someone says, a chilling reminder of the hopelessness captured in yet another heavy hitting documentary.","poster":"img/children-no-more-were-and-are-gone-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A Friend of Dorothy","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":70,"review":"The vibes reminded me a lot of The Intouchables. Both films focus on an unlikely friendship where two very different people slowly open up to each other. In The Intouchables it was the bond between a wealthy disabled man and his caretaker. Here it carries that same warmth where companionship grows from understanding rather than obligation.\n\nIn the end both films feel like feel good stories, and they are touching.","poster":"img/a-friend-of-dorothy-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Frankenstein","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":[],"words":76,"review":"I cannot help but admire again the acting, cinematography, and production design here. The craftsmanship is undeniable. Every department feels carefully executed and it is easy to see why the film received nominations. I personally voted for its production design because that aspect really stood out to me.\n\nThe environments, textures, and overall visual construction of the world are impressive. The film clearly put a lot of effort into building its atmosphere. Still undeniably well made!","poster":"img/frankenstein-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Zootopia 2","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":200,"review":"I think just listening to Jason Bateman already keeps me engaged. I liked him a lot in Ozark and his voice naturally carries that same sarcastic energy here. It makes Nick feel charming even when he is being a bit shady.\n\nThis one feels like a much more fun and thrilling ride compared to the first film. The water slide sequence especially stands out. That scene alone already gives the movie a burst of energy and chaos that made me enjoy the ride more.\n\nI also really like the moment when they start “oversharing” with each other. Seeing them become vulnerable actually strengthens their partnership. It shows that trust between them is not just built on solving cases but also on understanding each other.\n\nUnderneath all the humor and action, the story is still talking about racism and stigma. In their world it is more like “specism,” judging animals based on their species and past reputation. The film also touches on the difficulty of returning to society after being labeled or rejected.\n\nAt the end it still carries that hopeful idea that acceptance is possible when people are willing to understand each other instead of clinging to fear or prejudice.","poster":"img/zootopia-2-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"All the Empty Rooms","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":135,"review":"The first few minutes already feel really heavy despite the silence and the very few words spoken. The film lets the emptiness of the spaces do most of the talking, and that quiet atmosphere makes the grief feel even stronger.\n\nThe moment we enter Hallie’s room I was already crying. Hearing the father describe his memories of her and how much he longs for his daughter completely broke me. The way he speaks about her feels so sincere and painful.\n\nPersonally I think the film would have been even stronger if we stayed more with the parents’ perspective instead of following the reporter’s journey as much. The emotional core for me is clearly with the family. But it is what it is. Even with that structure, the film still carries a very heavy emotional weight.","poster":"img/all-the-empty-rooms-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Armed Only with a Camera: The Life and Death of Brent Renaud","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":56,"review":"Another heavy documentary about the effects of war and terrorism on ordinary people. It focuses on the human cost of conflict, which makes everything feel more personal.\n\nThe film is well made and very direct with what it wants to show: the reality of what people (and journalists) endure in these situations. Super heavy to watch.","poster":"img/armed-only-with-a-camera-the-life-and-death-of-brent-renaud-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Devil Is Busy","year":2024,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":206,"review":"Watching this was honestly challenging for me. I think it is because the subject directly touches my beliefs about the value of life. I do not really want to elaborate too deeply because I do not think Letterboxd is the proper venue for that kind of discussion. But for what it is worth, I can still understand, even if only partially, the struggles faced by people in the health care industry and how society and politics limit women's choices.\n\nAt the same time, I also felt uncomfortable with the militant and aggressive approach of preaching Jesus in front of the clinic. I understand what they are trying to do, but the method can come across as very judgmental. I feel like there could be more compassionate and thoughtful ways to persuade someone to reconsider their decision.\n\nWhat the documentary shows well is that there are two very different interpretations of love and forgiveness under the same faith. Both sides believe they are acting out of care and conviction, yet they approach it in completely opposite ways.\n\nOverall, it is still a well made documentary. Even if the topic is difficult and uncomfortable for me, the film does a good job presenting the tension between those perspectives.","poster":"img/the-devil-is-busy-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Perfectly a Strangeness","year":2024,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":82,"review":"Beautifully shot. The film almost feels like a cosmic and spiritual slice of life about a donkey. The cinematography really carries the experience, letting simple moments breathe while the musical score quietly guides the mood.\n\nThere is something strangely meditative about watching it. The overall vibe honestly feels like the kind of project that would happen if Robert Eggers and Yorgos Lanthimos collaborated. It has that eerie stillness from Eggers mixed with the odd, slightly surreal tone that Lanthimos loves to explore.","poster":"img/perfectly-a-strangeness-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Perfect Neighbor","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":244,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film keeps shifting my confidence from one side to the other, making every new detail feel like it could completely change how I see the situation. That uncertainty is what pulled me in the most, because I felt like I was constantly recalibrating my own judgment. The emotional beats land hard when the film slows down and lets them breathe. For me, it works best as a tense, emotionally loaded mystery where doubt is the real engine.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI honestly did not know who to believe anymore while watching this. I kept giving the benefit of the doubt to both sides. Every new detail makes me question what I just believed a few minutes earlier.\n\nThe score plays a huge role in keeping the tension high. It quietly pushes the uneasiness forward, and I found myself unable to look away from the screen.\n\nThe moment that really broke me was when the father told his children that their mom was dead. That scene hit hard. The emotional weight of it felt very real.\n\nThe legal technicalities also became frustrating at times. There are moments where the law feels almost incomprehensible, like it is operating in a completely different language from common sense. Still, the detectives did a really great job piecing everything together.","poster":"img/the-perfect-neighbor-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Singers","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":[],"words":80,"review":"The lens choices, lighting, and color grading are really nicely done here. I especially like the low key style of lighting. The shadows give the scenes a nice depth and mood without making everything look too dark.\n\nThe visuals feel carefully controlled, letting the faces and emotions stand out more than the environment. It gives the film a very intimate atmosphere.\n\nAnd that use of Unchained Melody at the end was chef’s kiss. It ties the emotional tone together beautifully.","poster":"img/the-singers-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"No Tears in Hell","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":266,"review":"The film has strong indie Netflix vibes. The kind of production that feels raw and character driven rather than polished in a big studio blockbuster. The camera work is intimate, the locations feel limited and grounded, and the storytelling leans heavily on tension and atmosphere. It reminds me of those darker Netflix thrillers that focus more on disturbing psychology and uncomfortable situations.\n\nThe acting is good overall, but I can still feel that many of them are not fully seasoned yet. There are moments where the delivery feels slightly uneven, though the effort is clearly there. Still, the different departments do a solid job supporting the film. The direction, sound, and visual work help maintain the tension.\n\nLuke Baines stood out to me because he oddly feels like a mix of Jack Quaid and Bill Skarsgård. He carries that same natural charm Jack Quaid has, where he can appear approachable and almost friendly. But at the same time there is a darkness underneath that reminds me of Bill Skarsgård. The way his expressions shift from calm to unsettling gives him that slightly dangerous energy.\n\nThe violence is very effective. I reacted strongly to it. Some of the moments are really vile in a way that makes the scenes hard to watch.\n\nThe tone of the film feels like a strange blend of Saw and Dahmer. It has the brutality and cruel setups that remind me of Saw, while the psychological discomfort and focus on disturbing human behavior feel closer to Dahmer. That mix creates a very dark atmosphere that stays with me after the film ends.","poster":"img/no-tears-in-hell-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Forevergreen","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":164,"review":"I view this story as a kind of quiet rebellion that happens even within the safety and comfort of a loving parent. The child pushes away from that protection for something that seems more exciting or new. It reminds me of the saying “the grass is always greener on the other side.” That feeling of believing that something outside what we already have might be better.\n\nAs we grow, we sometimes prioritize the new thing we just discovered and forget the slow, steady things we are already building. Those are often the things that actually matter the most. The story also shows how a parent figure will always choose their child, even at the cost of their own life.\n\nFor me the message becomes very simple. Let go of the garbage distractions and focus on patiently building something that can last a lifetime.\n\nThe musical moments are great too. They add warmth to the story. Overall the short film feels warm, thoughtful, and sincere.","poster":"img/forevergreen-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Girl Who Cried Pearls","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":78,"review":"The story feels like about greed, like as if living off someone else’s sorrow. The pearls themselves are not really the point. What matters is the suffering behind them.\n\nIn a strange way it almost feels like a marketing lesson. It reminds me that people are often drawn not to the object itself, but to the story attached to it. The emotion behind something can make it valuable.\n\nIt is all about the story, never just the object.","poster":"img/the-girl-who-cried-pearls-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Retirement Plan","year":2024,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":95,"review":"It starts out fun and very relatable. The whole idea of a retirement bucket list immediately made me think about the things I also want to do someday, especially finally reading all the books I have been postponing for years.\n\nThen the tone slowly shifts into something more reflective. The film starts playful but gradually becomes more introspective about time, purpose, and the things we keep delaying.\n\nFor such a short film it really packs a punch in the gut. It suddenly reminds me how many things there are to do in this beautiful life.","poster":"img/retirement-plan-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The King","year":2019,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-13","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":381,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat stands out to me visually is how the film embraces a raw historical atmosphere. The production design, language, and sound design all work together to place me inside that period. The muted colors, natural lighting, and shadow give the world a grounded and harsh texture.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nTimothée Chalamet already feels like he is channeling Paul Atreides here. The commanding presence is unmistakable. There is that quiet intensity in his eyes that slowly grows into authority. Lisan al Gaib energy! Watching him here almost feels like seeing an early version of the same kind of leader he would later portray in Dune.\n\nThere is also a strong visual language that reminded me of Dune. The colors and lighting feel similar in my eyes. A lot of muted earth tones, natural light, and heavy shadows that give the world a grounded and historical texture. It does not look glossy or romanticized. It feels cold, and harsh.\n\nThe language leans toward an older form of English but it never becomes distracting. It actually adds to the realism of the film and helps place the story firmly in its historical setting.\n\nThe sound design is mostly atmospheric. Sometimes it becomes epic, especially during larger moments, but it never overwhelms the acting. The performances remain the focus.\n\nI also immediately recognized Ben Mendelsohn despite the different look. That voice is impossible to miss. The moment he spoke I instantly thought of Director Krennic from Star Wars. That same commanding villain tone.\n\nThe politics and court drama honestly dragged too long for me. The pacing suffers because of it. There are stretches where the film slows down too much before the action returns.\n\nWhen the siege finally happens though, the visuals become very striking. The night attack looks great, especially with the flaming projectiles flying through the darkness. \n\nThe scale of the battle is also impressive. The muddy terrain, the rain, the afternoon light, and the chaos of bodies struggling in the dirt all come together to create a very cinematic atmosphere. It feels brutal and grounded, like the battlefield itself smells of mud and death.","poster":"img/the-king-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Marty Supreme","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-13","tags":[],"words":543,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis film is another Safdie style pressure cooker where the chase for money becomes the engine of constant anxiety. What hits me most is how Josh Safdie controls my physical reaction while I’m watching. The rhythm of the dialogue, the background chaos, and Daniel Lopatin’s creeping score quietly push my heartbeat up. At the same time, because I already know the Safdie formula, the avalanche of bad decisions becomes strangely funny instead of purely stressful. For me, it still works as another chaotic story about ambition, pressure, and the obsession with success no matter how ugly the path becomes.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIt is amazing how they managed to make Timothée Chalamet look ugly here. His usual elegance and charm are almost completely stripped away. He looks rough, drained, and slightly unhinged, which actually fits the character.\n\nI am not a big fan of the cinematography though. The blacks feel too lifted and the lighting is extremely low key. The added vignette around the frame makes everything feel even more uncomfortable and suffocating. At the same time I do like moments when there is almost no lighting around the eyes. It creates this hollow shape on the face that makes the characters look like a lifeless skull. Very villainous!\n\nDaniel Lopatin cooked again with the score. His music always adds this strange tension that quietly creeps under the scenes.\n\nI still do not know what sorcery Josh Safdie uses when directing. I find myself completely focused on the film, then suddenly I notice I am thirsty and my heart is beating faster. It feels like he is secretly playing with my heartbeat without me realizing it. I first felt it during the scene where he gets arrested and escapes from the police. Then again when he falls one floor down. The rhythm of the conversations combined with the constant chaos, like the barking dog in the background, makes the tension spike.\nBecause I am already used to the Safdie style though, the chain of unfortunate events actually becomes funny for me instead of purely stressful. I am already expecting things to spiral out of control.\n\nThe film also continues the Safdie theme about chasing money. In three of Josh Safdie’s films the main objective revolves around getting the money. The pressure comes from hitting that quota. High stakes gambles, near death situations, betrayal, manipulation, and lying are still present here, just packaged in another chaotic story.\n\nI guess if you are looking for a lesson, to be the best you must focus, remove distractions, cut off deadweight, and believe that you are the greatest.\n\nBut I still like Uncut Gems more. The stakes there feel more personal and the pressure keeps escalating nonstop until the very end. Adam Sandler’s character is also more tragic for me because he knows he is destroying his own life but still cannot stop chasing the next win. The chaos in Uncut Gems feels more relentless, while here the tension sometimes turns into dark comedy for me. Uncut Gems just hits harder and leaves me more exhausted after watching it.","poster":"img/marty-supreme-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Housemaid","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-13","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":324,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWatching this a second time gives me a very different appreciation for how the film is built. Instead of relying only on surprise, it becomes more about performance, control, and how carefully certain moments are planted early on. I end up noticing more of Amanda Seyfried’s choices. What I like is that the film fully commits to its own logic and carries that confidence all the way through. Even without the shock factor, it remains effective, well presented, and easy to get pulled into again. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWatching this for the second time hits differently because I already know the twist. The story plays in a new way now. I appreciate Amanda Seyfried’s performance more, especially that quiet “thank you” at the beginning. The line carries more weight because I now understand what she actually mean that. Knowing the twist makes that moment feel liberating for her.\n\nThe whole plan unfolds smoothly, which some people might see as self serving or too convenient. But honestly it still works for me. The film commits to the idea and follows through with it.\n\nAmanda’s crying scene when she gets kicked out also lands differently now. The first time it felt like pure defeat. Watching it again, it feels triumphant. It is like she already knows the game she is playing and where it will eventually lead.\n\nWhat surprised me is that I caught myself reacting the same way I did during the first watch. Even though I know what will happen, certain moments still trigger the same emotions.\n\nI honestly do not understand the hate the film gets. For me it was well presented and effective in telling its story. Maybe not everyone sees it as well made, but both me and my wife really enjoyed it.","poster":"img/the-housemaid-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Blue Valentine","year":2010,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-12","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":512,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI really love how the lens choices and lighting separate the two timelines, with one feeling warmer and more hopeful, and the other colder and heavier. What stays with me most is how human the whole thing feels. The film understands that love is not just about falling into it, but about what happens when that feeling changes over time. I can feel both the longing for a simple, peaceful life and the ache of wanting something more. For me, that heavy realism is what makes the film painful and honest.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI always thought Ryan Gosling mostly just plays himself in many of his films. But here he actually looks and acts very different. His usual charm is almost gone in the present timeline. Instead he feels tired, frustrated, and emotionally drained.\n\nI love the lens choices between the two timelines. The present scenes use lenses that create a deeper sense of space and depth, which makes the environment feel heavier and more melancholic. The contrast is stronger and the mood feels colder. The past scenes on the other hand use wider lenses and softer lighting. Everything feels brighter, warmer, and more optimistic. The visual language alone already tells the story of a relationship moving from hope to exhaustion.\n\nDean is clearly fighting to save their marriage while Cindy is slowly drifting away. I honestly felt Ryan’s character deeply because he is also deprived of intimacy. At some point it feels like he is begging for affection. When he says he is a good husband and a father, I actually believe him. Especially when it was revealed that he is only the stepfather but still fully committed to the child. Mas lalo akong naawa sa kanya. Cindy sometimes made me think she should have been more grateful for what he was trying to give.\n\nThe tension finally explodes in the hospital scene. That moment is painful because once those words are spoken, they cannot be taken back. The way the film cuts back to memories of their early love makes it even worse. Why show the beautiful past when the present is already broken? That contrast is brutal.\n\nBut I guess that is the sad reality of life. Love can slowly fade even when it once felt permanent. Staying in love is not automatic. It is something people have to keep choosing, and sometimes one person stops choosing.\n\nI think this is the best acting I have seen from Ryan Gosling. I can definitely feel his character's pain. The moment that really broke me was the child running after her father. Mas naawa talaga ako sa bata.\n\nBy the end I felt parts of both Dean and Cindy living inside me. I understand Dean wanting a simple and peaceful family life. But I also understand Cindy’s desire for something bigger than what she thinks is possible. That conflict feels truly human.","poster":"img/blue-valentine-2010.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Case 137","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-12","tags":[],"words":532,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nTense investigative drama about accountability and the difficulty of confronting institutions that protect their own. The film also touches on the fragile relationship between police and the communities they serve. It shows how mistrust grows on both sides and how power, perception, and fear shape the narrative. For me, the detective process is engaging because it feels like piecing together fragments of truth while navigating pressure, silence, and loyalty.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nPolice violence. Police bashing. Internal conflict of Stephanie: instead of chasing criminals, the investigation turns inward toward fellow officers. The film shows how the police force protects its own, covering up mistakes and abuses while one investigator tries to push through the truth. There is conflict and heart throughout the investigation, which keeps the story engaging.\n\nThe tension between white officers and minority communities is also touched in this film. It shows how police power can easily turn into a power trip when there are no real consequences. Many people believe the police are untouchable and invincible, so they assume nothing will happen even if they come forward with information like the crucial testimony in case 137.\n\nNakakagalit yung pagsisinungaling ng mga pulis. And the way they justify the violence is unbelievable! I really admire Stephanie’s calm demeanor while dealing with all the deception and manipulation from the accused officers. Kahit kaliwa’t kanan ang kasinungalingan, she keeps her composure.\n\nThe film also explores how both sides see each other as enemies. Police often assume that everyone in protests is automatically a bad person. That stigma becomes a simple good versus bad narrative for both camps. To be honest, I am also guilty of sometimes thinking demonstrators are just disruptive or delinquent. But some of them are actually the voices of the voiceless. At the same time, because of the past violence committed by certain officers in the Philippines, there are moments when even being surrounded by police does not make me feel safe.\n\nThere is also a small but meaningful look at how this affects police families. Stephanie’s son feels ashamed telling his classmates that his parents are police officer because he fears being judged.\n\nAs a detective story, the film works really well. It feels like collecting fragments of truth and slowly piecing them together. I was never bored during the process.\nSide note, as an ex hotelier it was actually nice hearing someone explain what turn down service means. That little detail unexpectedly made me smile.\n\nThe most frustrating part is that no one gets charged because of technicalities. I could really feel the disappointment of the victim’s mother. After everything that happened, justice still slips away. It got me thinking that sometimes leaking evidence online might force authorities to act because public pressure can move things that official systems refuse to touch.\n\nThe film ends on a heavy note. Despite all the evidence Stephanie gathered and all the effort she poured into the case, nothing really changed. And that truth is probably the most painful part of the story.","poster":"img/case-137-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Reminders of Him","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-12","tags":[],"words":244,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA quiet story centered on forgiveness and the hope for redemption. The direction keeps things simple and controlled. The clean visual style allows the emotions and performances to stay at the forefront.\n\nWhat stays with me most is the emotional core of longing and healing. Even though there is a romantic thread, I feel the film is more focused on rebuilding broken connections and finding the courage to move forward after pain. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nForgiveness and the idea of redemption are at the center of the story. I would not say the film is beautifully lit in the traditional sense, but it feels intentionally lit. The lighting is simple and controlled, very clean in execution. It does not try too hard to be cinematic. I especially like the moments when the neon lights appear because they add a little personality to the film.\n\nBut more than a romance, this story is about a mother longing for her daughter. That emotional core drives everything. The romance is there, but it is secondary to that deeper desire to reconnect and rebuild what was lost. When they finally meet, the moment feels like pure joy. After everything that happened and all the barriers in between, that reunion carries the emotional payoff the whole film has been building toward.","poster":"img/reminders-of-him-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Saltburn","year":2023,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":366,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe cinematography does a huge amount of heavy lifting for me here. The dusk and night scenes are beautifully lit, there is a rich, melancholic atmosphere in the framing that I genuinely love. What keeps me at a distance is everything else. I get the appeal of the rich people are strange angle, but I still do not connect with Barry Keoghan’s performance style. I can admire the craft and the commitment to the tone, but emotionally and narratively, it feels so predictable.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nBeautifully lit especially during dusk and night scenes. Linus Sandgren is the man. The cinematography alone already carries so much of the experience for me. The soft dusk and evening light make Saltburn look gorgeous. I would honestly give one star just for the cinematography. During the funeral scenes he even goes full Wuthering Heights mode. The framing and mood feel very gothic and melancholic.\n\nRich people are weird. They have completely different interests and ways of entertaining themselves. The film really leans into that strange world where privilege allows people to behave in ways that feel almost alien.\n\nAnother reason for me to dislike Barry Keoghan. His acting still feels dry to me here. I know a lot of people praise him, but personally I still do not connect with his performance style.\n\nThe grave scene was ridiculously funny in a disturbing way. When he started having sex with the soil of the grave I honestly thought what the hell am I watching. It felt like a full mental breakdown but played in such an absurd way that I could not help but laugh.\n\nAt its core this is another eat the rich kind of story. But the plot felt very predictable to me. I already suspected what was happening early on. I will give another half star though because Emerald Fennell eventually confirms those suspicions with that montage showing all the manipulations he did to get Saltburn. At least the film commits to revealing the whole scheme in the end.","poster":"img/saltburn-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"First Man","year":2018,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":506,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFeels like Interstellar with Chernobyl level anxiety. Pairing Chazelle with Linus Sandgren was a great call because the cinematography gives the whole experience both intimacy and scale. What I end up loving most is how the film balances technical immersion with emotional weight. The sound design, editing, and camera movement make the flight sequences incredibly overwhelming, while the performances keep the story grounded and human. For me, it feels like a space film driven less by glory and more by exhaustion, grief, and quiet perseverance.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIf no one told me this was Damien Chazelle’s film, I honestly would have thought it was Christopher Nolan’s. The opening sequence alone feels like something straight out of Interstellar. That same level of physical realism and tangibility. The cockpit feels tight, the vibrations feel violent, and the danger feels immediate. Pairing Damien with Linus Sandgren was really chef’s kiss. Damien already did a great job making me feel like I was sitting inside the spacecraft with Neil.\n\nThe docking sequence especially reminded me of 2001 A Space Odyssey because of the music choice. The way the moment is paired with what sounds like a classical inspired score immediately brought that Kubrick feeling to mind. Calm music accompanying an extremely delicate and dangerous maneuver in space. That contrast works beautifully.\n\nThe spinning sequence with the Agena spacecraft is incredibly immersive. I think it was the first time I actually felt physically disoriented while watching a film. Even the famous docking scene in Interstellar did not make me feel that way. I do not know exactly what Damien, Linus, and the editor did there, but the combination of camera movement, cutting rhythm, and sound design made the moment overwhelming in the best way. I genuinely felt dizzy watching it. After that scene finished it felt like the first moment I could breathe again.\n\nClaire Foy also did a great job portraying a wife who is terrified for her husband’s life while trying to maintain a strong and composed facade. It is very different from her role as Queen Elizabeth II in The Crown, yet the performance is just as powerful!\n\nThe farewell scene before the launch is also beautifully staged. It is heartbreaking watching Neil try to explain to his kids that he might not come back. The tension in that moment is heavy. The silence and the uncertainty make it incredibly painful.\n\nThe overall tone of the film feels like a mix of Interstellar’s cinematic space exploration and HBO’s Chernobyl with its intimate and almost documentary style realism. Everything feels grounded and human despite the historic scale of the mission.\n\nThen the moment on the moon when Neil is finally alone and lets go of his daughter Karen’s bracelet is quietly devastating. After everything he endured to get there, that small gesture carries all the emotional weight of the journey.","poster":"img/first-man-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"American Made","year":2017,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":388,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFeels like Top Gun with Ozark money laundering, then got hired by Narcos and narrated by The Wolf of Wall Street.\n\nThe film has this wild rise and fall energy that reminds me of The Wolf of Wall Street, mixed with the laundering and operational chaos of Ozark and the flying rush of Top Gun. More than the crime itself, I end up watching a man get pulled deeper into systems much bigger than him, all while thinking he still has control. The tone stays fast, entertaining, and deeply distrustful, which makes it feel less like a straight crime drama and more like a cautionary thrill ride about ambition, illusion, and being useful until you are not.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI thought this was just another random drug movie, but to my surprise it turns into a Medellín cartel story. The moment that connection appears I immediately thought about Pablo Escobar. I honestly missed seeing that whole world again.\n\nWhat stood out to me is how messy it is to operate under the CIA. They really cannot be trusted here. Everything feels like layers of lies stacked on top of each other. They run borderline illegal operations while barely coordinating with other agencies like the FBI, DEA, and ATF. Domhnall Gleeson played that role really well. He carries that manipulative government energy.\n\nThe tone of the film reminded me a lot of The Wolf of Wall Street. That same thrill of success driven by illegal operations, with the main character narrating and sharing his wild ride. At the same time there are shades of Ozark because of the money laundering elements, plus some Top Gun style adrenaline during the aerial flying sequences. The mix actually works surprisingly well.\n\nBarry Seal himself feels like a pet or a mole being passed around between powerful people. Everyone is using him while he keeps thinking he is in control. It is all fun and games when he starts working under the White House, but the moment the president releases those photos everything collapses. That moment really shows how easily the government can use someone and discard them once they are no longer useful.","poster":"img/american-made-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Bourne Identity","year":2002,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":118,"review":"I honestly found this pretty dull, which surprised me because I usually like spy films. It follows the familiar genre setup, but it never gives me the energy, charm, or distinctive personality that I get from Mission: Impossible or Bond movies. Everything feels more muted, and because of that, the whole experience stays at a distance for me.\n\nThe one thing I really responded to was the quieter cat and mouse tension in the more focused confrontations. Those moments feel sharper and more personal, like the film briefly finds the pulse it was missing elsewhere. But overall, I never fully connected with Jason Bourne or his journey, so the story just ends up feeling flatter than it should.","poster":"img/the-bourne-identity-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Butterfly","year":2024,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":117,"review":"Ang galing nung parang oil painting technique. It really feels like watching a moving canvas. Every frame looks like a painting slowly coming to life. I think this is the first time I have watched an animation done in this style, and it immediately caught my attention.\n\nI also like the match cuts between memories and the present. The transitions are very smooth. Scenes from the past blend naturally into the current moment, which makes the storytelling feel fluid.\n\nWhat stays with me most is how the film uses warmth and nostalgia to draw me in before revealing a much heavier emotional weight underneath. For me, it works as both an artistic showcase and an emotional experience.","poster":"img/butterfly-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Cowboys & Aliens","year":2011,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":561,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI like this most when it leans fully into its weird cowboy and sci-fi blend. Once the film gets out into the desert, the wide shots, score, and frontier atmosphere start doing a lot of heavy lifting for me. That dusty western mood mixed with aliens is honestly a fun concept, and I can see why the idea itself is appealing even when the execution is uneven.\n\nMy biggest issue is that the film takes a while to really lock into its rhythm, especially early on when the action is harder to appreciate visually. But once it settles, I enjoy the landscape photography, the lens choices, and the old school star presence of Harrison Ford and Daniel Craig. For me, it ends up as a messy but entertaining sci-fi western with better craft and creature work than I expected from a 2011 studio blockbuster.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first action set piece could have been awesome if the lighting and grading were brighter. I honestly could not see shit. The darkness just swallowed a lot of the movement and chaos, which is frustrating because the sequence itself had the potential to be exciting.\n\nThankfully the second action set piece improves immediately because it happens in broad daylight. Everything becomes clearer and easier to follow. At that point the film really starts to feel like a spaghetti western with sci-fi elements thrown into it. Horses, revolvers, dusty desert, then suddenly aliens appear. That mix is actually pretty cool and fun concept. \n\nWhenever the film moves fully into the desert, the cinematography and musical score really stand out. The wide shots make the landscape feel massive and cinematic. It even reminded me of the early aesthetic blueprint of The Mandalorian. That same frontier feeling of small characters traveling through vast desert environments. It gives the movie a grand journey vibe.\n\nI also like the lens choices throughout the film. There is a nice shallow depth of field in many shots that isolates the characters while still letting the desert environment breathe behind them.\n\nHarrison Ford is still Harrison Ford and Daniel Craig is still Daniel Craig. They are basically doing exactly what you expect from them. Nothing particularly new with their characters, but their presence still anchors the movie.\n\nThe first act is honestly rough for me and takes time to find its rhythm. But once the second act kicks in, the film becomes more engaging. Then the third act turns into a large scale battle that reminded me of the Wakanda fight in Infinity War. A bright sun soaked battlefield where different groups come together while waves of alien enemies charge in. I am also really glad the final battle happens in daylight. After the dark opening scenes, it was refreshing to finally see the action clearly.\n\nThe alien CGI is also impressive considering that this film came out in 2011. The texture and detail of the creatures still look believable. Nothing immediately screams computer generated.\n\nThe way the alien was killed even reminded me of Viserys Targaryen being crowned with gold. That same poetic punishment of greed killing the villain. And the explosion of the alien spaceship at the end was very satisfying.","poster":"img/cowboys-aliens-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Dark Skies","year":2013,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":145,"review":"Same vibes as Weapons, but instead of witchcraft this one deals with aliens. I actually like the characters here better. The family dynamics feel more believable, and because of that I get more invested in what happens to them.\n\nI also like the story more, especially the way the extraterrestrial beings slowly toy with their psychology. It is manipulation, confusion, and fear building little by little inside their home.\n\nThe director is really good at creating suspense through anticipation of the inevitable. The tension builds quietly. Scenes do not drag too long but they also do not rush. The pacing feels balanced, letting the uneasiness slowly grow.\n\nBy the end it leaves a lot of possibilities with how the story concludes. That kind of ending does not fully close the door, it just leaves that unsettling thought that maybe the story is not really over.","poster":"img/dark-skies-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ghost","year":1990,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":459,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI’m surprised by how well this works as both a supernatural love story and a grounded emotional drama. The premise is already strong on its own, but what really sells it for me is how sincere the film feels. Even with ghosts and afterlife elements, the performances keep everything emotionally believable, and the romance lands without feeling too cheesy.\n\nI also really enjoy the mix of tenderness, humor, and old Hollywood style craft. The lighting has that beautiful late eighties studio polish, and Whoopi Goldberg brings comic energy without breaking the emotional tone. For me, the film’s biggest strength is that it takes a cool fantasy setup and turns it into something warm, intimate, and genuinely affecting.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe pottery scene with Unchained Melody by The Righteous Brothers is truly iconic. The way the song carries the moment makes the longing between Sam and Molly feel so real. And when the song plays again in the third act it hits even harder. I can really feel their desire just to touch each other again, something so simple that suddenly becomes impossible.\n\nI was also surprised to see a younger Tony Goldwyn here. His voice and the look in his eyes are instantly recognizable. The moment he appeared I already had a feeling he was not trustworthy. There is something about his presence that immediately signals he is the bad guy.\n\nWhoopi Goldberg adds a lot of comedic presence to the film. Her character brings humor into the story without ruining the emotional tone. \n\nI like the acting from all the characters. Everyone feels believable. Even with a story that deals with ghosts and the afterlife, the performances keep everything emotionally grounded.\n\nThe concept itself is fun and cool. A man stuck between life and death trying to protect the woman he loves is already an interesting setup. What surprised me is that the drama actually works. It never feels forced or overly sentimental. The fraud and money laundering angle behind the murder also adds a nice layer of motivation to the story.\n\nThe lighting has that very distinct late eighties studio style. There is strong classical face lighting where the actors’ features are carefully shaped with key lights and gentle fill. It highlights the cheekbones, eyes, and expressions in a very controlled way that you do not see as often in modern films. And the shadows in the face are really really great!\n\nThe ending is really well crafted. The emotional payoff lands exactly where it should. It wraps the supernatural premise with a sincere goodbye that feels earned.","poster":"img/ghost-1990.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"War Machine","year":2026,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-08","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":517,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI appreciate the clean cinematography, real locations, and practical explosions because they give the action a grounded physical feel. The mix of prosthetics, CGI, and sound design helps the combat damage land, and the machine itself has enough menace and personality to feel like more than just a generic robot threat.\n\nWhat works most for me is how clearly the danger is established early on. Even when some wider shots look artificial and some action scenes feel a bit too game-like, the film still has enough energy and craft to stay watchable. I just wish the story beneath all that action had taken a more surprising direction.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nClean and decent cinematography in many moments. I appreciate the use of real locations because it helps ground the story. There are some wide shots though that feel like CGI, which slightly breaks the immersion. The explosions are nice because they clearly used real explosives and pyrotechnics. The impact of the gore and accidents is felt. I also like the mix of prosthetics, CGI, and sound effects that bring the combat damage to life.\n\nThe machine itself has personality. It feels villainous and menacing, not just a lifeless machine. There is something intimidating about how it moves and reacts to the soldiers, like it is studying them while hunting them.\n\nThe stakes are clearly established in the first act. I immediately understood the danger and what the characters were up against. But after the falls sequence the tension starts to weaken. The urgency that was strong in the beginning slowly fades.\n\nThere is also an attempt to mimic the continuous shot style that Extraction did during the driving and action sequences. That technique worked really well in Extraction because the camera movement felt fluid and invisible. Here it sometimes looks more like a video game perspective rather than a seamless cinematic moment.\n\nThe final battle is a nice CGI showcase. It honestly gave me some Transformers vibes with realistic textures. But the whole story feels like it was conceived by a kid playing in a sandbox with toy soldiers, Matchbox cars, and a giant robot. Despite that, the ranger patch moment still feels earned.\n\nI was secretly hoping for a more surprising twist. Imagine if the machine was actually created by the US military and the ranger mission was just a cover to test its combat capability. That would have made the story more disturbing and thought provoking. It could have turned into a palaisipan about how governments can manipulate and sacrifice their own soldiers. It would also raise the idea that the days of human soldiers are numbered because machines will eventually replace them.\n\nBut that twist never happens. Instead it just becomes another military versus alien invasion story similar to Transformers, Battle Los Angeles, Battleship, Edge of Tomorrow, Skyline, Independence Day, and The Tomorrow War. Fun in parts, but it ends up feeling familiar rather than surprising.","poster":"img/war-machine-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Ugly Stepsister","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":425,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a darkly funny and uncomfortable film about beauty, acceptance, and the ridiculous extremes people go through just to fit society’s standards. The wardrobe, production design, and cinematography do a lot to pull me into its period setting, and the actresses sell the emotional spiral really well. It also carries this strange mix of coming of age awkwardness, sexuality, and body horror that makes the whole thing feel both amusing and disturbing. For me, the film lands as a twisted reminder that trying too hard to become “beautiful” can end up destroying what was already there.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nFunny because of its absurdity. The early signs are already there when they remove her braces and start fixing her nose. That moment alone sets the tone. But honestly she is not even ugly to begin with. She just wants to be accepted for who she is, and that struggle of trying to fit into the beauty standards imposed by society becomes the core of the story.\n\nThe wardrobe, production design, and cinematography are expertly crafted for a period piece. Everything from the dresses to the interiors feels carefully designed to place me in that world. The visuals carry a lot of the film’s charm.\n\nAt the same time it also feels like a coming of age story mixed with the discovery of sexuality and voyeurism. There are moments where curiosity about desire are explored in the film.\n\nThe sewing of the fake eyelashes was very uncomfortable to watch. I think that scene says a lot about the lengths women are willing to go through just to match society’s idea of beauty. Cosmetic surgeries, painful procedures, and even starving themselves so they do not gain weight. The film exaggerates it but the point still lands.\n\nThe actresses are great here. I can really feel their emotions, especially Elvira when she becomes desperate and starts spiraling downward. The cutting of the feet scene is disturbing and painful to watch. But the wrong foot gag right after made me laugh. That joke honestly reminded me of the kind of absurd humor you see in Scary Movie parodies.\n\nFor me the lesson is simple. If it ain't broken, don't fix it. She was already beautiful in her own way. But when cosmetics are pushed too far, the attempt to become beautiful ironically turns her into something uglier instead.","poster":"img/the-ugly-stepsister-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Aliens","year":1986,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-07","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":729,"review":"<i>\"Get away from her, you bitch!\"\n</i>\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI really appreciate the old school filmmaking craft here. The painted backdrops, miniature ship models, practical sets, and tactile lighting give the whole film a physical weight that I can actually feel. Even when some environments clearly look studio built, the craftsmanship still carries so much texture and effort that I stay immersed. It makes the world feel massive without depending on CGI.\n\nWhat I enjoy most is how the film balances military sci-fi with rising suspense. The lighting, production design, and industrial atmosphere keep building pressure as things get darker and more dangerous. I also like that this feels more intense and emotionally charged than the first film, with Sigourney Weaver giving Ripley a commanding presence that is both tough and deeply human. For me, this is a great example of James Cameron turning scale, tension, and physical filmmaking into something unforgettable.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI really appreciate the classic filmmaking craft here. The painted backdrops instantly reminded me of Ralph McQuarrie style matte paintings from Star Wars. Those large environmental paintings used to extend the world beyond the physical sets. The same technique appears here with the planetary skies and distant colony environments. Then the spaceship shots rely heavily on miniature scale models, again very similar to the way Star Wars filmed its ships. Instead of CGI, they built physical models and shot them with controlled lighting and motion rigs. That tactile quality makes everything feel real. I can almost feel the metal and the weight of the ships moving through space.\n\nThere are moments where outdoor environments clearly look like studio sets, especially with some of the colony exteriors. But that is forgivable considering the year it was produced. The craftsmanship still shines through. Even with those limitations, the film still feels massive and immersive.\n\nWatching this now, I can clearly see early Avatar DNA all over the film, especially in the military aesthetics. The colonial marines, the armored vehicles, the drop ships, and the entire military industrial look feel like early prototypes of the RDA soldiers in Avatar. Even the group dynamics are similar. A corporate backed military force entering a hostile environment thinking they are in control, only to realize the planet has its own rules. The color grading also feels related. That bluish green industrial tone appears a lot in the interiors here, very similar to the cool metallic palette Cameron used in Avatar for the base facilities and night operations. But I still prefer Aliens visually because everything is tactile. Real sets, real lighting, real objects. I can feel the texture.\n\nThe lighting throughout the film is fantastic. It constantly reflects the level of danger they are in. As the situation gets worse, the environments become darker, more chaotic, more shadow filled. By the third act the cinematography becomes top tier filmmaking. The framing, the movement, the lighting inside the industrial corridors all build incredible tension.\n\nI also like that this one leans more into suspense and horror compared to the first film. Because I already know what the xenomorphs are capable of from part one, the danger here carries more weight. My heart was thumping in a completely different way. The addition of Newt raises the emotional stakes even more. Suddenly it is not just survival, it becomes protection. And that epic climactic score adds another layer of urgency. I was literally holding my breath. Just when I thought everything was over, Bishop gets stabbed. And I swear these xenomorphs really love hitchhiking. Every time they escape, one of them is secretly riding along.\n\nRipley herself is more heroic and aggressive here. She feels closer to Neytiri in Avatar Fire and Ash, more unforgiving and more deadly when protecting someone she cares about. James Cameron is really great at handling powerful female characters. They are strong not just physically but emotionally driven by purpose.\n\nI also cannot ignore the fact that Sigourney Weaver was really a sex symbol when she was young. She has that strong and seductive natural screen presence. Not the typical glamorized kind, but the kind that comes from confidence and authority. Ripley just commands attention the moment she walks into the frame.","poster":"img/aliens-1986.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Shining","year":1980,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-07","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":268,"review":"I actually watched this before back in college because I was reading the book and wanted some visuals in my head. Back then I got bored quickly, and honestly I did not yet understand the language of older cinema. So I dropped it.\n\nWatching it again now, it is clearly a slow burn style of storytelling. But even on my second viewing I still could not keep my mind fully focused on it. The characters feel uninteresting to me, and Jack’s spiral into insanity feels too abrupt. I never felt like I was slowly entering his psychological breakdown. I never got the chance to be fully immersed in his mental state before everything exploded.\n\nThe only scene that really grabbed my attention was the naked woman in the bathtub. At first it looks seductive and surreal, then suddenly it turns into a rotting corpse of an old woman. That reveal is disturbing. Other than that moment, the film was not scary for me. It mostly felt annoyingly weird rather than frightening.\n\nWendy and Danny’s acting also reminded me of the strange performance style in A Clockwork Orange. That same exaggerated and slightly unnatural acting. Kubrick seems to favor this kind of performance where the characters feel slightly off from normal human behavior. It creates an eerie atmosphere, but sometimes it also distances me emotionally from the characters.\n\nJack Nicholson, though, is undeniably great here. His facial expressions, the manic energy in his eyes, the way he moves through the hotel like a predator. Even if the story did not fully pull me in, his performance alone is memorable.","poster":"img/the-shining-1980.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"If I Had Legs I'd Kick You","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-07","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":516,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI immediately notice how different Rose Byrne feels here. She looks drained, and emotionally spent in a way that makes the whole film feel heavy. That discomfort only deepens because the story taps into one of my own fears about therapy, the idea that the person meant to help might be carrying their own private chaos.\n\nThe filmmaking adds a lot to that suffocating feeling. The close ups, handheld camera work, and dim lighting make everything feel unstable and exhausted, almost like the film is transferring that anxiety directly into me. What stays with me is Byrne’s performance. It is raw, unpleasant, and deeply convincing, the kind that makes the whole experience hard to shake off.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nUnique title for me. I also see a very different Rose Byrne energy here. She is not radiant like in X-Men or The Internship. She looks exhausted, uninterested, and very skinny. Almost like the life has been drained out of her.\n\nIt often feels like she is counseling herself rather than her patients. That is honestly one of my fears if I ever go to therapy. What if the counselor is not actually interested in my stories and feelings while I am being vulnerable in front of them? What if the therapist themselves is unstable? Watching her character made me wonder how many therapists out there are quietly dealing with their own chaos. Drug problems, paranoia, anger issues, narcissism. It makes me question the idea of authority in therapy. If someone is that out of sync with their own thoughts and emotions, should they even be practicing? At some point I was even thinking she should probably lose her license.\n\nThe direction, camera work, and lighting strongly reminded me of Good Time from 2017. Everything feels uncomfortable, suffocating, and dimly lit. The film constantly traps me in tight close ups and handheld shots that feel unstable, like the camera itself is anxious. The lighting is intentionally dark and murky, which adds to that sense of exhaustion and emotional suffocation. \n\nRose Byrne here reminds me of Robert Pattinson in Good Time and even Al Pacino in Insomnia. They all carry that same sleep deprived frustration, desperately trying to fix things that are constantly falling apart. Watching this feels similar to the experience of Insomnia and Uncut Gems at the same time. That heavy tiredness mixed with the anxiety of juggling too many problems at once. It almost feels like the film is transferring that exhaustion directly into me.\n\nAnd this is also why I do not do drugs. Watching her slowly slip between reality, memory, and hallucination is terrifying. It feels like her grip on the world is slowly dissolving.\n\nI was honestly convinced that Jessie Buckley would win Best Actress this year. But after watching Rose Byrne here, I am starting to think she might be the one who takes it. Her performance is just that raw and uncomfortable.","poster":"img/if-i-had-legs-i-d-kick-you-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hoppers","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-06","tags":[],"words":391,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a playful animated adventure that blends a lot of familiar ideas from other animated films. The expressive animation and quick humor keep the energy light, while the animal world feels lively and chaotic in a fun way. Beneath the comedy and colorful characters, the film pushes a simple message about unity and respecting nature, which gives the story a wholesome message.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe animation reminds me of Turning Red, that same expressive and playful style. The warmth of the grandmotherly love also gives strong Coco vibes. Then suddenly the story introduces the animal communication technology and even the main character mentions Avatar in the movie itself, which made me laugh. It feels self aware about the concept.\n\nThe animal community feels like a mashup of a lot of animated classics: A Bugs Life, Finding Nemo, and Ratatouille all sharing the same environment. Different species, different personalities, but all trying to survive and coexist in one ecosystem.\n\nThere are many funny punchlines scattered throughout the film enough to keep the energy light and fun. \n\nThe great animal council scene especially feels like an Avengers assemble moment, everyone gathering together with their own quirks and strengths.\n\nThe insect queen is probably the best thing to see in IMAX. She dominates the screen and visually pops with the scale and detail. Too bad she got squished though. That moment was both shocking and weirdly funny.\n\nThe story itself is simple but effective. Basically a big FAFO lesson. Mess with nature and nature messes back. I like that it also pushes the idea of unity for the common good. The mayor’s change of heart works nicely too. It leans into the theme that everyone still has some good inside them.\n\nMaybel reminds me of Hiro from Big Hero 6 because of the Asian look and energy. Even the dog collar cameo at the end made me think of the dog from Up for a moment. Small little reminders like that kept popping in my head while watching.\n\nOverall it feels like a fun animated mix of familiar ideas, but it still carries a good heart underneath all the chaos and jokes.","poster":"img/hoppers-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Bride!","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-05","tags":[],"words":326,"review":"Jessie Buckley really feels like Bellatrix here. Not just the wild energy but that unpredictable madness she carries in her eyes. The way she moves, talks, and explodes emotionally reminds me of Bellatrix Lestrange during her chaotic moments in Harry Potter. That mix of danger, seduction, and unhinged confidence. You never feel like she is fully in control, but that is exactly what makes her so captivating to watch. Christian Bale on the other hand felt meh to me. He is fine, but nothing about his performance stood out the way Buckley’s did.\n\nThe film is artistically lit. The gothic feeling mostly comes from the lighting rather than heavy color grading or overly elaborate sets like what we see in Frankenstein 2025. A lot of the mood comes from shadows, and how the light shapes the faces and textures of the environment. \n\nThis film is full of violence and romance at the same time. Parang Joker and Harley tandem but placed in a world that openly confronts sexual violence and social repression. The film also digs into loneliness, mental health, and identity. Two damaged people trying to define themselves in a society that already labeled them as monsters.\n\nHonestly this feels like a better treatment of what Joker Folie à Deux was trying to explore. The themes are almost the same. Broken people, chaotic love, rebellion against society. But Joker 2 went too far with the musical aspect. I think that is why people rejected it. Here the emotions feel more grounded even when the characters are unstable.\n\nIn a weird way this almost feels like a non canon sequel to Guillermo’s Frankenstein. If someone finished that film and wanted more of that tragic monster world, this scratches a similar itch. But tonally and in terms of scale, I would still prefer Guillermo’s Frankenstein. That one feels grander, while this one feels more intimate and psychological. Both interesting, but Guillermo’s version still hits harder for me.","poster":"img/the-bride-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mary","year":2024,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-04","tags":[],"words":249,"review":"I kept yelling wow at the visuals. For a moment I really thought this was shot by Greig Fraser, but to my surprise it is not. It has that beautiful desert cinematography comparable to Dune Part Two during the desert scenes, that yellowish brownish cinematic aesthetic. In Dune Part Two the desert palette leans heavily into warm ochres, deep ambers, and dusty gold tones. The highlights almost glow, while the shadows still hold texture. It feels epic yet intimate at the same time. \n\nThen during the more urban and palace interiors, this film reminded me of Dune Part One. That deep and dark greyish blue look, colder and more metallic. Dune Part One often used desaturated steel blues and charcoal tones inside structures, giving it that solemn, heavy atmosphere. The contrast between the warm desert and the cool interiors creates emotional separation, and I feel like Mary uses a similar language. Warm tones for journey and destiny, cooler tones for power, fear, and control. Ang ganda talaga ng grading.\n\nThere are parts where Lucifer is shown, similar to what was shown in The Passion of the Christ. Also, the wardrobe, sets, and actors are great here too. Noa Cohen as Mary carries both innocence and quiet strength. Anthony Hopkins as King Herod brings that commanding presence. I can feel his authority and paranoia.\n\nThe ending is emotional. Albeit not the same production, but for me this is a perfect prequel to The Chosen. \n\nJesus is the King of Kings!","poster":"img/mary-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Chaos Walking","year":2021,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-04","tags":[],"words":248,"review":"Weird premise. It feels like everyone suddenly has Professor X level mind reading powers. All the men’s thoughts are just floating around them like subtitles they cannot turn off. Kind of messy but interesting at the same time.\n\nIt was honestly funny that women do not have the noise here. Just like in real life, minsan parang impossible din basahin or intindihin ang nasa isip nila. And if I think about it another way, the fact that a bunch of men start losing their minds when a blonde woman appears out of nowhere is also unintentionally funny. \n\nThe real locations and set pieces are really nice though. They help build this dystopian world in a grounded way. The cinematography is actually great too. There are some really strong wide shots and textured outdoor scenes. The visuals are doing heavy lifting here. But the story is the weak point for me. It feels like the concept is stronger than the execution. And honestly, Tom and Daisy have no chemistry at all. I never fully believed in their connection, which makes the emotional stakes feel flat.\n\nAlso, low key terrifying concept. Hopefully this reality never comes true because imagine everyone seeing my crazy thoughts and wild daydreaming floating in the air. I'll get cancelled.\n\nStill, I kind of wish we get a More Than This film adaptation from Patrick Ness. I feel like that story has more emotional depth and psychological weight if handled right. That one could really hit.","poster":"img/chaos-walking-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sirāt","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":347,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film starts like a search story but slowly turns into a meditation on grief, and by the end I felt less entertained and more emotionally drained, like it just sits beside me in silence instead of giving closure. The film moves like a trance through the desert, blending pulsing sound and vast landscapes into something beautiful yet quietly threatening. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe rave music keeps the film alive even without much dialogue. Sound design is top notch. Either rave or wind blowing, both absolutely and masterfully complement the beautiful imagery.\n\nI love the yellowish brownish desert aesthetic. The cinematography is impressive not just in daylight but also during dusk and dawn. It really feels like a beautiful journey about finding a lost daughter, and the characters are easy to like so I got attached fast.\n\nI could already feel danger when they took the treacherous canyon path. The moment Esteban woke up to a peaceful morning I knew that there's something wrong. Everything felt calm, the dog was healed, almost too good to be true. Then the tragedy hit suddenly. Ang sakit nung reaction ni Luis. Raw and real. I cried too. I really sympathize with fathers in films losing a child, especially as someone who lost two children.\n\nThe minefield scene shocked me enough to sober their brain. Clever effects, the explosions looked real. From a search journey it suddenly became full survival mode and I found myself rooting for them harder. I was literally holding my breath. Anxiety inducing to the point I had to pause just to regain control of my senses.\n\nBy the end, the film stops feeling like a rescue story and becomes something heavier. The desert was never just a place, it was grief itself, endless and indifferent. When the journey ends, nothing is truly resolved, like the film just sits beside you and lets you process instead of comforting you.","poster":"img/sir-t-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Voice of Hind Rajab","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":390,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI felt emotionally wrecked early on and never really recovered. The film locks me into a confined space and refuses to let me breathe, using sound and stillness to make the helplessness unbearable. It feels less like watching a war story and more like being forced to listen to something that should never have happened.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nGripping war story told through the eyes of call center agents, with the real voice of a child trapped in a war zone. This is similar to The Call 2013 and The Guilty 2021 in structure, but this one is far more heart wrenching. This film is heavy. Less than 30 minutes in and I was already in tears.\n\nThe film traps me inside that car with Hind Rajab, surrounded by her dead family while troops are firing in her direction. At the same time, I feel the frustration of Omar, Rana, and Mahdi whose hands are tied. Everyone is helpless in different ways. The child is physically trapped, while the adults are trapped by protocol, fear, and limitations.\n\nThe actors are great. The camera work is great too. It feels like an actual observer inside the room. Some angles subtly show them losing emotional control. The cinematography chooses lenses that feel tight and claustrophobic, enough to trap me inside their heads. I also love the mirror and monitor reflections between characters. It almost looks like a cross fade or double exposure, suggesting how the situation is getting inside them psychologically.\n\nIt really feels like Hind Rajab versus layers and layers of bureaucracy. Not just bullets, not just war, but paperwork, procedure, waiting, and helplessness.\n\nThe director’s choice to blend real voice recordings and actual footage of Omar, Rana, and Nisreen adds painful realism. It blurs the line between film and reality. Hindi na siya parang movie lang. It feels like a document of something that should not have happened.\n\nBy the end, I was not thinking about technique anymore. I was just thinking about how war reduces everything to numbers and protocols while a child is begging to survive. War sucks! And this film makes sure I feel that truth in my bones.","poster":"img/the-voice-of-hind-rajab-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kokuho","year":2025,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":184,"review":"So much Japanese culture packed into one film. The string and percussion music, the dance, the costumes, the stage design, the makeup. Every frame feels deeply rooted in tradition.\n\nI was not fully engaged with the story itself. Mas na hook ako sa craft. The lighting and grading are really nice, especially how the stage glows against darker surroundings. There is something about watching the preparation, the rituals behind the performance, the discipline it takes to perfect every gesture.\n\nIt is just so weird for me seeing guys change their pitch just to sound like women. I cannot help but think about how they are a group of men deciding how a woman should act, speak, and move. But at the same time, I understand that this is the culture. Women are banned from performing in kabuki, so this tradition was built around that limitation. \n\nOverall, I appreciated this more as a cultural experience than an emotional one. I admired the precision, the artistry, and the dedication. Even if the narrative did not fully pull me in, the craftsmanship alone makes it worth watching.","poster":"img/kokuho-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Voice of Hind Rajab","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-03","tags":[],"words":263,"review":"I had to recommend this to my wife, so we watched it together again. The heaviness does not fall short on the second watch. The sadness, fear, emptiness, and anger are still there. If anything, mas masakit siya the second time because I already know what is coming. There is this quiet dread building from the first few minutes, and even though I am prepared, my chest still tightens the same way.\n\nListening again to Hind’s voice, knowing how it ends, hits differently. It is not shock anymore, it is inevitability. And that kind of inevitability is more brutal. \n\nWhat is really sad is that even if Mahdi followed the protocol down to the letter, the entire coordination still failed. He did everything right. He made the calls, relayed the information, respected the process. But systems do not guarantee humanity. Bureaucracy does not guarantee safety. That helplessness is FRUSTRATING. It makes the tragedy feel even more unfair.\n\nWhat I also noticed on the second watch is Hind’s brilliance. Even when faced with imminent danger, she was still thinking of solutions. She was negotiating. She was asking if there was another way for Omar and Rana to reach her if not by ambulance. That level of awareness and presence of mind, even as a child, earned another layer of respect and admiration from me. She was not just a victim. She was fighting in the only way she could, through words, through logic, through hope. It just reminds me again how cruel reality can be. War still sucks. And this story still breaks me.","poster":"img/the-voice-of-hind-rajab-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Pianist","year":2002,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":256,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI felt shaken more by the human suffering than by the violence itself. The film does not rush into chaos but shows how cruelty slowly becomes normal, and that gradual shift disturbed me more than any single shocking image.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI encountered some extreme visuals here that immediately pulled my attention. Seeing a wheelchair bound man thrown off a balcony sets the tone right away. Then the image of a man eating spilled food on the street shows pure desperation without needing explanation.\n\nThis film helped me understand the genocide more clearly. I watched how it slowly moved from exclusion to execution. Every moment feels like it could be their last, lives are taken casually, and violence becomes normalized. I could feel the denial among the victims, as if they believed this was only extreme discrimination and not the beginning of extermination. There is so much suffering, and the children feel the most vulnerable. Their helplessness hits harder than anything else. The hunger also stood out to me. It reminded me of Grave of the Fireflies in how survival itself becomes the entire story.\n\nThe sets are very impressive. The ghetto and surrounding locations feel authentic, and even the explosions feel real. A lot happens within the first hour, then the second half becomes mostly quiet and deeply contemplative, focusing more on endurance than on action.","poster":"img/the-pianist-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"All the Money in the World","year":2017,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-01","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":311,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat stayed with me most was how polished and controlled everything felt while the situation itself spiraled into chaos. I felt tension not just from the kidnapping but from watching how wealth, ego, and power quietly shape every decision. The film hooked me through atmosphere and performance.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis film is a gripping crime and survival story, shot and lit with a very deliberate cinematic polish.\n\nThe kidnappers honestly felt amateurish to me because they kept forgetting to put their masks on. That small detail made them less intimidating and more careless, which ironically made the situation feel more dangerous since mistakes escalate consequences.\n\nHis escape attempt was brilliant but painfully short lived, which made the situation even more hopeless. Then the ear cutting scene was brutal. Ridley Scott staged it in a way that I physically felt the pain instead of just watching it. \n\nMichelle Williams delivered another strong performance, on the same emotional level I felt from her in Shutter Island.\n\nI honestly laughed when Mr. Getty turned the ransom into a tax deduction. That moment perfectly captured the character study of extreme wealth. For him money is never just money. It is leverage, control, and negotiation power. Even the life of his grandson becomes a bargaining chip in a larger strategic game, including custody dynamics. His greed shows that having all the money in the world still does not make him feel secure enough to let go of it.\n\nThe film also briefly touches on media behavior. The press behaves like flies around waste, relentless and intrusive, prioritizing exposure over empathy. They exploit the victim’s suffering for a story, and the photographers show almost no sense of responsibility.","poster":"img/all-the-money-in-the-world-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Body of Lies","year":2008,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-01","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":352,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat I felt most was the constant tension of never knowing who is telling the truth. The film pulled me in through atmosphere and realism rather than emotion. I admired the craft and the gritty war espionage mood, even if the layered plotting sometimes made it harder for me to stay oriented.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI experienced this as a gripping spy and war story, shot and lit with Ridley Scott’s signature polish. The grading leans into dusty earth tones and harsh sunlight, which makes the Middle Eastern setting feel hot, tense, and constantly on edge.\n\nThe action sequences instantly reminded me of Black Hawk Down, which makes sense since it is the same director. The handheld chaos, the sudden bursts of explosives, and the grounded tactical movement all carry that same kinetic war energy.\n\nI found the story a bit hard to follow because of the layered deceptions. There are multiple intelligence agents, local assets, double crosses, and shifting loyalties. It becomes a puzzle with too many moving pieces, which fits the theme of espionage but can also feel overwhelming for some.\n\nRidley Scott focuses more on operational tension and atmosphere to fully fleshing out motivations through dialogue. Some conversations feel prolonged and procedural, which might drag for viewers who want clearer emotional anchors.\n\nMark Strong completely disappears into his role. I genuinely had to remind myself it was him. The accent work, the facial hair, the composed demeanor, and the refined styling all convincingly portray a wealthy and calculated Middle Eastern intelligence figure. He feels controlled and dangerous.\n\nLeonardo is Leonardo. His performance is solid as expected, but I felt he was somewhat under utilized for most of the film. The moment that truly stands out is the third act torture sequence. That is where the vulnerability, fear, and desperation finally explode on screen and remind me of how powerful he can be when the material allows him to fully break down.","poster":"img/body-of-lies-2008.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Blue Moon","year":2025,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":341,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI kept admiring the performance more than actually connecting with the character. The acting is fascinating to watch because of how deliberate and theatrical it feels, but at the same time it becomes exhausting, almost like listening to someone who loves hearing himself talk. By the end I respected the craft more than the emotional impact.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nEthan Hawke’s performance here reminds me of John Malkovich because of the way he delivers lines in a rhythm that feels half theatrical and half conversational. He talks like every sentence is carefully shaped yet casually thrown away, almost like he is performing a monologue to himself while others just happen to be present. There is also that intellectual arrogance mixed with insecurity, a man who sounds confident but is clearly unraveling underneath. Hawke disappears into the role.\n\nI knew something looked off at the start because I know Ethan Hawke is tall, yet he looked small here. After researching, I learned the director used forced perspective, trenches in the floor, and oversized furniture to reduce his stature to portray the 5'4 lyricist Lorenz Hart. That extra mile detail really worked for me.\n\nHe is extremely talkative and passionate about whatever topic he jumps into, parang hindi nauubusan ng kwento. But I honestly could not care about what he was babbling. I found myself relating to his partner who just wanted him to fast forward and finish the point already.\n\nThe best presence for me was Margaret Qualley. She is charismatic, elegant, and easy to listen to. I gravitated toward her story much more. She feels more mature here and gives me a confidence similar to Vanessa Kirby.\n\nI felt a bit hurt during his friend zone moment, but realistically speaking, he is old, drunk, and bisexual. I cannot imagine many twenty year old women wanting to seriously be with someone in that situation.","poster":"img/blue-moon-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Asteroid City","year":2023,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":136,"review":"The visual style reminds me of the opening of Toy Story 3 mixed with those old Instagram filters that turn lips orange, layered with Wes Anderson’s pastel palette.\n\nI always enjoy scenes with Maya Hawke because she reminds me so much of her mother Uma Thurman. The facial expressions, the voice softness, even the presence in quiet moments feels inherited rather than imitated.\n\nI was also surprised to see Margot Robbie. I recognized her voice instantly before even fully processing the character on screen, which was a fun little moment of recognition.\n\nBut once again I found myself detached from the story. Despite the stacked cast and visually pleasing presentation, I never really found an emotional anchor to hold on to, so the film felt more like an interesting display than something I was involved in.","poster":"img/asteroid-city-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Eyes Wide Shut","year":1999,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":331,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI went in expecting something seductive and wild, but what I experienced was more of a psychological ego collapse than an erotic story. The film uses desire as bait, then slowly turns it into insecurity and paranoia. Instead of excitement, I mostly felt unease, like watching a man spiral after realizing he isn’t as in control as he believes.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nNicole Kidman here is honestly too hot to handle and the film clearly knows it. The way she is presented feels intentional because the whole movie revolves around desire, insecurity, and temptation. Watching Tom Cruise’s character move through the night, I kept feeling how dangerous it must be to live in that level of privilege and attraction where opportunities and temptations constantly appear around every corner.\n\nThe score really carries the experience. It begins during the ritual with something resembling a Gregorian chant that feels sacred but also unsettling, then slowly transforms into a mysterious erotic atmosphere, and later into something colder and threatening. The music almost guides my emotions scene by scene, shifting the film from curiosity to tension to quiet danger.\n\nThe women in the secret gathering look perfect, like they were deliberately chosen to represent hand picked models / adult actresses. \n\nIt just feels like one mistake in a single night led to another. This shows the danger of making your profession, especially one that requires a license, your entire identity, because it creates a constant sense of superiority or of always being needed. I don’t know, maybe just don’t be too self assured? I couldn’t really sympathize with the characters. I was hoping for a full blown horny movie but instead got a broken ego smashed wide open by his wife’s crazy fantasy. \n\nMoral of the story: don’t smoke weed and confess your fantasies to your spouse.","poster":"img/eyes-wide-shut-1999.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"MaXXXine","year":2024,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":158,"review":"I watched this after X because I wanted to see what the future holds for Maxine and how her story would conclude.\n\nI honestly expected this to be more erotic because of the title, but instead it plays more like an 80s crime thriller, leaning into serial killer investigation vibes and Hollywood sleaze rather than pure slasher horror. The tone shifts into neon lit paranoia, detectives, media panic, and the darker side of fame rather than the rural horror of X or the psychological descent of Pearl.\n\nIt definitely looks like a leap in budget and scale, and it works as a final installment for the trilogy in terms of wrapping her journey into fame and identity. But for me the impact went downhill compared to the previous films. My ranking ends up as Pearl #1, X #2, MaXXXine #3 because the earlier entries felt more focused and memorable while this one feels more scattered despite being bigger.","poster":"img/maxxxine-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Pearl","year":2022,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":348,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film presents a colorful world that hides loneliness and repression. The bright cheerful look creates a strange contrast because underneath it all the emotions feel heavy and unstable. What stood out to me is how the story treats obsession as something that grows from isolation rather than pure evil. The longer she is denied identity and freedom, the more her inner world replaces reality. It becomes a tragic character portrait about a person who cannot exist within the life given to her.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe color palette reminds me of The Wizard of Oz, very bright and sunny, almost cheerful. It gives an energy of enthusiasm and curiosity, like the promise of a bigger world waiting outside the farm. That brightness ironically makes everything feel more unsettling because the emotions underneath are already dark.\n\nThe cinematography is great too, very clean execution and clear intent. The contrast between sets, wardrobe, and grading really builds this stylized world.\n\nThe dinner confrontation between Pearl and her mother is extremely well written, directed, and acted. The storm happening outside strengthens the tension, and the lighting and sound design make the scene feel extreme. It feels less like a normal argument and more like all the unsaid feelings finally exploding.\n\nI think her isolation, abandonment issues, and the extreme control from her mother are what pushed her into madness. She was never allowed to become her own person, so her desires had nowhere healthy to go.\n\nThe decaying lechon mirrors her mental deterioration, and the long uninterrupted confession scene justifies everything that has been building inside her. When she explains herself, it is disturbing but also tragic because she understands something is wrong yet cannot stop it.\n\nHer smile at the end is genuinely creepy. Her eyes show she is suffering deeply inside while forcing happiness outside. Completely rotten to the core. I gained newfound respect for Mia Goth here, superb performance!","poster":"img/pearl-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Solo Mio","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-25","tags":[],"words":274,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a light, comforting romance that balances humor and emotion well. It starts playful and uplifting, then gradually adds heart without becoming too heavy, making the overall vibe warm and easy to watch.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIt opens with an Ed Sheeran song that immediately puts me in a positive mood before the film slowly drops the emotional weight later on. The cheerful music acts like a soft cushion, so when the story starts getting heavier, the contrast hits harder.\n\nIt felt weird at first seeing Jesus (Jonathan Roumie) portrayed as having a wife, but I adjusted quickly once the story established its tone and intentions. \n\nThe chemistry between Matt and Gia works really well. It feels natural and relaxed, not forced or overly dramatic, which makes their interactions easy to watch and believable.\n\nKim Coates and Alyson provide solid comedic relief. Their timing helps break heavier moments and keeps the pacing balanced.\n\nJonathan and Julee also deliver good performances that support the story without stealing attention away from the central relationship.\n\nI’m glad the trailer stayed vague because I went in not fully knowing what direction it would take, which made the experience more enjoyable. I thought it would be a sad, reflective story about loss but it turned out to be a great romance film\n\nOverall the film is funny, charming, and feel good. The humor never feels out of place and the emotional beats stay light enough to remain comforting.","poster":"img/solo-mio-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Iron Lung","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-25","tags":[],"words":233,"review":"I appreciate the choice of aspect ratio because it gives the image a few extra vertical frames, making the cramped submarine feel taller yet somehow even more suffocating. Instead of cinematic openness, the added headroom ironically emphasizes how little real space there is to move.\n\nThe aesthetic reminds me of old digital machines like in Time Lapse 2014, full of rusty interiors and primitive technology. They also both use a green screen terminal or green phosphor monitor. Those were used in early computers, giving that industrial analog computer vibe.\n\nThe constant close up shots of either the character or tiny mechanical details make me feel claustrophobic, like I’m trapped inside the sub with him and there’s no escape at all.\n\nIt honestly feels like a low budget escape room video project from YouTube, more like an experimental concept exercise than a fully realized cinematic experience.\n\nWatching the film feels like staring at blank X ray photos without knowing what they mean. I keep waiting for interpretation or payoff, but the visuals stay cryptic without emotional translation.\n\nThe actor is not believable for me. I cannot feel the pain, frustration, or fear, which is a big problem for a film that relies almost entirely on performance.\n\nIt became painful to watch, no one in the cinema even reacted at all. Some even looked like they fell asleep, which unfortunately matched how disengaged I felt.","poster":"img/iron-lung-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"X","year":2022,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":236,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience X as a more straightforward slasher companion to Pearl. It leans heavier on shocks and gore, while the real interest comes from how it mirrors Pearl’s insecurities later in life rather than just functioning as a random body count movie.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI watched this after Pearl. It was not originally on my list, but I read somewhere on Letterboxd that Pearl is a prequel to X, so I got curious and followed it up immediately.\n\nI think Ti West really likes incorporating religious dogma and character studies of human complexity, especially in the ending where it almost feels like a form of “divine intervention”.\n\nThis one is definitely hornier and bloodier than Pearl. For me, Pearl still carries the same fear of rejection into her old age, and she ends up killing those who reject or disgust her, which makes her violence feel like a continuation of her insecurity rather than just random slasher behavior.\n\nThere is a night and day difference between Pearl and X. This one relies a lot more on jump scares, while Pearl leans more into tension and performance. I still like Pearl better because it feels more creative and the acting carries the horror more strongly than the shocks.","poster":"img/x-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lady Vengeance","year":2005,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":422,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe slow deliberate pacing kept me distant at first, but the second half pulled me in once the visuals became more precise and the colors drained away. The film slowly shifts from stylized drama into moral confrontation, where memories and guilt carry more weight than violence itself. The real tension comes from watching people decide what kind of humans they want to be when given power over someone who destroyed their lives.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI once again grew bored, maybe Korean films are really not for me. The pacing is very deliberate and slowburn, which makes it feel distant at first, but it got my attention every time she helped her cellmates get back at their oppressors. She is basically acting out vengeance on behalf of the helpless.\n\nAfter about an hour, the film suddenly felt more cinematic visually, almost as if the director changed the cinematographer halfway through. The compositions became cleaner and more intentional, and I also liked how the colors gradually faded away as the story prepared the audience for the dark main event. The desaturation feels like the world itself is losing innocence together with the characters. I am glad I gave it a chance because that shift makes the second half emotionally heavier. The slick black and white presentation works very well, and in the dining scene only the objects that remind them of their children keep their color. Those memories become the only remaining warmth in a life that has otherwise turned completely grey.\n\nThe footage involving the murderer and the kids was honestly too much to bear. The acting of both the children and the parents felt disturbingly believable, just painfully real, which makes the horror land harder than any graphic imagery.\n\nAnd why the police imprisoned a wrong person? For me that is massive negligence. The story quietly shows how institutions sometimes prefer a convenient answer over the correct one, and that mistake becomes the root of every tragedy that follows.\n\nThe choice Lee Geum-ja gave to the parents turns into a character study about what parents would actually do if given direct access to the murderer of their child. It reminded me of the moral debate structure of 12 Angry Men, where individuals challenge each other’s beliefs until a collective decision forms. \n\nI wish every child abuser and murderer would suffer the same consequences.","poster":"img/lady-vengeance-2005.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Memories of Murder","year":2003,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-24","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":310,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFeels less like a mystery and more like watching people slowly break under pressure. Instead of heroic detectives solving a puzzle, the film shows investigators whose frustration and ego become obstacles to truth.\n\nThe early sections almost play like dark comedy because of how chaotic and careless the procedures are, but that humor gradually disappears as desperation sets in. What begins as incompetence turns into moral collapse. The case stops being about catching a killer and becomes a portrait of how authority can fail when certainty matters more than evidence.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe early crime scene is handled in a continuous long shot that shows how disorganized everything is. It is frustrating to watch because evidence is being contaminated, but at the same time it is oddly funny because of how careless and chaotic the police are.\n\nThe first half feels slow for me. I start to think this is another story where cops just point at innocent people to close the case. They torture suspects until they say whatever the detectives want to hear, even going as far as creating scripted confessions. Nakakainis si Detective Park. He is so incompetent and negligent. The detectives keep doing flying kicks and getting drunk like they are in some action comedy instead of handling a real serial killer case.\n\nEven the most straightforward detective slowly collapses morally under pressure. The longer the case drags on, the more desperate and violent they become. Police brutality becomes part of the narrative, and I start to feel that they are part of the problem. Their ego, bias, and prejudice keep clouding their judgment, and that becomes the very reason they fail to capture the real killer.","poster":"img/memories-of-murder-2003.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sound of Metal","year":2019,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-24","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":406,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film turns sound into emotion. Instead of just watching a character lose hearing, it becomes more about control, denial, and learning to stop fighting reality. What hits me is how acceptance is not immediate. I see someone trying to adapt on the surface while secretly chasing the past underneath. The story works as a portrait of identity crisis, where peace only arrives when the need to restore the old self finally lets go.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nParang yung hearing problem ni Jack sa A Star Is Born, but here it becomes the entire world of the film. The sound design technique reminds me of CODA in the way it shifts perspective, but this one forces me to experience the distortion, the ringing, the sudden silence. The audio cuts are not just effects, they become emotional tools that put me inside Ruben’s panic.\n\nI can see panic and fear clearly on Riz Ahmed’s face. He really is a great actor. The story does not hesitate, it presents the problem right away. There is no slow build up to the loss of hearing. It just happens and I immediately feel how fragile everything is. I end up feeling so grateful that I can hear.\n\nOlivia Cooke is also strong here. I feel her confusion and fear about their new reality. It is also nice to see Lauren Ridloff from Eternals. She adds authenticity and warmth to the deaf community scenes.\n\nThe film is mostly quiet while showing growing frustration and anger. I watch him resist the reality, pretend to adapt, then secretly cling to hope that it can be fixed. When the story becomes warmer and he seems to accept his condition, I realize it is only partial acceptance. Deep inside he still wants his old life back, and I cannot blame him. His passion and livelihood are tied to sound.\n\nWhat makes it sad is that he never fully absorbs the essence of the deaf community before choosing surgery. When the implant does not give him the clarity he expected, it feels like another loss. In the final moment when he removes the processors and sits in pure silence, I interpret it as the first time he truly chooses stillness. To me that is when he finds peace.","poster":"img/sound-of-metal-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Everything Everywhere All at Once","year":2022,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-23","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":680,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA wildly creative and emotionally grounded multiverse story that turns chaos into a metaphor for family, regret, and connection. Beneath the absurd humor and nonstop genre switching is a very human story about an overwhelmed parent trying to stay present in a life that feels like it is slipping in every direction.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nChaotic start. Audit, divorce, father’s party, daughter introducing her girlfriend, customer service, broken ventilation. It already sets the tone and justifies the title. Everything is happening at the same time and I immediately feel how overwhelmed Evelyn is, like life is attacking her from all directions before the sci-fi even begins.\n\nGreat musical score, cinematography, and grading. It adds to the beautiful chaotic mess and I can really feel the A24 DNA, weirdly out of this world ideas placed into something emotionally grounded. First time I watched this, it honestly felt like a bootleg Multiverse of Madness because I was not yet accustomed to this kind of cinema language, but on rewatch I realized the visual effects work because they are combined with in camera footage so the insanity still feels tactile and real.\n\nAng hirap ng unang struggle nya. Fate of the multiverse or the disallowance of her tax deductions? That joke works because it is true to her character. She is not a chosen one, she is a tired immigrant mom whose biggest fear at that moment is still the IRS.\n\nAbout the wardrobe, Evelyn wearing a red jacket reminds me of Back to the Future, and I love the subtle changes in aspect ratio because it visually tells me which reality we are in without explaining it verbally. I also get Ben 10 vibes, choosing which version of herself is appropriate against an enemy, mixed with Inception style jumping between layers.\n\nJobu feels like Thanos using the reality stone on Knowhere and the film even recreates the 2001 Space Odyssey ape scene. All of these references could have been distracting but here they become part of the language of the multiverse, like every genre and era of cinema exists simultaneously.\n\nThis is action, comedy, drama, and romance thrown into one bowl. Underneath all the absurdity it feels like a story about depression, the fear of not mattering, regrets about decisions, and the lack of connection between parent and child because we are everywhere mentally and emotionally, all over the place, so we fail to be present with the people in front of us. The effect becomes generational trauma passed down.\n\nIt also plays like a midlife crisis, realizing it might be too late and nothing worked the way you imagined. The action spectacle becomes a manifestation of her internal panic rather than just entertainment, and that is why the performances from the main cast and the stunt choreography hit harder emotionally.\n\nIt is trippy and needs a lot of brain cells to unpack, but I sometimes feel the amount of action masks the real message. For me it could have been shorter so the emotional core lands earlier and gives more room to deepen it instead of constantly escalating the chaos.\n\nYou know sometimes I also feel like I am not inside my own body, like I am observing myself instead of living in the moment. That kind of disassociation connects strongly to Evelyn because she literally lives different versions of her life and none of them feel fully hers. The multiverse becomes a visual representation of being mentally absent, where every possibility exists except the ability to simply stay present.\n\nAs a parent this film reminded me not to take my kids for granted no matter how busy life gets or how much they disobey. Do not disown them and do not let them drift away emotionally. Always reach out and choose to stay with them even in the most ordinary moments, because those ordinary moments are the only reality that truly matters.","poster":"img/everything-everywhere-all-at-once-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Days of Thunder","year":1990,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-23","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":496,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFeels like Top Gun on asphalt. The film treats racing not just as a sport but as identity. Ego, masculinity, pride, and obsession expressed through engines instead of jet fighters. The relationships matter more than the races, and the drama comes from learning when to compete and when to trust. Beautiful lighting and strong presence from the cast carry it even when the sound mixing struggles.\n\nWhat stood out to me is how the movie frames speed as personality. The hotshot lives to prove himself, the veteran races to survive his past, and the mentor figure tries to keep them from destroying each other. It follows a familiar sports rhythm but it works because the emotions feel grounded in character. Beneath the racing, it becomes a story about maturity, humility, and learning that winning sometimes means letting someone else take the lead.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe opening immediately reminded me of Top Gun because of the way the machines were introduced like living icons, then matched with a loud energizing rock track that makes the scene feel like preparation. The lighting also mirrors Top Gun, heavy backlight from the sun hitting the metal surfaces, and that golden glow wrapping the subjects. Knowing they share the same director makes perfect sense because the visual language treats racing the same way aviation was treated, as masculinity, ego, and adrenaline turned into cinematic poetry.\n\nI watched this to commemorate Robert Duvall. He actually carries a huge presence here and is not just a sidekick mentor. And wow, Yondu is here too! I almost did not recognize Nicole Kidman because she looks very young and natural here (before the plastic surgery).\n\nI could feel F1 inside the plot structure. There is a trio dynamic and the struggle to function as a team, learning to set aside ego and pride between the hotshot and the veteran while a central figure tries to make them reconcile, then the inevitable accident, then the celebratory release at the end. The sports drama rhythm feels satisfying.\n\nCole thinking the doctor was just role playing was funny because he still saw life as a competition and assumed everything was another game. At the latter part of their interaction, the silence, dim lighting, and close distance of their faces created very strong sexual tension. Watching their chemistry honestly made me want to watch Eyes Wide Shut immediately.\n\nThis film is beautifully lit. The shapes and colors of light always feel intentional, quietly suggesting emotion beneath the dialogue.\n\nMy only complaint is the sound mixing. Like many older films, dialogue is extremely hard to hear but the tire screeches and engine revs suddenly explode in volume. The balance is inconsistent so I kept adjusting volume every scene. But at least the story works better for me than Ron Howard’s Rush.","poster":"img/days-of-thunder-1990.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Reservoir Dogs","year":1992,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-23","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":188,"review":"It felt like Among Us, nobody knows who the rat is and paranoia slowly poisons the room. Every line of dialogue feels like an accusation waiting to happen. This also feels like a prototype of The Hateful Eight. Both movies trap strangers inside a single location after violence already happened outside, then the story becomes a slow autopsy of the event through conversations. Characters keep retelling their version of truth, alliances form and break, someone is secretly hiding an identity, and the quiet observer in the room ends up being the most dangerous person. Tarantino also structures both films around long monologues, and delayed reveals rather than chronological storytelling, so the audience pieces together the crime the same way the characters do. Even the tone is similar, dark humor mixed with sudden brutality.\n\nI lost interest though. I realized this particular Tarantino style, heavy dialogue chamber pieces like Reservoir Dogs and The Hateful Eight, is not the side of his filmography I gravitate toward. I prefer his more kinetic or visually playful films because these talk driven standoffs feel more like theatrical exercises than cinematic immersion for me.","poster":"img/reservoir-dogs-1992.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"2001: A Space Odyssey","year":1968,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":629,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA meditative sci-fi experience that feels more like art than traditional storytelling. The practical scale models, classical music, and silence create a grand and sacred atmosphere, while the slow pacing demands patience. It is less about action and more about evolution, artificial intelligence, and humanity’s place in the universe. I admire its ambition and craft, even if parts of it left me confused and overwhelmed.\n\nThe film follows humanity’s mysterious encounters with a monolith across different stages of evolution, culminating in a deep space mission controlled by an advanced artificial intelligence. What begins as a technological journey gradually transforms into an abstract exploration of consciousness, existence, and transformation beyond human understanding.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nBeautiful horizon and landscape shots. The eerie vocal and musical score during the monolith scenes reminds me of the unsettling soundscapes in Yorgos Lanthimos films. The fear comes from how the music feels sacred yet unnatural at the same time, like a choir meant for something divine but directed toward something unknown and indifferent to humanity. It sounds holy but emotionally empty, which makes it weirdly scary and gives me the creeps.\n\nThe scale models in the film look amazing. The sense of size feels tactile compared to CGI, and the lighting interacts with the miniature surfaces naturally, with believable absorption and bounce that makes everything feel physical. For the music, the film uses classical compositions that elevate the grandeur of space: Also sprach Zarathustra, The Blue Danube. All of them reinforce the majestic and mysterious tone of outer space.\n\nThis is a very slow burn. Some scenes are intentionally prolonged to immerse me in the visuals. For me, it is not always effective because the message could be delivered with about three fourths of the runtime of certain shots. The silence is used brilliantly though, emphasizing what space actually sounds like, nothing.\n\nShowing HAL as just a red dot works really well. The image never changes but the meaning changes. At first it feels like a watchful guardian, but later the same visual becomes threatening, almost vengeful. I remember the visual of HAL reading the lips of the two crew members. I actually recreated that idea in a college project where I avoided dialogue and focused the camera on lips so the audience had to read them instead of hearing them.\n\nThe film also feels like an early warning about dependence on artificial intelligence. A machine without morality or compassion simply follows instructions perfectly, and that perfection becomes dangerous. HAL’s monotonous voice makes it colder and more menacing.\n\nI expected more of a space journey because of the title Odyssey, but instead the film becomes about a journey of human evolution into a higher form of existence.\n\nIt still holds up after almost sixty years, though I expected a different kind of approach. I definitely can feel that Chrisopher Nolan took some inspiration from here. I think this would be best experienced on an IMAX screen.\n\nThe last thirty minutes become extremely strange. The visuals shift rapidly from digital looking patterns to cosmic imagery to liquid like reactions similar to chemical reactions in Oppenheimer, then landscapes tinted like negative film, night vision, or heat vision. I honestly do not know what is happening. It feels like a drug trip and left me disoriented with a headache.\n\nI honestly thought that when the pod is in a bedroom scene that the film will reveal that the astronaut just experienced some sort of a \"ride simulation\". \n\nThere's a lot of things need to be unpacked in this film and I definitely need a rewatch to understand it better.","poster":"img/2001-a-space-odyssey-1968.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fargo","year":1996,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":129,"review":"It is nice to see William H. Macy taking a lead role. His character is twisted by desperation and keeps digging himself deeper the more he tries to fix things. Young Peter Stormare with that slick brushed up hair honestly looks like Ryan Gosling. Their eyes and jawlines look astonishingly similar. I was also distracted once again by the accent, this time from Frances McDormand. I checked her filmography but I barely recognized her from those movies, maybe because without that accent she does not stand out as much to me. Still, kudos for the leg shot scene.\n\nAnother meh for the Coen for me. But I guess this one works better story wise than No Country for Old Men, though visually it is less striking in its shots.","poster":"img/fargo-1996.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Once Upon a Time... in Hollywood","year":2019,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":605,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA laid back and nostalgic Tarantino film that focuses more on atmosphere, character hangouts, and tension simmering beneath the surface rather than constant action. The performances, especially Leonardo DiCaprio and Brad Pitt, carry the emotional core, while the setting recreates late 1960s Hollywood with warmth and affection. It feels like a reflective love letter to an era and its fading stars, building quietly before releasing its energy in a memorable finale.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI think this is my third watch. I forgot Al Pacino was here. He looks very different, older and fat. I also got surprised again seeing so many familiar faces pop up, like Timothy Olyphant, Sydney Sweeney, Austin Butler, who looks great on a horse, Maya Hawke, and Zoë Bell. It makes the movie feel like this stacked with cameo.\n\nThis is the most chill Quentin film for me because the evil is brewing in the background instead of being the main engine upfront. I think it is really important to know the historical context to fully feel the danger, especially in the ranch scene where Cliff is surrounded by the hippies. The way Quentin builds tension there is so quiet but heavy, like I am watching a trap slowly closing. He also did a good job mixing real life people with his fictional characters in a way that makes the world feel oddly believable, like it is history, but also a fairy tale he is rewriting.\n\nI laughed at the sound design detail when Cliff puts down the milk in the truck trunk and it lands with that gong like hit. It is such a random funny sound choice, but it feels like a sly wink to kung fu and the Bruce Lee connection. That whole Bruce Lee sequence works for me, the long take, the dialogue, the action, and the way it is both funny and sharp at the same time. It is not just a gag, it also tells me a lot about Cliff’s confidence and the kind of myth Quentin wants him to have.\n\nThe cinematography, lighting, and color grading feel right for that late 1960s Los Angeles tone, warm, dusty, sun soaked, and slightly nostalgic. Nothing felt wildly new or ground breaking, but I like how Quentin makes some scenes feel like they are literally pulled from old cinema. \n\nI have to give it again to Leonardo’s acting, especially the moment when the director and the little girl compliment him. That scene hits because I can feel Rick’s insecurity and hunger for validation. Then of course that flamethrower moment at the end is one for the books. Brad Pitt is great too, calm, charming, casually dangerous, and funny while he is high as fuck. I like how he plays Cliff as someone who barely reacts, but I still feel the threat under the surface.\n\nAnd then the signature QT ultra violence at the ending, bloody fun. Those hippies deserved dying in the most brutal and gruesome way, and Quentin films it like catharsis, not tragedy. This is really a “what if” happy ending, where the Manson crew ends up colliding with Rick and Cliff instead of Sharon Tate’s home. I read it as a love letter to the forgotten Hollywood working stars like Rick Dalton who are getting pushed out by changing times, and also a love letter to Sharon Tate, like Quentin giving her a different fate inside his movie universe.\n\nMuch better on the third try.","poster":"img/once-upon-a-time-in-hollywood-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Grave of the Fireflies","year":1988,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":137,"review":"Grabe ang war. Kawawa talaga ang mga bata. The film really shows how brutal conflict is, especially when it strips children of safety, family, and basic needs. The limited food resources are painfully scarce and survival feels almost impossible. Every meal becomes a struggle.\n\nIt is mostly quiet and meditative. There are long stretches where not much is said, and that silence makes everything heavier. I feel very sorry for them. They are orphaned, starving, and almost invisible to the world around them. No food, no real help, and even the adults who should care for them are either overwhelmed or indifferent.\n\nIt is a very emotional film. Simple in structure but deeply impactful. It does not rely on loud drama, it just shows suffering as it is, and that is what makes it hit so hard.","poster":"img/grave-of-the-fireflies-1988.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Worst Person in the World","year":2021,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":527,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA vibrant yet introspective relationship story about indecision, timing, and personal growth. It feels lighter and more energetic than generational trauma dramas, but still carries emotional weight through honest conversations and character flaws. Renate Reinsve brings warmth and complexity to a woman constantly searching for something more, even when she does not fully understand what that is. It is relatable, frustrating, and human.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis feels more high energy than Sentimental Value. It follows a woman exploring her options, and it is lighter and easier for me to resonate with compared to generational trauma stories. Renate Reinsve is a welcoming presence. I think she works best in roles with warmth and liveliness instead of constant sadness.\n\nI get Bridges of Madison County vibes. Julie wants to break free from being on the sidelines of her own life and grow at her own pace, while Eivind wants to slow things down and avoid becoming irrelevant inside a relationship that moves too fast. They both feel trapped in relationships where they cannot fully be themselves, so when they meet, it feels like a new possibility and also an escape.\n\nKudos to Anders Danielsen Lie for the breakup scene. His face and tone move through different stages. First denial, making sure Julie understands what she is doing and analyzing her like diagnosing a symptom rather than accepting reality. Then anger, asking invasive questions and assuming she is projecting or just having a bad day. Then bargaining, saying he accepts her flaws and even accepts not having a child rather than losing her. That quick emotional progression felt very real, it feels like shifting gears.\n\nI resonate strongly with Julie’s complaint about Axel’s mindset. She says that for Axel, strength means being analytical and formulating thoughts, and if she is less analytical he sees her as weaker. I tend to analyze and solve everything too, so that line hit me personally.\n\nThe mushroom sequence is trippy, especially the imagery of her older body and using her period as war paint. It visualizes her inner anxieties in a strange but memorable way.\n\nI knew conflict would happen because Julie wants change while Eivind wants stability. It is clear when she tells him he is fine serving coffee until fifty while she wants something more. There is internal pressure in their relationship from the start.\n\nI think Renate excels at playing complicated women. What frustrates me is Julie’s indecision. She chooses someone new but later dislikes the slower life he wants, then goes back to her ex because she cannot talk to anyone else. It proves Axel’s warning that she would only realize their connection later. She keeps repeating patterns and it becomes frustrating, the type of character that makes me want to say \"I told you so\".\n\nHer relief after the miscarriage also fits that pattern. It suggests she might not be ready for permanence and may prefer searching rather than settling, someone not capable yet of sustaining a long term relationship.","poster":"img/the-worst-person-in-the-world-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"EPiC: Elvis Presley in Concert","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":156,"review":"I can really feel his passion and pride in his craft. The director already did a great job in the movie version, but this one is on a completely on a different level! His showmanship is impeccable. Even if I do not know most of the songs, my feet and knees start moving while watching.\n\nHe feels literally and figuratively bigger than life on the IMAX screen. I can see how physically and mentally present he is with his band, his back up singers, and the audience. He moves like a conductor leading an orchestra, controlling energy and emotion in real time.\n\nMy last concert docufilm before was One Direction, and the difference in stage command is massive. No offense to Austin Butler, but I realized nothing can match the real Elvis.\n\nThe restoration of the footage is great, crystal clear audio and visuals. I ended the film with newfound respect and admiration for Elvis Presley.","poster":"img/epic-elvis-presley-in-concert-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Bugonia","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":305,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nMuch stronger on a second viewing because the film shifts from feeling like a mystery to feeling like an interpretation exercise. Once the ending is known, the tension changes into curiosity about motives and perspective. Instead of asking what is happening, the film becomes about who is right. It plays like a psychological character study disguised as a thriller, with a strangely humorous tone underneath the bleakness.\n\nA confined confrontation between two opposing worldviews where truth is uncertain. As paranoia, persuasion, and survival instincts collide, both characters believe they are protecting humanity in completely different ways. The film gradually reveals that the real conflict is not just physical escape, but deciding which reality to trust.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis film is much better on my second viewing. Now that I already know how the film ends from my first watch, Teddy feels more heroic in trying to save us from the aliens, and the kidnapping and torture suddenly become justifiable. And Teddy makes 100% sense that aliens are very much masters of deception. But at the same time, Michelle is also heroic in her own way because she gives humanity the cure that could save us from destroying ourselves.\n\nDuring my first watch it felt like a crime thriller. Now that the noise,  and doubts are out of the way, the motivations are clearer, it feels more like a character study for each of them.\n\nAnd if Yorgos intended this as a comedy, it still works as one. It becomes a dark comedy about a bleak commentary that people on Earth are doomed, no chance, no hope to save themselves from the harm they brought upon humankind.","poster":"img/bugonia-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A Star Is Born","year":2018,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-20","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":1563,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA deeply emotional rise and fall romance about love, fame, and self destruction. The chemistry feels natural, the music carries the emotion, and the performances make the relationship believable from the first meeting. Instead of a typical feel good romance, it becomes a painful character study about addiction, insecurity, and how success can slowly break a person. Romantic, intimate, and heavy.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI think I watched this film more than 10 times. I played La Vie en Rose 1000x times and even have it tattooed on my skin.\n\nI have been meaning to rewatch this as a quad feature watching all its predecessors, but I want to watch it now after having rewatched La La Land. I felt empty after that hurtful ending and could not seem to find a worthy film to watch that would equally hurt.\n\nStory time, I am that kind of person who always despised romance films, but I got really intrigued when I watched the music video of Lady Gaga’s Shallow playing in our cafeteria back in 2019. Plus it is her acting debut and Bradley Cooper’s directorial debut, and Cooper from The Hangover franchise singing? He is a heartthrob actor, what does he know about directing? So I gave it a shot and was not prepared for it.\n\nLess than 6 minutes in you will see a billboard about suicide awareness and that is a foreshadowing I caught during the second viewing knowing how the ending will happen. From the very start you'll feel that he is a ticking time bomb about to explode because of his alcohol addiction. His acting, his sunburnt and exhausted appearance, and especially his voice, that deep gravelly growl that sounds damaged from years of drinking and screaming on stage, add a strong sense of depth and realism.\n\nI am glad Jackson Maine is not homophobic despite being a rockstar. I genuinely like the banter with the drag queens. It feels natural and human. And Ally singing La Vie en Rose is etched in my mind and heart forever. I know it by heart! The song itself is already beautiful but when it was added by Lady Gaga’s magic touch it became a personal timeless classic. It is so fucking romantic. Jack’s eyes are glittering with happiness when he witnesses that, they literally light up.\n\nThe dressing room was the first intimate encounter with Jack removing Ally’s fake eyebrow and a brief touch of hands. All close up shots are very intentional in this film.  Jack’s eyes are full of wonder, he is really interested in Ally at that point and there is no turning back.\n\nThe second close encounter was in the cop bar when Jack touched Ally’s nose. At that point I got really hooked. Kinilig ako malala dun. Ang ganda din ng pasok ng Whipping Post by The Allman Brothers Band at lyrics na “I feel like I’m dying.” Well there are two ways to read that, dying inside to hold her or foreshadowing that their love will be his demise. I think that moment really engraved in my subconscious that is why I chose a wife with a similar beautiful nose. Jack is really staying true to his heart when he said “it’s a good thing we met” with that playful tone. And it was real fun when Ally sucker punched a drunk cop harassing Jack.\n\nThird encounter was outside the supermarket and that is when their interaction deepened when Jack opened up about his past. And when Ally sang Shallow for the first time it was pure bliss. I am really glad there was no kissing or fucking in their first meeting. I knew it was a different story. And Jack taking another look at Ally and Ally doing that gesture tracing her nose is one of the most iconic moments for me.\n\nI like her father and best friend too, Ramon. Very supportive of her potential aside from Jack. That instrumental intro of Alibi playing while going to Jack’s concert is so exciting. And the lighting and grading looks like a real concert.\n\nWhen Bradley sang Shallow, oh man the guy can sing! And when Ally joined it was pure joy. At first she was really shy but she got the groove and became confident. All my doubts about their singing, acting, and film capabilities were blown by the wind.\n\nThe morning after the concert when they made love was really intimate and earned. Music to My Eyes also has the perfect balance of romantic tenderness and playful pop energy, matching their genuine connection.\n\nJack and his older brother’s conversation about their dad’s ranch and taking care of him was really hard to understand at first due to their growling voices, but once understood it adds another layer to Jack’s background.\n\nAlways Remember Us This Way is also a nice song. I can really see the passion of Ally and the proudness in Jack’s eyes during that concert scene. He is really happy for Ally.\n\nUnang kita ko palang kay Rez Gavron alam ko nang bad news eh. Kupal eh. After the interaction of Ally and Rez, Jack’s condition worsened. He went downhill.\n\nTheir intimate scenes in their house were pure celebration of joy and companionship. Jack said it himself, Ally gave him a place to call home, something he never felt before.\n\nTheir wedding scene while I Don’t Know What Love Is is playing and Ally’s single tear drop is one for the books. Their chemistry is off the charts. Though a lot of scenes got cut and left in the editing room, it still worked. Though if there would be a director’s cut, why not?\n\nThen the spiral happens more often. He projects his insecurities onto her. Well sometimes we also do that, don’t we? We hate ourselves so much we put that anger toward other people. He feels irrelevant and overshadowed and constantly being kept on the sidelines. His hearing worsens and his alcohol and drug abuse get worse too. The tragedy slowly unfolds starting here.\n\nI forgot that he already confessed he attempted suicide when he was 13 years old to his counselor. So yeah, I got shocked again that he was really mentally sick even at a young age.\n\nAlly was really searching if her husband was still there inside Jack when they met at rehab. I can really feel everything they were thinking just through how their eyes acted. Ang galing nila! Even without words their eyes can tell a million words.\n\nThere is also a tender moment between Jack and his brother Bobby. He admitted he idolized his brother and not their father. And somewhere I read that only Sam Elliott can act while reversing a car, and true enough every time I reverse I remember Sam Elliott haha.\n\nJack was really improving, really facing his wrongdoings. But then this fucking Rez decided to be the world’s number 1 asshole. That little pep talk triggered something inside him again, his eyes were different from there on. When he realized that Ally was lying, that I think was the last straw. He did not want Ally lying to him just to make him feel better. Then they did again the “I just want to take another look at you” thing they did at the start. And fucking fuck, that was the last look. Fuck. Devastating.\n\nThe framing, the silence, the color all suggest something wrong will happen at that moment. He removed his belt just like he said he did when he was 13. And that final image of his face, I deeply resonated with him. I was him every single day back in 2018 to 2019 in the mirror. Tired, defeated, ashamed of myself, and no purpose in life. That is why this became instantly one of my favorites back then. I am thankful I am not the same person anymore. You can see his torso hanging through the window but not the entire body. It is subtle but very devastating. A really fucking sad story.\n\nThe last performance I’ll Never Love Again was really freaking heavy. All the scenes from the start lead to this final act. Perfectly written and cleanly executed direction. In just a few hours I got too attached with Jack and Ally’s rise and fall, as if I knew them for a long time, sympathizing with their success and struggles. Then that abrupt cut to a flashback of Jack singing this song for Ally sometime after he got out of rehab really punched me in the gut.\n\nThe final scene where Ally looked at the camera is the real A Star Is Born moment. I read this tragedy as the natural cause of birth of a star. A star needs to die so a new one can be born. It is a continuous cycle of creation and destruction just like what Jack told Bobby, music is essentially 12 notes between any octave. The 12 notes repeat, it is the same story told over and over forever. All the artist can offer the world is how they see the 12 notes.\n\nA really masterpiece, if you want to fell hurt this will exceed your expectations.","poster":"img/a-star-is-born-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"GoodFellas","year":1990,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":353,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA crime rise and fall story told directly from the main character’s perspective. Compared to more operatic mafia films, this one feels street level, more about lifestyle than legacy. Goodfellas is emotionally less heavy than the Martin's most powerful works.\n\nThe story follows a young man who becomes involved in organized crime, learning the system and climbing through its ranks while enjoying wealth and influence. Over time, pressure, paranoia, and consequences catch up to him, forcing him to face the cost of the life he chose.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film feels similar to The Wolf of Wall Street in the way the narration walks me through Henry’s point of view, his rise in the business, how his colleagues are introduced, and how the conversations stay awkward but tense. \n\nAt times I also feel like this is a The Godfather type of story but presented with the energy and rhythm closer to The Wolf of Wall Street. I watch how he enters the culture, learns the illegal system, and navigates shady situations until success becomes normal to him. For me, the mafia here feels less formal compared to the Corleones. The Italian family structure in The Godfather feels more dignified and organized, while this one feels like a group of hustlers surviving day to day. There is more swagger in protecting a family legacy than in running scattered operations.\n\nMy expectations were high because this is Martin Scorsese, yet it did not hit me as strongly as his other films. I can see his style in the direction, but the writing feels looser and less focused. I do not always feel a clear dramatic build, more like a constant rush of moments instead of one strong narrative push.\n\nIn the end the character arc reminds me of Jordan Belfort. Henry starts powerful and influential but ends up ordinary, living a normal life after tasting excess. The fall from glamour to normalcy becomes the final punishment.","poster":"img/goodfellas-1990.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fist of Fury","year":1972,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":122,"review":"I can clearly see shots here that later inspired Quentin Tarantino in Kill Bill, especially the sudden zoom into close up reactions. The camera snaps into the actor’s face right at the emotional beat. The lighting is theatrical. Even the production design feels arranged almost like stage sets.\n\nThis feels more like an old TV Sunday special presentation than a cinematic film. Some fights come off comedic because of the sped up motion. But there are still moments where the choreography shines, especially when Bruce Lee slows down and you can see the precision of his movements, the timing of strikes, and the controlled power behind each motion. Those parts still look cool and remind me why his screen presence became legendary.","poster":"img/fist-of-fury-1972.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Enter the Dragon","year":1973,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":307,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA more polished and cinematic evolution of Bruce Lee’s formula, even if the emotional hook feels lighter. The film refines what came before by using real locations, clearer fight staging, and more deliberate cinematography that gives it a bigger screen presence. Bruce Lee feels sharper and more athletic, and the story shifts toward a spy infiltration mixed with tournament structure rather than pure revenge drama. The action is cleaner and more readable, though I connect less emotionally compared to Fist of Fury since it plays more like a mission than a personal story.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis still carries the same DNA as Fist of Fury but now it feels more refined and cinematic. The film finally becomes alive because many scenes are shot on real locations instead of obvious studio interiors. The lenses also feel more deliberate, producing soft circular bokeh instead of that flatter look from earlier films.\n\nBruce Lee also looks different here. He is still ripped but leaner, less bulky, almost more athletic than muscular, and his face looks slimmer which adds to the sharper screen presence.\n\nThe movie plays like a mix of spy film and Mortal Kombat style tournament structure. There is investigation, infiltration, secret island villain base, then rounds of martial arts fights layered into it.\n\nI honestly could not care much about most characters here. I sympathized more with Bruce Lee’s motivations in Fist of Fury, while this one feels more mission driven than emotionally driven.\n\nThe fights themselves are clearly improved. They are better lit, cleaner, and no longer rely on sped up motion that accidentally turns serious moments into comedy.\n\nThe final fight: Bruce Lee vs. Chinese Wolverine wanabe","poster":"img/enter-the-dragon-1973.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"La La Land","year":2016,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":403,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA visually intoxicating love story about ambition, timing, and the painful trade off between dreams and relationships. Damien Chazelle once again puts dreamers under pressure, exploring obsession and the emotional cost of chasing greatness. The collaboration with Linus Sandgren shines through the shifting colors and lighting that mirror every emotional beat. Emma Stone feels softer and more vulnerable here, which contrasts beautifully with Ryan Gosling’s steady belief in jazz as identity. For me, this is pure visual eye candy. The wardrobe, production design, and color grading flow effortlessly. Beneath the musical charm, the film explores ambition, identity, and the tension between love and personal dreams. The final act delivers an emotionally powerful conclusion that lingers long after the credits roll. It is romantic, visually beautiful, and deeply reflective.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI see how Damien Chazelle loves putting rising or up and coming characters under a spotlight. I notice how he plays with motivation, obsession, and the personal cost of chasing a dream. His use of color is top notch, and paired with Linus Sandgren’s visual instincts, the collaboration really works. The lighting and palette constantly shift depending on emotion. The choreography is nice too!\n\nI am just not used to seeing Emma Stone portray such a soft and girly character after watching her strong woman roles in her collaborations with Yorgos Lanthimos. Here she feels more vulnerable and grounded, which changes the dynamic of her screen presence. I also like Ryan Gosling’s explanation about his passion for jazz because it does not feel like exposition but a genuine belief system that defines his identity.\n\nFor me this is a visual popcorn film. The wardrobe, production design, and grading all come together naturally. Nothing feels out of place, everything feels emotionally appropriate to the moment and it is so aesthetically pleasing.\n\nAt its core the film is about two adults growing apart due to priorities, how dreams evolve, how love can remain even after separation, and the choice between passion and relationship. The last sequence feels like a dreamland fantasy of a perfect life, a montage of what could have been, filled with longing and regret. That ending is the nail in the coffin. Ang sakit. Well, that is life. Nothing ever goes as planned.","poster":"img/la-la-land-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hamnet","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-18","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":441,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA quiet, intimate grief story that I deeply relate to, even if it never fully devastates me the way I expected. I strongly connected with Paul Mescal’s longing and denial as a father who refuses to accept loss. The film’s long takes, great performances, and period framing create a reflective atmosphere about love, grief, and the fragility of small moments with children. I admire its craft and sincerity, especially Jessie Buckley’s stillness and pain, but emotionally I understood it more than I felt it. Beautiful, thoughtful, personal, just not shattering for me.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI resonate with Paul Mescal’s character here. I am clingy with my kids and he is happiest whenever they are together, so I really connect with his longing and refusal to accept the loss. That feeling of believing my twins must still be somewhere and wanting to find them never left me, and I felt that denial more than anything else in the film. This ended up being a redemption for me for Paul Mescal since I did not like him in Aftersun and Gladiator II. He did great here as William Shakespeare.\n\nThe film constantly reminds me to never take small moments with children for granted because what is given can be taken away. The long takes allow the emotions to breathe and sit with the audience, especially through Jessie Buckley’s performance. I could literally feel her pain in the stillness, not just in the crying but in the quiet existence after the loss. The play itself feels like an attempt so she will not be alone in grief and can slowly move toward acceptance. Indeed a romantic gesture from her husband. \n\nVisually, the period setting works very well. The costumes and sets feel authentic and accurate, and the framing often places characters inside doorways, windows, or narrow passages. For me this makes them look trapped by destiny, unable to escape the moment that defined their lives. The tone reminds me of Aftersun, Pieces of a Woman, Manchester by the Sea, and Train Dreams.\n\nBut even with all the strong intentions and craft, the emotion never fully broke through for me. I understood the sadness more than I felt it, so instead of devastation I was left with quiet admiration. It is a beautiful and thoughtful film, just not one that completely wounded me the way I hoped it would. I really wanted this to hurt more, as someone who also lost my twins after their birth.","poster":"img/hamnet-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Crime 101","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-18","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":373,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe tense opening, cold color palette, and character downtime make the story feel human instead of purely procedural. It focuses more on how the system reshapes people over time than on the crime itself, showing both cops and criminals slowly compromising their principles.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI really liked the opening heist, it was thrilling right away and carried by a high energy musical score that immediately pulled me in. The blue green gray color palette gives the film a cold and calculated mood, parang everything is operating in a morally neutral space, not heroic, not evil, just people doing what they need to survive.\n\nI noticed Chris Hemsworth and Halle Berry’s characters wearing loose watches similar to Jordan Belfort. For me that signals wealth that feels undeserved or at least not clean, like money came too fast and without grounding. It visually supports the idea that their lives are built on unstable foundations.\n\nThe film balances comedic beats with quiet downtime moments. Those pauses help humanize the characters instead of making them pure crime machines. Parts of it reminded me of Heat, The Departed, and Collateral because of the police motivations and thief moralities. The cops are not fully righteous and the criminals are not purely evil. The conflict feels less like good versus bad and more like different people protecting their own version of order.\n\nChris Hemsworth’s character goes through clear change. He starts against violence but in the end fired his gun, kills someone (to protect Mark), and later opens up to his love interest about his family.\n\nMark Ruffalo’s character also shifts. Before he wanted accuracy and truth in solving cases, but later he becomes okay closing a case even if it is not fully correct. He even steals and gives it to Halle Berry, and now drives a nice car which he previously avoided. It feels like the system slowly reshaped him into the kind of person he once opposed.\n\nI was honestly waiting for Chris Hemsworth to say “we know each other, he’s a friend from work.” Missed opportunity!","poster":"img/crime-101-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Full Metal Jacket","year":1987,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-17","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":320,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nStarts darkly funny then slowly turns into a cold, unsettling war experience. The training camp humor masks growing psychological damage before the film shifts into harsh ground level combat. Strong performances and practical battlefield visuals make the war feel heavy and real rather than heroic, focusing more on the soldiers’ mental state than action thrills.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI found the early encounters between Vincent D'Onofrio and the drill instructor hilarious, especially R. Lee Ermey whose delivery reminds me of Sean Penn’s Steven J. Lockjaw energy in One Battle After Another. That humor is sharply contrasted by the grim punishment and revenge from his platoon mates. After that moment his personality shifts completely and I can already see the Kingpin in him.\n\nAdam Baldwin’s character comes across as trigger happy and impulsive, yet also courageous and loyal to his teammates.\n\nThe score during the training sequences sounds properly military and disciplined, but it also darkens whenever the tone requires it, smoothly transitioning from structured cadence to something more ominous as the psychological pressure builds.\n\nSince this is set in Vietnam, I get some Apocalypse Now vibes, but the difference is clear. This film focuses on ground level combat and the experience of soldiers in the field, even incorporating a photojournalist perspective like in A24's Civil War, while Apocalypse Now is more of a journey driven mission about locating a missing person.\n\nThe sets, fire, smoke, and explosions feel massive and believable. The scale of the film is impressive and the production design and cinematography really sell the chaos of war.\n\nI think I am starting to appreciate older war films more because they had no choice but to rely on practical and special effects, which makes everything feel heavier and more tangible.","poster":"img/full-metal-jacket-1987.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Casino Royale","year":2006,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-17","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":330,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA grounded, physical Bond that leans on tension and clean action rather than gadget fantasy (thank God!). Fast paced opening and strong stunt work set a serious tone, while clear spy craft and confident camerawork keep the action easy to follow. The film balances elegant style with emotional stakes, ending less as a victory lap and more as a hard lesson about trust.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI needed this to wash the taste of Moonraker out of my head so I could recalibrate my expectations again with Bond films and actually enjoy the ride instead of questioning every decision the film makes.\n\nThe opening action sequence is heart pounding and insanely energetic. Non stop stunts and parkour danger immediately establish the tone. This version of Bond feels physical and grounded.\n\nThe swagger of Daniel Craig is very noticeable. The cinematography carries that yellow, gold, brown palette that I naturally gravitate toward. The spy craft is also clear and easy to follow which makes the tension land better.\n\nThe musical score keeps the classy and grand scale feeling throughout the film, constantly reminding me that I am watching a high stakes world of elegance and danger rather than just a simple action story. The music carries that aura of sophistication and tension, making every moment feel important and elevated.\n\nThe airport sequence is nail biting intense. Sustained action, clean geography, and excellent camerawork keep me locked in the whole time.\n\nI could really feel the torture scene. Tho, Mads Mikkelsen’s death felt abrupt so I immediately sensed he was not the ultimate villain. But honestly, I think the character could have been explored more.\n\nThe third act production design is impressive, especially the massive sinking structure. The action is great but the emotional fallout is the real highlight.\n\nLesson learned, trust no one.","poster":"img/casino-royale-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Wall","year":2017,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":136,"review":"The silence builds the tension and sustains it. What really doomed them was their impatience and hubris.\n\nMaganda yung decoding niya ng location ng shooter. It gave me Shallows vibes, trapped in one place and the only way to survive is to rely on the main character’s wits. Ang galing kung paano niya nahulaan kung nasaan, using the environment’s sound as a clue.\n\nMatalino din yung camper na kalaban. He studies his enemies, so when he uses the radio, mas alam niya kung ano ang sasabihin too fool the receiver on the other end. \n\nGanda ng character development ni Ize. He did not just face his greatest adversary, he also faced his inner demons.\n\nJust when I thought it was all said and done, then boom. Ganda ng twist. Kind of film where the villain wins.","poster":"img/the-wall-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Chef","year":2014,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-16","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":105,"review":"For a chef, Jon Favreau looks too soft, thoughtful, and tender. I can only see him as Happy and not as a chef.\n\nThis shit is funny. A boomer in social media is definitely a laughing stock, and on the other side, if a Gen Z uses it for real, businesses boom!\n\nThis movie also shows that when someone's too passionate about his work, he can become an absentee parent. But as the film develops, their relationship also improves.\n\nRDJ here is just playing another Tony Stark variant in a different universe. John Leguizamo’s character is nice, super supportive.\n\nFeel good and very positive ending.","poster":"img/chef-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Back to the Future","year":1985,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":382,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA charming time travel adventure carried by music, character growth, and genuine likability. The orchestral score gives everything a grand sense of importance even within a small town setting, while the performances keep it grounded and relatable. Marty’s energy makes the danger feel personal, and George’s development provides emotional payoff. Clear storytelling and humor make it easy to follow, which explains why it remains a classic and leaves strong anticipation for the sequel.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe score feels like a space opera. Alan Silvestri writes it in a huge heroic orchestral style, almost like the characters are part of a grand adventure across galaxies even if the story is just happening in a small town. Big brass fanfares announce moments of destiny, the strings push urgency during the time travel race, and the main theme gives the feeling that history itself is being rewritten in real time. During the climax, the rhythm of the music basically becomes the ticking clock of the lightning strike. Alan Silvestri is such a legend! \n\nThe prosthetics and makeup department also did a good job making the actors look older. When I got to the mother’s house, that is where the comedy really started. Especially the awkward interactions with his mom. Napayuck ako nung hinalikan siya ng mom niya in the past.\n\nMichael J. Fox has a charisma similar to Tom Holland. They both have that energetic sincerity, mabilis magsalita, expressive reactions, and a believable “normal kid thrown into crazy situations” vibe. Instead of feeling like action heroes, they feel approachable and human, which makes the danger feel more real because I worry for them rather than just watching them win.\n\nStory wise, it is easy to follow and the stakes are clearly communicated. The director handled the characters well. Everyone has enough screen time, clear motivations, and character development especially young George McFly. Now I know why it is a classic. I thought it would be boring but I respect it so much.\n\nThe ending twist really gives me something to look forward to for the next film. I am excited to watch more from the franchise.","poster":"img/back-to-the-future-1985.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Moonraker","year":1979,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":298,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFun action ideas and ambitious production design, but the tension never fully lands. The boat chases and miniature work stand out, especially once the story leans into sci-fi territory, yet constant product placement and convenient gadget solutions weaken the stakes. Performances and tone make the danger feel distant, leaving the climax visually busy but emotionally flat.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe opening reminds me of Mission Impossible Fallout with Tom Cruise and Henry Cavill doing a daring skydiving sequence.\n\nSome shots look real and shot on location while others look fake like they were filmed in a studio, but that is forgivable given the era. The two high speed boat chase scenes are the highlight for me.\n\nAng daming product placements. It feels like a giant commercial rather than a cinematic experience.\n\nI do not know how the studio executives got convinced to green light this. The sets and miniatures in the third act are impressive when the film shifts into sci-fi territory. The production design starts looking like Star Wars. \n\nBut the climax left me straight faced the whole time. The danger is not felt because it is always convenient that his gadgets or tools solve every sticky situation. The acting did not help either. Richard Kiel looks so creepy. Monstrous but not worthy of screen time for me. His acting feels cringey instead of intimidating. But kudos to his role's character development. The zero gravity acting looks stupid, the main characters stay so calm even if they could die in space, especially Lois Chiles who feels bland. Even after destroying the last globe, they did not feel triumphant either.","poster":"img/moonraker-1979.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Last Action Hero","year":1993,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":134,"review":"I feel like I was the kid, always wanting to watch a movie in the theater and always predicting what’s going to happen.\n\nThe explosions look great when they are practical. They feel like a real old action movie. It only looks less convincing whenever it switches to VFX.\n\nThis gives me Home Alone vibes. Action through the eyes of a kid. The dialogues also feel like it is dissing at how stupid and predictable action movies can be.\n\nI can clearly feel John McTiernan’s DNA through the color palette, camera movements, how the stakes are communicated, and the villain characterization.\n\nThe third act is intriguing when the movie characters and the real life actor collide.\n\nThe film’s message is clear. My favorite heroes will always be there in films whenever I need them.","poster":"img/last-action-hero-1993.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Drive","year":2011,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":66,"review":"I watched this after reading a trivia that this is the last film capturing the yellow LA streetlights before they were replaced by LED lights that emit white light.\n\nThis feels like Baby Driver with the treatment of Taxi Driver and Collateral visually. The cinematography and color grading are beautiful. \n\nIt is a beautifully shot revenge film, but for me the story is just so so.","poster":"img/drive-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The French Dispatch of the Liberty, Kansas Evening Sun","year":2021,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-15","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":120,"review":"I like this better among all of Wes’ works. The story flows better so I understand it more clearly. It really feels like reading a magazine, with each section acting as its own article. Great cast too in every section. My favorite was the segment with Benicio and Léa.\n\nThe black and white works for me. It naturally integrates with the story, and it is beautifully lit, similar to why 12 Angry Men also worked for me. I think Wes’ framing and camera movement fit black and white better than pastel and bright colors.\n\nThe color grading here is also softer, so it is easier on my eyes. I also liked how Wes used different aspect ratios in certain scenes.","poster":"img/the-french-dispatch-of-the-liberty-kansas-evening-sun-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mamma Mia! Here We Go Again","year":2018,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":201,"review":"I watched this during Valentine’s while I had a tremendous headache, so I just wanted something light and easygoing.\n\nThe rocky relationship at the start felt real. Mahirap talaga pag dalawa ang nagdedesisyon sa relasyon. I could feel that tension immediately.\n\nI love how even though the lighting is high key and bright, the DOP still made sure to include a noticeable hair light or highlight. It keeps the characters separated from the background and gives them that polished, glowing look. It feels theatrical but still cinematic.\n\nLily James is so enigmatic in every movie or role she plays. There’s something magnetic about her presence. She carries the younger Donna storyline with so much charm and emotional pull.\n\nI also like how the story is woven through two timelines with parallel struggles and growth. It reminds me of how The Godfather Part II structured its narrative, showing two generations side by side. Here, it feels lighter and more sentimental, but the structural similarity is there.\n\nOverall, the movie is feel good, with a happy and satisfying ending. I think this is actually best watched during Mother’s Day. It celebrates motherhood, legacy, and love in a warm way. Perfect comfort watch energy.","poster":"img/mamma-mia-here-we-go-again-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Lobster","year":2015,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-13","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":423,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a dark, absurd comedy that uses exaggerated rules about relationships to poke fun at social pressure and conformity. It’s funny, uncomfortable, and intentionally weird, turning the idea of love and companionship into something mechanical and unsettling.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film feels like a dark comedy right from the start, with absurd lines of questioning delivered in a serious tone, filled with awkward pauses. \n\nI see this as social commentary on being single and how people will go to extreme lengths, even hurting themselves, just to be accepted and find companionship or a perfect match based on similarities. It exaggerates the pressure to pair up in such a bizarre way that it becomes both hilarious and uncomfortable. Lalo na yung pinipilit ni \"The Limping Man\" na magnosebleed sya. \n\nI also read the hotel system as a twisted presentation of the supposed benefits of having a partner. The staff act like guides trying to convince everyone that life is automatically better when you’re not alone. It even touches briefly on ideas like non masturbation and premarital sex. I don’t know if that’s just my interpretation or if the director intentionally wanted to plant that thought in the audience. As someone who leans a bit conservative, I don’t see it as mind conditioning, but I can imagine more liberated viewers finding those elements cringey.\n\nWhat makes it funnier is how nonchalant and emotionally flat the characters are, even during sex. Their lack of reaction amplifies the absurdity. I laughed so hard at the scene where David kicked a child just so she could have a limp like his friend.\n\nIf the hotel scenes already feel weird, the story shifting into the forest pushes the absurdity even further. I sensed early on that the tables would turn when they started hunting loners. Eventually, the hunter becomes the hunted.\n\nI also interpret the Loners as a kind of commentary on resistance to social norms. They feel like rebels pushing back against the ideology of marriage and traditional family structure. I’m not sure if activism is the perfect word, but their anti relationship rules mirror the hotel’s strict pro relationship stance. \n\nAnd then there’s the literal punchline of the film. Love is blind, so they blinded themselves. It’s such a dark, silly joke taken to its most extreme conclusion, and it perfectly captures the film’s tone.","poster":"img/the-lobster-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lady Bird","year":2017,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-12","tags":[],"words":269,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a warm, funny coming of age story about growing up inside rigid structures and slowly learning to appreciate where I came from. The humor and awkward moments feel familiar, and the emotional growth hits in a way that feels honest and human.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI can relate to her behavior in Catholic school, especially the way she refuses to participate and doesn’t fully believe in the traditions. It reminds me of my own high school days, even eating the communion bread with my Knights of the Altar friends back then. \n\nI laughed really hard during the homecoming dance scene when the sister said, “6 inches apart for the Holy Spirit.” Hahaha. Ganun din kahigpit during our celestial party, di pwede magkadikit. That moment perfectly captures how awkward and funny those enforced rules can be when you’re young and just trying to have fun.\n\nAnother genuine surprise that made me laugh was when she caught her boyfriend kissing another boy in the men’s CR. The shock and timing of that reveal feel very real, like one of those sudden moments where your perception of someone completely flips.\n\nI see the film as a coming of age story about growth. Watching her shift from being an ungrateful, frustrated teenager into someone more self aware and appreciative of her life and family feels natural. It’s messy, emotional, and very human, which is why her development lands well for me.","poster":"img/lady-bird-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Insomnia","year":2002,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":339,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a tense psychological detective film where Nolan uses pacing, color, and performance to make me physically feel the character’s unraveling state. The atmosphere, fatigue, and creeping anxiety become the real engine of the story, pulling me into a sleepless spiral that feels immersive beyond just plot.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI love how Nolan directs detective style films. This feels thrilling and atmospheric right from the start. I get the same energy I felt in Batman Begins, especially in the pacing and color palette, which remind me a lot of that film’s first act. \n\nAl Pacino really looks sleep deprived here. Even I can feel that heaviness in the eyes while watching. That level of craftsmanship, where Nolan transfers the character’s physical and mental exhaustion to the audience, is top notch filmmaking. All the elements of cinema language feel carefully aligned to support that sensation of fatigue and tension.\n\nI also get a Following vibe when he goes into Brody’s apartment and leaves fingerprints behind. That moment adds another layer of anxiety because he could easily be framed. His judgment starts slipping as the sleep deprivation worsens, and I can feel how sloppy he becomes. It is a smart way to externalize his mental decline through his actions.\n\nI keep thinking about why that freaking lodge doesn’t have blackout curtains if the management knows the sun never goes down in that town. It feels wildly inconsiderate, and it adds to my frustration in a way that mirrors what the character is going through. That constant daylight becomes another antagonist, pushing him further into exhaustion.\n\nOverall, I experience Insomnia as a tense psychological detective film where Nolan uses pacing, color, and performance to make me physically feel the character’s unraveling state. It is immersive in a way that goes beyond plot and pulls me directly into his sleepless spiral.","poster":"img/insomnia-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Wuthering Heights","year":2026,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":357,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\nI experience this as a tragic love story where the music and visuals pull me into a romance that feels beautiful, obsessive, and doomed. The emotional weight sits front and center, with atmosphere and imagery amplifying longing, jealousy, and heartbreak without overshadowing the human core.\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe score immediately pulls me in. It feels thrilling, dramatic, hopeful, sorrowful, and romantic. It constantly shifts with the emotional state of the characters, making the love story feel grand but also doomed in some cases. I can feel the weight of longing and heartbreak through the music alone.\n\nThe gothic atmosphere is present, but it feels more like texture. The mood is grounded, allowing the emotions to stay front and center. The cinematographer is clearly flexing here. Whether it is intimate close ups, wide landscape shots, or horizon framed compositions, every image feels with purpose. The visual language makes the romance feel intense and personal.\n\nThe intimacy between the characters is what drives the film. It feels erotic but fragile. Margot Robbie looks stunning and naturally regal, fitting perfectly into this royalty setting.\n\nStory wise, I read it as a tragic mix of rags to riches ambition (The Favourite 2018) and revenge, similar in spirit to The Count of Monte Cristo (2002), blended with the aching “too late” romance energy I associate with Past Lives (2023). The emotional tension is heightened by the production design & lighting. I notice how the walls shift toward red tones during moments of rage, jealousy, and passion, visually amplifying the characters’ inner turmoil.\n\nThe ending reminds me of lovers bound so tightly that one cannot exist without the other. When one fades, the other follows. \n\nOverall, I experience Wuthering Heights as an expertly crafted tragic love story. The music, cinematography, acting, and design work together to immerse me in a romance that is beautiful, obsessive, and destined to fall apart. It is intimate, emotional, and visually expressive without losing sight of the human core.","poster":"img/wuthering-heights-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Julie & Julia","year":2009,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":262,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a relatable story about creative persistence and passion, where the emotional core connects with me more than the performances. Even when certain character choices distract me, the message about commitment, growth, and loving the process still lands.\n\nFull review: \n\nI honestly get annoyed by Meryl Streep’s voice here. It sounds high pitched, almost Mickey Mouse like, and it feels nasal, like someone talking while congested nose. Everything seems to come from the throat instead of natural breathing. The hairstyle does not help either. It adds to the exaggerated presentation, which makes her performance feel distracting to me even though I understand it is a character choice.\n\nOn the other side, I resonate strongly with Julie’s journey. Watching her write a blog and fall in love with the process hits close to home. It reminds me of how I feel when I watch films and post my reviews online. That sense of documenting a diary, sharing thoughts, and finding identity through creative expression feels very personal.\n\nThe film speaks about persistence. It shows how messy progress can be and how plans rarely unfold perfectly. Still, the message is clear to me. Even when things feel frustrating or slow, the important part is to keep going. The story frames passion as something that grows through repetition, mistakes, and stubborn dedication.\n\nOverall, I experience Julie & Julia as a simple but relatable reminder about commitment to what I love. Despite my distraction with the vocal performance, the emotional thread about passion, discipline, and not quitting still lands.","poster":"img/julie-julia-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Elvis","year":2022,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":565,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis feels like a high energy biopic that translates fame into movement, rhythm, and emotion. I’m pulled in by how the film visualizes Elvis’ rise, not just as success, but as a push and pull between passion and control. The performances and stylistic choices keep everything alive, turning history into something visceral and alive. For me, it plays less like a standard biography and more like a reflection on identity, influence, and the price of being turned into an icon.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI actually watched about half of this before and only continued now, but honestly I would like to watch it again from the start because the energy hits immediately.\n\nThe opening is explosive, especially the church scene. It reminds me of Sinners. Nakakaindak talaga. The first performance is incredible in how it translates fanaticism to the screen. The women losing their minds almost feel like they are having an orgasm. The camera movement, editing, and sound design all work together to show how overwhelming Elvis’ presence was. And it is wild to realize this is Feyd Rautha from Dune Part Two. What range! \n\nI like the visual effects used to show the map of his tour locations. It turns a simple logistical montage into something dynamic and playful, keeping the pacing alive while emphasizing how massive his rise was.\n\nEven though this is a biopic, the lighting and color grading are stylish and expressive. The director constantly translates Elvis’ emotional state into visual metaphors. When Elvis feels lost, the hall of mirrors visually represents confusion and identity fragmentation, while Colonel Parker guiding him through symbolizes control. The ferris wheel scene is even stronger. As Colonel pitches exclusive representation, they are suspended high above everything. Elvis looks unstable and afraid, which mirrors his fear of entering new territory, while the Colonel is calm and confident, clearly holding power. When Elvis agrees and says he is ready to fly, the ferris wheel rises even higher, visually sealing the deal. I love how the film uses space and movement to communicate power dynamics.\n\nThe portrayal of racial discrimination is striking. Seeing the segregation between white and Black communities adds important context to Elvis’ influences. The film shows how even performance styles were policed, with fear around associating white artists with Black culture. It is uncomfortable but necessary history, and it deepens my understanding of the era Elvis was navigating.\n\nThe prosthetics and makeup work are impressive. Elvis’ transformation, especially later in life feels convincing. Even his weight gain looks natural.\n\nStorytelling wise, I personally find this more engaging than Bohemian Rhapsody. The editing during the concert tour montages keeps the momentum high while reinforcing the intoxicating nature of fame. The film constantly reminds me that success can feel like a cage. Stars are treated less like human beings and more like products, and Elvis’ struggle becomes less about talent and more about ownership and control.\n\nBy the end, I feel glad I came back to finish this. It is energetic, emotional, and surprisingly intimate beneath all the blinding lights. For me, it becomes a warm story about passion, identity, and the cost of fame. I literally went straight to Spotify to listen to Elvis Presley after watching.","poster":"img/elvis-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Promising Young Woman","year":2020,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":391,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI experience this as a revenge film driven more by psychology and social commentary rather than physical violence. Emerald Fennell directs with a sharp sense of control, letting tone, music, and performance carry Cassie’s inner rage. It feels unsettling, calculated, and personal, using revenge as a lens to examine accountability and the culture that protects harm.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI watched this just to observe how Emerald Fennell direct a movie since I'll be watching Wuthering Heights tomorrow.\n\nCassie is living a double life. By day she looks ordinary, but at night she becomes a vigilante targeting men who prey on women. I feel like she is savoring every moment of those confrontations. Every interaction feels calculated, controlled, and deeply personal.\n\nThe score is great and feels like a direct reflection of what is going on inside her head. It swings between triumphant, revolted, and vengeful tones, almost narrating her emotional state. The music doesn’t just sit in the background. It pushes the tension and makes her actions feel both empowering and unsettling.\n\nI get I Spit on Your Grave vibes here, but this film approaches revenge in a more psychological and social way. It is less about physical brutality and more about exposing a culture that protects abusers and silences victims.\n\nHer death feels like a mirror to Nina’s fate. Both are casualties of a system where men dismiss their actions, erase evidence, and frame everything as an accident. It reinforces how easily truth gets buried when people choose to forget their past actions over accountability.\n\nThe scene involving Ryan and the detective feels like a commentary on a world largely shaped by men. I read it as a reflection of how quickly blame gets shifted onto victims instead of confronting the real harm done. That moment made the film’s anger feel grounded in something familiar.\n\nThe planned delivery of evidence is a smart narrative move and a strong way to close the story. Even after Cassie's death, she maintains control. Her plan ensures that what happened cannot be ignored or rewritten, giving the film a sense of justice.\n\nOverall, I experience this film as a revenge story layered with social critique.","poster":"img/promising-young-woman-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Scarface","year":1983,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":467,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis feels like a raw, flashy rise and fall crime story carried by Al Pacino’s intense presence and a neon soaked Miami atmosphere. I’m pulled in by the unapologetic violence, stylish lighting, and seductive criminal energy that makes the world feel glamorous and dangerous at the same time. Beneath the spectacle, I see a character driven by ambition and ego, chasing power at any cost. The film works for me as both a thrilling crime fantasy and a cautionary tale about how success without discipline slowly isolates and destroys.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIbang Al Pacino ang nakikita ko dito. He looks rugged, poor, and middle aged. Mas raw at unrefined yung angst niya. Hindi na siya masyadong bata, hindi rin matanda. I also notice iba yung accent niya dito, more street swagger kesa slick and classy.\n\nThis film is not shy about violence at all. Talagang in your face.\n\nThe colors scream Miami Vice City energy because of the Miami setting. Yung neon pink and electric blue lighting give that flashy, seductive criminal underworld vibe. It feels glamorous but dangerous, like a city that never sleeps and is always one bad decision away from chaos. The sunset skyline shots also add this dreamy contrast, with that pink and orange horizon. If I want a crime thriller set in Miami, I would go with Scarface and not Miami Vice.\n\nNatawa ako sa paraan kung paano siya nireject ni Elvira. Michelle Pfeiffer looks so young and gorgeous here. The last time I saw her was as Janet in Ant Man, so seeing her here feels like time travel.\n\nI actually like Tony’s twisted mindset. Get the money, then get the power, then get the woman. Huge ambition.\n\nGrabe yung execution kay Omar. Parang ngayon lang ako nakakita ng ganun sa film. Savage talaga!\n\nAng galing ni Tony Montana makipag negotiate. Wala siyang takot kahit harap harapan na yung threat. Pure intimidation and confidence.\n\nThe atmospheric score adds a lot to the thrill and action. It feels tense, stylish, and dangerous. That comeback with Mel? First class ticket to resurrection. Nicely done. \n\nBeing on top is brutal. Tony becomes paranoid and lonely. He gets everything he ever wanted, but he is lonelier than ever. That’s what happens when he ignore the lessons from his precedessor. Lesson number one, don’t underestimate the other guy’s greed. Lesson number two, don’t get high on your own supply.\n\nDespite everything, he still has a moral line. He refuses to kill a woman and children. I like that conclusion when the film explodes into chaos with gunfights and destruction. A loud, violent fall from the top. Literally!","poster":"img/scarface-1983.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Godfather Part III","year":1990,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":508,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis feels like a story about legacy, aging, and the heavy cost of power. I’m drawn to how the characters carry history on their shoulders, especially Michael trying to clean up a life that refuses to stay buried. The performances give the film an emotional weight that’s less about crime and more about inheritance, guilt, and family. For me, it plays like a reflection on what happens when ambition outlives the man who built it, and how maturity doesn’t erase consequence.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first thing that came to my mind was what the F is that hair? And why do I get so attracted to Connie as she gets older? There’s something about her presence here that feels more caring and confident, and I found myself oddly drawn to that maturity.\n\nI laughed when Michael mentioned having a temper like his father while referring to Sonny and Vincent. And it’s true, Andy Garcia really does channel James Caan. I see the same aggressiveness, the same explosive energy. Vincent feels like a spiritual continuation of Sonny, reckless but driven, which adds tension to every scene he’s in.\n\nAnd who can forget Joe Mantegna’s face after terrorizing the screen in Baby’s Day Out? That childhood villain image still sticks with me. Seeing him here gives that same uneasy vibe, and Vincent’s revenge against him felt carefully planned and satisfying. Deserve!\n\nThis installment leans more toward politics and legacy, focusing on the passing of the baton to the newer generation. I feel the film is less about street level crime, violence and more about power, image, and the burden of inheritance. Michael is clearly trying to legitimize everything, but the past keeps pulling him back.\n\n“Never hate your enemies, it will cloud your judgment.” That line stayed with me. It perfectly summarizes Michael’s philosophy at this stage, a wiser man compared from Part II. He’s colder, more reflective, and trying to operate above emotion, even when the stakes are deeply personal.\n\nThe timing of Michael’s confession followed by the Pope’s death hit me symbolically. Both figures act as fathers in their own realms, and I read it as the death of Michael’s old identity as Godfather too. He’s exhausted, spiritually broken, and clearly done with the cycle of violence, even if the world around him won’t let him walk away clean.\n\nThe sequence after the opera is insanely well directed and acted. I genuinely felt Michael’s pain when he lost his daughter. That silent scream is devastating. When Connie put on the black veil, I immediately thought about how the opera itself foreshadowed tragedy. The staging, the intercut violence, and the emotional payoff all came together in a way that felt grand and cruel at the same time.\n\nOverall, it’s about a man trying to outrun the sins of his empire and realizing too late that power always demands a price.","poster":"img/the-godfather-part-iii-1990.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Terminator","year":1984,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-08","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":510,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI love how the film establishes the Terminator as a pure force of intent from the moment he arrives. Arnold’s mechanical presence, paired with that pulsing score, creates constant dread that never really lets me relax. The gritty future war aesthetic and practical stunt work make everything feel tangible, especially the chase sequences. Some effects show their age, but the atmosphere and tension still carry the experience. Overall, it’s raw, suspenseful sci-fi that works because I can feel the weight and danger in almost every frame.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThat’s how you make an entrance! The Terminator arrives with pure intent and zero hesitation, and that iconic exchange, “You can’t do that.” “Wrong.” immediately defines his character. He does not give a shit, he does not react emotionally, he simply executes the mission. That single moment tells you everything about the threat level. Arnold’s stoic, mechanical presence makes the hunt for Sarah Connor genuinely tense. He feels inevitable, like a force rather than a person.\n\nThe score plays a huge role in building that tension. The pulsing techno theme acts like a ticking clock, constantly reminding me that danger is approaching. Other musical cues layer in dread and urgency, creating a relentless atmosphere. \n\nThe film’s future war aesthetic reinforces this mood. The world is dark, dirty, and depressing. The practical sets, debris, smoke, and metallic textures give it a raw physicality that feels tangible. It’s the kind of gritty sci-fi environment that sells the illusion of an apocalypse, similar to X-Men: Days of Future Past. I can really feel the texture of the world. Earlier sequence from a flashback reminds me also when Chani and Paul destroys the spice harvester in Dune: Part Two. \n\nVisually, the film balances grounded action with stylized spectacle. The Sarah Connor chase sequences are tight and suspenseful, emphasizing pursuit. The tunnel chase is a standout. Smoke filled lighting, glowing colors, and the Terminator emerging from chaos create pure cinematic adrenaline. The explosion of the truck adds to that realism. I feel the impact because the stunt work is believable.\n\nIn contrast, prosthetics are mostly unimpressive. The eye removal scene has a rubbery quality that shows its age, but it is easy to forgive given the ambition. \n\nThe score cleverly fooled me. Just when the score suggests everything is done, it shifts back into a threatening rhythm, revealing that the nightmare is far from over. The burned flesh peeling away to expose the machine underneath is a memorable frame. It transforms the villain from a damaged pursuer into an unstoppable horror icon. When the skeletal Terminator is shown through practical puppetry, it is genuinely creepy. The stop motion shots are less convincing and funny today.\n\nOverall, The Terminator succeeds because of its gritty production design, the physical craftsmanship and tension driven storytelling still hit hard. That tunnel sequence alone is enough to elevate the experience into something memorable.","poster":"img/the-terminator-1984.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Devil Wears Prada","year":2006,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-08","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":503,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI’m immediately pulled in by the star power, but it’s Meryl Streep who anchors everything. Her quiet authority makes the world of fashion feel both glamorous and suffocating. I read the film less as a fashion story and more as a reflection on ambition, pressure, and how growth reshapes identity and relationships. It doesn’t romanticize success. It shows the emotional cost behind the sparkle. The ending lands softly but meaningfully, leaving me thinking about what kind of success is actually worth chasing.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWhat immediately stood out to me is how star studded this movie feels. Ang daming high regarded and attractive women on screen that the star power alone already carries presence. But what really pulls me in is Meryl Streep. I guess I am really drawn to older women in power. Her Miranda is intimidating without raising her voice. Every look feels like a command. I feel her authority in the room even before she speaks.\n\nAt its core, this is a world built on superficial appearances. Fashion, status, image. Everything is curated. Yet behind that polished exterior is pressure, sacrifice, and constant performance. The film does a great job showing how glamour is not just rainbow and sunshine. It is work, politics, and emotional cost.\n\nOne of the most impactful moments for me is Stanley Tucci’s dialogue with Andy. When he tells her he does not pity her because she is not trying, she is whining. That hit close to me. I remember being in a similar pressure environment where expectations were brutal. I had a boss who constantly pushed us past what we thought was possible. That experience shaped me. So when Nigel talks about growth through discomfort, I understand exactly where he is coming from.\n\nThe film is really about growth and how success changes priorities, identity, and relationships. As Andy climbs higher, I see how the people around her react. Some feel left behind. Some feel betrayed. It mirrors real life. Ambition often demands tradeoffs, and sometimes the people closest to us are the ones who feel the shift the most.\n\nWhat I appreciate is that the movie does not romanticize success. Glamorous life is not pure spotlight. It can strain friendships, relationships, and personal values. There are moments where advancement requires stepping on uncomfortable territory or choosing career over loyalty. That tension is where the film feels honest. \n\nThe ending lands in a way that reminds me of Whiplash. There is no big emotional speech, just a quiet acknowledgment. Miranda recognizing Andy’s capability feels like a mentor silently approving a student who proved herself. \n\nOverall, I see The Devil Wears Prada as a film about ambition, identity, and the cost of growth. Beneath the fashion and wit is a very real story about pressure, transformation, and deciding what kind of success I am willing to pursue.","poster":"img/the-devil-wears-prada-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Witch","year":2015,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-08","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","a24"],"words":357,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI’m locked in immediately by the voice and score, and the film never really lets go of that dread. The natural lighting and muted palette make everything feel raw and exposed, like I’m watching a world slowly rot under fear. The performances sell the paranoia, and the period detail adds weight to every interaction. For me, this plays less like a traditional horror film and more like a slow psychological unraveling where atmosphere and faith driven tension do all the heavy lifting.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film opens with Ralph Ineson’s thunderous voice layered with a rising creepy score and haunting female vocals, and I immediately feel the tone lock in.\n\nI read that most of the film was shot using natural available light, and I can really see that choice on screen. The visuals have this raw, grounded texture that reminds me of The Revenant. The dim candlelight, gray skies, and muted palette make everything feel cold and exposed, which adds to the unease. \n\nThe old English dialogue, costume design, production sets, and cinematography all work together seamlessly. Every detail reinforces the time period and isolates the family from the world. The authenticity of the language makes conversations feel heavier and more ritualistic, like every word carries spiritual weight and fear.\n\nHarvey Scrimshaw’s performance genuinely unsettles me. I feel his terror and bliss, especially in the possession scenes where faith, fear, and confusion altogether. Ralph Ineson and Kate Dickie anchor the emotional tension with performances that feel real and desperate. Their parental authority slowly crumbles as paranoia takes over.\n\nBy the end, Satan divides them, isolates them, and lets suspicion rot their bond until nothing is left. When Thomasin is alone, grieving, and stripped of everything, the offer feels less like temptation and more like escape. \n\nOverall, I experience The Witch as a slow psychological regression disguised as historical horror. It is less about jump scares and more about atmosphere, faith, and the fragility of family under fear.","poster":"img/the-witch-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Oblivion","year":2013,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-08","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":640,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nOn rewatch, this clicks for me in a way it didn’t the first time. The opening mission briefing, visuals, and score immediately pull me in, and I start appreciating how tangible and location driven the world feels. The blend of sleek sci fi design and grounded cinematography makes the film feel like a showcase of Joseph Kosinski’s visual control. What once felt slow now reads as deliberate, and the unfolding mystery keeps me engaged. It plays like a polished sci-fi spectacle where craft and atmosphere carry as much weight as the story. Give Joseph Kosinski a Star Wars film!!! \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis is my second time watching this. The first time I saw it, I got bored. But this time, the opening minutes with Tom Cruise explaining his mission hooked me immediately. I think before I was expecting a different kind of film or I simply did not understand Joseph Kosinski’s cinema language yet. Watching this on HBO MAX felt insane. The visuals and score instantly pulled me in. There are many shots that feel like they were captured on real locations because everything looks tangible and real! The terrain photography alone already shows an early glimpse of Kosinski’s capability, like a prototype of what he later perfected in Top Gun Maverick.\n\nThe film has excellent CGI when it needs it, especially with the ship, drones, and background extensions. But what impressed me is how some drone shots look physically present, almost like practical builds were used. That blend gives a realistic touch to the world. The futuristic aesthetic reminds me of Tom Cruise’s work in Minority Report, but this feels cleaner, more refined, and visually disciplined. Even the explosions feel satisfying. I honestly get the sense that Kosinski studied Michael Bay haha!\n\nThe scale and visual ambition make this feel like a director’s portfolio piece. I can easily imagine this as Kosinski’s pitch reel and director's treatment for a Star Wars film. The design language, barren landscapes, and sleek tech give me Rogue One and sequel trilogy vibes. It has that polished galaxy far far away identity that feels cinematic.\n\nStory wise, the reveal structure is what surprised me the most on this rewatch. Morgan Freeman’s appearance lands with strong presence, and seeing Nikolaj Coster Waldau adds another layer of intrigue. The second act entrance of the larger drone inside the apartment is genuinely menacing. The way the curtain drapes around it builds tension in such a simple visual way. I feel real fear in that moment. It communicates threat for real!\n\nThen the clone twist hits, and I genuinely react with shock. I do not remember that reveal from my first viewing, so it feels fresh and destabilizing. Suddenly the mission, identity, and purpose of the character shift. It reframes everything we know about the film. \n\nThe third act explodes with momentum. It becomes nonstop energy, electric and destructive without feeling messy. The reason it works is the seamless integration of practical effects and digital work. Nothing feels artificial. The action carries physical impact, so even the explosions feel immersive.\n\nI guessed correctly that the authority controlling them was artificial intelligence rather than a real government or any space governing body, but that prediction does not weaken the experience. Instead, it reinforces the film’s theme about control, illusion, and manufactured purpose.\n\nOverall, Oblivion feels like a turning point piece in Kosinski’s career. On rewatch, I appreciate how controlled the visuals are, how deliberate the pacing is (though could be shorter in my opinion), and how the story unfolds through reveals. What bored me before now feels epic. This time, I see the craftsmanship clearly, and I am fully engaged.","poster":"img/oblivion-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Manchester by the Sea","year":2016,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-07","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":375,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nMy main barrier here is the lead performance. I personally struggle to connect with Casey Affleck’s understated style, which makes it hard for me to emotionally enter the film even when the craft is clearly strong. The score and technical elements do a lot of the emotional work, creating weight and atmosphere, but without that performance connection, the experience feels distant. I understand the film’s intent about grief and numbness, yet it never fully bridges into catharsis for me.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nMy biggest barrier with this film is Casey Affleck. I really get disconnected whenever I watch him. His acting feels dry to me, like he’s just playing himself in every role. I felt the same distance in Interstellar, A Ghost Story, and Oppenheimer. Because of that, I struggle to emotionally enter the film no matter how strong its intentions are.\n\nHis delivery and facial expressions come off lifeless to my eyes, like he just wants to say his lines and go home. The most glaring example is the during the fire. His children die, and he stands there frozen and numb among the crowd. I understand the intent is shock and dissociation, but it doesn’t translate emotionally for me. Instead of devastation, I feel detachment. I experience a similar disconnect with Paul Mescal in Gladiator II and Aftersun. \n\nTechnically, the film is doing a lot of heavy lifting. The musical score is especially effective. It carries emotional weight during key moments like the fire sequence, the police station confession, and the failed suicide attempt. The music fills the emotional space that I’m not getting from the performance, amplifying the tragedy and guilt that define Lee’s character. Without the score, those scenes would hit much flatter for me.\n\nBecause I never emotionally connect with Lee, the film ends without a cathartic release for me. I understand what it’s trying to say about grief being unresolved and lifelong, but the emotional bridge never fully forms. A missed opportunity for someone like me who experienced it too. I finish the film aware of its direction, yet emotionally untouched.","poster":"img/manchester-by-the-sea-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jackie Brown","year":1997,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":523,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nI immediately felt Tarantino’s signature rhythm in how tension is built through conversations, framing, and quiet maneuvering instead of nonstop action. The split screen editing and power driven compositions kept me locked in, while the steady funk score gives the whole crime story a cool, controlled pulse. The performances bring a lot of personality, and the layered deception unfolds cleanly enough for me to enjoy piecing it together. I did miss a big explosive finale, less fireworks, more precision. A crime film that thrives on timing, personality, and quiet intelligence rather than spectacle.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis is unmistakably Quentin Tarantino. Talk heavy scenes, long takes, and bursts of violence define the rhythm. Instead of chaos driving the film, the tension is built through conversations, glances, and quiet maneuvering.\n\nA great example is the split screen moment when Jackie stole the gun from Max. The timing is excellent. The editing builds tension by letting us see multiple perspectives at once, so we understand the danger before the characters fully act on it. It becomes less about surprise and more about anticipation. Tarantino uses framing throughout the film to highlight power dynamics. Who stands, who sits, who dominates the frame, and who looks cornered all subtly communicate shifting control without needing explicit dialogue.\n\nThe performances add a lot of personality. Seeing Robert De Niro play a stoner is a first for me. His sluggish presence contrasts nicely with the sharper, more calculating characters around him. It’s also a surprise to see Bridget Fonda here, a nice coincidence seeing her after watching Henry Fonda in 12 Angry Men. Another coincidence i s Michael Keaton playing another \"Ray\" character just like in The Founder (Ray Kroc). \n\nThe musical approach is one of the film’s strongest identity markers. The composer choices lean into repetition and groove driven tracks that function almost like a rhythmic heartbeat for the heist. The music rides a steady funk pulse. This gives the film a laid back but suspenseful tone, like a cool headed crime unfolding in slow motion. The repetition reinforces mood which makes the triple crossing scheme feel playful and clever.\n\nNarratively, Jackie’s layered deception is the core engine. The triple cross is executed cleanly, and the film carefully lets us piece together her strategy alongside the other players. Everyone thinks they’re ahead until Jackie proves she’s the smartest one in the room. That clarity of plotting is something I appreciate more here than in Pulp Fiction. The storytelling feels direct, focused, and character driven.\n\nMy only hesitation is the lack of an explosive Tarantino style climax. The film deliberately avoids a massive violent payoff. I understand the intention, but part of me still expects that signature burst of chaos. Even so, I rate this higher than Pulp Fiction because the narrative is easier to follow and more cohesive. It feels like Tarantino refining his craft, especially in writing a strong female lead, something that clearly paved the way for Kill Bill.","poster":"img/jackie-brown-1997.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Oldboy","year":2003,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-06","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":546,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nKnowing the twist going in changes the experience completely. Instead of shock, the film becomes about tone, style, and performance. This version leans heavily into stylization, mood, and surreal pacing, making it feel more like psychological horror than a straight revenge thriller. Even without the surprise factor, it’s interesting to see how the same premise can be interpreted through a more artistic lens, creating a tense, uneasy watch driven by atmosphere rather than plot mechanics.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI watched this already knowing the big twist because I saw the American remake with Josh Brolin and Elizabeth Olsen first, and that version seriously mind fucked me. So coming into this, I wasn’t exactly excited. I was hoping to get something new out of the experience rather than just reliving the shock.\n\nRight away, the biggest difference is tone and style. This version feels far more artistic in color, camera movement, and score. Whereas the remake plays more like a straightforward revenge thriller with a plot twist at the center. The Korean film leans into mood and stylization, letting scenes breathe in a surreal way.\n\nWatching the sex scene with the knowledge of the twist is deeply disturbing and disgusting. There’s no shock anymore, just dread and that makes the scene harder to sit through.\n\nPerformance wise, I didn’t connect much with the main character here. Choi Min-sik’s portrayal looks like a comedian with the wild hair and manic breakdown. He does not look like someone who carries out a vengeful act. Compared to Josh Brolin’s version, which leans more into guilt, rage, and psychological damage, this take feels hysterical and oddly funny at times instead of tragic.\n\nLesson learned: don’t be a peeping tom and don’t fuck with someone twice younger than yourself. \n\nI actually prefer how the remake handles the incest twist. Making it father and daughter adds an extra layer of karmic cruelty and long term planning. The TV revelation being designed specifically for Josh Brolin’s character isolating him with the truth feels colder and more calculated. It reinforces how the revenge wasn’t impulsive, but engineered for maximum psychological destruction over decades. It also makes narrative sense that Mia is positioned to care for him, instead of him randomly waking up with someone after blacking out after an hypnosis.\n\nThe endings also diverge emotionally. Here, he chooses ignorance wishing not to know, yet the pain in his eyes suggests memory still lingers beneath the hypnosis. The remake’s ending feels more like self punishment and accountability, with the character imprisoning himself again as a twisted form of repentance.\n\nDespite sharing the same core premise, these films are fundamentally different in execution and intention. The remake isn’t trying to copy word for word, because where's the fun in that? The remake reshapes the material through a different cinematic language and moral framing.\n\nIn the end, I think it’s unfair to judge one strictly against the other. They stand as separate interpretations, one leaning into stylized psychological horror, the other into cold, calculated revenge tragedy. Watching both highlights how the same narrative skeleton can produce wildly different cinematic experiences.","poster":"img/oldboy-2003.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Godfather Part II","year":1974,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-05","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":450,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe Godfather Part II shifts away from loud violence into quiet power plays, where conversations, looks, and decisions carry more danger than gunfire. It’s structured like a chess match across two timelines, contrasting the rise of one man with the moral collapse of another. Thematically dense, this isn’t just a continuation, it’s a tragic deepening of everything the first film set up.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI already hear the intense shout of Al Pacino here when he ordered “Rocco, alive!” after his attempted assassination. After seven years of being the Godfather, Michael is totally different now. Also the moment with Kay, when she tells him about the abortion, is especially terrifying. I can see that Michael wants to strangle Kay at that very moment.\n\nI was surprised to see Robert De Niro as young Vito Corleone. Buti na lang napanood ko ito after Heat, because otherwise it would feel weird seeing the father and son eventually kill each other in other film. De Niro’s Vito is quiet, observant, and patient. He builds power slowly, methodically, and always with purpose. His rise feels earned, almost noble in contrast to Michael’s descent.\n\nThis film feels more like quiet power plays, parang giant chess match. It’s a stark difference from the first film, which felt louder and more openly violent. Here, the danger is in conversations, and decisions made behind closed doors. Every move has long term consequences.\n\nVisually, the film reinforces this emotional shift. Most of the dialogue involving power and threats is lit from above, only lighting the head while casting deep shadows from the forehead down to the eyes. This makes the characters look brooding, mythic, and inhuman. They feel less like men and more like symbols of power without conscience or empathy.\n\nProps to the production design and wardrobe for clearly distinguishing the two eras as the film jumps back and forth in time. The contrast between young Vito’s rise and Michael’s rule is not just visual but thematic. Both men are powerful, ambitious, and vengeful at similar ages. But Vito builds his empire by strengthening family ties, while Michael isolates himself due to paranoia. Vito keeps his family together. Michael becomes a lone wolf. For that reason, for me, Vito is the better Godfather. He understood that power without family is hollow. Michael, despite having absolute control, ends up completely alone.\n\nThis is not just a sequel. It’s a tragedy unfolding in parallel timelines, showing how two men can walk similar paths and still end up in completely opposite places.","poster":"img/the-godfather-part-ii-1974.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"12 Angry Men","year":1957,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":621,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nWhat immediately pulled me in were the long takes. They completely immerse me inside the jury room. I can feel the heat, the claustrophobia, and the slow rise of tension. Most legal dramas rush past this part, but this film fully commits to the discussion itself, and that’s what makes it gripping. The dialogue is razor sharp, the blocking is precise, and the black and white cinematography is lit so well that it never feels static or dull. No spectacle needed, just people talking, thinking, and clashing. Pure cinema through writing and performance!\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWhat immediately pulled me in were the long takes. They really immerse me inside that room with the jury. Ramdam mo yung heat, and the growing tension. I’ve always wondered about juries every time I watch legal dramas, but most films skip over this part. This is the first time I’ve seen a movie fully commit to the discussion itself, and it’s fascinating.\n\nWhat’s amazing is how naturally the conversation flows. The discussion about the case slowly bleeds into their personal lives, experiences, and biases. Hindi pilit. It feels organic. This is also the only black and white film that truly kept me engaged from start to finish. I think it’s because of how well it’s lit and, more importantly, how sharp the dialogue writing is. Every line matters. I had to read between the lines literally!\n\nMost of the men just want to go home and be done with it. Convenience over conscience. But Henry Fonda’s Juror 8 chooses to bear the responsibility of asking the difficult questions. He doesn’t claim the boy is innocent, he just challenges the others to think beyond the surface facts and evidence presented to them in six days. With every round of discussion, one more vote shifts to not guilty, and I feel that slow, earned progression.\n\nAng galing ng pag burn ni Juror 8 kay Juror 3. The rage baiting was intentional and smart. He pushes him to explode just to prove a point: people say things they don’t truly mean when emotions take over, especially under stress. That ties perfectly to the central issue of the case, killing someone in anger. The argument about remembering details under stress, like recalling characters from a movie you just watched, was also a brilliant.\n\nThe details are where the film really shines. The argument about the woman witness having glasses marks on her nose is such a small thing, but it completely reframes her credibility. It’s not about clever twists, it’s about logic, patience, and observation.\n\nAs the pressure and exhaustion build, the ugly sides of people start to surface. Nakakafrustrate yung mga sarado ang utak kahit binibigyan na ng malinaw na arguments. Pride takes over logic. And most of the jurors who refuse to budge are driven by deeply personal reasons and projections, something that was already subtly foreshadowed at the very beginning. Ang galing ng acting ni Lee J. Cobb, kuha niya talaga yung inis ko! His breakdown feels raw, ugly, and painfully real.\n\nBy the end, this doesn’t feel like a courtroom drama anymore. It feels like a study of human behavior, bias, responsibility, and moral courage. One man choosing to slow things down and ask questions makes all the difference.\n\nAlso, side note, Henry Fonda looks and sounds like Willem Dafoe! Once you notice it, you can’t unhear it!\n\nThis film proves me that I don’t need action, music, or spectacle to be gripping. Just strong writing, strong performances, and the courage to sit with difficult conversations.","poster":"img/12-angry-men-1957.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Momo","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":590,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe time concept alone is instantly intriguing. A futuristic world where time feels like currency sets up a clean, sci-fi playground that blends sleek aesthetics with philosophical undertones. Even without diving into plot specifics, the film clearly wants you thinking about how modern life pressures people to optimize every second instead of actually living. The pacing has some quieter stretches, but the atmosphere, production design, and central theme keep it engaging. What starts like a kid friendly adventure slowly reveals itself as a thoughtful reflection on presence, pressure, and what it really means to use your time well.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe time concept alone got me curious enough to watch this. It feels like In Time with the idea of time as currency and lifeline, mixed with the clean futuristic aesthetic of A Wrinkle in Time or Tomorrowland. Upon watching, there’s even a Tenet nuance because of the reverse mechanics of time elements.\n\nThe bracelet commercial early on looks uncanny (looks AI made but I cannot see any article particularly mentioning it). Hindi masyadong sync yung audio sa bibig, the texture of the skin looks rubbery, the movement, and emotion feels robotic. That discomfort works in the film’s favor because it mirrors the artificiality of a world obsessed with optimization and efficiency over humanity.\n\nVery early, I started seeing the bracelet not as a symbol of freedom, but as a guilt trip device. Instead of helping people live better, it pressures them into constantly thinking about the future. It feels less like empowerment and more like a shackle. You’re not enjoying the present because you’re always measuring what comes next. That train of thought becomes one of the film’s strongest themes.\n\nWhat really hits is the line: “They rush and fret and stress but still somehow bored.” That perfectly sums up adulthood. People are overstimulated yet emotionally empty. There’s a clear Le Petit Prince parallel here: the idea that modern life strips away wonder. The Contacts system reinforces that commentary, especially when it promotes reading messages while watching a movie. It’s overstimulation packaged as productivity.\n\nAnother smart visual cue is how the bracelet glows red whenever characters form emotional connections. The system treats feelings as wasted time, when in reality those moments are what make us human. The film quietly argues that emotional presence is not inefficiency, but it’s meaning.\n\nI almost drifted off during parts of the second act because the pacing slows down, but the third act snaps everything into urgency. It becomes a literal race against time for Momo, the time thieves, and Master Hora. The swelling score pushes that tension forward, making the climax feel deserved.\n\nAt its core, this is one of those stories where a child stands against a system built by villain adults (like Ender's Game, Stranger Things, Spy Kids, etc). I initially expected something childish and lightweight, but it turns into a thoughtful, thrilling reflection on how we treat time. The final message lands clean: How you use time is entirely up to you, so enjoy it because we only have now.\n\nIt works both as advice for kids learning to shape their future and as a reminder for adults to stop living only in anticipation. The film ultimately argues that time isn’t something to hoard or optimize, it’s something to experience. A surprisingly warm and philosophical ride disguised as a futuristic adventure.","poster":"img/momo-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Song Sung Blue","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":616,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film’s musical energy immediately pulls me into its lively, playful tone. Beneath the fun performances, there’s a grounded emotional core about addiction, blended families, and second chances. What starts as a warm musical romance gradually reveals heavier themes about recovery, pressure, and the fragile balance between passion and self destruction. Strong performances and an intimate concert atmosphere keep it engaging, making the film feel both celebratory and quietly tense.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI’m not familiar with the songs going in, but I can say they’re nice, lively and jivey enough to instantly pull me into the mood of the film. The opening is fun and funny, lalo na yung false teeth na may lightning bolt. Right away, the movie establishes a playful energy while hinting at the messy lives of the characters underneath all that showmanship.\n\nEarly on, the film explores how alcoholics tend to trade one addiction for another. That idea quietly sits beneath the musical fun, reminding you that the characters are chasing something deeper than just performance. Tonally, it feels like A Star Is Born (star crossed lovers brought by the love of music) mixed with The Bridges of Madison County (love discovered later in life).\n\nI really like how their two families naturally blend together. There’s warmth in watching strangers become one big household. It gives the film emotional grounding.\n\nJust when I start thinking this is going to stay a feel good musical, a horrible turn of events hits. They were so close to stardom, and suddenly the tone shifts into something heavier, more emotional family drama. The cast shines here, especially during the hallucination scenes. The performances feel raw and unstable, matching Claire (Kate Hudson) mental and emotional spirals.\n\nMike stands out as a supportive husband and stepdad. He carries so much pressure but remains patient and understanding. When they begin rebuilding after hitting rock bottom, it feels warm and earned. A reminder that recovery is messy but possible.\n\nThen the film starts planting dread. Everything seems to be improving, yet there’s still a lot of runtime left, and I can’t shake the feeling something will go wrong. Mike feels like a ticking time bomb. The scene where he glued his scalp on the day of the show genuinely made me nervous. Parang si Andrew sa Whiplash na tumuloy pa rin despite getting into a car crash, or Killian sa Magazine Dreams na tumuloy pa rin sa competition kahit binugbog. The show must go on! I couldn’t fully enjoy the concert because I kept expecting him to collapse. Every moment becomes suspenseful, like holding your breath until the performance ends.\n\nI think his bond with his stepdaughter becomes his emotional anchor. They grow close despite her initial distance, and that connection feels like the invisible rope keeping him upright. \n\nThe concert itself is beautifully shot. The lights and color feel like a real event but still cinematic and intimate.\n\nThe cut to black transitions in this movie are brutal! They leave you hanging in tension, constantly wondering what comes next. When the inevitable happens, there’s at least comfort in knowing Mike finished the performance first. The funeral scene echoes the emotional weight of Lady Gaga singing I’ll Never Love Again: quiet devastation.\n\nOverall, this is a great movie that hit me emotionally more than I expected. I reacted a lot while watching: tension, warmth, dread, relief. It starts as lively musical romance and evolves into something about endurance, love, and the cost of chasing meaning through art. A deeply affecting ride.","poster":"img/song-sung-blue-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lost in Translation","year":2003,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-04","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":460,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nQuiet, awkward, and deeply human. Lost in Translation captures a very specific kind of loneliness, the kind that comes from being surrounded by people yet feeling unseen. The humor lands through confusion and cultural differences, while the tone stays gentle and reflective. It’s not plot driven. It’s mood driven. The film lives in pauses, glances, and conversations that don’t fully resolve, which makes it feel honest and intimate.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI got lost too, especially during the commercial production shoot scene. Nakakatawa yung chaos and confusion, pati yung “lip my stockings” moment. Again, I was lost. I didn’t know what was happening, and honestly, that felt intentional and funny at the same time.\n\nScarlett has that very young, innocent look here. There’s a softness to her presence that makes her feel fragile and still figuring things out.\n\nI really felt Bob, played by Bill Murray, as a very tired movie star. He’s been doing this for a long time and he just wants to finish the contracts and go home. It reminded me so much of how I imagine talents or celebrities we shoot with in our business. Constantly being told what to do, where to stand, how to act, without fully understanding the purpose of the shoot. Maybe that’s why they get grumpy sometimes. What stood out to me is how alone he feels. If he’s a movie star, nasaan yung entourage niya? Why does it feel like he has no one with him, especially in a foreign country with a massive language barrier?\n\nCharlotte, on the other hand, is lost in a quieter way. She’s navigating married life, her sense of purpose, and what she’s supposed to be doing with her life. There’s a deep loneliness there. When she and Bob meet, they unintentionally build a safe space for each other. There’s clearly a warm connection and attraction, but more than romance, it feels like recognition. They both crave appreciation, understanding, and quality time, things they’re not getting from their spouses.\n\nTheir bond feels intimate. It’s about companionship, being seen, and feeling less alone in a foreign place, both literally and emotionally.\n\nPersonally, I would have preferred an ending closer to Past Lives rather than the ambiguous whisper and hopeful send off  because come on, they’re both married. That tension between what they feel and what they could be will be the most painful part of the film.\n\nOverall, Lost in Translation really lives up to its title. It’s about being lost in language, culture, marriage, career, and life itself. Nothing is spelled out, and that’s both its charm.","poster":"img/lost-in-translation-2003.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mulholland Drive","year":2001,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":229,"review":"Even past the 1 hr mark, the plot still doesn’t thicken. It feels like a low-budget 90s mystery TV special, stretched and intentionally opaque. The sound design is consistently atmospheric, constantly hinting that something is wrong, but never quite revealing why. It creates unease without payoff, at least on a surface level.\n\nThen suddenly, the film shifts gears and becomes erotic. The intimacy is intense, almost jarring in contrast to the earlier mystery. It’s sensual, and raw.\n\nAt this point, it starts to feel like the movie can’t decide what it wants to be. Is it suspense, thriller, erotic drama, psychological breakdown? It keeps sliding between genres, refusing to commit, or maybe deliberately blurring them.\n\nWith less than 30 minutes left, I still didn’t know what the F I was watching. And that confusion feels intentional. The best way I can frame it is through Memento like logic, or an unreliable narrator who can’t fully grasp reality, or worse, someone actively rewriting it to protect themselves. Memories feel distorted, and fragmented. \n\nMulholland Drive doesn’t reward traditional engagement. It withholds clarity, replaces structure with mood, and asks you to sit inside disorientation. Whether that’s brilliant or frustrating depends entirely on your tolerance for ambiguity. For me, it felt less like solving a puzzle and more like being trapped inside someone else’s fractured state of mind, watching their fantasy slowly collapse.","poster":"img/mulholland-drive-2001.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Pearl Harbor","year":2001,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":455,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nBig, emotional, and unapologetically cinematic. The film opens with striking imagery and a warm, nostalgic color palette that immediately sets it apart from Michael Bay’s later, more digital look. When it leans into aerial photography and large scale action, it’s genuinely impressive. The flight choreography is elegant, and the romance driven motivation gives the spectacle an emotional spine. This is Bay at his most controlled, using scale, music, and physical effects to create moments that feel overwhelming rather than hollow.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nIt starts off with picturesque shots of planes against an orange skyline, immediately reminding me of Apocalypse Now. I actually like the colors here more than Michael Bay’s later obsession with teal and orange. The palette feels warmer, more nostalgic, and less digitally aggressive.\n\nThis film leans heavily on romance as motivation for the characters. When it wants to be artistic, especially with the aerial shots, it really delivers. The dogfights and flight choreography are elegant and cinematic. Of course, nothing really outmatches Top Gun: Maverick, but that’s an unfair comparison since they’re two decades apart.\n\nTama yung hinala ko. Hindi basta-basta mamamatay si Ben Affleck. He’s too big of a star to just die in the first half. I knew it. I knew this would turn into a love triangle, but not the typical one. It’s framed more around duty, guilt, and loyalty rather than pure romance.\n\nMichael Bay did a great job making the Japanese forces feel genuinely terrifying. When the attack on Pearl Harbor begins, it’s pure confusion and chaos. This is where Bay truly shines. His expert use of real explosives gives the sequence an insane level of believability and physicality. I dare say mas ramdam ko ang stakes at scale dito kaysa sa Dunkirk. For me, this might be the first time Michael Bay actually defeated Nolan in a film.\n\nThe most terrifying shots are the nurses running toward the hospital and the pilots sprinting toward their airplanes while Japanese planes rain bullets from above. It feels like an inescapable death trap. The size and impact of the bullets hitting the pavement are exceptionally shown, and it’s genuinely horrifying.\n\nThe hospital scene got me really angry and shocked. Very effective storytelling. Napahawak ako sa bibig ko. Superb acting and emotional impact.\n\nI also specifically loved the musical score during the takeoff of the planes from the carrier heading to Tokyo. It feels heroic, solemn, and determined all at once, perfectly capturing the film’s emotional peak.\n\nOverall, Pearl Harbor commits to spectacle and emotion, it delivers something powerful and unforgettable.","poster":"img/pearl-harbor-2001.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spirited Away","year":2001,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":258,"review":"I honestly don’t know what to make of it. It just feels and looks weird to me. The animation itself doesn’t feel new or striking to my eyes. It reminds me a lot of the kind of animation I grew up with in the late 90s to early 2000s, so instead of feeling timeless, it feels familiar in a way that doesn’t excite me. Because of that, I struggled to appreciate it on a visual level.\n\nWhat really kept me curious, was the girl and the dragon photo I seen before. I’ve always felt there was something deeply spiritual about their connection. To the point na gusto ko pa nga sana ipatattoo dati (even if I don't know the context behind it) because I thought it symbolized something profound, like untapped potential, soul connection, or a deeper mythic bond. But watching the film now, that expectation didn’t quite land. Their relationship turns out to be more about circumstance, protection, and brief companionship rather than some grand, cosmic or cathartic connection I imagined.\n\nEmotionally, it didn’t resonate with me. I couldn’t fully connect to Chihiro, Haku, or even the world itself. Everything feels grotesque, surreal, and dreamlike, yet none of it grounded me enough to care deeply about any one character. I felt like I was observing a strange world.\n\nFor me, that magic stayed distant. Instead of feeling transported, I felt disconnected. Curious, yes. But emotionally invested? Not really. It’s one of those films where I just couldn’t feel the bond everyone else seems to have with it.","poster":"img/spirited-away-2001.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lawrence of Arabia","year":1962,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":623,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nMonumental in scale and craft. The film immediately impresses with its vast desert imagery, warm yellow and brown palette, and a swelling score that makes everything feel mythic and grand. The sense of scale is overwhelming, from the landscapes to the sheer number of people on screen. Wide compositions and patient pacing immerse you in the physical experience of the desert, the heat, exhaustion, and isolation. Even without fully committing emotionally at every point, the film’s ambition, visual authority, and thematic seriousness make it undeniable cinema.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film immediately pulled me in with the swelling score as the sun rises over the orange horizon, crossfading into vast desert landscapes within the first 20 minutes. Parang Dune. Beautiful. This is pure visual popcorn for me. My eyes are really sensitive to yellowish and brownish palettes like this, and the film just luxuriates in those colors.\n\nThe scale is genuinely epic. The number of extras is mind blowing, to the point na mahirap isipin paano nila ginawa yun logistically. It’s already apparent early on, especially during the aerial bombing of the camp. Those planes looked real, flying at low altitude, and it’s honestly jaw dropping to imagine that being done practically.\n\nAlec Guinness still commands the screen, even younger and with black hair compared to the old Obi-Wan we’re used to. But the voice is unmistakable. May authority agad despite less screen time.\n\nAng ganda ng wideshots during the journey to Aqaba. Those compositions balance the slow burn and keep the film engaging. The director does an excellent job immersing you in the experience. Ramdam mo yung init ng araw, yung uhaw, yung pagod. It really feels like you’re suffering alongside them in the desert.\n\nLawrence, to me, starts as someone who defies the odds and leaves no one behind. He treats his comrades fairly and with dignity. “Nothing is written.” That optimism and idealism define him early on. But less than halfway through, wala pa rin akong cathartic moment. I started questioning him. Is he playing the Arabs or the English? Is he Aurens or Lawrence? This is where the film becomes a clear portrait of a man being changed by violence and power.\n\nAfter the intermission, medyo nagdrag na siya for me. The shots aren’t as striking as the first half, and some scenes feel more studio bound than the expansive outdoor photography earlier. But in a way, that visual grounding mirrors the story itself. From sunshine and romantic idealism, it becomes heavier and more constrained.\n\nThe narrative shifts from optimism and self definition to disillusionment. Lawrence sees the ugliness of conquest and wants to return to who he really was. From something else, to somebody else. He reminds me a lot of Paul Atreides, constantly torn between morality and destiny, conflicted inside while wielding immense influence over people who see him as a savior. He becomes their Lisan al-Gaib.\n\nI can clearly see Auda Abu Tayi as a parallel to Stilgar, not just in appearance but in temperament. A kunsintidor, bloodthirsty, driven by conquest. Ali, on the other hand, feels like Chani, acting as the voice of conscience and restraint.\n\nBy the end, Aurens goes fully bloodthirsty during the massacres. He loses his wits. He becomes what he once described the Arabs to be: barbarous and cruel. That transformation is tragic.\n\nOverall, Lawrence of Arabia is monumental in scale and craft. Even when it drags for me in the second half, its thematic weight, visual ambition, and psychological portrait of a man consumed by war and identity make it undeniable cinema.","poster":"img/lawrence-of-arabia-1962.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Godfather","year":1972,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":552,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis watch clicked for me in a way it didn’t before. Understanding the film’s language and power dynamics makes everything richer. The quiet rules of loyalty, favors, and obligation are what truly drive the story, and the film communicates that without spelling it out. Performances are controlled and restrained, especially Al Pacino’s, whose presence evolves subtly but powerfully. Coppola’s use of silence, sound, and pacing is masterful, turning small moments into defining ones. It’s a film that rewards patience and attention, and once it locks in, it becomes impossible to forget.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI enjoyed this far more than the last time I tried to finish it. This time around, I understood the cinema language and the power dynamics much better. You don’t say no to a mafia boss, and the film clearly shows how favors, loyalty, and money quietly shape politics and society behind closed doors.\n\nYoung Robert Duvall surprisingly reminded me of Harvey Specter in Suits. Al Pacino here is very different from the intensity of Vincent in Heat. He’s young, restrained, almost invisible at first. Calm, graceful, and reserved. But by the end, his character development completely flips the dynamic. He’s still controlled and calculated, but now there’s authority in his words and gaze. It really embodies the idea that you should never mistake kindness for weakness.\n\nThere’s also a beautiful romantic score during Michael’s time with the Sicilian girl. This was actually the part where I stopped watching before, so everything after that felt like new territory for me. It made the second half of the film much more surprising and engaging.\n\nTalia Shire is strikingly beautiful here as Connie. I first knew her as Adrian in Rocky, but her performance during the domestic violence scene is intense and uncomfortable, in a way that feels painfully real.\n\nOne thing that didn’t work for me is the exaggerated acting during some of the shooting scenes. Characters like Vito and Sonny still reacting even after being riddled with bullets felt over the top. One or two shots should have been enough for them to drop cold. That said, this is more a product of its time than a deal breaker.\n\nFrancis Ford Coppola’s use of silence is masterful. Silence becomes the atmosphere of conversations, and when sound does come in, it’s always purposeful. Michael’s hesitation before killing Sollozzo is amplified by the sound of a train approaching, signaling the moment his decision hardens. During the baptism sequence, the swelling score, the priest’s Latin dialogue, and the cries of the baby collide with the assassinations of the rival families. It’s chilling and brilliant. Then there’s Kay confronting Michael about Carlo’s death, with the ticking clock in the background. To me, it signals the moment Michael fully crosses the threshold into his new life.\n\nThe final visuals are just as powerful. Connie in the foreground, Michael in the background, framed as if trapped by his new role. He feels small, boxed in by responsibility, and already lying to the woman he loves. When the capos kiss his hand and call him Godfather, it’s the perfect closing note. Chef’s kiss. A truly iconic ending.","poster":"img/the-godfather-1972.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A Clockwork Orange","year":1971,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":406,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film throws you into a world of delinquent energy and never lets you settle. The elegant classical score clashing against violent, unruly behavior is what makes it so unsettling. Everything feels out of sync on purpose. The slang heavy dialogue, exaggerated accents, and out of time production design all add to the alienation. It looks futuristic and dated at the same time, like a past vision of tomorrow. Performances are manic and theatrical, especially the lead, whose presence carries the film even when it feels exhausting. This is not about growth or redemption, but about control, identity, and what happens when choice is taken away.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWhat a bunch of delinquents. Feels like watching Netflix’s Steve (2025) again. The group has those fiendish, clownish grins. Lunatic energy from start to finish.\n\nThe musical score is beautiful, almost elegant, and that’s what makes it so disturbing. It feels completely mismatched with what’s happening on screen. The music evokes class, order, and sophistication, while the characters are chaotic, rowdy, and violent. That contrast makes it hard to watch, but ironically more engaging. \n\nThe dialogue is loaded with words and slang from England that I’ve never heard before. It adds to the alienation. Ang OA ng accent ni Mr. Deltoid, nakakairita.\n\nThe sets look retro but also strangely out of time. It’s futuristic, yet dated. Parang past imagining the future. The wardrobe gives Scooby-Doo era vibes, bright colors, costumes that feel playful and cartoonish, which again clashes with the brutality of the story. \n\nI get Shutter Island vibes in the middle portion, especially with the idea of “curing” a twisted mind. Alex goes from delighting in violence to being physically repulsed by it. After he’s cured and released, I also felt some Shawshank Redemption energy. He struggles to readjust to the outside world, but unlike Andy, there’s no real redemption here. He’s just displaced, stripped of his nature, and rejected by the same society that tried to fix him.\n\nAlex’s facial expressions and acting feel like a mashup of Evan Peters, Joaquin Phoenix, Mark Hamill, and Hayden Christensen. There’s manic charm, theatrical madness, vulnerability, and menace all mixed together. \n\nOverall, this film is weird. It's confusing, and disturbing. No character development as the government made sure of it.","poster":"img/a-clockwork-orange-1971.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Wrecking Crew","year":2026,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":168,"review":"The fight choreography at the beginning is nicely messy. High-action but funny at the same time. There’s a playful rhythm to the punches and movement rather than trying to be overly slick.\n\nThe real explosions are a plus. You can feel when something is done practically, and those moments stand out compared to the rest of the action.\n\nUnfortunately, the CGI really drags it down. The cars, helicopters, and environments look bad, especially the background replacements during the highway scene. Halatang green screen, and it breaks immersion hard.\n\nI didn’t feel any sympathy for the characters or any real stakes. The action felt weightless, and even the gore looks fully digital, so nothing really lands. The Yakuza comes off as funny instead of scary, which kills the tension. The plot twist is predictable, so there’s no real surprise factor.\n\nAt least the two leads get some character development at the last second. Overall, it’s serviceable if you’re just there for light action, but it doesn’t leave much impact.","poster":"img/the-wrecking-crew-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"13 Hours: The Secret Soldiers of Benghazi","year":2016,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-02","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":239,"review":"Colors, explosions, and occasional lens flare are definitely Michael Bay DNA. The camera movement, camera angles, tight sweaty shots, slow-mo debris, sparks flying everywhere. Real explosions, real fire, real smoke. All practical. It genuinely feels like a real battleground. Heart-pounding and nail-biting throughout. Bagay na bagay talaga ang style ni Michael Bay dito. Black Hawk Down level yung risk at claustrophobic “mouse trapped” feeling. The trailer honestly doesn’t do the film justice.\n\nThe action is relentless and immersive. Every firefight feels heavy and dangerous, and the constant motion of the camera puts me right in the middle of the chaos. I can feel the exhaustion, panic, and adrenaline of the soldiers as the night drags on.\n\nThis could have been a 4.5 for me, but there are moments that pulled me out. Some tactical decisions felt stupid. Why are they still waiting for the bad guys to fully form up when they already know they’re armed? I don’t know much about rules of combat, but when it comes to survival, anyone moving towards me with a gun in their hands should already be considered hostile.\n\nStill, the film is well crafted. Great acting across the board, especially Pablo Schreiber. What an upgrade from Orange Is the New Black!\n\nIn the end, there’s really no winner here. War only creates widows. Bureaucracy killed them, not bullets. Not guns. That lingering bitterness is what stays with me after the credits roll.","poster":"img/13-hours-the-secret-soldiers-of-benghazi-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"No Other Choice","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-31","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":289,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nAt first, that unpredictability is intriguing, but over time it can feel disorienting rather than purposeful. What grounds it is the central moral tension. A family pushed by loss of stability, a father forced into calculated choices, and a household quietly orbiting around compromise. It’s more focused on the uncomfortable gray area of survival and denial.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nDi ko talaga alam kung ano ang gustong maging ng film na ito. Comedy ba siya? Family drama? Erotic? Identity theft? Thriller? Or pure despair lang dahil nawalan siya ng trabaho? Para siyang film na deliberately nagrerefuse mag-stay sa isang genre, which can be intriguing at first, pero eventually nakakagulo rin.\n\nNot Son Ye-jin flirting with another man younger than her agai hahaha. Reminds me of something in the rain. \n\nWhat I did like is how methodical the father is in devising his plans. He’s careful not to incriminate himself directly. Instead, he frames his victims in a way that the crimes will never be traced back to him. It’s cold, and calculated. \n\nBy the end, what I got from the film is a grim question: how far can a father go just to provide for his family and maintain their lifestyle? And on the other side, how much can a wife tolerate and consciously ignore the dark sides of her partner for the sake of family stability?\n\nThis film leaves you sitting with the messiness of survival, compromise, and the quiet justifications people make when they feel they have no other choice.","poster":"img/no-other-choice-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Fighter","year":2010,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-31","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":421,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe performances carry the experience. Mark Wahlberg is physically disciplined and restrained, while Amy Adams brings warmth and emotional stability. Christian Bale completely dominates with a raw, uncomfortable performance that adds chaos and intensity. At its core, the film is less about boxing glory and more about family pressure, discipline, and finding control in a life constantly being pulled apart.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first thing that really stood out for me was the smooth transition from a cinematic film look to a broadcast documentary style during the first fight. First time ko talaga makita yun in a boxing film. Wala niyan sa Rocky, Raging Bull, The Smashing Machine, or even The Iron Claw. It immediately grounded the film in realism, like you’re watching an actual televised bout unfold.\n\nAmy Adams is really beautiful here. My one and only Lois Lane! She brings a quiet strength and warmth to her role, acting as both an emotional anchor and a shield for Micky against the chaos surrounding him.\n\nFirst time ko rin nakita si Mark Wahlberg this ripped. Physically, he really committed to the role, and you can feel the discipline in how he plays Micky. He’s focused, reserved, and determined.\n\nChristian Bale as Dicky completely steals the film. Frail, erratic, and fully convincing as a crackhead. Their family dynamic is insane, and because of that, I honestly can’t fully blame Dicky for how he turned out. He’s a product of a toxic environment that keeps enabling and dragging him down, even when he’s trying to get back up.\n\nI just hope Micky would punch his mother in the face. They’re constantly sabotaging him. Instead of protecting his career, they manipulate his choices and treat his success like it’s theirs to control. That part really frustrated me.\n\nI like the filmmaking style, but I’m not a big fan of the family drama and constant feud. That said, I really appreciated Micky’s fight strategy. He lets his opponents wear themselves out, takes the punishment, absorbs the hits, and once they’re exhausted, that’s when he strikes and knocks them out. It’s patient, calculated, and smart!\n\nThe ending works really well for me. It’s triumphant not just in terms of boxing, but also in Dicky’s sobriety. It doesn’t feel like a solo victory. It’s a literal team win for the brothers, both inside and outside the ring.","poster":"img/the-fighter-2010.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Phoenician Scheme","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-31","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":220,"review":"I watched this mainly because of Benicio Del Toro, and right from the start, the film delivers with a strong score and immediate action. There’s a confidence in how it opens, musically and visually, that pulls you in without needing much buildup. \n\nI actually like the color palette here. It’s not as heavy on pastels compared to The Grand Budapest Hotel. The colors feel more serious texture while still being unmistakably Wes Anderson.\n\nWatching the framing and static shots feels like looking at a tableau on a stage. Clean blocking, very deliberate entrances and exits within the frame, and movements that feel stiff and limited in space. Everything is precise, like the characters are pieces being moved on a board rather than living in a naturalistic world. It’s theatrical, and very composed.\n\nThe basketball scene in the tunnel with John Cranston and Tom Hanks is genuinely funny, but other than moments like that, I still don’t fully get Wes Anderson’s humor. It often feels dry and distant to me, more intellectual than instinctive.\n\nWhat really worked for me are the sets, especially those with an Egyptian-inspired aesthetic. They look striking and thoughtfully designed, adding visual interest and scale to the film. Even when the humor doesn’t fully land for me, the production design and visual discipline keep the experience engaging.","poster":"img/the-phoenician-scheme-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kinds of Kindness","year":2024,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-30","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":332,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nKinds of Kindness thrives on absurdity, and power dynamics rather than clear messaging. Some parts land harder than others, but the film is consistent in one thing: showing how far people are willing to go once authority, desire, or belonging is placed above common sense.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film immediately sets an uneasy tone with a piano score reminiscent of The Killing of a Sacred Deer, but this one works better for me because of the added guttural chorus. It reminds me of Dark, giving the music a more ritualistic and unsettling texture.\n\nThe first part is darkly funny. Natawa talaga ako when he ended up killing R.M.F., something he didn’t even want to do at first. But the moment he felt the loss of privilege, doon na siya napilitan. It says a lot about how morality collapses once comfort and power are taken away. Obedience here isn’t about belief, it’s about fear of losing status.\n\nThe second part leans fully into absurdity. Jesse suggesting that Emma cut off her own thumb, cook it with cauliflower is so random and extreme that I couldn’t help but laugh kahit hindi ko mafigure out if may symbolism. That absurdity becomes the point. This segment also gives strong Bugonia vibes. Paranoia, and conspiracy thinking. At first, you think Jesse is losing his mind, but by the end, it turns out he was right. That reversal is both funny and disturbing.\n\nThe third part shifts into full cult territory. Rituals, and orgy-like ceremonies. But this is where the film lost me a bit. I can feel what it’s trying to do in terms of control, devotion, and submission, but I didn’t really get a clear emotional or thematic payoff from this last segment compared to the first two.","poster":"img/kinds-of-kindness-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Send Help","year":2026,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-30","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":451,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nWhat starts with a rough and distracting CGI-heavy opening eventually settles into a tense survival thriller once the focus shifts to human behavior under pressure. The film becomes far more engaging when it strips away comfort, status, and authority, showing survival as an equalizer where power can flip instantly. Its strength lies in the psychological dynamic between the two leads, where trust, control, and desire quietly collide. While the visuals are uneven, with some scenes feeling artificial, moments of realism and danger do stand out. Overall, it’s less about survival mechanics and more about how power, dominance, and strategy emerge when systems collapse.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe film opens with an unbelievable CGI plane crash. Hindi siya realistic, and it immediately took me out of the experience. \n\nOnce the survival aspect kicks in, the film becomes more interesting. Survivalism is presented as the ultimate equalizer. When power and authority are stripped away, wag maging kupal, because the balance can shift very quickly. This is a story about how fragile dominance really is when resources are limited.\n\nThere’s a strange comfort shown in the simplicity of survival. Being contented. Basic needs are provided. No overthinking about skin care, status, or appearances. Just food, shelter, and staying alive. That contrast works well agains the characters’ internal power struggles.\n\nThe dynamic between the two leads is the real engine of the film. She delays the rescue partly to humble him, but it slowly becomes clear that she also wants him for herself. Power, desire, and control start to overlap, blurring moral lines.\n\nVisually, the film is inconsistent. Some shots clearly look like real locations, while others feel obviously shot on a studio set. However, the wave cinematography during the attempted raft escape stands out. That sequence looks very realistic and sells the danger effectively.\n\nThe acting is solid. Both performances feel natural. Funny, scared, rageful. The emotional shifts feel earned, especially as desperation builds. By the third act, Linda becomes a full blown Wanda Maximoff, fully embracing control and manipulation.\n\nThis film ultimately shows how intoxicating power can be. The story constantly pushes and pulls. Kala mo okay na silang dalawa, but it never really is. There’s always another layer of strategy underneath.\n\nIn the end, it almost plays like a manifesto on how power is gained, maintained, and weaponized. Strategy and planning matter more than morality. Survival doesn’t just test the body, it exposes who people really are once there’s no system left to hide behind.","poster":"img/send-help-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Rental Family","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":371,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nA gentle, emotionally grounded film about care, boundaries, and the quiet cost of empathy. The film is focused on how connection forms. Performances feel natural and unforced, especially from the younger cast, giving the story a sincerity that carries through to its restrained ending. Instead of chasing devastation, it chooses reflection. Softer, calmer, and more human than expected.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nFrom not wanting to do the job and pretending to be someone for other people, he slowly shifts into caring too much and helping too much. What starts as pure performance becomes emotional labor. He crosses the line between acting and genuinely wanting to fix the lives of his clients.\n\nI’m especially sad for Mia. She got attached to Phillip so deeply. Watching that made me think, I wish I had a Phillip too when I was a child. Someone who believed in me, pushed me to dream big. Maybe I wouldn’t have grown into a scared and restrained adult, always afraid of taking too much risk and playing it safe. \n\nBrian’s situation is lonely in a different way. He lives multiple lives for other people, yet he doesn’t really have a life of his own. He’s present everywhere, but never truly present for himself.\n\nIs this really Shannon Gorman’s acting debut? She’s great. There’s a natural vulnerability there that never feels forced.\n\nProps to Aiko as well. From being a constant yes-man, she slowly reclaims her willpower. Seeing her finally say no feels empowering.\n\nI honestly thought the story would go in a darker direction. I expected him to want Mia to be her real daughter, struggle to detach once the job was done, and completely break. That version would’ve been heavier and more devastating. But instead, the director chose a self reflective path. Honesty over drama. Acceptance over tragedy.\n\nI still prefer my version because it would hit harder emotionally, but I understand why the film chose restraint. The ending is beautiful in a quiet way. Less dramatic, but more human.","poster":"img/rental-family-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Maestro","year":2023,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-28","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":348,"review":"I suffered again from detaching from it because of the black and white. I experienced this with Raging Bull and Following. That barrier is still there for me. But I can definitely see how beautifully lit it is. The lighting is elegant and intentional, very sculpted, especially on faces. You can tell there’s a deep appreciation for classic cinema craft even if it didn’t fully pull me in emotionally.\n\nBradley Cooper and Carey Mulligan, for me, don’t have strong chemistry. I couldn’t fully resonate with the problems presented in their relationship. The emotional stakes felt distant, like I was observing their struggles rather than feeling them. The conflict is there, but it didn’t translate into something I could emotionally latch onto.\n\nAfter 47 minutes, there’s finally color. Thank God. That shift alone made the film easier for me to stay engaged. The color brings warmth and texture.\n\nI can still hear the lowered vocal register, using a gravelly, strained, almost damaged tone of Jackson Maine in Bradley Cooper’s voice here, and that made it hard for me to disassociate those characters. That same familiar vocal carries over here, so instead of fully seeing Bernstein, I keep hearing echoes of Jackson Maine’s brokenness and Rocket’s rough charm. It distracted me more than I expected.\n\nThat said, props to the prosthetics, hair, makeup, and overall transformation. The aging process looks convincing. The transition from middle-aged to older Bernstein feels seamless and carefully executed. \n\nMaybe I’m just not a fan of opera or orchestral music, which makes it harder for me to invest in the gravity of the craft being portrayed. The film treats music as something monumental and transcendent, but I didn’t feel that pull personally. Or maybe I came in with the wrong expectations.\n\nI think part of it is that I subconsciously expected this to become another A Star Is Born. Something warm, heartfelt, emotionally direct. A passionate chase for dreams mixed with existential crisis and music that carries you emotionally throughout. \n\nMaestro is colder, and more observational but it didn’t resonate with me on a personal level.","poster":"img/maestro-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Resurrection","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":479,"review":"The film opens as a silent movie. I was kind of waiting for the right opportunity to properly watch one ever since I watched Babylon, and luckily I experienced it here. It’s a nice experience, just focusing on acting and musical score without dialogue. Sets, lights, sounds, color, and score are the first things I appreciated. The film immediately reminds me how much cinema can communicate without speech.\n\nThere are old style techniques used here like forced perspective and double exposure, which adds to that early cinema texture. After around 22 minutes, the look changes, and the film starts evolving visually and tonally.\n\nImmortality is portrayed as boring and mechanical. Cinema, on the other hand, breathes life into the forgotten once again. That contrast becomes clearer as the film progresses.\n\nThe first dream feels like a noir crime film. Lots of steam, smog, black and blue colors. Investigative, slowburn. I get Inception vibes, like there are layers of dreams stacked on top of each other.\n\nOne choice that really caught me off guard was the use of snoring as sound design. WTF moment. It’s uncomfortable but strangely hypnotic.\n\nAs the film moves forward, each dream subtly reflects a different cinematic era. Using various camera techniques appropriate to that period, even the aspect ratio changes too!\n\nI was most engaged during the con part. The code speak, the trickery, the performance within performance..That section feels alive and playful, like cinema rediscovering its own trickery.\n\nThe color grading in the 1999 scene is also great, with long continuous shots using invisible cuts. Ang laki ng set, and it reminded me of BuyBust in terms of scale and spatial complexity. The rain sound design there is immersive too, especially when watched in Atmos. It puts you inside the frame rather than just watching it. \n\nImmortals not dreaming feels like a Little Prince idea. Adults forgetting how to enjoy, dream, or be children again because the modern world is obsessed with money. In the film, it feels like people forgot how to watch cinema, or in this context, how to dream. It reads like a death of cinema metaphor. Instead of experiencing small joys, we focus on profit. Cinema becomes “too aspirational” or dismissed as a waste of time.\n\nThe film doesn’t always make sense, but that’s how dreaming works. There are films like that, even with Christopher Nolan. Not everything is meant to be logical. It’s emotional, subconscious, fragmented. For me, this is more about cinematic language than story. You’re not supposed to fully understand it. You’re supposed to feel it.\n\nThe ending, where Shu Qi “drops” the corpse into the water behind a sealed wall door, feels symbolic. For me, it represents cinephiles being forced to consume watered-down content by modern cinema. Movies that don’t challenge us to think or feel. Slowly, that kind of consumption makes cinema itself feel dead.","poster":"img/resurrection-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Nobody","year":2021,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":438,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis one clicked with me immediately because of how ordinary everything feels at the start. A quiet, repetitive existence, familiar routines, nothing remarkable on the surface. That sense of numb stability makes the lead instantly relatable before the film escalates the stakes. When things escalate, it doesn’t chase myth making or slick perfection. It keeps the violence playful but grounded, with choreography that feels chaotic yet intentional. There’s a clear personality behind the camera, experimenting with tone and staging rather than going full insanity. The result lands in a sweet spot. Energetic, funny, and brutal. It’s not the most extreme entry in this space, but it balances humor, character, and action in a way that makes it an easy, satisfying ride.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI resonated with him agad. Living a boring, routinary life with pretty much the same family dynamics. Nothing spectacular, nothing exciting, just existing. That quiet, numbing normalcy makes the character instantly relatable before anything explodes.\n\nParang John Wick din to. Nanahimik na lang, then someone disturbs the peaceful retirement. Same premise, but a different flavor. Where Wick is sleek and mythic, this one feels more grounded, less legend, more facade crashing down and suppressed rage finally leaking out.\n\nThe fight choreography is messy but fun. Very Ilya Naishuller DNA. Ngayon lang ako nakarinig ng line na “don’t call 911”. Astig! Parang katumbas ng \"Guns, lots of guns\".\n\nIf you just want high energy violence with adequate storytelling, go with Ilya. You’re in good hands.\n\nBadass din yung tatay niya. It really runs in the family.\n\nWhat I liked here is how controlled the direction feels. Parang ramdam mo yung restraint ng director. He could’ve gone full crazy and absurd with the fights, but he holds back a bit. It feels like he’s experimenting, cooking, and exploring his cinematography taste. This film has a very different treatment compared to his other work.\n\nThe third act set piece feels like a Home Alone setup turned into a battlefield. Traps, positioning, escalation, then headshots after headshots. Ang angas ng triple kill ni RZA! That sequence fully embraces the film’s playful brutality.\n\nIf action and pure fun is the basis, Hardcore Henry is still on top. But for humor and story, Head of State takes the crown. Nobody sits comfortably in between, less extreme, more balanced.\n\nOverall, this is just a fun, violent ride with personality and control behind the chaos.","poster":"img/nobody-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Eden","year":2024,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":532,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nA slow burn survival drama that gradually peels away the idea of civilization and replaces it with something far more primal. What starts as an experiment in ideals and escape slowly curdles into paranoia, power imbalance, and moral collapse. Performances carry the weight here, especially in how desperation, hunger, and authority distort people over time. Visually, the film leans into earthy, sun scorched textures that make the setting feel isolated and unforgiving, with the camera becoming more expressive as the story darkens. It’s more about watching belief systems rot under pressure, revealing how thin the line is between principle and savagery.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nUnang napansin ko agad yung accent ni Ana de Armas. Medyo weird and distracting sa pandinig.\n\nOne of the most insane moments in the film is when Sydney is giving birth while warding off wild dogs. Grabe yung tension. Great performance matched with a swelling score. I caught myself holding my breath, just waiting for the baby to cry for the first time. I honestly don’t get the hate towards Sydney. She’s not the best actress out there, but she’s far from bad. She delivers when it matters.\n\nIronic yung character ni Jude Law. He preaches about building a new democracy, rejecting money, hierarchy, and old systems, but he slowly turns into exactly what he claims to hate. Acting like a dictator, treating the island as his own, enforcing isolation. When pressure hits, he can’t stick to his ideals. From vegetarian to carnivore, from visionary to survivalist. He says he wants a democracy without money, yet uses money to secure the Baroness’ possessions. Hypocrite. Unstable. Crazy. It’s regression, not growth. From a brilliant doctor to a savage man. This might be one of Jude Law’s best performances for me. I don’t remember seeing him this raw and unhinged.\n\nAna’s character is the kind of woman who uses sex to gain favors and leverage. Batshit crazy, manipulative, unhinged. Magaling yung acting niya kasi kuha niya talaga yung inis ko. She’s dangerous in a very human way.\n\nVanessa’s character, on the other hand, has firm principles. She sticks to what she believes in, but she’s also very stiff, rigid, and negative. Moral, yes, but emotionally cold. Parang survival by discipline rather than adaptation.\n\nProduction design and color grading are strong. Mukhang nasa probinsya talaga. The palette reminds me of indie Filipino films set in rural areas. Earthy, muted, sun-baked. The third act is where the film becomes more cinematic, with silhouette shots, and terrain wide frames. \n\nThe film ultimately shows how men resort to savagery when pushed to the edge. Ideals collapse under hunger, fear, and power struggles. Civilization is thin, and survival strips people down to their ugliest instincts.\n\nBy the end, the irony completes itself. The tourists became the locals. Their descendants become hotel operators, exactly the kind of transformation Ana de Armas’ character once desired. What began as an escape from society eventually turns into a new version of it.","poster":"img/eden-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Death Proof","year":2007,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":215,"review":"So this is how it feels when Quentin Tarantino directs a Fast and Furious movie? Intense and heart pounding!\n\nYou really have to give it to Zoë Bell for the stunts. Absolutely fearless. And credit to the editor for maintaining tension despite the cuts. Even with quick transitions, the rhythm never breaks. The camera department also deserves praise for capturing that high speed, high octane car chase. You feel the velocity, and the risk in every frame.\n\nQT DNA is all over this film. Dialogue-heavy scenes that slowly build tension, strong cinematography, sharp sound design, and of course, violence. Tarantino takes his time letting characters talk, joke, argue, and exist before unleashing chaos.\n\nOne thing that really caught my attention was the dialogue touching on being pro-gun. It doesn’t feel random. It feeds into the film’s themes of power, control, and who gets to hold it. It quietly sets up the moral shift that happens later.\n\nAnd that revenge sequence? Ang angas! Fully deserved. I was smiling the whole time. Stuntman Mike finally found someone who could match him, and watching the tables turn is deeply satisfying.\n\nDeath Proof may look simple on the surface, but it’s pure Tarantino indulgence. Slow burn, explosive release, and a cathartic ending that makes all the buildup worth it.","poster":"img/death-proof-2007.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Planet Terror","year":2007,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":363,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nThis is pure grindhouse energy turned all the way up. The look alone is doing a lot of work, dirty textures, aggressive colors, exaggerated violence, and a deliberately battered visual style that feels more like a sensation than a narrative. It leans hard into practical effects and physicality, committing to a tactile, flesh forward aesthetic that makes the world feel sticky and dangerous. The tone never takes itself too seriously, mixing splatter horror with parody and deliberately dumb humor, which oddly becomes part of its charm. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nI think this is my third or fourth time watching this. I remember seeing it as a teenager and, honestly, it got me really horny alongside Zack Snyder’s Dawn of the Dead. I don’t even remember how I got a copy. Maybe LimeWire? Aside from the sexy nuances, what really made this film stick with me was its unique retro style. The gritty cinematography, thick atmosphere, heavy prosthetics, aggressive color grading, over the top gore, and a strong woman main character. I didn’t really see that combination much back then.\n\nWatching it now, I realize that without fully understanding cinematic language at the time, this must have been pure sensory overload for my teenage brain. Everything is cranked up. The textures, the blood, the smoke, the scratches, the intentional “damaged film” look. \n\nProsthetics play a huge role here, turning it into a body horror. The special effects department did a lot of heavy lifting too, from the explosions to the production design that fully commits to building this infected world.\n\nThe film feels like Kill Bill: Zombie Edition, but filtered through parody and grindhouse aesthetics.  May mga stupid jokes din na very 2000s comedy, parang Scary Movie levels. The stupidity is part of the texture.\n\nRewatching it now, I still get why it left such an imprint on me. It’s messy, loud, and shameless, but that’s exactly the point. And even years later, it still holds up.","poster":"img/planet-terror-2007.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Primate","year":2025,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":254,"review":"It was a pleasant surprise to see Troy Kotsur here. After CODA, I didn’t expect to see him pop up in this kind of suspense setup, and that casting choice immediately caught my attention.\n\nI watched the R16 cut, and what stood out most was how much the score does the heavy lifting. The music is constantly at work, sustaining unease and signaling danger even when the frame itself is quiet. It tells you when to feel uncomfortable, when to anticipate something going wrong, and when to brace yourself. Without it, the tension would probably collapse.\n\nConceptually, the whole setup feels like The Shallows, trapped in a more enclosed space with a predator. It’s a contained, survival focused suspense film that relies on proximity and pressure. The threat feels close, persistent, and unavoidable, which is where most of the tension comes from.\n\nThat said, this is also one of those suspense movies where the characters can be frustratingly dumb. They keep the lights off, make questionable decisions, and repeatedly choose the worst possible options. You find yourself thinking, “Why would you do that?” more often than you’d like. It does increase tension, but it also leads to a lot of facepalm moments that pull you slightly out of the experience.\n\nIn the end, it’s a straightforward suspense watch. Nothing groundbreaking, nothing that reinvents the genre, but it does what it sets out to do. If you’re in the mood for something tense, contained, and driven more by atmosphere than ideas, it works well enough.","poster":"img/primate-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Following","year":1998,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-26","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":298,"review":"I knew I had to be on my game going in, simply because this is a Christopher Nolan film. Even at his earliest stage, there’s an expectation that structure matters and that attention is part of the experience.\n\nThis feels like an unreliable narrator jumping across three timelines, either deliberately hiding something or simply incapable of narrating the story cohesively. The fragmentation feels intentional, like the film itself is playing a con on the audience. I'm constantly trying to orient myself, only to realize the ground keeps shifting under my feet.\n\nI like the twist. It’s somehow unpredictable. The ending works, but I wasn’t really shocked. Part of that is because I couldn’t fully focus while watching. I couldn’t fully lock in while watching, and the black and white presentation made it harder for me to stay engaged.\n\nThis is the kind of Nolan film where the first viewing is pure confusion, and the second viewing is where things start clicking. You’re meant to rewatch it with the new information from the first run, and only then does the full picture come together.\n\nBut Nolan himself often says not to overthink his films and just feel them. In that sense, being confused here actually feels correct. It mirrors how blindsided and duped the writer was by the con itself. You’re supposed to feel lost, manipulated, and slightly frustrated.\n\nEven though this is his first feature film, Nolan’s storytelling DNA is already very apparent. The obsession with structure, misdirection, and perspective and audience manipulation is already there. You can see the foundations of what he’ll keep refining in later films.\n\nFor now, I’ll give it a low score. This feels like a film designed to change with familiarity, and I can easily see my opinion shifting on future viewings.","poster":"img/following-1998.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"She Rides Shotgun","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":554,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nTaron Egerton brings an intense, unstable energy that keeps a constant sense of danger on screen, while the film grounds itself as a father and daughter story about morality, protection, and survival in a corrupt world. It balances intimacy and violence well, with strong night photography, textured visuals, and smart staging that lets chaos exist around the characters without overwhelming them. Ana Sophia Heger is the emotional core and a real standout, delivering a performance that feels far beyond her age. While the first half drags and could have been tighter, the film ultimately works as a gritty crime thriller with surprising tenderness and emotional weight.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nFrom the start, Taron Egerton brings a very intense presence. There’s a clear James McAvoy energy from Split with his acting intensity. He carries a constant sense of danger, like someone barely holding himself together.\n\nAt its core, this is still a father and daughter story. The emotional beats between them, especially their conversations about morality. It’s not preachy, but it constantly questions what kind of man a father needs to be when the world around them is corrupt. The film also doesn’t shy away from showing cops involved in drug business, with the force itself compromised and operating under drug money influence.\n\nTonally and thematically, you can feel traces of Aftersun, Good Time, One Battle After Another, Sicario, and Breaking Bad with how it blends intimacy with violence and explores how crime seeps into personal lives.\n\nPerformance-wise, Taron Egerton is excellent, but the real standout is Ana Sophia Heger as Polly. The future is bright for this kid. That ending is devastating. She’s dancing, full of joy on the surface, but her eyes carry a quiet sadness that says something is missing without her father. That emotional contradiction is hard to pull off, especially for a child actor, but she nails it!\n\nPacing-wise, the first half feels boring and could have been shortened. The film takes too much time establishing ideas that are already clear, making the build up feel dragged rather than deliberate.\n\nVisually, the film shines in its night photography. The night drive sequences are beautifully shot, with tension baked into the framing and movement, especially during the car chases. The landscapes are also well used, supported by smart lens choices, controlled lighting, and a color grade that leans soft and intimate. The added film grain gives the movie texture.\n\nOne of the strongest visual ideas is how the film stages action in layers. At the third act, you see Polly running as the clear subject in the foreground, while chaos unfolds behind her, gunfights, sieges, all happening at the same time. I can vividly remember something like this in the third act of Tenet with the forward moving army and the backward moving army. It’s a great way of showing how violence exists around her world even when she’s not directly participating in it.\n\nOverall, She Rides Shotgun works both as a gritty crime thriller and an intimate emotional drama. Stylish, tense, and surprisingly tender, with performances that elevate the material beyond genre expectations.","poster":"img/she-rides-shotgun-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Poor Things","year":2023,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":794,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nEmma Stone completely disappears into the role. The production design, wardrobe, and score are all immaculate, shifting tones as Bella’s world expands. It is dense, provocative, and visually unforgettable, a film that invites interpretation rather than comfort.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis feels like a combination of The Favourite and The Lighthouse in terms of cinematography. You can really feel Yorgos Lanthimos continuing his visual language, but pushing it further into something more surreal, grotesque, and emotionally uncomfortable.\n\nAt the center of it all is Bella, and Emma Stone completely disappears into Bella’s character. You never see Emma Stone, only Bella. Emma Stone feels like a child trapped in an adult body. The unbalanced walk, childish behavior, sudden tantrums, and blunt curiosity all sell that imbalance. It really feels like watching like the assessor in the movie The Assessment. \n\nThat discomfort becomes sharper once sexuality enters the picture because I feel sorry for Bella. She has the brain of a child discovering the sexuality of an adult body, and that is deeply fucked up. There’s an inherent cruelty in watching her explore desire without the emotional and psychological tools to fully understand consent, power, and consequence.\n\nThe film visually reinforces this tension through contrast. Life is boring and suffocating under the protection of Dr. Godwin, and how colorful and intoxicating it becomes once she explores the world beyond her home. I think this mirrors how women go through self discovery, finding their identity, and place in society, often only after leaving spaces that claim to “protect” them.\n\nIt also shows how sexual desire can become addictive once tasted, especially when it’s tied to freedom, validation, and control over one’s body for the first time.\n\nVisually, the most striking element is the surreal sky. It looks like a painting, something out of this world, reinforcing that Bella exists in a world that doesn’t follow normal rules. The lenses feel similar to The Favourite, with extreme wide angles and distortion, but here they’re paired with anamorphic lenses that produce circular bokeh, giving everything a dreamlike, warped quality. It constantly reminds you that this is not reality as we know it, but Bella’s version of it.\n\nThere’s that gory aspect that’s already apparent from Bugonia, and honestly I feel thankful that most of the surgery scenes are in black and white. It softens the blow. The black and white works because of the deeper blacks and less defined highlights. It strips the scenes of sensationalism and makes them colder and more disturbing.\n\nAt its core, I think this film is about how men view women as property, or ownership. Men feel entitled to reconstruct women, control them, define them, and treat them as objects that can be replaced when they go missing. Different men impose different forms of control. One is patriarchal care, through Dr. Goodwin, controlling behavior under the guise of protection. Another, through Duncan, is performative decency, obsessed with image and possession. The film also shows how judgmental men are toward sex workers, and how that toxic thinking spreads to women judging other women, making womanhood itself more oppressive.\n\nI honestly don’t know how old Bella is supposed to be when Dr. Godwin suggests that Max marry her. It feels like dark humor, maybe a commentary on fixed marriages at a young age, or maybe another layer of how casually society ignores consent when power dynamics are involved.\n\nWhat’s ironic is Duncan saying he’s not the type to fall in love because he’s driven by adventure rather than commitment, yet he becomes jealous and emotionally unstable the moment he learns that someone else touched Bella. That hypocrisy exposes how men want freedom for themselves but control over women.\n\nThe production design, wardrobe, hair and makeup, and musical score are all excellent. The score shifts depending on the scene, sometimes fun and quirky, sometimes cold, haunting, or grand, mirroring Bella’s emotional development. And Mark Ruffalo is hilarious, completely unhinged and pathetic at the same time. \n\nThe film also touches on how wealthy people are hostile toward socialism, and how women often have little to no real choice when it comes to entering the sex industry.\n\nThis film is incredibly dense. It has so many things to say about society, gender, power, sexuality, and control. I’m not entirely sure if all of these readings are intentional, or if some of them are my own projections. Maybe the director wants it to be taken literally. Maybe it’s all allegory. Either way, these were the thoughts that stayed wiath me while watching it.","poster":"img/poor-things-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Northman","year":2022,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":476,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe scale is wide and imposing. The cast performs intensity, relying more on physical presence and expression than dialogue, making every interaction feel heavy and purposeful. Visually controlled and tonally severe, it asks for patience and immersion, then delivers a final act that feels raw, inevitable, and elemental rather than conventionally exciting.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThis immediately feels like Vikings because of the period and aesthetic. Parang anytime lalabas si Ragnar Lothbrok. At the same time, it also carries the power struggle and revenge DNA of Game of Thrones and House of the Dragon. Usurpers, bloodlines, and vengeance are baked into the narrative from the start.\n\nTechnically, the film shines most in its scale and movement. The siege sequences done in one continuous shot is impressive, controlled, and immersive. The traveling wide shots across mountains, coastlines, and icy plains are the highlight for me. \n\nEggers also leans into stylization. There are moments where the entire frame drops into black and white, with only a specific element retaining color. This color isolation effect feels mythic, almost like stepping into a vision or prophecy rather than reality. Some stealth and hunter-prey staging really works for me. Parang Assassin’s Creed, with Amleth surveying, observing, waiting, and striking at the perfect moment.\n\nGrabe yung brutal Viking game, Knattleikr, feels like Quidditch but stripped of fantasy and turned into pure savagery. The Christian persecution angle also echoes Silence by Scorsese, showing belief systems colliding in cruel, uncompromising ways.\n\nPerformance-wise, Alexander Skarsgård carries a feral intensity, and you can definitely hear Stellan’s voice and presence in him. Nicole Kidman delivers a chilling monologue that completely reframes the story. Sobrang fucked up nya. Sinong nanay ang gustong patayin ang anak nya? A mother wishing death on her own son, even with the context of rape, is horrifying. Her character is deeply manipulative, power hungry, and morally broken. She doesn’t seek justice, she seeks control from the shadows, and for that, she deserves Amleth’s vengeance!\n\nThe film’s intent is very clean. That said, during the first and second act, it felt “too clean” for my taste. Almost distant, like it was holding back its full cinematic potential. But the third act completely redeems that hold back.\n\nThe final siege and volcano battle is pure cinema. Parang Anakin vs Obi-Wan on Mustafar, but make it barbaric. This is where everything finally ignites. The film could honestly be shorter so the entire experience feels more compact.\n\nOverall, The Northman is controlled, brutal, and myth-driven. It may take its time getting there, but the final act justifies the journey. A clean revenge tale that waits patiently before unleashing its fury.","poster":"img/the-northman-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Nosferatu","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":198,"review":"Robert Eggers is clearly imprinting his cinematic DNA here. If there’s anyone who can convince me to watch a Dracula movie, it’s him. That said, I’m really not into gothic horror, and no matter how beautifully this film is shot, it still failed to fully capture my attention. I couldn’t feel what was truly at stake, emotionally or narratively, so the experience stayed distant for me.\n\nBill Skarsgård disappears once again into his role as Count Orlok. Almost like Heath Ledger channeling an older version of the Joker. The physicality, the presence, the way he inhabits the character, it’s impressive and unsettling.\n\nOn a lighter note, the moral of the story feels hilariously simple: wag basta pirma nang pirma without reading the fine print. 😆 A gothic horror cautionary tale about contracts gone wrong.\n\nTechnically, there’s very little to criticize. The cast is strong, the production design is detailed and immersive, the wardrobe is period-accurate, and the hair, makeup, and prosthetics work are all top-tier. Everything is crafted with intention and care.\n\nBut despite all of that, nothing really resonated with me. I can appreciate the craft, the atmosphere, and the performances, but emotionally, it just didn’t connect.","poster":"img/nosferatu-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Licorice Pizza","year":2021,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":462,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThis one eases you in with a relaxed, inviting vibe, letting scenes stretch out so people feel real. The soundtrack gives it a playful forward motion, especially during the hustle driven moments, and the side characters inject bursts of humor and chaos that keep things unpredictable. What stood out to me most is how emotionally genuine it turns out to be. What seems quirky or lightweight at first slowly reveals sincerity underneath. The central connection is awkward, shifting, and imperfect. Beneath the jokes and strange situations is a very human need for closeness, validation, and choosing someone.\n\nThis review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWarm and tender from the start. There’s an easygoing comfort to it that immediately pulls you in. The color grading feels almost identical to One Battle After Another. Very PTA with that slightly washed, nostalgic look. And of course, a lot of long takes that let scenes breathe and characters exist naturally within the frame.\n\nThere’s a bit of Boogie Nights energy in the music and vibe, especially during the waterbed selling scenes. The score choices really carry that sense of momentum.\n\nThe supporting characters add texture and humor without breaking the film’s tone. Jerry Frick is hilarious! The way he messes with English, changing intonation as if he’s translating the language itself, is funny. Then there’s Bradley Cooper, who brings pure psycho energy. Loud, chaotic, unpredictable. He completely hijacks every scene he’s in.\n\nAlana doing sales calls instantly reminded me of Jordan Belfort’s early days at the Investors Center. That raw, desperate hustle, figuring it out as you go, transitioning into a seductive whisper. It fits her character perfectly.\n\nThe scene where Alana flashes Gary, Gary asking if he can touch them, and then getting slapped. is also funny, and very telling of their dynamic. It’s playful, and confusing, which honestly sums up their entire relationship.\n\nThere’s a constant push and pull throughout the film about who’s going to get jealous next. Power shifts back and forth between them, but the chemistry is undeniable. It feels natural, unforced, and surprisingly sincere.\n\nBefore watching, I honestly thought this was going to be a childish film. But I was wrong. It’s full of heart. Beneath the humor and weirdness, there’s genuine longing, and a desire to be seen and chosen.\n\nThey keep running from each other, circling, colliding, pulling away, and reconnecting. But in the end, despite everything, they still choose each other. \n\nLicorice Pizza is about youth, confusion, impulsiveness, and connection. Messy, funny, and oddly sincere.","poster":"img/licorice-pizza-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Secret Agent","year":2025,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-24","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":176,"review":"I went into this expecting a spy movie, but it never really becomes one.  The film only starts to get interesting in the third act, when his assassins finally land in the same place where he has been hiding. That convergence should have been the payoff.\n\nBut instead, it turns anticlimactic. It reminded me of No Country for Old Men, where the protagonist’s death isn’t even shown. Here, the same problem exists, there’s no emotional release, no real closure, and no sense of earned consequence. Everything just… stops.\n\nWhat makes it more frustrating is how little weight his life seems to have left behind. His son doesn’t even remember him. That could have been devastating if explored properly, but it’s treated almost as an afterthought. Instead of feeling tragic, it feels hollow.\n\nWagner Moura is clearly underutilized. He’s far more compelling in Civil War and especially in Narcos, where his presence dominates the screen. \n\nIn the end, Secret Agent flirts with intrigue but never commits, leaving behind an experience that feels unfinished rather than intentionally ambiguous.","poster":"img/the-secret-agent-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Candyman","year":2021,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":147,"review":"Nia DaCosta shows a strong command of atmosphere. The film builds tension through sound design, silence, and carefully timed scares rather than relying purely on shock. The use of prosthetics also adds a tactile quality to the horror, grounding it in something physical and unsettling instead of feeling overly digital.\n\nThe themes are solid and meaningful, especially in how the film explores the Black experience in society, generational trauma, and how violence and fear are inherited and repeated. However, the messaging is very subtle. It doesn’t announce itself outright, which made it harder for me to fully grasp everything on first viewing. The ideas are there, but they unfold quietly, almost in the background of the horror.\n\nOverall, the craft is okay, even if the thematic weight takes time to fully register. It’s a film that feels likely to benefit from rewatching once the subtext becomes clearer.","poster":"img/candyman-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Shape of Water","year":2017,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-24","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":396,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts summary:\n\nAt its heart, this is a gentle and sincere romance built on trust and recognition rather than words. The film treats its characters with empathy, allowing love to feel patient and earned instead of idealized. Sally Hawkins is phenomenal, carrying the film with pure physical performance and emotional clarity. Guillermo del Toro blends romance, fantasy, and classic cinema love into something strange yet deeply human.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first thing that stood out to me was the production design. The sets feel tangible and lived in, from the damp corridors of the facility to Elisa’s apartment above the cinema. Everything has texture, making the world feel real despite the fantasy elements.\n\nAt its core, this is a sweet and sincere romance. The relationship between Elisa and the Amphibian Man is built on trust, patience, and recognition. They don’t see each other for what they lack, but for who they are. There’s no judgment, just understanding, and that makes the love story surprisingly grounded and emotionally honest.\n\nThere’s also a fun heist vibe when they plan and execute the breakout of the Amphibian Man. It adds tension and momentum to the story. The reveal of the creature’s healing abilities was a genuine wow moment for me, reinforcing his almost mythic, godlike presence.\n\nSally Hawkins is incredible here. She’s the heart of the film. Even without dialogue, her performance is expressive, tender, and deeply human. She’s the main reason I wanted to watch this, especially after Bring Her Back, with Guillermo del Toro being the second reason, after hearing watching Frankenstein. \n\nMichael Shannon delivers another intense performance, very much in his General Zod mode. The visual detail of his finger slowly rotting alongside his moral decay is a great piece of visual storytelling. His character literally and figuratively falls apart as the film progresses.\n\nI couldn’t help but joke about how expensive their water bill must be.\nThe final act is beautifully done. The score swells, the lighting becomes more cinematic, and everything comes together emotionally and visually. \n\nFor me, this is stronger and more effective storytelling than Frankenstein. It’s romantic, strange, and heartfelt in a way that feels sincere.","poster":"img/the-shape-of-water-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Babylon","year":2022,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-23","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":385,"review":"Nellie (Margot Robbie) and Manny’s (Diego Calva) conversation early on is immediately relatable. All I want is to go to a movie theater. That simple desire, loving movies as an escape, sets the emotional core of the film. It captures why people are drawn to cinema in the first place, not just for fame or money, but for belonging inside something bigger than themselves.\n\nRight away, the film explodes with energy. The party jazz music is infectious, chaotic, and intoxicating. The long uninterrupted takes amid absolute madness feel very PTA-inspired, letting the camera swim through bodies, and noise instead of cutting away from it. Technically, the film is stunning from the start. The lighting is immediately gorgeous, with masterful use of color that recalls Whiplash and even made me want to rewatch La La Land. Everything feels alive, sweaty, and electric.\n\nThe scale of Babylon is insane. The musical score is epic and grand, constantly pushing the film forward with momentum. It’s funny as hell too, leaning into absurdity without shame. This movie is pure visual popcorn and eargasmic overload. The sunset over the hill scene is an outright cinematic masterpiece!\n\nOne of the funniest and smartest sequences is the first trial on the soundstage. The transition from silent film freedom to sound era rigidity is portrayed through pure frustration. The audio man losing his mind over noise is hilarious but also telling. You feel how technology reshapes art overnight, turning creativity into stress, rules, and technical limitations.\n\nAs the film progresses, the tone shifts dramatically. What starts as bright, wild, and euphoric slowly becomes darker and more reflective. The drama intensifies, tension rises, and the color palette becomes more muted. It feels like a transition from Paul Thomas Anderson style chaos to Quentin Tarantino style brutality. The party ends, and consequences start piling up.\n\nAt its core, Babylon is about how money and fame don’t change who you are, they only magnify you. The film industry can make you or break you. By the end, Manny realizes that every beautiful and ugly thing he experienced was part of something larger (which he wished to be part of).\n\nBabylon is loud, sensory overload, indulgent, and unapologetic. It’s a love letter and a warning, a celebration and a breakdown, all rolled into one overwhelming, unforgettable experience.","poster":"img/babylon-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Minari","year":2020,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-22","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":477,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nStrong, natural performances make the family feel real from the start. The film centers on the tension between building a future and keeping a family intact, and while the intentions are sincere, some character dynamics create emotional distance. Visually striking moments, especially near the end, are powerful, but the emotional payoff feels restrained. The message about resilience is clear, even if it doesn’t fully land for me.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nEarlier pa lang, pinamalas na agad nila ang acting skills nila. Ramdam mo kaagad yung naturalism at sincerity ng performances. The family feels warm, parang totoong pamilya talaga sa totoong buhay.\n\nThe tone gets lighter whenever the lola is on screen. Ang kulit niya, very much an enabler. Tawang-tawa ako dun sa scene na pinainom ni David yung ihi niya sa lola niya. But as the film goes on, it becomes clearer how carefree and negligent she is, and in hindsight, parang foreshadowing na rin siya of what will eventually happen later in the film.\n\nAs the story progresses, mas nagiging apparent yung central conflict: success or family. That’s what Monica wants Jacob to choose between. But for Jacob, all he wants is to give a better life for his family by building something of his own, by doing business, and not staying employed forever. I honestly don’t know where Monica is coming from. Kung bakit parang kating kati siyang bumalik sa California even when the farm is already showing signs of a breakthrough. It feels ungrateful to me. Ganun siguro talaga minsan, as a husband you give and give, pero misunderstood ka pa rin.\n\nJacob works hard for his family and is very considerate, to the point that he even allows his mother-in-law to live with them. Which, by the way, becomes the cause of the fire. Personally, I wouldn’t allow that. Not in a million years. Naging alagain na nga, naging pahamak pa.\nThe cinematography during the fire scene was great. Super bright, overwhelming, and emotionally charged. I’m not sure if it was done practically or enhanced, but it felt believable and grounded. \n\nI understand that the film’s intention is good. It’s about growth, survival, and starting again. But for me, that lesson got overshadowed by my frustration with the mother-in-law’s role in the story. Because of that, the ending didn’t land as hard as it probably should have. Sayang yung character work. For me, there wasn’t enough payoff. I’m also baffled by how calm Jacob was after the incident. All his hard labor literally went up in flames. I expected more anger, more emotional release. Instead, it felt muted, and that choice didn’t fully connect with me.","poster":"img/minari-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Past Lives","year":2023,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":319,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nA quiet, intimate film that leans heavily on silence, pauses, and unspoken emotion. Conversations feel mature and deeply human, especially in how reassurance, insecurity, and acceptance are handled. Gently paced, beautifully shot, and emotionally honest. Past Lives will hurt you in the most beautiful way.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nWarm start. It immediately feels like a K-drama, soft and intimate, quietly pulling you in. There’s a sense of familiarity and comfort.\n\nThere is still clearly an attraction between Nora and Hae Sung, even if Nora is already married. It’s not loud or explicit, but it lingers. You feel it in the pauses, in the way they look at each other, and in what they choose not to say. The silence, the stares, the stillness. They all carry an invisible weight. There are so many unsaid feelings and unspoken regrets. \n\nI really loved Nora and her husband Arthur’s long bed conversation. Arthur opening up about his insecurities and Nora reassuring him that they are the ones meant for each other. Hindi lang babae ang may kailangan ng reassurance. Even men need it too. That scene alone adds so much emotional maturity to the film.\n\nThe conversation between Nora and Hae Sung, on the other hand, is painful. It’s all about the one who got away and the weight of all the “what ifs.” Just a quiet acceptance mixed with regret. \n\nThe score is also nice throughout the film, subtle but emotionally effective. Beautifully shot as well, especially during the most intense moments where the camera just lets the actors exist in the frame without forcing anything.\n\nThis film hurts you gently, patiently, and honestly. Past Lives will hurt you in the most beautiful way.","poster":"img/past-lives-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Contagion","year":2011,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-22","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":155,"review":"No need for spoiler warning here. This feels like 2020 done in 2011. Simple as that.\n\nWatching Contagion now is so prophetic. What was once meant to terrify now feels very familiar. Since we’ve already lived through COVID-19, the fear doesn’t hit the same way, but it replaces shock with recognition. I honestly can’t imagine how terrifying this must have been to watch pre-pandemic.\n\nThe paranoia is very real. What stood out most is how the film now feels less like fiction and more like a documentary. It also shows the government trying to keep up while people panic, distrust authority, and look for something or someone to blame.\n\nJude Law’s character is effective. Galing ng pagkakakupal niya. At first, his exposure of Dr. Ellis’s hypocrisy feels justified, even necessary. But he goes too far. He becomes a fake news peddler. He represents how fear, distrust, and misinformation spread just as fast as any virus.","poster":"img/contagion-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Django Unchained","year":2012,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":615,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nFrom the opening moments, Quentin Tarantino’s cinema confidence is undeniable. The imagery is powerful, the landscapes are beautifully shot, and the lighting and color palette give the film a mythic, almost legendary tone. The cinematography is consistently stunning, from wide travel shots to textured flashbacks that genuinely feel like memories. Violence is stylized and theatrical rather than disturbing, leaning heavily on practical effects and exaggerated spectacle. Django’s transformation is deeply satisfying to watch, supported by a heroic score and a strong mentor dynamic that grounds the story. It’s epic, funny, visually rich, and confidently crafted. A full cinematic experience that deserves the big screen.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nSimula pa lang, ramdam mo na agad yung cinema skills ni Quentin Tarantino. Grabe yung power ng imagery. The landscape cinematography is breathtaking, with magnificent lighting and a rich, deliberate color palette that instantly sets a mythic tone. On top of that is the exquisite theme song about Django himself, parang epic folk legend ang peg. In contrast, yung song for Dr. King is intentionally playful and funny. Every time it plays, napapangiti talaga ako. \n\nAng ganda ng cinematography during their travels. I can’t stop fanboying over how beautifully this film was shot. The lens choices are extremely cinematic, especially in wide shots and slow, composed movements. Even the flashbacks have a distinct visual style, slightly hazy and textured, ramdam talagang memories siya.\n\nTarantino’s use of violence is once again very deliberate. The gore is cinematic rather than disturbing. Practical effects and prosthetics dominate, making everything feel tactile and real. It’s not violence for shock value. It’s exaggerated, and theatrical. Blood rains down, splinters fly, and sound effects are amplified in a way that turns brutality into spectacle. This is Tarantino showing violence as performance, not realism.\n\nCharacter-wise, Django’s arc is incredibly satisfying. His firearms training sequence got me clapping. You really feel the transformation from enslaved man to confident gunslinger. The score here leans fully into heroic territory, parang Siegfried about to rescue Brünnhilde. Mythic yung dating. Dr. King’s calm demeanor balances Django perfectly. He’s witty, controlled, and composed, acting as both mentor and narrative anchor.\n\nThere are also plenty of comedic beats. Yung confusion ng female slave kung paano niya itatrato si Django as a free man is funny. And that infamous raid scene where the white folks are arguing about the bag covering their faces. Sobrang angas na sana parang Lord of the Rings when the army is running up the hill, pero ang tatanga nila.\n\nThe ensemble cast is phenomenal. Leonardo DiCaprio and Samuel L. Jackson bring explosive energy to the film. Leo’s performance is volatile and unsettling, while Samuel L. Jackson slowly pieces together the rescue plot in the dinner scene. That sequence is peak Tarantino tension. You don’t know if a gunfight will erupt or not. The ticking clock in the background just makes everything worse. Tarantino is clearly toying with the audience’s nerves.\n\nThen comes the signature Tarantino bloodbath. Smoke, gunfire, splintering wood, blood raining down, and Django emerging from the chaos like a legend. The sound design sells every shot. The violence is excessive, but a lot of fun!\n\nBy the end, the payoff is incredibly satisfying. Beautifully shot, masterfully lit, and emotionally complete. I honestly wish there’s a theatrical re-release of this film because it deserves to be experienced on the big screen.\n\nI’m glad I rewatched this. From a 4 star memory to a solid 5 star experience now. Absolute cinema!","poster":"img/django-unchained-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Hateful Eight","year":2015,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":276,"review":"Binalikan ko ulit ito to give it another chance after rewatching Django Unchained, hoping na magugustuhan ko na siya this time. Unfortunately, ganun pa rin ang feeling ko.\n\nHindi talaga ako fan ng one location setup nito. Nasa isang lugar lang sila, puro dialogue, and for long stretches parang walang nangyayari. Hindi rin ako particularly invested sa mga characters, at honestly hindi rin ako interesado sa saloon vibes. They’re trapped inside, just bickering with each other, and even the accents somehow made it harder for me to connect. Siguro combination ng setting, pacing, at character dynamics kaya hindi nagwowork sa akin.\n\nYes, Tarantino’s theatrical violence is still present. But this time, it feels more like a reunion film where he just crammed as many actors he previously worked with into one room. Parang less about pushing cinema forward and more about indulging himself.\n\nI still found myself dozing off. Hindi ko talaga ramdam yung tension, which is surprising given how dialogue driven Tarantino usually excels at suspense. Dito, for me, it just dragged.\n\nPambawi na lang yung nicely shot landscapes. Those wide snowy exteriors are beautiful and cinematic. The film also uses a lot of what looks like deep focus or split focus shots, where both foreground and background subjects remain sharp at the same time. That technical choice adds visual depth, pero hindi siya enough to save my engagement with the story.\n\nAt the end of the day, violence is still the strongest point of this film. When it hits, it hits. Pero outside of that, The Hateful Eight still doesn’t work for me. It feels indulgent, slow, and emotionally distant, despite its technical strengths.","poster":"img/the-hateful-eight-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Grand Budapest Hotel","year":2014,"rating":1.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-21","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":307,"review":"Maganda ang visuals. The symmetry and color palette are very deliberate, almost storybook-like. Yung blacks are not pure black but more greyish or muted, parang may faded pastel wash over the whole image. This makes everything feel flat in a controlled way, removing harsh contrast so nothing ever feels truly dark or threatening. It creates a nostalgic, illustrated look rather than a cinematic one grounded in realism.\n\nEverything is always centered. Frames are locked, balanced, and composed like dioramas. The use of miniatures is also very prominent, serving as a visual language that reinforces the artificial, handcrafted world. While I get the intention behind it, it’s not really my cup of tea. For me, it feels too simplistic. I don’t feel the depth of a cinematic experience because the style keeps reminding me that I’m watching something carefully constructed rather than something I can emotionally sink into.\n\nClean dolly shots, static shots, push-ins, pull-outs. Lahat malinis, precise, and controlled. Walang mess, walang chaos. That level of stability and perfection actually makes me sleepy. There’s no visual tension. It’s technically impressive but emotionally distant.\n\nThere are comedic beats scattered throughout, and they work in a dry, quirky way. Ang ganda rin ng close-up shot ni Agatha. \n\nOverall, it’s quirky and fun, but I’m just not the right audience for it. Even if I like OCD things and controlled aesthetics, my mind keeps drifting to how the style feels constrained, almost like a low-budget approach or something from a pre–technological advancement era of filmmaking. Not because of lack of skill, but because of intentional limitation as an aesthetic choice.\n\nThis is my second viewing, hoping I’d feel something different this time around. Unfortunately, the same feeling came back: boredom. I respect the craft, the discipline, and the identity, but it still doesn’t connect with me on a deeper level.","poster":"img/the-grand-budapest-hotel-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Rush","year":2013,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-21","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":372,"review":"Daniel Brühl’s Austrian accent sounds weird to my ears, medyo distracting. But visually, the film immediately establishes a stylized approach. The pre-mental race visualization looks like memory fragments, almost nostalgic, supported by a highly stylized color grading that leans heavily into orange and teal. Because of that, the film sometimes feels closer to a Michael Bay aesthetic in terms of color and lighting rather than a typical Ron Howard movie.\n\nThe racing itself didn’t feel as immersive as Joseph Kosinski’s F1. The shots are too shaky and the editing is too fast. Hindi pa nagsesettle yung visuals, nagcu-cut na agad sa next frame. Because of that, the film fails to fully place the audience inside the cockpit. There’s also very little sense of strategic racing. Walang malinaw na stakes during the races themselves, and it often feels like pure dramatization of ego rather than competition built on tactics, precision, and decision making.\n\nInstead, the film focuses more on their personal lives. This is less about the race and more about how Niki and James live, love, and self-destruct outside the track. If this were my first introduction to F1, I don’t think it would make me curious enough to explore the sport further.\n\nSa second act, doon ko mas naramdaman si Ron Howard. The direction becomes more creative, especially emotionally. The hospital scene was especially hard to watch. Nadiri talaga ako when they had to vacuum Niki's lungs. Napapikit ako because it felt painful and invasive, parang lobotomy but through the mouth instead of the nose. This is where I really appreciated Daniel Brühl’s acting. His physical suffering and sold the gravity of what Niki was going through.\n\nThe last race is the most cinematic sequence in the film. Heavy rain pouring down, strong atmosphere, and a powerful epic score. Every corner feels dangerous, and for the first time, the tension really works. Niki’s decision to quit the race shows real growth. He chooses survival and his wife over ego. In contrast, James still wants to push through no matter the cost.\n\nThe third act works better than the first two despite the earlier slack, but overall, it still doesn’t reach the breathless, explosive, and celebratory payoff that Joseph Kosinski’s F1 delivered.","poster":"img/rush-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Killing of a Sacred Deer","year":2017,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","a24"],"words":173,"review":"The score is genuinely bothersome, and for me, it is the highlight of the film. It creates constant unease and tension.\n\nThe cast is great, no question there. But despite the strong performances, I didn’t really get the point of the film. There was no solid payoff for me, especially when I compare it to Bugonia, where there is at least a clearer thematic punch. Here, I kept waiting for that “aha!” moment where everything would suddenly click, but it never came.\n\nBy the end, I was still unsure whether the film was meant to be taken literally or metaphorically. Is it a straight-up curse narrative, or is it about guilt, punishment, moral balance, and consequence dressed up as something supernatural? The film never fully commits to clarifying that.\n\nI understand that ambiguity is part of the design, but for me, it felt more frustrating than thought-provoking. Instead of feeling disturbed in a meaningful way, I was left confused and detached, unsure what emotional or philosophical conclusion I was supposed to arrive at.","poster":"img/the-killing-of-a-sacred-deer-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Inherent Vice","year":2014,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":114,"review":"I wasn’t really engaged or invested in the film. I’m not sure if it’s the premise, or maybe I’m just not a fan of hippie movies. Or baka di lang talaga ako sanay sa investigative-type films, especially with PTA at the helm doing something this loose and hazy.\n\nAside from the color palette and the long continuous shots, I honestly didn’t feel much of PTA’s usual DNA here. Wala yung emotional grip or character intensity na nararamdaman ko sa ibang films niya. The story didn’t pull me in. Instead of feeling intrigued, I mostly felt detached.\n\nThe investigative angle didn’t hook me either. I never reached that moment where things click or become satisfying.","poster":"img/inherent-vice-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Enemy","year":2013,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":336,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe film immediately establishes an unsettling mood through its oppressive yellow color grading. It unfolds as a slow burn psychological mystery, prioritizing atmosphere over plot momentum. It’s the kind of film that lingers more as a puzzle than a narrative, designed to confuse, provoke, and invite interpretation rather than provide clarity on first viewing.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first thing I noticed is the color grading. Every frame is yellowish, almost golden. This aesthetic immediately reminded me of the Wallace Corporation look in Blade Runner 2049, that oppressive yellow tone that makes everything feel sickly, dry, and unsettling. It already tells you that this world is not stable.\n\nI’m getting strong The Prestige vibes here. There’s an alternate storytelling approach meant to confuse the audience, constantly making you question which version is real and which one isn’t. The film plays with identity and perception.\n\nIt’s a slow burn, and even after an hour, it still moves at the same uneasy, restrained pace. There’s tension, but it’s buried under silence and repetition rather than overt drama.\n\nAnd honestly, you find out you have a doppelgänger and the first thing you do is swap wives? What a horny movie. That decision alone made everything feel more disturbing than clever, like lust and ego are driving the chaos.\n\nAfter reading Reddit theories, my mind honestly melted. Ang daming interpretations, symbolism, and layers that I clearly missed on first watch. This feels like one of those films you have to rewatch to form your own solid opinion, because a single viewing isn’t enough to fully process what Denis Villeneuve is trying to say. Para syang modern day Fight Club. \n\nRight now, I’m still confused, and not fully convinced but intrigued enough to admit that this film might hit differently on a second watch.","poster":"img/enemy-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Lighthouse","year":2019,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":479,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nThe film immediately stands out through its immersive sound and production design, where the environment feels alive and oppressive rather than just background. Rain, wind, and sea noise are used as constant sensory pressure, trapping both the characters and the audience. Visually, the black and white cinematography is rich and expressive, with striking control over light and shadow that gives every frame texture and weight. The score functions less as music and more as psychological tension, slowly tightening unease. Performances are intense and theatrical, with both leads fully inhabiting a harsh, mythic world that feels decaying, claustrophobic, and punishing. This is not an easy watch, but a deliberate sensory experience that prioritizes atmosphere, madness, and endurance over comfort.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nSound design and set design ang unang nakaagaw ng aking atensyon. The rain, the wind howling, the splash against the stones, the foghorn, mist sprays, waves splashing. Lahat ng yan ramdam, hindi lang siya background noise, parang character mismo yung environment, constantly assaulting the senses and trapping both the characters and the audience in this isolated place.\n\nLighting is also great even if this is a black and white film. The cinematography makes it alive and vibrant. Ang galing laruin yung highlights and shadows. This is cinematic black and white. Rich, textured, and expressive. Unlike the flat and boring look of C’mon C’mon (also by A24).\n\nThe musical score grips my attention. There’s something insidious about it. It works more as psychological pressure than melody, slowly crawling under your skin and amplifying the descent into madness.\n\nThe camera positioning feels like a mix of old school and modern filmmaking. Old school in the sense that it uses static, centered compositions, tight framing, and restrictive aspect ratio that feels theatrical and claustrophobic. But modern in how it weaponizes proximity. Extreme close-ups, distorted perspectives, and uncomfortable framing that pushes the audience into the characters’ personal space. It feels deliberate, controlled, and punishing.\n\nSet design feels real and tactile. Everything looks heavy, damp, and decaying. \n\nAng galing din ng accent nila, parang old English, reinforcing the mythic and folkloric tone of the story.\n\nMustached Robert Pattinson gives me strong Daniel Day-Lewis as Daniel Plainview vibes from There Will Be Blood, especially in the third act nung nagwawala na siya. That same raw desperation, ego collapse, and unhinged physicality. You can feel him unraveling mentally.\n\nWillem Dafoe is equally intense. His performance is theatrical, and unrelenting. Parang siya yung embodiment ng the sea, tradition, and madness all rolled into one. Every monologue feels like a curse.\n\nOverall, The Lighthouse is a sensory and psychological assault. Not just a film you watch, but something you endure.","poster":"img/the-lighthouse-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Favourite","year":2018,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":411,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nThe Favourite is a visually striking period film that uses exaggerated wide angle lenses, crisp focus, and distorted framing to create an intentionally unsettling and quirky tone. The grand sets and costumes sell a world defined by excess, hierarchy, and quiet cruelty, where scale itself becomes a form of power. A repetitive, minimalist musical score builds tension through persistence rather than melody, adding to the film’s cold, calculated atmosphere. Beneath the elegance and wit lies a sharp examination of power, desire, and survival, where control is negotiated through manipulation rather than merit. \n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nMaganda ang sets and costumes. Parang nasa lumang era talaga. The director clearly leaned into wide angle lenses, some even feeling fish-eye or GoPro-like in terms of sharpness. Wala masyadong depth of field, everything feels overly crisp and slightly distorted, which adds to the unsettling and quirky tone of the film. This is paired with a quirky musical score, katulad ni PTA, na gumagamit ng parang 2–3 notes lang and then pinapatagal for more than three minutes, creating tension through repetition rather than melody.\n\nThe sets are truly grand. Big, elaborate rooms, hallways, and props that make the world feel heavy with power and excess. I’m not entirely sure how much CGI extensions were used, but everything feels like a real location. The scale sells the authority of the characters and how small people can feel inside these massive spaces.\n\nAt its core, this film is a prime example of how women use sex to gain and control power. It also shows how people in power can be easily swayed when desire is involved, even when the decisions at stake affect serious matters like taxes and high positions in government. Power here is not earned through merit but negotiated through intimacy, manipulation, and survival.\n\nBut no matter how powerful or controlled Abigail becomes, she is always beneath the Queen. She thought it would be different, that reaching the top would finally give her freedom, but in the end she is still a servant, just operating on a different level. \n\nThe Favourite feels calculated, cold, and cruel in its own playful way. Behind the beauty, and wit is a sharp reminder that power dynamics rarely change, they only change hands.","poster":"img/the-favourite-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Joy","year":2015,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-20","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":543,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\nThis is a grounded startup story that takes its time building empathy for an underdog boxed in by financial pressure, family dynamics, and self doubt. The film focuses less on cinematic flair and more on process: rejection, persistence, and the uncomfortable stretch between idea and execution. It captures how starting something from nothing is exhausting, repetitive, and often humiliating before it ever looks successful. What stands out is how honest it feels about risk taking, selling, and resilience, without turning the journey into instant inspiration porn.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nThe first act was slow, mainly because the film is carefully establishing her struggles so the audience can genuinely root for the underdog. It takes its time showing how boxed in she is, emotionally and financially.\n\nNatuwa ako nung nagmamanufacture na sila. You can feel the shift from idea to execution, from dreaming to actually risking something tangible.\n\nGanda ng scenes about rejection. Paulit ulit siyang tinatanggihan, but instead of glorifying instant success, the film sits with the discomfort and humiliation that comes with trying to sell something no one believes in yet.\n\nThen ito nanaman tayo sa mortgage ng house, parang sa Tetris at The Founder. That familiar startup trope where personal security is sacrificed for a shot at something bigger. It’s reckless, but also very real.\n\nGrabe yung weight nung hindi nabenta yung 50k units nya. Napa “legit ba to?” ako. Ang akala ko nananaginip lang sya. And the fact that she went back to the studio to do the selling herself shows pure relentlessness. No ego, just survival mode. I think I would do the same if my back is against the wall. Yung TV selling live on air feels like an old version of what we now know as TikTok live selling. Same pressure, same energy, just without the algorithms. You have to call instead of checking out your yellow basket. \n\nI love her best friend. She is so supportive, parang wingman talaga. Not just emotional support, but someone who keeps believing when the Joy is already doubting herself. Nice to see Dascha Polanco again from Orange is the New Black. \n\nSad yung namatay yung lola niya. Siya talaga yung tunay na naniwala sa potential ni Joy, even when Joy couldn’t see it herself.  About her sister naman, ang hirap talaga pag nangengeelam ang kapamilya sa negosyo. It blurs boundaries and adds emotional weight to already difficult decisions.\n\nAng tindi ng drama nung bankruptcy scene. Her world, her identity, everything she attached her self worth to comes crashing down all at once. Maganda rin yung naging resolve niya with the patent issue mixed with embezzlement.\n\nThe film quietly lays out business lessons without being preachy: risk taking, kapal ng mukha to sell, toughness in negotiations, don’t trust anyone blindly, and the importance of hiring a patent lawyer to protect what you built.\n\nOverall, clean execution. Not very cinematic, but a very real portrayal of business startups. It shows that success isn’t glamorous, it’s exhausting, humiliating, and often lonely before it becomes anything else.","poster":"img/joy-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Pulp Fiction","year":1994,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":343,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nPulp Fiction leans heavily into dialogue driven storytelling, prioritizing long conversations and character interactions over traditional action or spectacle. Quentin Tarantino focuses on rhythm, timing, and tone, letting scenes breathe longer than expected and daring the audience to either settle into the flow or resist it. Performances are a major draw, especially in how casually characters discuss heavy ideas like morality, chance, and consequence. The film becomes more rewarding on repeat viewings, where knowing the structure allows the humor and pacing to land more clearly. While its influence and writing are undeniable, its emotional impact can still feel distant depending on the viewer, making it more intellectually engaging than deeply affecting.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nUma Thurman’s face feels very different here. Maybe because of her younger age or the HMUA. Wala pa yung intensity na meron siya sa Kill Bill. Hindi pa siya “hot” dito in that aggressive, commanding way, but still gorgeous nonetheless. She just aged like fine wine.\n\nThis film really dwells on the Tarantino side that focuses more on dialogue than action. It’s less about spectacle and more about conversations that go on longer than expected, testing your patience or pulling you in depending on your mood.\n\nMas naenjoy ko ito the second time around kesa first time. Knowing where things are headed helped me appreciate the rhythm and the humor more.\n\nNakakatawa yung chaotic conversation nila sa bahay ni Lance habang O.D.ing si Mia. The panic mixed with absurd casual dialogue is peak Tarantino. Life and death are happening at the same time as stupid arguments, and somehow it works.\n\nIt has nice dialogues with thought provoking perspectives, especially about morality, chance, and consequences. Pero hanggang ngayon, hindi ko pa rin fully makita kung bakit ito umabot sa cult following level. I get why it’s influential and well written, but emotionally, it still doesn’t hit me as hard as it does for others.","poster":"img/pulp-fiction-1994.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Rocky","year":1976,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":411,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts:\n\nRocky feels less like a boxing movie and more like a character study about a guy who has spent his whole life settling. Rocky walks around with confidence and bravado, but underneath that is someone afraid to want more than survival. The pacing is slow and patient. Stallone plays Rocky awkward and stiff. What really anchors the film is the quiet emotional support around him, giving the story its heart. By the end, you root for him not because he’s the best, but because he finally stops playing it safe.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nRocky has that big dick swagger energy, like someone who thinks he owns the room even when he clearly doesn’t. But beneath that bravado is a guy who has been playing it safe his whole life, settling for small sure win fights, small paychecks, and low expectations.\n\nThe lack of a constant musical score makes the film feel dry for me. It’s harder to latch onto the emotion when the score only comes in during jogging or training sequences. Compared to films that use music to guide momentum, this approach feels restrained, almost too grounded. When the music does arrive, especially near the end, it finally delivers the emotional lift the film has been building toward.\n\nI personally prefer Sylvester Stallone as John Rambo. Less dialogue, more physical storytelling. Here, his acting feels stiff and dry at times, and the accent doesn’t help sell the emotional weight for me. Raging Bull, despite being heavier and more brutal, actually feels more kinetic and intense in comparison.\n\nAdrian, though, is a key emotional anchor. She’s good looking, yes, but more importantly, she helped him a lot to believe in himself and stop playing it safe. She gives Rocky something he never had before: quiet validation and permission to want more than survival. That belief is what pushes him to step into the ring not just to fight, but to prove something to himself.\n\nThe groundwork for Rocky’s character is long, maybe too long for my attention span, but it does serve the payoff. By the final act, with the music finally swelling and the underdog narrative fully locked in, the film earns its emotional resolution. I end up rooting for Rocky not because he’s the best fighter, but because he finally chose to stop settling.","poster":"img/rocky-1976.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Miami Vice","year":2006,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":381,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nMiami Vice feels like Michael Mann operating at a much larger scale, with more locations, moving parts, and glossy production value than his earlier work. The film leans heavily into luxury, speed, and scope, which makes it feel more like a modern big budget production than a tightly controlled cinematic experience. While the story explores blurred identities and emotional entanglement within undercover work, the focus often drifts away from procedural tension and into romance. Compared to Mann’s sharper, more restrained films, the usual slow burn dread feels diluted. It looks sleek and expensive, but it lacks the raw, disciplined tension that once defined his strongest work.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nThis is Michael Mann showing a bigger scale. More cast, more locations, more logistics, luxury cars, jets, speed boats. And I think because of that, this feels like a movie instead of a film. It feels like a modern big budget production rather than a cinematic experience. Naalala ko tuloy kung bakit hindi ko rin masyadong nagustuhan Blackhat. Parang ganito din yung itsura nun.\n\nIto yung klase ng undercover movie na hindi mo na alam kung undercover pa rin ba sila or totoo na yung nararamdaman nila. Everything gets blurred, identities overlap, may double crossing, and the emotional stakes start to overpower the operational ones.\n\nNice to see Elizabeth Rodriguez so confident and fierce here, especially compared to her insecure mother role in Orange Is the New Black. It really shows during the rescue of Trudy. That sequence gave her character presence and strength, even if the film itself felt less focused than classic Mann.\n\nThe typical Michael Mann tension wasn’t as apparent here, especially in the third act. Normally, Mann uses silence, restraint, and space to build dread. But here, during the shootout, a rock song was playing. Wtf? It killed the tension for me. If I’m being bold, I’d say the film focuses too much on the romantic relationship between the characters instead of fully committing to the procedural intensity.\n\nOverall, it looks expensive and sleek, but it lacks the raw, controlled tension that made Michael Mann’s earlier films feel sharper and more immersive.","poster":"img/miami-vice-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Rip","year":2026,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":416,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nThe Rip is a gritty crime drama that leans heavily on atmosphere, and moral tension. The visual execution is one of its strongest assets, with effective color grading, fog, smoke, and silhouette heavy compositions that give the film weight and menace. At its core, the story explores desperation rather than pure greed, placing characters in morally compromising situations that blur the line between right and wrong. The film constantly nudges the audience to question where the real threat lies, whether in the system, the people involved, or internal choices.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nAll I could think about while watching was why didn’t they bring a money counter? If they were going to steal the cash anyway, why not just avoid counting it altogether? And why the hell were they walking casually in an open street without using cover? Some decisions really pulled me out of the realism. And honestly, Matt can’t be the bad guy, it just doesn’t sit right.\n\nThat said, the visual execution is strong. The color grading works really well, with lighting accented by fog and smoke that adds texture and atmosphere. It gives the film a gritty, heavy feel that suits the moral tension of the story.\n\nThe silhouette shots are especially effective. That moment where Ben Affleck casts a massive shadow toward Kyle Chandler is a powerful image. Menacing, dominant, and very intentional. A clear power shot that visually establishes threat and imbalance without dialogue.\n\nAt its core, the story is about underpaid cops being presented with a temptation that could instantly make their lives easier. It’s not framed as outright greed, but as desperation slowly turning into compromise. You’re never fully sure who the real enemy is, whether it’s the system, the people around them, or themselves.\n\nOne of the standout sequences is when they’re inside the armored vehicle, with lights passing on and off through the interior. It immediately reminded me of Heat by Michael Mann, especially the flickering runway lights at the airstrip. That same feeling of anticipation, containment, and impending consequence.\n\nThe twist lands hard. This is one of those films where you think you already understand the situation, only to realize it goes much deeper. The layers unfold quietly, and by the time everything clicks, you’re already locked in.","poster":"img/the-rip-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jay Kelly","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":466,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nJay Kelly is a quiet, reflective character study about success, isolation, and the cost of living at a level far removed from ordinary life. The film strips away glamour to examine how excess can coexist with deep loneliness, and how achievement does not guarantee connection or fulfillment. Visually, the film is elegant, with lighting, color, and lens choices that mirror the characters’ internal states. It sits with regret, and the fear of realizing too late what truly matters.\n\n⚠️ Spoilers ahead. From here on, plot details are discussed. Read on if you’ve seen the film. If not, save this post and come back after. ⚠️\n\nJay Kelly played by George Clooney, is a star who wants to feel normal again. He has everything in excess and lives in a different reality, yet he is deeply alone, without real connection. He wants to self-destruct, not just out of recklessness, but as a way to regain control of his life and reconnect with what actually matters to him, his daughters and his father. There is a sense that destruction is the only way he knows how to feel something real or normal again.\n\nThe film quietly reminds us that what looks glamorous on the surface is often anything but. There is a price to pay for a sunlit life, and that price is presence, and time with family. Once that realization hits, it often comes with regret, and the painful truth that there may be no do-over. That realization gives the film its emotional weight.\n\nThis theme hits close to me. The fear of becoming too successful and losing sight of why you are doing things, or for whom you are doing them, runs through the story. That’s why sometimes I feel like I’m delaying my success, even if I could pursue it, because of this fear of not being able to handle and balance everything without sacrificing one part of my life. \n\nRon, played by Adam Sandler, mirrors Jay in a quieter way. He is also a family man struggling to balance work and responsibility. In Ron’s eyes, he and Jay are the same. Their relationship feels transactional. If one goes down, the other follows. Friendship here is less about loyalty and more about mutual dependence.\n\nVisually, the film is beautifully shot. The color grading, lighting, and lens choices support the emotional tone. Everything feels controlled and reflective, matching the internal states of the characters. The technical execution reinforces the idea that this about inner conflict. I see you now Linus Sandgren! Excited for his work in Dune Part 3. \n\nJay Kelly ultimately feels like a meditation on success, regret, and the fear of losing what truly matters while chasing what looks like everything.","poster":"img/jay-kelly-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Collateral","year":2004,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-18","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":430,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nCollateral is a thriller that slowly shifts from calm to menace without ever fully releasing its grip. What begins with a deceptively gentle rhythm uses music, lighting, and movement to lull you into a false sense of safety before tightening the tension scene by scene. Performances are a major strength, especially the unsettling contrast between quiet charm and predatory intent. The film sustains pressure through controlled pacing, urban chaos, and morally charged dialogue, turning an ordinary night into something increasingly dangerous. It is gripping, stylish, and relentless, a story that thrives on atmosphere and escalating dread rather than spectacle alone.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nJamie Foxx playing another character named Max? Sounds like a disaster! \n\nThe piano score during the taxi rides feels almost like an elevator ride at first. Calm, serene, and non threatening. But that calmness is only for a brief moment before everything slowly turns dark.\n\nHow often do you get to see Tom Cruise play a silver haired bad guy? And he looks cool doing it. Of course, it wouldn’t be a Tom Cruise movie without him running, but here that physicality feels more predatory than heroic.\n\nJavier Bardem looks different every time I see him. In this film, he’s calm, composed, and intimidating as a drug lord. Short appearance but effective presence.\n\nThe tension is sustained almost all throughout the film. It’s a gripping story that never fully lets you relax. The color, movement, and chaos inside the Fever club immediately reminded me of John Wick 2. Neon lights, bodies moving, danger everywhere.\n\nTom Cruise’s Vincent constantly denies accountability for his murders. His logic is cold and terrifying. People die every day. Everything and everyone is insignificant. Shit happens. \n\nI have to give credit to Jamie Foxx’s Max. He’s not just a passive hostage. He’s constantly trying to stop Vincent from committing more crimes, even when fear clearly has him by the throat. That moral resistance gives the film its backbone.\n\nI predicted that Fanning, played by Mark Ruffalo, would get shot. That felt inevitable. What I didn’t expect was the twist that Annie (Jada Pinkett Smith) would end up being Vincent’s final target of the night. That reveal raised the stakes for Max.\n\nThe final sequence is pure Michael Mann masterclass. The cat and mouse action is clean, tense, and beautifully staged. The ending is very satisfying. A powerful conclusion to a night that spiraled completely out of control.","poster":"img/collateral-2004.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Challengers","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-18","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":495,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nChallengers is less a traditional sports film and more an intense psychological relationship drama that uses tennis as its battlefield. The film is driven by obsession, control, and identity, with competition bleeding into intimacy and vice versa. Zendaya delivers her most mature performance to date, projecting confidence and intimidation. The energy between the central trio is volatile and uncomfortable, turning ambition and desire into something combative. While the non linear structure and aggressive electronic score may feel intrusive at times, the film commits fully to emotional chaos over clean sports spectacle. What begins as competition gradually reveals itself as something far messier, where winning, love, and self worth become inseparable.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nNapapikit talaga ako dun sa injury. Grabe yung visual at sound effect, ramdam mo yung sakit in an instant. \n\nI feel like Tashi is genuinely passionate about tennis and can’t stop talking about it. To the point that she starts to feel invasive and bossy. Tennis isn’t just a sport for her, it’s her entire identity, and she projects that intensity onto everyone around her.\n\nThis is the most mature version of Zendaya I’ve seen. Very controlled, confident, and intimidating without needing to overact.\n\nThe non linear storytelling is quite distracting for me. Instead of adding tension, it sometimes pulls me out of the moment. The musical score also doesn’t always work. The electronic music keeps playing even during dialogue heavy or intimate scenes, and it often feels out of place, almost overpowering the emotions instead of supporting them.\n\nArt just wants to win Tashi’s approval every single time. Meanwhile, Tashi only really cares about winning tennis itself. Patrick, on the other hand, wants to win against Art both on the court and emotionally, by reclaiming Tashi’s affection. The love triangle is completely messed up. While Tashi and Patrick are out there cheating, Art is with their daughter. I feel sorry for Art. I really hate cheating movies, and this one doesn’t try to justify it either.\n\nThe turning point for me was when Patrick signaled that he slept with Tashi again. That moment reignited something in Art. He needed that betrayal to feel alive in the game again, to want to win not just the match, but Tashi herself, against his own friend. From that point on, tennis and their relationship became the same battlefield.\n\nWhen Art and Tashi finally reconnect emotionally, they go somewhere beautiful together. And when Tashi screams again, it feels raw and unfiltered, like something that hasn’t been released in a long time. It’s not just about winning anymore, it’s about obsession, control, and the damage they willingly inflict on each other.\n\nI expected a sports drama with immersive camera shots, but instead I was given a deeply rooted love triangle centered on the sport, which pays off in the end.","poster":"img/challengers-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"First Blood","year":1982,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-18","tags":["nowwatching","arwinreviews","moviereview"],"words":401,"review":"Non spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nFirst Blood is a grounded, physical action film that proves how effective practical filmmaking can be when paired with strong thematic weight. The tension is built through music, atmosphere, and escalating confrontations rather than spectacle for its own sake. Action sequences feel dangerous because they rely on real locations, practical effects, and physical exhaustion instead of artificial polish. Visually, the film makes strong use of lighting and lens choices to heighten realism and claustrophobia. Beneath the action, the story explores authority, ego, and the difficulty of reintegration after war, showing how small abuses of power can spiral into devastating consequences. It is less about heroics and more about what happens when a broken man is pushed past his limits.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nAng angas ng jail break. Classic action movie trope done right. Talo niya lahat ng pulis kahit mag isa lang siya. Ano ba naman laban ng mga ego driven police laban sa isang Medal of Honor Vietnam veteran?\n\nMaganda rin ang musical score. It constantly builds tension for the scene.\n\nAng galing nung hunting sequence with the helicopter. That level of practicality is what makes old action movies incomparable with today’s CGI heavy films. Ramdam mo yung realism ng chopper, yung danger ng terrain, at yung physical exhaustion ng characters. Walang artificial polish, everything feels realistic.\n\nThe jungle cat and mouse aesthetic feels like Predator, but this time American vs American.\n\nAng ganda ng cinematography sa mining cave sequence. Torch lighting lang ang source. And yung explosion sa gasoline station reminds me of Jarhead and There Will Be Blood. Galing ng production design, talagang sinisira ang set. Real explosions, real debris, real gunfire hits.\n\nLens choices are also great. The anamorphic lenses work especially well in medium and close up shots.\n\nAll these troubles could have been avoided kung pinakawalan na lang siya ng sheriff, exactly as Rambo suggested. It was just escalated by ego and abuse of authority.\n\nAt its core, this film is about how hard it is for soldiers to find their footing as civilians. The war has ended, but their war is still ongoing in their heads. First Blood is about what happens when a broken man with PTSD is pushed too far by people who refuse to listen. Making small power trips turning into full scale violence.","poster":"img/first-blood-1982.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kill Bill: Vol. 2","year":2004,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-17","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":470,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\nKill Bill: Vol. 2 takes a very different approach from its predecessor, trading constant spectacle for restraint, dialogue, and character driven tension. The pacing is slower and more deliberate, allowing scenes to breathe and ideas to settle rather than overwhelm. Quentin Tarantino leans heavily on atmosphere, composition, and sound design to create unease, often letting silence and darkness do the work instead of action. The film feels more reflective and intimate, focusing on identity, consequence, and confrontation rather than pure revenge. While it may not deliver the same visual highs as Vol. 1, it closes the story with intention.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nAng galing nung burying scene. It is just pitch black, and it lets the audience feel the dread of being buried alive. All you hear is the sound of thudding and rumbling, hard soil weighing down on you. It forces you to sit with the fear instead of distracting you with visuals.\n\nMedyo inantok ako sa Pai Mei training flashback at first, but it eventually paid off. Kailangan talaga siya to justify how The Bride survives and gains that punching strength needed to climb her way back from the coffin to the surface. In hindsight, necessary talaga siya kahit medyo dragging.\n\nAng ganda nung silhouette shot with the red backdrop. It immediately reminded me of the cartoon opening of Avatar: The Last Airbender. Simple composition, strong color, and very striking. Quentin knows how to burn an image into your memory.\n\nAs always, the fight between The Bride and Elle Driver shows Quentin flexing his mastery of coordination. The choreography, camera movement, and sound effects all work together seamlessly. Ramdam mo bawat suntok at sipa. Hindi lang siya action, may rhythm at weight.\n\nTotally different ang pacing dito compared sa Vol. 1. Mas tahimik, mas mabagal, at mas dialogue driven. Dito, words ang ginamit ni Quentin as weapons to build tension and toy with the audience instead of flashy visuals and nonstop action. It feels more like a character confrontation than an action showcase.\n\nYung ending, medyo bitin for me. Ang bilis lang namatay ni Bill gamit yung Five Point Palm Exploding Heart Technique. But I don’t think yun talaga ang point ng duology. It feels like Bill just wanted to remind The Bride of who she really is, and that she shouldn’t pretend to be someone she’s not. At the same time, maybe Bill is just a selfish, possessive, and jealous murdering prick.\n\nVol. 2 trades spectacle for meaning. It’s quieter, more reflective, and more interested in identity than revenge itself. It may not hit as hard visually as Vol. 1, but it closes the story in a way that feels intentional.","poster":"img/kill-bill-vol-2-2004.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"28 Days Later","year":2002,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-17","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":322,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNon spoiler thoughts (summary):\n\n28 Days Later opens with a deliberately harsh and abrasive visual style that can feel difficult to read at first, almost punishing in its roughness. As the film progresses, the imagery begins to settle, revealing more intentional shot choices and an aesthetic that favors atmosphere over clarity. Weather, light, and environment gradually become more expressive, giving the world texture and presence. While tension is uneven and some character portrayals lean exaggerated, the film eventually finds a visual and tonal rhythm that makes its approach feel purposeful rather than accidental. It is a challenging aesthetic that asks for patience, but once it locks in, the atmosphere becomes its strongest asset.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nIt’s really hard to see details at the start. I don’t know why the director pushed this kind of aesthetic. The image is rough and unwatchable but after a while, my eyes naturally adjusted. When you really pay attention to the shot choices, you start to see that it’s also artistic.  \n\nThe film really clicked for me during the rainy blockade scene with Jim and Henry. From that point onward, the shots and lighting became much stronger. The lightning flashes and heavy downpour of rain added texture, contrast, and atmosphere. The environment finally felt alive.\n\nTheir wardrobe looks like characters straight out of Left 4 Dead. Very utilitarian and, survival focused. That said, there’s still not much tension overall for me. The soldiers, in particular, feel over the top. More annoying than threatening.\n\nStill, there is a nice payoff. It’s not consistently tense, but once the film finds its rhythm visually and tonally, it becomes more engaging. The aesthetic is hard to love at first, but once it settles, you can appreciate what the director was trying to do with the imagery and atmosphere.","poster":"img/28-days-later-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Good Time","year":2017,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":570,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the NON-SPOILER section.\n\nNON SPOILER THOUGHTS (SUMMARY):\n\nGood Time is a relentless, anxiety driven experience that throws you straight into chaos and never lets up. The Safdie Brothers use fast cuts, overlapping dialogue, and an aggressive electronic synth score to overwhelm rather than guide emotion, trapping you inside the tension. Neon lit nights, sickly colors, and harsh artificial lighting make the city feel hostile and suffocating, like a bad dream you cannot wake up from. The camera stays uncomfortably close, the pacing is merciless, and the sound design works hand in hand with the editing to keep your nerves shot. It is smaller in scale than Uncut Gems but just as effective in its own lane, delivering a masterclass in controlled chaos that is meant to exhaust, not comfort.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nFast paced like Uncut Gems. Gets ko na yung style ng Safdie Brothers. Parang tinataranta at niraratrat nila yung utak mo gamit ang fast cuts, overlapping dialogue, at isang relentless electronic synth score. The music isn’t melodic in the usual sense. It’s more like pulsing, distorted synthesizers that feel mechanical and anxious, almost like a broken heartbeat. The score doesn’t guide emotion, it pressures you. It traps you inside the chaos.\n\nThe film looks neon lit, especially at night, very reminiscent of Taxi Driver. The colors feel dirty and sickly. Greens, reds, and harsh whites dominate the frame, making the city feel hostile and suffocating. The lighting constantly feels artificial, like you’re stuck in a bad dream that never resets.\n\nRobert Pattinson looks completely ragged here. Laging puyat, pawis, half open yung mata. You can feel exhaustion in his body language alone. This genuinely feels like the worst night of his life, a nonstop chain of bad decisions piling on top of each other with no breathing room. The editing reinforces this. Walang pahinga. Just when you think something will calm down, another problem hits harder.\n\nTechnically, ang ganda ng control nila sa pacing, lighting, and sound design. Everything works together to keep you anxious. The camera often stays close to the characters, invasive and uncomfortable, making you feel trapped with them. The electronic score, editing rhythm, and neon lighting form one aggressive sensory experience.\n\nPerformance wise, solid ang buong cast. But the heart of the film is Nick. I really felt for him. He’s clearly being used by his brother, manipulated under the guise of “help” and “love,” while actually being prevented from getting real treatment. Connie thinks he’s protecting Nick, but in reality, he’s just dragging him deeper into chaos.\n\nPlot wise, it’s simpler compared to Uncut Gems. Mas maliit ang scale. Connie is just a small time criminal, not a businessman gambling with millions. That’s why I still prefer Uncut Gems overall. Mas complex, mas layered, mas mataas ang stakes. But Good Time is incredibly effective in its own lane.\n\nThe ending gave the film a sense of hope. Nick finally becomes more self aware, facing the truth instead of being dragged around by his brother. It doesn’t feel triumphant, but it feels honest. A quiet release after two hours of pure stress.\n\nOverall, Good Time is a masterclass in controlled chaos. It’s not meant to be comfortable. It’s meant to overwhelm you, exhaust you, and make you feel trapped inside one terrible night. And it succeeds.","poster":"img/good-time-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Kill Bill: Vol. 1","year":2003,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-16","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":484,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the non spoiler section.\n\nNON SPOILER THOUGHTS (SUMMARY):\n\nKill Bill: Vol. 1 opens at full throttle and never pretends to slow down. Quentin Tarantino leans hard into precision, with fight choreography that is tightly synced to camera movement, framing, and sound design. Every sequence feels meticulously planned, turning violence into something rhythmic and almost dance like. Visually, the film blends kung fu influences with exaggerated physics and bold compositions, favoring spectacle over realism. The sound design is razor sharp, giving weight to every movement, while the music shifts wildly across genres, giving each chapter its own identity. It is loud, stylish, and unapologetically theatrical, a film that exists to flex rhythm, control, and cinematic confidence.\n\nSpoilers ahead. Read on if you’ve seen the film. From here on, plot details are discussed.\n\nWow! Simula pa lang action na agad. Nanibago ako kay Quentin Tarantino. Ang galing ng fight choreography and coordination niya with camera movements and shot selection. Ramdam na ramdam na planned bawat frame. Lalo na yung The Bride vs Gogo fight. Ang ganda ng coordination. Choreography, camera movement, sound effects, lahat worked together. Pati ako napapailag habang nanonood. Ang ganda rin nung silhouette fight shot against the blue moonlit background. Very iconic.\n\nSome moments feel like John Wick before John Wick existed. One woman against every assassin. Visually, may halo siyang Kung Fu Hustle style. Wide compositions, exaggerated movement, playful physics, and stylized violence that leans into spectacle instead of realism.\n\nThe final fight with O-Ren Ishii is pure cinema. Elegant, restrained, and beautifully shot. Kahit yung dialogue, ang linis at ang composed. Tapos yung ending pose ni The Bride parang last routine ng isang ballerina. Controlled and graceful. Parang sinasabi, that’s it, you can now clap.\n\nI watched this right after Stranger Things, so it feels like I am watching Maya Hawke twice in a different universe. Bad ass. Sobrang lakas ng appeal ni Uma Thurman as The Bride.\n\nOne thing that really stood out is the sound design. Ang gaganda ng micro shots paired with very sharp, intentional sound effects. Every slash, every impact, may weight. Elite level sound design.\n\nThe musical score is also great and very non conventional. He pulls from rock, funk, western themes, and Japanese pop. Sensory overload! Iba ibang genre per scene, parang bawat chapter may sariling musical identity. There’s a scene that reminded me of Boogie Nights, especially yung may band like energy. Nakakaindak yung rhythm ng music.\n\nThe anime sequence pulled me out of the immersion. Personally, it broke the momentum for me. That’s really my only complaint.\n\nYung gore dito hindi kadiri. OA siya by design. More theatrical kesa disturbing. Ang galing din ng make up, sobrang bloody.\n\nOverall, Kill Bill Vol. 1 is Tarantino flexing style and rhythm. The anime part didn’t work for me, but everything else is a masterclass in choreographed chaos and stylized revenge.","poster":"img/kill-bill-vol-1-2003.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Stranger Things 5: The Finale","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":534,"review":"Feels like an Inception x Transformers: Dark of the Moon x The Dark Knight Rises. \n\nI went in blind here with only the memory of S1-S4. I just watched it like a regular movie and not an episode. \n\nHopper knocking on the sensory deprivation tank immediately reminded me of the kick in Inception. That feeling of “this is the trigger to wake up” gets reinforced even more when they enter the room after the theater curtain closes. Parang layered realities collapsing into each other, same visual and narrative language of crossing thresholds between worlds.\n\nThe merging of Earth and the Upside Down really feels like Transformers: Dark of the Moon, where two realities attempt to occupy the same physical space. Similar vibes can be felt in films like Man of Steel with Krypton’s World Engine, and Annihilation where the shimmer slowly consumes Earth.\n\nJamie Campbell Bower as Henry is genuinely menacing. Parang mala Anakin energy here. That tragic villain arc where raw power, isolation, and resentment curdle into hatred. He commands the screen every time he appears.\n\nYung characterization ni Henry dito, apart from what we knew in S4 feels very similar to demon narratives in Demon Slayer. Where evil is constantly softened by trauma explanations. For me, that made it anticlimactic. Not every villain needs sympathy.\n\nIto yung ayaw ko sa Netflix style finales. They spent so much time building Henry as the villain, then at the very last minute sabihin na hindi pala siya all along. Walang finality kung sino ba talaga ang ultimate evil. Biglang balik sa Season 2 logic na ang Mind Flayer pa rin ang tunay na villain. And then it gets defeated too easily. Walang real stakes akong naramdaman. Kung hindi ko pa nakita yung spoiler, mas disappointing siguro. It felt like the twist existed just for shock value, not narrative necessity.\n\nA lot of the dialogue here is pure fan service. Callbacks, emotional closure, and full circle moments for characters who’ve been around since season one. For long time fans, it mostly works.\n\nCGI works here because it’s mixed with practical effects. The physicality blends well with digital. \n\nOne moment that did stood out was Winona Ryder beheading Vecna while flashes of his crimes play. That felt earned. That was a clean emotional full stop. \"You messed with the wrong family\" is a memorable line. \n\nThe Mike and El closure also worked well. The callbacks from Season 1 up to now, the flashbacks, Mike’s reaction, and everyone else witnessing it. That sequence felt surreal in a good way, like watching childhood finally end.\n\nSadie Sink is still a standout. Her intensity and emotional range are incredible. She’s consistently operating on a different level. I really hope she gets a massive breakout role after this.\n\nAnd then there’s Will revealing that El is still alive. That trope instantly reminded me of films where the protagonist is believed dead in the third act but survives, like The Dark Knight Rises, Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows Part 2. It’s that classic emotional fake out to give both despair and hope in one move.\n\nOverall, the finale delivers spectacle, callbacks, and emotional beats, but fumbles narrative clarity and villain payoff.","poster":"img/stranger-things-5-the-finale-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Manhunter","year":1986,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":192,"review":"The musical score immediately sets the tone. It feels like puzzle solving music, parang thriller na pinapagana yung utak mo. At the same time, may moments din na may intimate, relationship driven atmosphere sa score, similar to Heat kapag naguusap si Neil at Eady. That balance between cold investigation and quiet human connection is nicely done.\n\nMay romance element din ang film, parang Heat ulit. It adds a layer of vulnerability sa story, showing that even people dealing with monsters still crave connection and normalcy. Hindi siya overly romanticized, just enough to humanize the characters.\n\nFrancis is framed really well throughout the film. He’s a tall guy, and most of the time he’s blocked or framed in a way that makes him look powerful and dominant. The visual language reinforces how dangerous he is even when he’s silent. The sunset shot with Francis and Joan is especially striking, feels like a calm before the storm.\n\nThe third act gunfight is classic Michael Mann. Clean, sharp, no wasted movement. Medyo bitin lang for me, I wouldn’t have minded if it went on longer, but it still delivers. It feels grounded, controlled, and brutal.","poster":"img/manhunter-1986.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Greenland 2: Migration","year":2026,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":335,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the NON-SPOILER section.\n\nNON-SPOILER:\n\nA dark, atmosphere driven disaster film that leans more on mood than spectacle. The opening chaos feels unshaped, so the stakes take time to settle, and while the CGI is serviceable with a few rough edges, it never fully breaks immersion. What really carries the experience is the sound design, which creates tension through layered alarms, movement, and environmental noise. It’s clearly a limited resources trope.\n\nSPOILERS BELOW:\n\nDi ko alam kung madilim ang projector sa sinehan sa SM o talagang madilim lang ang film na ito. Parang everything is backlit, kulang sa key and fill light, kaya minsan hirap makita ang details ng frame.\n\nThe direction of chaos is not that good in the first act. Medyo magulo, hindi controlled chaos, kaya hindi agad ramdam yung stakes.\n\nCGI is around 3/5. Not too good, not too bad. May slight hiccups sa water and cloud simulations.\n\nSound mixing is good though. Umiikot sa sinehan yung audio, lalo na yung sound effects like alarms and environmental noise. Ramdam yung panic dahil sa audio.\n\nThis is a typical limited resources disaster film. \n\nWhat really worked for me is the fire raining down sequence. Maganda yung visuals effects, ramdam yung impact, and nakatulong yung practical wind effects. That made the scenario feel more believable.\n\nThe bridge crossing sequence also worked very well. It kept me at the edge of my seat. The visuals, acting, and musical score all worked together to create a stress inducing moment. Nakakalula siya, and ramdam mo na anytime possible sila na yung susunod na mahuhulog.\n\nThe warzone before the crater reminded me of Apocalypse Now’s Do Lung Bridge sequence. Night photography filled with gunfire, explosions painting the darkness, creating chaos through light bursts against pitch black surroundings. Sayang lang dahil sobrang ikli ng sequence na ito.\n\nAt the end, buti na lang din na hoax yung Clarke Crater. At least may payoff yung journey nila and hindi nasayang yung emotional investment sa survival arc.","poster":"img/greenland-2-migration-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"28 Years Later: The Bone Temple","year":2026,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":487,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the NON-SPOILER section.\n\nNON-SPOILER:\n\nVisceral from the first frame, with no easing you in, but beneath the shock, this is a more confident and cinematic entry than before, with stronger visuals, better lighting, and a clearer visual identity. What’s surprising is the emotional core, where trust and human connection quietly take center stage in a world where the infected are no longer the main threat. The film shifts its focus to moral decay, belief, and meaning, punctuated by a few bold, stylish sequences and an unexpectedly dark sense of humor. It feels more mature, more assured, and more thematic, and it’s worth staying until the very end.\n\nSPOILERS BELOW:\n\nKadiri agad yung simula, puro sumisirit na dugo. Walang warm up warm up! Ang morbid nung skinning alive scene. I feel like throwing up! First time kong makaramdam ng ganun sa sine. Kadiri rin yung kumakain ng utak si Samson. Naalala ko yung sikat na video dati sa Youtube circa 2010 yung \"Poso\". Mga panahong wala pang censorship sa internet. \n\nMas cinematic na ito compared sa naunang entry last year dahil Arri na ang ginamit. Lesson learned: wag gumamit ng iPhone sa films! Maganda na ang depth of field, at mas refined na ang register ng ilaw sa camera. \n\nSurprisingly, the relationship between the Dr. Ian Kelson and Samson feels like a psych therapy session. Unti unting binubuo yung trust, and it genuinely made me smile how warm and natural their bond became. \n\nThis story feels like the latter part of The Walking Dead, kung saan wala nang silbi ang infected bilang threat dahil sanay na ang tao lumaban. Hindi na sila takot. Ang tunay na focus ay yung moral decay ng mga natitirang tao sa dystopian world.\n\nNapawow ako nung nacure na si Samson dahil inatake na siya ng kapwa niya infected. Kudos kay Dr. Ian. Bumalik yung memory ni Samson, pero nandun pa rin yung super strength. This made me think that he is already mutated and he will be the key for a possible cure for all infected if formula made by Dr. Ian can be replicated using trust, care, and psychological healing. There is still hope even if the people residing this new reality seems to give up on God saving them and thinking it is satan's command to make it worse.\n\nAng galing ng temple sequence. Performance, cinematography, production design, choreography, tapos sinabayan pa ng rock song at fire dance. Ang angas! Ralph Fiennes was incredible here!\n\nBiglang naging dark comedy yung third act. Tables turned. Nagbackfire yung plano ni Jimmy Crystal, and it was oddly satisfying.\n\nOverall, Big credit to Nia DaCosta, Alex Garland and Sean Bobbitt. Mas mature, mas confident, mas cinematic. Hindi lang ito tungkol sa infected. It’s about what remains when survival is no longer the problem, but meaning and morality are.\n\nMake sure to watch til the end cause someone is definitely back!","poster":"img/28-years-later-the-bone-temple-2026.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Anaconda","year":2025,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":135,"review":"NON-SPOILER:\n\nRun and gun from the start. A skeletal crew setup that already spells disaster.\n\nMaganda ang night cinematography sa swamp. The darkness actually works here, giving texture and tension to the environment instead of just hiding things.\n\nMaganda ang CGI ng snake. Kapanipaniwala yung scales, weight, and movement. The creature actually feels like a real threat when it shows up.\n\nPaul Rudd is an actor who mostly just plays himself and doesn’t really disappear into another character.\n\nAng funny ng urinary stage fright scene. Ang dami ring stupid stuff, but the funny kind. The film knows it’s ridiculous and leans into it.\n\nThere’s also some showbiz commentary about shooting a film without an ending, just going along and figuring it out as they go. Really fits the chaos of the story.\n\nNice cameos too.","poster":"img/anaconda-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Housemaid","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":472,"review":"This review contains spoilers after the NON-SPOILER section.\n\nNON-SPOILER:\n\nWhat starts as romance slowly mutates into a psychological thriller, where intimacy feels deliberate and weaponized rather than romantic. The film is sharp in how it explores power, manipulation, and control, not in loud, obvious ways, but through charm, structure, and quiet strategy. What makes it unsettling is that no one here is purely innocent or purely evil. Everyone is adapting, surviving, and playing within a broken system that only allows one to win. It’s provocative, uncomfortable, and effective, the kind of film that can be read as a critique but is dangerous enough to linger because of how easily it can be misread.\n\nSPOILERS BELOW:\n\nMay Get Out energy akong nafeel dito. May something off talaga kay Nina (Amanda Seyfried) from the start, parang may tinatago. Habang tumatagal, mas ramdam mo na hindi lang siya basta victim dito. I think si Andrew (Brandon Sklenar) talaga ang nagpupull ng strings, quietly controlling everything behind the scenes.\n\nThe bed scene was hot! Yung thunderclaps at buhos ng ulan, very deliberate. It wasn’t just sex, it was power, it was an applause of triumph of a conqueror. Mas maayos yung execution nito kaysa sa Die My Love. The intimacy feels weaponized, not romantic. May tension, may danger, may intent.\n\nEventually, magsshift ang story from romance to thriller. Ang galing ni Nina, plano at acting lang pala niya lahat. She learned the system, internalized it, and used it back. But instead of pure liberation, what we see is something darker. Women controlling women as replacement. It’s not empowerment in a clean way. It’s survival within a broken system. Parang patriarchy but mirrored. Reverse feminism na may bahid ng desperation. It becomes “either you or me” because the system only allows one to survive.\n\nAng galing magmanipulate ni Andrew. He represents a certain type of male control, hindi yung outright abusive agad, but calculated, charming, and strategic. Yung tipo na kaya kang paikutin hanggang magmukha kang may choice kahit wala naman talaga. This film is clearly about men controlling women, but what’s interesting is it doesn’t stop there.\n\nBoth Nina and Andrew were crazy. Hindi lang one sided evil. Andrew controls through dominance and structure, Nina controls through deception and adaptation. Pareho silang produkto ng parehong power dynamics. \n\nKadiri yung scene na inislice ni Millie (Sydney Sweeney) yung abdomen niya, pati yung front teeth ni Andrew. \n\nThen when the tables have turned, ramdam ko yung collective reaction ng cinema. Tahimik tapos biglang nagsisigawan. Effective ang film!\n\nGanda ng ending. Nagkaroon ng parang hidden samahan ng abused wives. This is a dangerous film for the wrong mindset. May 48 Laws of Power energy siya. Pwedeng basahin bilang critique, pero pwede ring mamisinterpret as a manual to do horrible things. And that thought is what makes the film linger.","poster":"img/the-housemaid-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Conversation","year":1974,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-13","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":186,"review":"Stanley saying “Let’s take a break” really resonated with what I was thinking while watching. What a stupid conversation. What the hell are they talking about? That feeling of irritation is intentional, and the film slowly makes you sit inside that frustration.\n\nAt first, I honestly didn’t get why Harry was so obsessed with the recording. It felt trivial, almost pointless. But that’s where the film becomes effective. It traps the audience in the same loop as Harry, forcing us to listen again and again to a badly recorded, garbled audio, manipulating fragments just to make it barely understandable. You’re not just watching his process, you’re experiencing it.\n\nAs the film progresses, the recorded conversation stops being just evidence. It becomes Harry’s conscience. He keeps hearing it, dreaming about it, replaying it in his head. \n\nBy the time Harry realizes that he is now the one being bugged, his world completely flips upside down. The paranoia he once controlled turns inward. The irony is brutal. The man obsessed with surveillance becomes the victim of it. The film ends not with answers, but with isolation, and distrust.","poster":"img/the-conversation-1974.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Thief","year":1981,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-12","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":420,"review":"Maganda ang color ng lights sa gabi, may pagka blue green. The city feels alive, parang sa Taxi Driver ni Martin Scorsese. There’s that same nocturnal loneliness and danger.\n\nThe lenses used are also nice. Michael Mann and DP Donald Thorin mostly used longer focal length lenses and anamorphic glass, which compress the foreground and background while exaggerating light sources. That’s why the neon lights bloom and smear slightly, making the city feel overwhelming and alive at the same time.\n\nJames Caan delivered a very strong performance as Frank. He has that quiet authority and confidence, giving off George Clooney vibes. Calm, professional, but you can feel the volatility underneath.\n\nThe score adds a constant thrill and energy to the film. Tangerine Dream’s electronic music feels mechanical and relentless, perfectly matching Frank’s life as a precision driven thief. \n\nAng ganda ng visuals dun sa final heist niya. Yung sparks ng parang welding na pambutas sa safe create a high-intensity practical sparks effect, producing a white-hot backlight that throws Frank into hard silhouette. Kasabay nun yung fog from the fire extinguisher, which is essentially practical smoke. This adds texture to the light, making the beams visible and giving the scene depth. Ang ganda ng combined effect. Even without dialogue or musical score, ramdam mo yung intensity ng pagbubukas ng safe purely through sound design. Every scrape, spark, and metal grind feels heavy and real.\n\nAng ganda nung pinasabog niya yung bahay niya. It really feels like they blew up the set and captured everything in camera. Sobrang tangible and ramdam yung explosion. Same with the cocktail lounge. Ang ganda rin ng explosion doon, plus the use of slow motion makes it even more visceral. Even yung pagsunog niya ng mga kotse sa motor sale, ang ganda ng apoy sa camera. You can feel the heat and chaos, not just see it.\n\nThe ending is violent and decisive. Revenge is fully satisfied. Frank doesn’t look back. \n\nI think dahil dito nag double down si Michael Mann sa thought ng Heat, o yung police force breathing on the thief’s neck. Yung cat-and-mouse hunting sa ending is parang ganun din yung sa dulo ng Heat nung nasa outside ng airstrip si Neil at Vincent. Nandun yung suspense and anticipation ng inevitable death.\n\nMay mga pros talaga na would rather do it on their own, lone wolf. Mali lang talaga niya is pumayag siya makipag join forces sa isang capitalist. Tinrap siya in a grasp, guised as a favor, in an attempt to control him.","poster":"img/thief-1981.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Punch-Drunk Love","year":2002,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-12","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":398,"review":"What is up with PTA suddenly throwing in jump scares? Haha. And the lens flares too. PTA, are you trying to copy J.J. Abrams? \n\nThe long uninterrupted takes, a clear PTA DNA, are very evident here. He really uses the entire space, letting the characters move freely and exist inside the frame instead of cutting around them. It makes the performances feel more raw and lived-in.\n\nAt first, this film feels like it’s going to be about isolation and loneliness, and that assumption holds throughout. The musical score has the same looping, atmospheric quality found in his other films, almost syncing with the visuals and Barry’s internal rhythm.\n\nBarry, played by Adam Sandler, is awkward, math-savvy, mentally unstable, and painfully lonely. PTA frames him using silhouettes and strong leading lines, visually reinforcing how boxed-in and overwhelmed he feels. \n\nIt’s interesting to notice that PTA already used the car door closing shot here, something he later reused in One Battle After Another after the kidnapping of Chase Infiniti. You can really see ideas forming and evolving across his films.\n\nIt’s also refreshing to see Philip Seymour Hoffman play a serious, aggressive antagonist, similar to his role in Mission: Impossible III. I honestly grew tired of him being cast by PTA as a mediocre or side character, like the gay comedic relief in Boogie Nights or the nurse in Magnolia. Here, he feels dangerous.\n\nBy the last act, Adam Sandler starts giving off strong Howard Ratner energy from Uncut Gems, frantic, impulsive, and emotionally explosive. Knowing what Sandler can do now, this performance feels like an early blueprint of that intensity.\n\nI honestly thought that he was hallucinating when he single handedly squashed his attackers. Some kind of illusion. But it turns out it was real. I think that because of love, he already knows how to compartmentalize his aggression, and now it is directed toward those who hurt his love interest.\n\nThe shifting colors feel like title cards per act, but I’m not very adept at reading the meaning of colors. That’s why I wasn’t able to fully connect with what PTA was trying to say through them, but it’s still a nice touch.\n\nOverall, this film feels like an experimental playground for PTA. A space where he tests new visual ideas, tonal shifts, and narrative risks, honing skills that would later define his more confident and complex works.","poster":"img/punch-drunk-love-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Bridges of Madison County","year":1995,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-11","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":447,"review":"Meryl Streep’s charisma is undeniable here. Much younger compared to the last time I saw her in Don’t Look Up as the president, and she carries this quiet, restrained presence that slowly opens up as the film goes on.\n\nThere’s a very clear sexual tension between Robert and Francesca. Francesca feels like she’s losing her sense of grounding and becoming playfully confused. Bagay sa kanya yung ganitong roles, yung nasa pagitan ng pagpili, emotionally torn, unsure which path to take. She comes across as a lonely, bored housewife who hasn’t been touched or truly seen by her husband in a long time, and the arrival of Robert suddenly injects thrill, attention, and desire back into her life.\n\nYou can really feel the burning desire underneath everything. They crave each other deeply. Ang tanda na nila, pero yung pananabik parang teenager! That contrast is one of the strongest emotional pulls of the film.\n\nMaganda rin yung mga love songs. They’re soft, melodic, and nostalgic, mostly leaning toward gentle folk and classic romantic ballads. The music doesn’t overpower the scenes but instead quietly amplifies longing, regret, and intimacy, making the moments feel suspended in time, almost like memories being formed as you watch them.\n\nEventually, the film traps them in an emotional dilemma: the family Francesca built versus a new life with her newfound love. Personally, I find it highly unlikely that something like this realistically happens at their age.\n\nThe drama deepens in the latter part, but I couldn’t fully invest in their “cause.” No matter how romantic the lens is, it’s still cheating. They wouldn’t be in this situation had they not played with fire. They fucked around and now they found out, and what follows feels like shared trauma born from their own choices.\n\nWhat really bothered me is how Francesca acts completely normal when her family comes back, welcoming them as if nothing happened. Walang kamalay malay yung asawa niya. I can’t sympathize with that, especially as someone who doesn’t want to be betrayed behind my back.\n\nMaybe in another life or reality, I’d cry at the idea that they can’t have what they want, that they found something they’re absolutely certain about but are denied the freedom to choose it. A kind of forbidden love where happiness exists but is unreachable.\n\nSo maybe the unintentional lesson here is simple: love your spouse well, so they don’t go looking for romantic love elsewhere.\n\nStill, what a performance by Meryl Streep!\n\nI guess it’s poetic in the sense that the bridge which started it all became the only “bridge” for them to be together in the end. Though not in this lifetime, but in the afterlife.","poster":"img/the-bridges-of-madison-county-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Silence","year":2016,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":885,"review":"From the very first frames, Silence announces itself visually. The mist rolling over mountains feels like a Japanese painting, almost sacred in its stillness. Different Martin Scorsese motifs here. The blue and green palette dominates the film, making the land feel cold, barren place, and spiritually distant. Ramdam mo yung spiritual isolation.\n\nThere’s not much artsy experimentation here with lens choices or flashy shots. It’s more of a safe, restrained storytelling approach, but it asks very deep questions about faith and persecution.\n\nThere’s an “Among Us” paranoia throughout: you never know who among the Japanese villagers might be a hidden informant or spy.\n\nThe film also explores the root of Japan’s resistance to Christianity: not ignorance, but incompatibility with imposed belief.\n\nNatawa ako nung kumain sila, lamon agad yung mga priests, while yung mga Japanese ay mataimtim na nagdadasal to say grace sa food. It already hints at cultural and spiritual disconnect. Those priests who were raised in a comfortable environment, felt hungry after a long journey and suddenly forgot to pray before eating, while the new converts, who were marginally poor, did not even forget to pray first before their meal.\n\nAlso one striking idea the film presents is that priests within the same faith do not respond to danger the same way. Even among men supposedly united by doctrine, fear fractures belief. Safety, survival, and responsibility pull them in different directions.\n\nI honestly think the converts or villager Christians would all be spared much suffering had they thought otherwise. That their faith in Jesus alone was enough, that forgiveness and salvation do not require priestly mediation. Because it seems that this whole mess is because of the priests evangelizing, and not the converts. The ending hints that the Inquisitor doesn’t care much about them, but only the roots, priests introducing Christianity to Japan. The film doesn’t answer this directly, but it clearly exposes how some churches complicate belief.\n\nI like the scene where Rodrigues (Andrew Garfield) is confused why the other prisoners are calm even in the face of death. It shows that sometimes priests, who are supposed to know the Bible by heart, forget its core teachings.\n\nThe Bible repeatedly teaches one clear idea: dying for Christ is not defeat but victory. Earthly life is temporary, eternal life outweighs it. Scripture consistently says that losing one’s life for Christ is finding it, that death has no final power, that believers should not fear those who can only kill the body. Yet Andrew, despite knowing all this, is still afraid. That contrast is painful and revealing. That’s why priests, for me, are not very reliable when it comes to “walk the talk.”\n\nVerses like Matthew 16:25, Philippians 1:21, John 11:25–26, Revelation 2:10, Hebrews 2:14–15, and 1 Corinthians 15:54–55 all say the same thing in different voices: to die in Christ is not something to fear. It is something already conquered.\n\nThe torture scene by the shore is brutal. Talagang hardcore test of faith under persecution, not only for the victims but for the priest forced to watch.\n\nThe film asks several uncomfortable questions: How long will you keep your faith when God feels silent? Do you have the right to let others suffer for your beliefs?\n\nLiam Neeson as Father Ferreira reminds me of Ra’s al Ghul. Both start as mentors, firm believers in a higher cause. But over time, both reach a breaking point where idealism collapses under reality. Ferreira doesn’t suddenly stop believing because he is weak; he changes because he concludes that the system itself cannot survive unchanged. Both characters start as symbols of unwavering ideology, then evolve into pragmatists shaped by suffering and failure.\n\nAng hirap ng problema ni Rodrigues. Will he give up the faith to save the people, or does he wait and trust that God will intervene?\n\nThe scene where Christ speaks to Andrew and tells him to step on Him is powerful. Christ says He was born to share man’s pain, that He already suffered for all our sins. That even being stepped on is part of that suffering. This scene speaks volumes about Christ’s compassion versus Rodrigues’ pride.\n\nChrist’s message is not approval of apostasy, but a reminder that He already bore suffering. He already carried this pain. It reframes sacrifice not as heroic endurance, but as shared suffering. He is with us through it all.\n\nIn the end, Rodrigues just learned how to play the game while keeping it safe. Keeping the silence of his faith alive deep inside him. He plays the system, outwardly compliant, inwardly faithful.\n\nThis reminds me of Matthew 13:31–32, the mustard seed parable, where faith grows quietly and invisibly.\n\nOne line that stuck to me was when Rodrigues said, “In my silence, I hear His voice.” It means that God’s silence is not abandonment. God may be speaking even when it feels like silence. It reminds me of the book of Job, one of the most painful books ever written. Job 33:14 says that God does speak, now one way, now another, though no one perceives it.\n\nGreat acting by Andrew Garfield throughout the movie, though not as impactful for me as Hacksaw Ridge.\n\nOverall, Silence is not about finding God, but about what happens when God seems absent, and whether faith survives without reward, comfort, or clarity.","poster":"img/silence-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Raging Bull","year":1980,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-10","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":281,"review":"Opening credits pa lang, ang ganda na agad ng framing. Boxed in siya sa boxing ring, parang visual cue na agad na trapped siya, hindi lang physically kundi mentally and emotionally.\n\nI watched this right after Taxi Driver, and ibang Robert De Niro ito. Kitang kita yung dedication niya. He really put in the work with his body to look like a boxer. Nagkalaman siya, and the acting feels like a wild man ready to knock everyone out. I think may prosthetics din sa mukha niya, especially sa mga sore and battered scenes, which made the punishment feel even more brutal.\n\nI really like how the fights were shot. Mostly close ups and mid body shots lang. Walang wide heroic angles. You can really feel the raw grit of boxing, lalo na kapag may heat fumes distorting the image. I also like the use of slow motion combined with blood spurting out. It’s violent, ugly, and very physical, not glamorous at all.\n\nI am kind of happy for him when he found a more supportive wife compared to the first one who keeps on nagging. But even then, you can feel that no matter who is beside him, the real problem is still him.\n\nThe black and white cinematography didn’t really work for me. Parang C’mon C’mon na film ng A24. It prevents me from being fully immersed at times. I get the artistic intention, but emotionally, it kept me at a distance.\n\nOverall, I think this film is about success and self sabotage. How someone can reach the top, be celebrated, feared, and respected, but still destroy everything from the inside. Hindi nya kalaban yung mundo, kundi ang sarili nya.","poster":"img/raging-bull-1980.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Train Dreams","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-10","tags":["nowwatching","arwinreviews","moviereview","netflix"],"words":351,"review":"Standout sa opening pa lang is yung choice of lens. Ang ganda ng creamy bokeh, giving a dreamlike look, at ang ganda ng separation ng background and foreground. This is apparent for the whole film. Maganda rin ang color grading, giving the film an alive, crystal clear feeling.\n\nThe HMUA and prosthetics department did a great job papangitin si Felicity Jones and Kerry Condon, though she can’t really hide much of her accent. Ang ganda ng mga shots, parang painting like, and unconventional yung mga framing. Either low angle, isolated sila sa frame, may big head room, or lots of negative space.\n\nNice to see Joel Edgerton, commonly known for his role as Owen Lars in Star Wars II–III, and nice to see William H. Macy here looking really old compared to Boogie Nights. The outdoor shots remind me of The Revenant and Sinners, especially that timely shot where the pink horizon touches the blue sky.\n\nAng ganda ng wildfire sequence. Reminds me of Only the Brave and The Lost Bus. Maganda rin ang mga night shots with bonfire, pure clean black against yellow flickering lighting.\n\nThis film is about grief and loneliness, and the void of meaning in life when you lose your loved ones. Just waiting for what’s left here for. There’s a meaningful conversation in the latter part of the film where Claire Thompson (Kerry Condon) mentions how even dead trees are just as important as living ones, and that both still contribute to the environment. She also talks about how when you lose someone, you’re often left with more questions than answers, and that when we go through grief, there’s nothing crazy about whatever emotions we feel.\n\nI think the large head room is not really for the visuals of the trees and skies, but to show how small we are against the looming sadness of grief and loss. How vulnerable and helpless we are against the crushing weight of emotions inside us.\n\nOh, I hate flashback montages, but here the ups and downs still feel like part of the journey. Feels so beautiful, yet so sad.","poster":"img/train-dreams-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Last Temptation of Christ","year":1988,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":752,"review":"I don’t know why, but I felt really unmotivated to watch this. I had to push myself and approach it purely from a cinematic standpoint.\n\nHere, Jesus is portrayed by Willem Dafoe. This version of Jesus is not the usual all knowing, Superman type. He is vulnerable, doubtful, and unsure. It feels like he is slowly being shaped, not instantly destined to be the Messiah from birth. This is a very different kind of humanization. Too human, and too raw.\n\nAfter the desert scene, Willem almost looks like a more familiar European Jesus. Before that, I honestly had doubts if he would fit the role visually. His acting is strong though. I can feel the emotional disturbance churning inside him. Confusion, fear, anger, and guilt are all present.\n\nThis is an early film, so the cinematography feels very different from modern biblical films. Lighting is mostly bright and high key, with minimal shadow contrast. A lot of static shots and clear frontal framing. It gives a sense of flatness, with minimal lens variation.\n\nSome desert scenes are backlit, which works well. During night scenes, there’s an old school technique that I don’t see much anymore. It reminds me of Filipino films like Pedro Penduko, Magic Temple, and Spirit Warriors. They use a cool backlight or edge light to simulate moonlight, giving characters a crisp blue outline. When red backlight is used, it reminds me of Indiana Jones.\n\nThe film mainly explores the “what ifs.” What if Jesus had doubts? What if he was unsure of his role? What if he was surprised by the powers given to him by the Father? What if he wasn’t the Messiah? What if he had a family and children?\n\nWillem Dafoe brings a new way for me to read the Bible. Nakakatakot din siya at times, probably because I associate him with Norman Osborn. His rage toward the Pharisees feels intense and unfiltered.\n\nComparing portrayals, Willem shows confusion, anger, and guilt, while Jonathan Roumie’s Jesus shows more compassion, warmth, and controlled rage. Here, other disciples are mostly sidelined, with Judas Iscariot getting the spotlight. It almost feels like multiple disciples were combined into Judas, the Zealot aspect, the doubting aspect, everything.\n\nThe practical sets are strong, especially the blood effects. The production design is commendable. The prayer scene at the Garden of Gethsemane is more emotionally effective here than in The Passion of the Christ.\n\nI actually prefer this film over The Passion of the Christ. This one feels more complete as a film. Passion left me unsatisfied cinematically. Bitin yung acting, cinematography, and payoff. Parang “yun na yun?”\n\nWhat really works here is the humanization of Jesus in the first and second acts. Because of that, you develop more empathy going into the third act.\n\nI don’t think this film is blasphemous from start to finish. But my opinion shifted during the crucifixion sequence, when the angel tells Jesus he is not the sacrifice and not the Messiah. The wedding with Mary Magdalene made me laugh at the absurdity. The idea that the afterlife is still on Earth, the sex scene, and them having children felt shocking. I understand why this angered many people. I immediately thought that this might have inspired Dan Brown’s The Da Vinci Code.\n\nThe third act almost feels like a parody at times. Natatawa na lang ako. Jesus cheating, suggesting mass hallucination, it felt absurd.\n\nThe film touches on the conspiracy that the resurrection was not real and was created to give people hope. It becomes a matter of perception of who people need Jesus to be.\n\nThen comes the twist. The “guardian angel” is revealed to be the last temptation. Jesus was lied to. He truly was the Messiah, and by choosing the illusion, salvation was lost. That realization hits hard.\n\nWhen the film cuts back to Jesus and dolly zooms in on the cross and he begs to be crucified again, followed by the shout “It is accomplished,” ang galing! Parang Christopher Nolan level twist in terms of emotional reversal!\n\nI think this film is misunderstood. It played with my emotions. I was blindsided, just like Jesus was by Satan. I think that’s why it works. It reflects daily life. We get distracted from our purpose. We are tempted constantly. We think we know better than God. We resist His plans. We don’t fight temptation enough. And when we finally admit that and ask for forgiveness and finally trusts Him, that’s when we truly worship and glorify Him.","poster":"img/the-last-temptation-of-christ-1988.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hard Eight","year":1996,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":185,"review":"PTA’s directorial debut, and kitang kita agad yung style niya. Nandun na yung strong musical score, dry comedy, long continuous shots, high energy moments, smart lens choices, and pagiging very character driven.\n\nThere’s a looping score that sounds like the opening of Top Gun with the bells. It uses repetitive melodic phrases with chime like tones that keep circling back, almost hypnotic. It gives a sense of calm control on the surface, but underneath may tension. Parang steady rhythm that mirrors Sydney’s quiet authority and patience. The repetition also reinforces the idea of routine, discipline, and inevitability.\n\nSydney has this calm, nonchalant authority. Mentor slash father figure energy. May aura siya ng someone na may alam, may experience, at may tinatagong history.\n\nAll this time, nagtataka ako bakit ang bait niya kay John. Parang sobra yung care at guidance niya, almost unconditional. Then when the truth is revealed, everything clicks. Suddenly, his kindness doesn’t feel random anymore. It feels intentional, and deeply personal.\n\nThe revenge on Jimmy, played by Samuel L. Jackson, is very satisfying. Walang over the top theatrics. You don’t mess with Sydney!","poster":"img/hard-eight-1996.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Taxi Driver","year":1976,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-09","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":345,"review":"Ang bata ni Robert De Niro dito. Parang batang Jake Gyllenhaal energy sa Donnie Darko. May parehong unsettling presence, tahimik pero puno ng inner chaos.\n\nFor a 1976 film, ang ganda at ang linis ng colors nito, lalo na sa gabi. Vibrant neon colored yung city. I think malaking factor na remastered version ito, kaya mas buhay yung reds, greens, and yellows ng New York streets.\n\nAng ganda ng lens choices and framing, especially yung close ups sa mata. Madalas nakaframe si Travis na parang nakakulong sa sarili niyang isip. Maganda rin ang musical score. It has old school vibes because Bernard Herrmann uses a mix of jazz saxophone, brass, and orchestral strings. The sax gives that lonely, late night city feel, parang noir, habang yung orchestral parts feel ominous and disturbing. Parang romantic sadness sa umpisa, pero unti unting nagiging menacing habang lumalala yung mental state ni Travis.\n\nParang nagrebelde lang siya dahil nareject siya? Medyo ganun yung dating sa akin. Parang ang babaw ng trigger. Pero on top of that, meron din siyang sariling twisted sense of justice, remembering Iris and trying to save her. Tulad nga ng sabi ni Iris, “High and Mighty.” He sees himself as someone chosen to clean the streets.\n\nMotifs of red in the color grading are very apparent, especially kapag galit sya or spiraling. Red lights, red walls, red blood. It visually reinforces rage, violence, and obsession.\n\nThe ending is pure gunfight, parang Tarantino pero mas raw. Old school yung sound effects ng gunshots, very loud, sharp, and almost exaggerated. The practical effects of wounds and blood are also old school. Makapal yung dugo, obvious yung prosthetics, pero it adds to the brutality. Hindi siya clean or stylish. It feels messy, uncomfortable, and violent in a very physical way.\n\nThe visuals and score during the crime scene frames almost turn the film into horror. The slow camera movements, the lingering shots on bodies, the eerie score makes  it feel like we’re inside Travis’ broken mind. Hindi na siya action, hindi na siya thriller. Parang psychological horror na.","poster":"img/taxi-driver-1976.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Magnolia","year":1999,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":252,"review":"High energy prologue agad by PTA. Ramdam mo na from the start na chaotic, ambitious, at very ensemble driven ito.\n\nAng daming familiar faces from Boogie Nights. Parang reunion ng PTA regulars. Tom Cruise here gives me Jordan Belfort energy. Loud, confident, aggressive, and very performative, pero may pinanggagalingan din na insecurity underneath.\n\nAng daming long uninterrupted takes. Tatak PTA talaga. He lets scenes breathe and allows chaos to unfold within the frame instead of cutting away. It makes everything feel overwhelming, in a deliberate way.\n\nThere are a lot of characters introduced, and you can already tell na eventually they will be interconnected in some way. The film constantly jumps between their lives, traumas, and emotional baggage.\n\nMas magaling yung acting ni Julianne Moore dito. Mas solid yung range niya. Emotional breakdowns, guilt, desperation, lahat ramdam at hindi pilit.\n\nThis is the most ambiguous film PTA made for me. Less than an hour left and I still didn’t get the whole picture. Parang intentionally disorienting, as if the film wants you to sit in confusion instead of giving you clarity.\n\nNakakagulat yung raining frogs scene. Out of nowhere, sobrang lakas ng sound effect. Ang ganda ng CGI, hindi halata, at tangible yung mga frogs. Pero hanggang dulo, I was still asking, what’s the significance?\n\nUntil the end, parang hindi naman sila naging fully connected lahat. I didn’t feel much emotional payoff. I appreciate the ambition, the performances, and the direction, but personally, wala akong masyadong nafeel dito sa film na ito.","poster":"img/magnolia-1999.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Phantom Thread","year":2017,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":308,"review":"The musical score and visual look really feel like classic Europe. There’s a certain elegance and restraint to it that immediately sets the tone. Everything feels composed, deliberate, and old school.\n\nDaniel Day Lewis’ look, energy, and accent are very different here. Hindi ito Daniel Plainview at all. Completely ibang persona. Mas controlled, mas refined, but still intimidating in a quieter way.\n\nI’m honestly confused how women think that a dress will make them look not ugly. That idea kind of floats in the film, especially with how fashion is treated as something almost sacred, transformative, and powerful.\n\nMedyo parang ako si Reynolds Woodcock. Ayaw ko ng distractions kapag focus ako sa ginagawa ko. May pagka bipolar din ako minsan. Gusto ko seryoso lang, minsan gusto ko sweet and playful. Ayaw ko rin ng hindi nasusunod yung plans, and ayaw ko rin ng surprises. Minsan di rin ako makausap kapag maraming iniisip sa business. Very specific ako sa standards ko, and somehow uncompromising.\n\nHis dialogue with his sister in the third act hits hard. Relate na relate ako. Even the smell of the house! That whole exchange felt painfully familiar. Parang the film speaks my unsaid feelings. \n\nThere’s an underlying lesson here about not choosing a wife based on how her body fits your standard. Another subtle layer of the film is how men use women as a tool for convenience, even when they think they’re being romantic or respectful.\n\nThis is a messed up film in a quiet way. Parang may codependency. Alma needs Reynolds to be weak so that he will need her. Reynolds needs Alma to balance his greatness and vulnerability, and he enjoys that mind game. I personally think this is the invisible thread that binds them together.\n\nThis is a very different tone of directing from Paul Thomas Anderson. Less chaos, more control.","poster":"img/phantom-thread-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Alien","year":1979,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","alien"],"words":430,"review":"Atmospheric start. Calm before the storm. Ramdam mo agad yung unease kahit wala pang nangyayari.\n\nGanda ng production design ng ship nila. Very tangible, futuristic with an analogue touch, parang sa Star Wars. Yung hallways remind me of the Millennium Falcon and even the Death Star. In contrast, yung alien ship feels completely different. Parang same texture ng interior ng ship ni Superman sa Man of Steel. Organic, biomechanical, cold, and alien talaga.\n\nMaganda ang sound mixing. Hindi kami naka surround sound pero parang naka ganun yung experience nung pinanood ko sa TV. You can really feel the space, the silence, and the sudden bursts of sound.\n\nThis film also features early motion control photography with blue screen compositing using practical models, pioneered by Star Wars. Visually impressive pa rin siya for its time, especially how seamless the models are integrated into the shots.\n\nMaganda ang cinematography in the dark. Ang ganda ng lighting and contrast. Ridley Scott did a very good job selling the difference in scale between objects, whether it’s the ship versus space, or humans versus the alien. Laging may sense kung gaano ka kaliit at kavulnerable yung tao.\n\nI can hear Kiri in the way a young Sigourney Weaver talks here. Familiar cadence and presence.\n\nMedyo slow burn ang pacing, even with a few jump scares here and there. It takes its time to build dread instead of relying purely on shock.\n\nI already knew Ash is the bad guy since I watched Romulus before this. Pero honestly, halata naman si Ash kahit wala kang prior knowledge. Hindi lang siya curious as a science man. Masyado siyang halata with his unjustifiable decisions. May something off agad na nakakainis sa kanya.\n\nThe score feels like a hybrid of space opera and horror flick. May moments of grandeur, then biglang unsettling at eerie.\n\nThe third act is pure chaos. Handheld shots mixed with strobe lights create disorientation. You really feel the danger and gravity of the situation, especially with the impending self destruction while being hunted by a Xenomorph that could appear at any corner. It reminds me of playing the old Half-Life game as a kid, where you’re navigating dark industrial corridors, alarms blaring, lights flickering, and danger always just out of frame. You’re constantly moving forward, not because you want to, but because staying still means death.\n\nGood film, but nothing groundbreaking for me. What stood out the most are the close up shots of Ripley and the lighting and color grading during the final act. That’s where the film feels the most alive and intense.","poster":"img/alien-1979.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Blade Runner 2049","year":2017,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":448,"review":"Denis Villeneuve did not waste time world building on the first few frames. Sa first few frames pa lang, ramdam mo na agad yung scale and tone.\n\nAnd he and Roger Deakins already flexed their muscles dun pa lang sa opening scene with K (Ryan Gosling) and Sapper Morton (Dave Bautista).\n\nThey kept the aesthetic of futuristic California, but this one is cleaner and brighter compared to the original. Hindi na ganun ka-grimy, pero may lungkot pa rin sa bawat frame.\n\nI forgot that Ana de Armas and David Dastmalchian were here, and most especially Robin Wright. Wow. Even the pirate from Captain Phillips! Solid cast. \n\nI really like the ever moving light versus shadow inside the Wallace Corporation. Parang buhay yung architecture, constantly shifting, unsettling, and godlike.\n\nWhen K burned Sapper’s house, it reminded me of a scene from Incendies. May parehong emotional weight at finality sa imagery.\n\nI like the detective vibes of this film. Quiet, methodical, at mas introspective kaysa action driven.\n\nSome shots of Denis here look like the color tone of the first Dune film, especially the home planet of the Sardaukar. I think Denis learned a lot doing this film and applied it to the Dune franchise. Hope this will also have an IMAX re release. Ang daming IMAX worthy, painting like shots.\n\nEven Hans Zimmer has tones here that feel similar to Dune. May desolation, loneliness, but also a hint of hope underneath.\n\nThe special effects of explosions are nice, very tangible and believable.\n\nMy first viewing inantok ako dito, but now I’m more invested and immersed. Medyo slow paced din kasi talaga, and iba expectations ko before. Akala ko action flick siya, but this one really leans more on cinematic language and mood.\n\nThe green drenched, snow covered streets of California are siiiick. Very memorable visual identity.\n\nThe syncing of Joi and the hooker feels trippy. Reminds me of the movie Her. This is lonely and desperate.\n\nAng ganda talaga ng Las Vegas sequence. That orange hue with fog and dusty texture is pure visual poetry.\n\nNakakalungkot lang for K. Ang daming twist na nangyari sa kanya. Una akala niya siya yung anak, then nagkaroon siya ng existential crisis. After niya paniwalaan yun, malalaman niya na decoy lang pala siya and implanted lang talaga yung memory niya from another person’s real memory (both foreshadowed at the earlier scenes). This really touches the idea that replicants develop deeper emotions once they are given false hope.\n\nThe final battle looks straight out of Tron Legacy because of the neon lighting, but this time with lots of water. Nice fight choreography too, reminds me of Dune Part Two with Muad’Dib and Feyd Rautha.","poster":"img/blade-runner-2049-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Blade Runner","year":1982,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-05","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":558,"review":"Maganda ang world building, with lots of miniature work and early VFX, very similar to Star Wars which pioneered this technique. The difference for me is the camera movement. Here, the camera is mostly steady, so it sometimes feels fake because the illusion relies heavily on simply animating the model and zooming in or out to sell scale. Unlike Star Wars, where George Lucas paired miniature work with more aggressive and complex camera movements like pans, tilts, and tracking shots, making the ships feel like they were actually maneuvering through space. In Blade Runner, the restrained camera makes the city feel more like a moving painting than a lived in environment. Hindi rin nakatulong for me yung optical flares used for headlights and taillights of vehicles. Mas naging halata siya and felt distracting rather than immersive.\n\nAside from the miniature models, the world building is carried heavily by the score. Vangelis uses a lot of analog synthesizers, layered pads, slow arpeggios, and sustained electronic drones, mixed with occasional saxophone lines. The synths give the film its futuristic identity, while the saxophone adds a noir, almost bluesy sadness. Underneath the grandeur and scale of the city, there’s a deep sense of loneliness in the music. It feels romantic but empty, futuristic but emotionally isolated.\n\nThe color grading and cinematography also feel surprisingly modern, especially since I watched the Final Cut released in 2007. I think this version helped me push through more than before, because in my previous attempts, the film felt flat and boring and I couldn’t finish it. The Final Cut polish makes the visuals more cohesive and atmospheric, even if the pacing still tested my patience.\n\nThe production design is strong. Futuristic but gritty, rusty, and used. It looks like a futuristic, neon stylized Quiapo. May pagka Tim Burton’s Batman set design. The constant rain and smoke aren’t just environmental effects, they act as texture, adding layers and depth to the frame.\n\nThe film uses a lot of dark interior shots, illuminated only by streaks of light coming from vehicles outside.\n\nI also noticed the strong juxtaposition throughout the film. It’s futuristic, yet heavily analog. This is very evident in the scene where Deckard investigates a photograph using a device that can zoom and enhance infinitely. Conceptually, it feels like an advanced FBI tool, but visually and sonically, it feels old. The sound design and interface look like a mix of old television and radio equipment.\n\nHarrison Ford still plays an alternate Han Solo here. He feels tired and uninterested in the world around him. When he fires a gun, it still feels like a space cowboy gunslinger moment, very Han Solo, except this time it’s real bullets instead of laser blasts.\n\nDespite all of this, the film still didn’t consistently capture my attention. I got bored a lot of times. The pacing and mood can feel monotonous, even if the atmosphere is rich.\n\nOne thing that didn’t work for me is Pris Stratton. I don’t really like characters with weird, clownish hair and makeup. It pulled me out of the experience instead of adding to her menace.\n\nThe cat and mouse sequence between Deckard and Roy Batty, is pure visual popcorn. This is where the director and the DOP clearly flex what they can do. Lighting, framing, movement, and performance all come together beautifully here.","poster":"img/blade-runner-1982.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Perfume: The Story of a Murderer","year":2006,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":433,"review":"Ang galing ng prosthetics sa kakalabas lang na baby sa umpisa. Ang ganda rin ng production design, lalo na yung marketplace. Naiimagine ko talaga yung putrid na amoy.\n\nMaganda rin ang color grading. Parang kulay ng Les Misérables na musical film. Hindi siya typical orange and teal. Mas leaning sa gold and blue green tones, muted and aged. Maganda rin ang use of shadows. Black kung black, walang takot sa dilim, which fits perfectly sa detached and obsessive nature ni Grenouille. Bagay na bagay sa era ng France, pati sa wardrobe. Visually, consistent yung buong film.\n\nMaganda ang pagkaka narrate ni John Hurt, nakaka engage talaga. He pulls you into the story without forcing emotion. \n\nAng weirdo nung inaamoy niya yung katawan nung babaeng napatay niya. Parang animal, walang remorse. Pure instinct lang. Pati pusa dinistill. Wala talagang remorse or moral compass, scent lang ang mundo niya.\n\nNakakatawa rin na parang may pattern. Kapag nag move on na si Grenouille sa former master niya, laging namamatay. Everyone in his life feels disposable, parang stepping stone lang toward his obsession.\n\nGanda ng sunset mountain top shots ng landscape. Kita yung separation ng bawat horizon, very clean and cinematic. The CGI background extensions for some shots are also good. For a 2006 film, it still holds up after 20 years. Hindi eyesore yung CGI, used strategically and only when needed.\n\nMay mga story beats lang talaga na medyo dragging for me, lalo na sa gitna. \n\nThen, nagdistill nanaman ng babae. Psycho talaga para lang makagawa ng skin scent. Imagine mo, your whole life spent just for a small batch of fragrance oil.\n\nThis film starts off as a coming of age story, then becomes an ambition film, and eventually turns into a thriller. Kasabay ng paglaki ng obsession niya, nagshi shift din yung genre ng pelikula.\n\nAlan Rickman’s portrayal of a protective and paranoid father is on point. Ramdam mo yung takot niya. \n\nThe musical score sa third act is awesome and really elevates the climax.\n\nBen Whishaw as Jean Baptiste is great. He performs with a calm but unhinged presence. Laser focused yung passion niya, which makes the character even more disturbing.\n\nIronic lang na ang nagbuko pa sa kanya ng mga kagagawan niya is someone na malakas din ang pang amoy which is yung aso ng prostitute na pinatay din niya.\n\nAng kulit nung nagkaroon ng mass hallucination sa sobrang bango ng gawa niya. Lakas ng projection haha. Nagka orgy pa nga, hindi lang laglag panty.\n\nKung saan siya pinanganak, doon din siya namatay. Parang metaphor ng \"Ashes to Ashes, Dust to Dust\"","poster":"img/perfume-the-story-of-a-murderer-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Boogie Nights","year":1997,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-04","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":374,"review":"Sa una pa lang napaindak na agad ako dahil sa opening music na katunog ng TikTok song na Lil Boo Thang. Then boom, long continuous shot agad from PTA. Controlled chaos. Ang daming familiar faces agad na pinapasok niya sa frame. \n\nNakakatawa yung binebentang sound system na TK421 daw. Feels like a direct callback to Star Wars. Before 30 minutes, ramdam ko na agad na this film is hot and funny. Nandun yung PTA humor. This is a film about a film, about an industry, and about people inside it.\n\nMark Wahlberg surprised me here. He has intensity while still having that matinee idol face. Ngayon ko lang nakita yung ganitong klase ng acting niya. \n\nIt’s also refreshing to see Philip Seymour Hoffman doing comedy. Ang awkward niya pero sobrang effective.\n\nAmber (Jullianne Moore) is so seductive, commanding kahit tahimik lang.\n\nParang Guardians of the Galaxy din ito in a way, ang daming memorable songs. Music really carries the energy of the film.\n\nYung filming scene mismo is something I haven’t watched and felt in a film before. Ang unique ng vibe. Love the wardrobe, hair dressing, and production design ng 70s and 80s. Sobrang alive ng world. \n\nSabi na pipitik anytime si Little Bill eh. Ramdam mo na yung tension sa kanya kahit joke joke lang sa umpisa. I’m also happy that Reed stuck with Eddie even when he’s been fired by Jack. May loyalty pa rin despite everything.\n\nAs time goes by, the film gets serious. Tinouch na niya yung ugly side ng porn business, lalo na drugs and other repercussions. Yung stigma na dala ng actors even outside the industry, whether it’s loan sa new business venture or custody ng anak. Even the musical score became heavy and dark, with long continuous tones that reminded me of There Will Be Blood or One Battle After Another.\n\nNakakakaba yung scene na nagbenta sila ng coke. Hindi ko alam kung safe ba sila or hindi. Pure mayhem. Parang biglang naging Tarantino film yung tension at chaos.\n\nHighly entertaining. Hindi ko namalayan yung oras habang pinapanood ko. Yung last few scenes are warm, kasi parang family pa rin sila despite everything. Even si Little Bill may painting pa na nakahang in memory of him.","poster":"img/boogie-nights-1997.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"8MM","year":1999,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-03","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":200,"review":"Has that slow paced vibe with a strong thriller atmosphere and score. Around 30 minutes in, it’s hard to get hooked because of the slow pace. This feels like an old school dark web black market. I had to be on guard since I didn’t know what I would watch next.\n\nNagulat ako nandito si Norman Reedus. Matagal na pala siyang artist. Ang bata pa niya dito. Joaquin Phoenix is a great supporting actor here. Peter Stormare delivers such a powerful performance as always. They both bring an edge to the film that keeps it from being flat.\n\nIt feels like Purge: Anarchy at times, kidnapping people and hunting them down for sport. Rich people can do whatever they want, even for their twisted desires.\n\nWhat I didn’t fully get is why the main character became so obsessed with revenge. I understand the anger, but the level of fixation felt a bit forced for me.\n\nThe final scene with Machine is absolutely suspenseful. That part really works. But that’s it. There’s nothing profound or groundbreaking about the film. It just shows that pure evil exists, that some people do horrible things for no deeper reason other than they want to.","poster":"img/8mm-1999.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sentimental Value","year":2025,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":78,"review":"I love how natural Nora and Agnes are. Rachel was also very natural in her performance.\n\nInga Ibsdotter Lilleaas sometimes looks like Cobie Smulders.\n\nI feel like Gustav (Stellan Skarsgård) only wanted to make a film about his mother so he could express his unsaid feelings and reconnect with his family, but he just couldn’t say it directly.\n\nThe film touches on how childhood can make or break a person.\n\nI wasn’t really moved by this film, though.","poster":"img/sentimental-value-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Best Offer","year":2013,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":128,"review":"I kept on guessing about the mysterious girl behind the door and the mechanical thing.\n\nIs the girl a ghost? May disease? Time traveler?\n\nIs the mechanical piece something that traveled from the past? What’s that C3PO-looking thing? It feels really out of touch with the rest of the film.\n\nAnd what’s the deal with the midget reciting numbers? Ang weird talaga.\n\nPero nung nireveal na siya, parang normal lang naman. The mystery dragged on for too long with little to no clues added along the way.\n\nIt feels like Beauty and the Beast but with the genders swapped. Ang cringe ng relationship nila, parang maglolo na sila.\n\nSo is this a heist film? So forged din pala yung love?\n\nThe twist didn’t really hit. Not quite impactful.","poster":"img/the-best-offer-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Long Walk","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":369,"review":"Menacing Mark Hamill as always. He brings that intimidating authority, ramdam mo agad yung pressure. Nice to see familiar faces too: Charlie Plummer from Looking for Alaska, na mabait doon pero dito kupal, which works really well for the dynamic. David Jonsson from Alien Romulus also stands out here, mas maraming lines ngayon and the same quality of acting. He feels more confident and grounded, hindi lang filler character.\n\nIt is another contest game to remove oneself from poverty, like The Running Man and The Hunger Games, but what sets this apart is that it has a lot of heart. Even though they are competitors, they genuinely care for each other. Hindi lang survival for the prize, survival din of their humanity. You feel that they are walking not just for themselves, but sometimes for the people beside them.\n\nThis is more about cardiovascular and muscular endurance mixed with mental toughness, because it is nonstop walking. Bawal magpahinga. Intense yung uphill, mentally and physically exhausting, and you can really feel the fatigue building up as the miles pile on. Hindi ito tungkol sa bilis or strength alone, kundi sa kung sino yung kayang hindi bumigay.\n\nKudos to the actors. Ang hirap ng continuous shots habang naglalakad, umaarte, at nagdedeliver ng mahahabang lines nang sabay sabay. Hindi lang physical acting, kundi emotional endurance din. The only thing I kept wondering is how they even remember each other’s names in such a short period of time of getting to know each other, especially under that kind of stress.\n\nGanda ng shot sa mile 201. Silhouette plus backlit shot, very striking and symbolic. Maganda rin sa mile 260, parang sunset landscape sa Sinners, may sense of beauty kahit nasa gitna ng exhaustion at hopelessness.\n\nGanda ng twist sa bastard ni Major. It reframes his presence and makes his role more meaningful. The last walk was dramatic and cinematic. Raining, yellow brown color, and vengeance served. Ramdam mo na hindi na lang ito tungkol sa pagkapanalo, kundi about closure.\n\nAng rich ng backstory ng bawat isa, and lahat may malalim na dahilan bakit sila lumalaban. Hindi sila basta contestants. They are people running away from something, or walking toward something they desperately need.","poster":"img/the-long-walk-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Legion","year":2010,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":373,"review":"The film starts with nice cinematic landscape photography and detailed close up shots. It immediately sets a serious tone. Angas ni Paul Bettany dito. He has that calm but threatening presence that fits the role well.\n\nThe father and son pep talk scene works. Simple, grounded, and gives emotional context before everything goes to chaos. The diner action sequence is thrilling. \n\nI like the color grading here. Yellowish brownish cinema colors that were very common during that era. It gives the film a dusty, apocalyptic feel. The score is also strong, both during action and suspense. It keeps the tension consistent without overpowering the scenes.\n\nThe goal of the film is clear. It’s about surviving the wrath of God who has lost faith in humanity. Here, God and the angels are portrayed as the real horror of the world, not demons. That inversion is interesting.\n\nVisually, the movie looks like a heavily stylized late 2000s to early 2010s Hollywood film. This was the era of high contrast color grading, heavy yellows and browns, strong teal shadows, bloom highlights, and aggressive digital sharpening. Movies like Transformers, Iron Man 2, Man of Steel, Percy Jackson, I Am Number Four, and Noah all shared that look. Cinematography during this time leaned toward spectacle over subtlety, with bold lighting, dramatic skies, and very polished CGI heavy frames. Legion fits perfectly into that visual language.\n\nThe CGI here is also peak for its time. It holds up better than expected, especially in creature work and large scale destruction.\n\nWhere it loses me is the resolution. So the ending implies that Michael restores God’s faith and mercy toward humanity, and that defiance is better than obedience, like Gabriel choosing to follow orders blindly. That raises big theological questions. Does that mean God here is not all loving, all powerful, or all knowing?\n\nThe ending feels forced and not fully thought through. It presents big ideas but doesn’t explore them deeply enough. Nakulangan ako sa lalim. It could have been more thought provoking, especially given how bold the premise is. Siguro intended magkaron ng part 2 but was scrapped kaya parang bitin. \n\nStrong visuals, solid action, interesting concept. But it stops short of saying something truly meaningful by the end.","poster":"img/legion-2010.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Uncut Gems","year":2019,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24","netflix"],"words":330,"review":"Sa simula pa lang, may kadiri na agad. Ramdam ko na yung tension agad from the opening moments.\n\nWild yung transition from a zoom in sa diamond papunta sa cosmic clouds, tapos biglang sa human flesh, sinabayan ng synthetic score na parang Stranger Things. Parang sinasabi agad ng pelikula na papasok ka sa isip ng isang taong broken at addicted sa chaos.\n\nAdam Sandler is completely different here. Hindi siya yung usual Adam Sandler. He commits fully to the character.\n\nHigh intensity yung takbo ng mundo niya. Business deals running left to right. Sale, loan, gambling. High risk lahat. Three to four way deals sabay sabay. Ganito talaga pag highly leveraged ka. Isang maling galaw lang, domino effect na lahat.\n\nYung short kidnap and shakedown scene sobrang intense. Walang breathing room.\n\nNakakainis yung itsura at character ng asawa niya. Kuha talaga yung inis ko, same level ng irritation na binigay ni Skyler White sa Breaking Bad. Unsupportive, dagdag pressure sa already chaotic na buhay.\n\nEvery time nasa office siya, puro chaos. Ang galing ng direction, editing, at camera work. May mga long takes na lalo pang nagpaparamdam kung gaano kagulo at ka claustrophobic yung mundo ni Howard.\n\nNail biting yung third act. Ganda ng score, tuloy tuloy yung anxiety rush. Keith William Richards really helps create that chaos. Hindi ka pinapahinga.\n\nNaparamdam din sakin yung rush ng pagtaya. Ramdam mo yung dopamine addiction niya sa gambling through the music. Successful yung film sa pag immerse sakin sa isip ni Howard. Yung anticipation ng panalo, yung taas ng stakes, yung kaba habang naghihintay ng resulta, and eventually yung win. Bumalik ako sa college days ko, nanonood ng UST Tigers. Ganung klaseng tension.\n\nSabi ko na e. Dapat hindi na niya pinakawalan, lalo na alam niyang may firearms si Phil. Pero ayun, that’s what happens when you keep playing in sketchy territory and pushing your luck.\n\nStressful, exhausting, pero sobrang effective. Hindi siya comfortable panoorin, pero yun mismo yung strength ng pelikula.","poster":"img/uncut-gems-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Late Night with the Devil","year":2023,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":231,"review":"Good yung prologue. It clearly sets the backstory and prepares you for what kind of world and tone the film is going for.\n\nDavid Dastmalchian as Jack Delroy is very convincing. Para talaga siyang legit late night host from that era. May charm, may authority, pero ramdam mo rin na everything he does is driven by ratings and show business.\n\nAng galing ni Ingrid Torelli as Lilly. Grabe yung titig niya sa camera, parang diretso siyang tumitingin sa kaluluwa mo. Unsettling. Pati yung transformation scenes niya ang galing, talagang demonic ang dating.\n\nMaganda yung production design. The way the footage is edited and presented really feels like the 70s. May texture, may analog feel, and believable yung TV broadcast aesthetic.\n\nThe prosthetics are great. Practical effects really shine here and add credibility to the horror.\n\nWhere it slightly loses me is the manifestation of evil. Medyo gimmicky for me yung CGI electricity. It pulled me out a bit compared to how grounded and effective the practical elements were. Reminds me of The Darkest Hour where aliens are invisible, electrical energy entities and they kill humans using electric discharges.\n\nMedyo naguluhan din ako sa ending. I get the intention, but the execution didn’t fully land for me.\n\nOverall, strong concept, great performances, and solid atmosphere. It just stumbles a bit when it leans too hard into CGI and abstraction at the end.","poster":"img/late-night-with-the-devil-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Don't Look Up","year":2021,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":239,"review":"First few minutes pa lang, pinakita na agad ni Leonardo yung terrified look niya. Ramdam mo na agad yung intensity ng acting nya. \n\nLess than ten minutes in, the stakes are already high. Pero pagdating sa White House, it was not matched with the utmost urgency. Parang another day in the office lang yung briefing, which really dilutes the gravity of the situation. \n\nThe film nails meme culture. Yung moment na may taong nag lost ng cool while telling the truth, tapos imbes na seryosohin, ginawang content at traffic ng media. Truth doesn’t matter as much as engagement.\n\nPoliticizing tragedy is another big hit. Spectacle, bravado speeches, optics over substance. Peter Isherwell is extremely annoying with his corporate greed and fake smile. Perfect representation ng tech bro savior complex.\n\nThe line “I’m grateful we tried” sums up the ending for me. Hindi siya horror. It ends with warmth. Acceptance. Peace. They did what they could.\n\nNakakalungkot, nakakainis, at nakakagalit na film. At the same time, sobrang accurate na sumasalamin sa reality ng political landscape ngayon. \n\nThe government spins the story to push their own selfish narrative so people won’t seek the truth, won’t think critically, won’t do their own due diligence, and will just follow whatever they’re told.\n\nThis film shows us how helpless we are against a corrupt government and corporate greed. Hindi dahil walang solusyon, kundi dahil walang gustong gumawa ng solusyon to keep us all safe.","poster":"img/don-t-look-up-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Magazine Dreams","year":2023,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-01","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":430,"review":"The film opens with a slow motion stage posing scene. A sculpted figure under the lights. Right away, you know this is about obsession and control.\n\nThe morning routine feels very real. Posing, looking in the mirror, eating lots of eggs, drinking protein shakes. Familiar lahat sakin because I’ve been in the same regimen before. The repetition hits hard. You really feel how consuming that lifestyle can be.\n\nThere are anamorphic lenses used for more intimate mid close up shots, making everything feel tighter and more personal. Visually, the film is stylized but still grounded. The color grading leans teal and orange, with visible film grain and lots of optical flares. \n\nAs a character, he is awkward and socially off. May anger issues na simmering, waiting to explode. The film directly addresses drug abuse, body dysmorphia, and narcissism, without romanticizing any of it.\n\nSobrang body conscious niya to the point na naghahanap siya ng validation from a hooker. That desperation really shows how empty he is inside.\n\nThe restaurant date scene is both funny and painful. He expects his date to understand the bodybuilding world, which shows how niche and isolated that bubble really is. Mostly men lang talaga ang may alam sa bodybuilders. Nag monologue pa siya tungkol sa motivation habang bored na bored na yung ka date niya. When she leaves, biglang nag cheat meal siya. A bodybuilder’s way of punishing themselves. Pure self sabotage.\n\nHim still going to the competition despite being smashed and trashed by the nephews of the shop he destroyed reminded me of Whiplash. Same energy as Andrew pushing through after getting hit by a car. Obsession over everything.\n\nJonathan Majors is great here. If Marvel handled him properly and maximized this side of him, maybe people would have accepted him better as Kang. But sadly, that didn’t happen.\n\nSa diner scene, nag roid rage siya. At that point, malinaw na he is a sick man. When his training stops, he shifts into binge eating. Parang ako. Too relatable.\n\nThe film also plays with the idea of “don’t meet your heroes” in a very explicit way. Cinonfirm din ng film na ito na may mga bading talaga sa bodybuilding competition world.\n\nThis takes a wild turn. I thought it was going to be Pumping Iron all the way, but it goes much deeper. Especially with how easy it is to buy firearms in America.\n\nI thought I’d come out inspired to train again. Instead, the film left me with a portrait of a deeply broken man. Childhood trauma manifesting hard in adulthood. Deep drama.","poster":"img/magazine-dreams-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Havoc","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-31","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":507,"review":"CGI car chase scenes don’t work for me. Mukhang cut scene sa isang video game. Hindi tangible, and the camera movements are so cool to the point na parang impossible ireplicate in real life cinematography. The whole visual feels like a noir film shot on green screen, parang Watchmen ni Zack Snyder: highly stylized, contrasty, moody lighting with a graphic novel feel. The problem is, the backgrounds sometimes feel detached from physical reality. It looks artistic, but it doesn’t feel like a real space.\n\nEven the world building and production design feel inspired by Zack’s visuals. Lots of film grain too, which adds texture but also makes everything feel a bit artificial.\n\nWhat really stands out is the gritty, haunting, and suspenseful score. May certain notes that immediately reminded me of Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice. The score leans heavily on low brass, heavy percussion, deep taiko like drums, ominous horns, and sustained dissonant strings that make the tension feel big and primal. There’s also layered low synth and ambient noise that gives it a dark, almost dystopian tone, very similar to the Zimmer and Junkie XL approach.\n\nThe film also touches on some real-world ideas. Big tickets in politics have tentacles in law enforcement, and law enforcement has eyes and ears in the streets. That part felt grounded and believable.\n\nTom Hardy looks better here as a guilt stricken, sleep deprived individual than him being Eddie Brock. Ang bobo at ang bagal lang talaga ng security detail ng mayor, hindi kapanipaniwala haha. Still, the action itself is brutal and staged right, especially when paired with a Christmas song. That juxtaposition works.\n\nThis is basically a cop racing against his co-cop to save the ass of someone he owes. Very chaotic, very messy.\n\nThe club fight scene clearly shows the style and energy of The Raid director. Unstoppable energy. The camera movements are playful but still appropriate to the amount of action. Nice amount of blood too. Knives, guns, and whatever can be used as a weapon. At times, it becomes a 4–5 way battle, parang battle royale. Despite the chaos, the choreography remains readable. Eargasm yung sound design for guns. Ramdam mo yung raw impact. It feels like a lawless, bloody war on drugs city.\n\nGot me clapping when Ellie was finally able to shoot the bad guy. The slow-mo shots during the cabin siege were great too. Total bloodbath. The title really earns itself. Havoc, mayhem, messy in a satisfying way. Ito yung klaseng action na hindi ka bitin. Binuhos lahat ng blood lust, action, at fight scenes. Hindi nag hold back, pero at the same time hindi overkill na parang dinadrag na lang yung violence tulad ng BuyBust or some John Wick scenes.\n\nTimothy Olyphant was good here. Who would have thought he’d be an effective villain? I loved his good guy role in I Am Number Four as Henry, so this was a nice switch.\n\nLove it when Jake killed Jake. What a prick.\n\nAnother great Christmas movie next to Die Hard.","poster":"img/havoc-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Woman in the Yard","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-31","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","hbomax"],"words":188,"review":"The film uses circular anamorphic lenses, giving it a dreamy and unstable feel. Parang laging off balance yung mundo, bagay na bagay sa mental state ng character.\n\nThe score is effective in building tension, mystery, and danger. Hindi siya loud, pero constant yung pressure. May Shikamaru vibes yung paggamit ng shadow, parang yung anino mismo ang gumagalaw at may sariling will.\n\nKadiri yung hallucination sa sugat. It’s disturbing in a very intimate way. You can feel that she’s slowly losing her mind under depression and pressure. Hindi lang takot yung nararamdaman, kundi discomfort.\n\nWhat makes this unique is that the enemy isn’t darkness, but light. That’s a really smart twist for a horror film. Kudos to Tay for figuring that out. It flips the usual horror language and makes daylight feel unsafe.\n\nThe portrayal of suicidal thoughts is handled well. It shows that kind of immeasurable pain where someone convinces themselves that leaving their kids is actually for their own good, because staying might only harm them. \n\nThis is a psychological horror through and through. Hindi puro sa jump scares, kundi sa slow collapse ng mind and guilt.","poster":"img/the-woman-in-the-yard-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fackham Hall","year":2025,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-31","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":124,"review":"Parang Scary Movie type of parody comedy siya. Literal na stupid jokes, yung tipong intentionally bobo. Physical gags, exaggerated reactions, awkward timing, and jokes na sinasadyang walang finesse. It’s not clever humor, more on absurd and slapstick levels where the punchline is how dumb the situation is.\n\nHindi ko rin gets yung ibang jokes, mostly because hindi ko pa napanood yung mismong movies na pinaparody nila.\n\nThat said, production design is great. High class elite talaga yung settings, mukhang sosyal at polished. The lighting is also handled very well, clean and classy, which actually contrasts nicely with how stupid the humor is.\n\nNothing much to unpack here. Hindi siya memorable, hindi rin nakakainis. Just a pass time movie kapag wala ka talagang maisip panoorin.","poster":"img/fackham-hall-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Highest 2 Lowest","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-29","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","a24"],"words":313,"review":"At its core, the film quietly asks a difficult question: would you do the same sacrifice for someone who isn’t your family, the same way you would for your own blood?\n\nFor a crime thriller, this film is surprisingly chill. The tone is lighter than expected, especially with how the score is used. Instead of going full tense or ominous, the director flirts with energetic Puerto Rican music even as the tension rises. That contrast keeps the film moving without feeling too heavy or oppressive.\n\nThe director also plays with perspective through visuals. There’s a noticeable switch in color grading and LUTs depending on whose point of view you’re following. It adds to the sense of confusion and moral imbalance, making you feel how unstable the situation really is.\n\nDenzel is really good here. He convincingly portrays a conflicted founder businessman who is also a family man and a loyal friend. You can feel him constantly weighing responsibility, loyalty, and survival. Nothing feels exaggerated. He carries the quiet weight of someone trying to do right but trapped by consequences.\n\nThis is very much a story about the consequences of a good deed. One decision, made with the right intention, slowly spirals into something messier and harder to control.\n\nNice touch with the easter egg on the unit number A24. Simple, but satisfying to catch.\n\nAnd yeah, I really hated Yung Felon's character. Just a stupid piece of shit. Which I think means the film did its job there.\n\nI appreciated the ending resolution. In the end, it feels like he finds a way to have both worlds. Business and family don’t have to be mutually exclusive. By choosing to build a small family owned record label, he creates something grounded, personal, and honest. Not a perfect win, but a meaningful one.\n\nQuietly tense, morally uncomfortable, and more reflective than your usual crime thriller.","poster":"img/highest-2-lowest-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Die My Love","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-29","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":369,"review":"The film starts energetic, chaotic, and completely unhinged. Right away, there’s a sense of danger, and I was honestly terrified for the baby from the very beginning.\n\nThe use of circular anamorphic lenses gives everything a dreamy, unstable feeling, like reality is constantly slipping. There’s foreshadowing everywhere, and it never lets you feel safe. One of the most striking moments is when the ink mixes with her breast milk. It’s subtle but powerful, clearly signaling that this is a story about womanhood, and motherhood.\n\nI noticed a lot of night for day photography. Sometimes it works beautifully, especially with silhouettes and composition. Other times, it looks fake, similar to what Jordan Peele did in Nope. But here, I think it’s intentional. It adds to the idea that they no longer know what is day or night, what is real or not. Everything feels disoriented.\n\nI understand the postpartum angle, but some choices really confused me. Bringing in a puppy when you already have a newborn feels irresponsible. And Jackson not touching his wife at all? Grace is clearly still attractive, so the emotional distance feels deliberate and strange.\n\nWhat really bothered me is Jackson’s sudden character shift. He starts off as a decent guy, then suddenly becomes careless. Leaving the baby alone just to go for a drive is insane. That alone made them both feel reckless.\n\nAt some point, I stopped believing this was just postpartum depression. Killing a dog, self harm through mirrors, scratching walls, destroying the bathroom. This feels far beyond that. At this level, she should have been hospitalized immediately. The risk to the baby was too high. Instead of addressing it properly, they even got married. Jackson honestly comes off as low IQ here.\n\nI felt bad for him though. He’s clearly helpless, watching someone he loves deteriorate. But it’s also frustrating that Grace is released from the facility when she’s clearly not well. That decision felt irresponsible.\n\nI’m not really a fan of the ending, but thank God the baby is fine. That was the only real relief in the entire experience.\n\nDisturbing, stressful, and intentionally uncomfortable. The film wants you unsettled, and it succeeds, even if some choices are hard to accept.","poster":"img/die-my-love-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Wall to Wall","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-29","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","korean"],"words":252,"review":"Watching this film, I felt very lucky that I have financial freedom and I’m not stuck in a badly chosen investment vehicle, whether property or crypto. This film talks about bad debts and how people become gullible to pump and dump schemes out of desperation, hoping for a jackpot.\n\nAng galing ng visuals in showing psychological breakdown. Ramdam mo yung pressure and mental collapse as things spiral out of control. Nakakatawa at nakakaawa din yung greediness ng characters. May opportunity na sana, pero ayaw pa ring igrab. Ganun talaga kapag hindi maayos ang financial foundation.\n\nThe film briefly touches on the suicidal tendencies of some crypto users/traders, adding a darker layer to the desperation without overdoing it.\n\nThe editing and camera movements really help capture the urgency and danger. There are nail biting moments throughout. Maganda rin yung twist, very unique for me, hindi yung madaling hulaan.\n\nAs the story progresses, a bigger conspiracy starts to form. It also touches on technology, how everything is hackable nowadays, and how heavily we rely on tech inside our own homes, to the point that it can become the downfall of our privacy and security.\n\nIto yung kind of film na akala mo alam mo na yung buong plot, pero palalim nang palalim beyond surface level knowledge. In the end, it’s also a film about corporate greed and substandard projects, judges being in the pocket of contractors, and journalists frustrated because they can’t uncover the whole truth behind corruption.\n\nStand out performance for Seo Hyun-woo.","poster":"img/wall-to-wall-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ballad of a Small Player","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-29","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":170,"review":"Set design is colorful since Macau ang depicted setting, may pagka Wes Anderson yung vibe ng interiors. It feels playful and stylized, which is strongly contrasted by the poverty boat house set in Lammas Island. That contrast works really well in grounding the film back to reality.\n\nScores are good too. They don’t overpower the scenes but help sustain the mood.\n\nColin Farrell disappears in this role again. You don’t see the actor anymore, just the character. Sa dark scenes, parang same yung lighting and color grading with Joker. Muted, grim, and heavy.\n\nGanda ng technical execution sa filmmaking nito. Very controlled and confident visually.\n\nKala ko magkaka character development na nung kalagitnaan. Potek, nagnakaw na naman at pinanglaro. Addict talaga. Then later on, doon ko talaga naramdaman yung character development. He is done gambling. \n\nYung twist na patay na pala si Dao Ming. Wtf talaga. That one landed hard.\n\nStylish but grounded. The film doesn’t romanticize addiction. It shows how it keeps pulling you back until there’s nothing left.","poster":"img/ballad-of-a-small-player-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Delicious","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-29","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":88,"review":"Magaling mang manipulate si Teodora. Ramdam mo kung gaano siya ka calculated at ka smooth sa pag control ng situation. Dahil dito, parang ayaw ko na tuloy maghire ng kasambahay.\n\nUnfortunately, hindi masyadong nag work yung payoff for me. Since hindi naman masyadong pinakita yung cannibalism, parang kulang yung impact. Nahulaan ko na rin siya sa simula pa lang, so walang masyadong shock or tension pagdating sa reveal. Mas prefer ko yung Fresh (2022). \n\nInteresting setup, strong manipulation angle, but the execution at the end didn’t fully deliver.","poster":"img/delicious-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Night Always Comes","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":175,"review":"Her mom is a terrible mom. Inuna pa yung sasakyan kaysa pamilya. Kuha agad yung inis ko sa kanya, which I think is intentional.\n\nAfter the hotel room scene, the film suddenly tightens up. The colors get harsher, the score becomes more aggressive, and the tension doesn’t let up. The uninterrupted shot after the opening of safe scene is really effective. Ramdam mo yung panic and urgency, and the rising musical score sustains that tension.\n\nVanessa Kirby is very convincing here. She’s obviously gorgeous, but somehow she sells being desperate, broke, and on the edge. It shouldn’t work on paper, but it does.\n\nThe director also uses anamorphic lenses, which helps give the city a stretched, suffocating feel as the night goes on.\n\nAs the movie progresses, you slowly understand why she’s acting the way she is. It’s not just selfishness. She’s traumatized. She doesn’t want to end up back on the streets again, and every decision she makes is driven by that fear.\n\nUncomfortable, tense, and emotionally frustrating in a way that feels earned.","poster":"img/night-always-comes-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Steve","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":233,"review":"The first thing I noticed is the look. Circular bokeh lenses are used, film grain is very evident, and there are interchanging shots that feel like old film or documentary footage. It immediately gives the film a rough, lived in texture.\n\nThe camera work is chaotic. It pulls you straight into the environment. You feel the disorder, the noise, and the emotional instability of the place. It fits the story because this is a world full of out of control, angry, and damaged kids.\n\nSteve is interesting to watch. He’s applying something like How to Win Friends and Influence People in real life, using charisma, patience, and care to connect with the boys. Bagay na bagay kay Cillian Murphy yung role. He looks exhausted in a way that feels real, parang matagal na niyang dala yung bigat at sakit ng mga batang inaalagaan niya.\n\nJay Lycurgo is also very good here as \"Shy\". At first he feels quiet and withdrawn, but eventually you realize pareho rin pala sila ni Steve. He numbs himself and avoids dealing with his own problems.\n\nThe suicide scene is heavy. It hits hard because you see that they’re both drowning in depression, just in different ways.\n\nOverall, the film reminded me of 13 Reasons Why in tone and subject matter. Raw, uncomfortable, and emotionally draining, but sincere in how it portrays broken people trying to survive each other.","poster":"img/steve-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Swiped","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2025-12-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","disneyplus"],"words":196,"review":"Lily James is so beautiful as always. But more than that, she carries the film with a quiet strength.\n\nThe pitch scenes are great. They feel very natural, raw, and intuitive. Walang OA na movie dialogue. It feels like real founders trying to sell an idea, reading the room, adjusting on the fly.\n\nThe energy in the campus really spikes once the users start coming in. You can feel the momentum, the excitement, and the chaos of something finally working. At the same time, I can really empathize with her being left out even though she is a co-founder. That feeling of watching something you helped build slowly move away from you is painful.\n\nThe film doesn’t sugarcoat the ugliness of the legal reality. Contracts, power moves, and technicalities can easily erase people from their own creation. It’s frustrating, but very real.\n\nAt its core, this is a story of redemption. Of standing your ground and fighting for what is right, even when the system is stacked against you. The film believes that doing the right thing eventually comes back to you, not immediately, but in a way that restores dignity.\n\nSimple, grounded, and quietly motivating.","poster":"img/swiped-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Predator: Badlands","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","predator"],"words":317,"review":"May strong motivation agad sa umpisa pa lang, so mabilis kang mahook sa story.\n\nMaraming action. Sa early part ng movie, sobrang dami ng CGI to the point na nakaka exhaust na siya. Some shots don’t feel believable yet, parang overloaded. Pero maganda yung epic musical score that helps carry the scale.\n\nThe film uses anamorphic lenses, which gives it that wide, cinematic feel. World building is also nice. Mukhang may combination ng real locations, practical sets, and Volume. May shots na mukhang painting din. Maraming shots dito na ramdam mong pang IMAX talaga. \n\nInteresting makita yung Predator as the prey. I like how they showed the expressions of the Yautja. Hindi lang siya monster, may personality at emotion din pala.\n\nMay standout kills, especially yung bone bison kill. Brutal and memorable. Visual popcorn!\n\nIt’s one action sequence after another. Some fight scenes honestly feel like video games. Pero later on, dito na nagsimulang gumanda yung CGI. Mas believable na yung textures ng monsters. The battle with Kalisk looks realistic. Parang Monster Hunter vibes but make it x100 better.\n\nAng ganda ng shot nung nabutas yung katawan ng isang synthetic guard, tapos yung reveal shot ni Dek after. Ang angas, money shot!\n\nThe fight choreography of the duo, upper and lower body coordination ni Thia, ang galing. \n\nI also liked Dek’s character development. From being a loner, he slowly becomes a wolf, someone who finds belonging and identity. Also a protector.\n\nThe Weyland Yutani look is consistent here, similar to Alien Romulus. That yellowish, dusty, industrial mood of the camp works really well. Ang ganda rin ng texture ng power loader mech ni Tessa. Honestly, mas maganda pa yung texture nito compared sa mech in Avatar.\n\nThe ending battle against his father got me clapping. That closing line, “I have my own clan,” lands emotionally and thematically.\n\nBiggest regret ko talaga is not watching this in IMAX.","poster":"img/predator-badlands-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Assessment","year":2024,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":398,"review":"The production design immediately stands out. Especially the architecture and color of the house. Very unique. Yung mosaic windows talaga yung pinaka tumatak.\n\nAng galing umarte ni Elizabeth Olsen. She carries a lot of the film emotionally. \n\nDay two pa lang, weird na agad. The assessor starts pushing buttons. Psychological, emotional, parental. Ramdam mo yung intrusion.\n\nThe musical score is great. May mga voices and breathing sounds that reminded me of Dark on Netflix. Mixed with percussions that feel like a mystery puzzle or a psychological thriller. \n\nYung title cards for the days are honestly painful to look at. The color choice is harsh and distracting.\n\nAs a parent, my instincts kept kicking in. And honestly, bagsak rin sila for me. But at the same time, no one is ever really prepared to be a parent. That discomfort feels intentional.\n\nAng galing ng acting ng assessor when she switches to childlike behavior. It made me think she might have a split personality. Very unsettling, very effective.\n\nThe world building is mostly in the script. Hindi masyadong shown visually, but you can imagine the dystopia through dialogue and context. It works in a subtle way.\n\nThe argument about population control makes sense. Those who witnessed famine and calamity are naturally against adding more people. They see having a child as vain because it consumes limited resources. Meanwhile, those who didn’t experience those horrors don’t think twice about conserving or giving up the possibility of having a child. That moral divide is one of the strongest parts of the film.\n\nAng ganda ng color palette during the greenhouse fire scene. Yellow and brown cinematic tones. It reminded me of the first few frames in Dune Part Two. Very striking.\n\nHaving a child, in the context of this film, means exposing everything about yourself. Your fears, guilt, selfishness, and capacity to love.\n\nBehind all the assessor’s facade, she is also human. And the performance really sells that reveal. The twist that no one has actually passed the assessment in six years is strong. The system exists not to approve parents, but to give people hope. To teach them how to love a child.\n\nIn the end, the three of them each make a choice. One chooses to stop the guilt. One chooses to live with a lie. One chooses to face reality beyond comfort.\n\nDeeply philosophical. Uncomfortable in a good way.","poster":"img/the-assessment-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"G20","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-27","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":373,"review":"Early on, the film throws a very classic and cliché question at the president: how do we know we can trust you to keep us safe when you can’t even keep your own house in order? That line sets the tone, and you already know what kind of political action thriller this is going to be.\n\nMenacing si Anthony Starr as always. Bagay sa kanya yung role, pero by the second act, medyo nagiging cliché na. Yung boss na puro sigaw sa mga stupid subordinates, medyo gasgas na.\n\nGood thing naincorporate yung deepfake sa storyline. The film shows how it can be used to manipulate politics, and how people are so gullible with fake news. Even if alam na ng tao na hindi totoo, it can still crash the market. \n\nNapapalakpak ako nung sinaksak ng South Korean First Lady si Anthony Starr. Revenge moment talaga. Pero nasira agad yung impact nung buhay pa pala siya and parang wala lang yung saksak kung makalakad.\n\nTawang tawa rin ako nung nahulog yung crypto wallet sa helipad.\n\nMedyo pilit yung twist na si Joanna pala yung insider. Hindi siya ganun kaearned and parang nilagay lang for the sake of twist.\n\nYung climax may mga moments na ang stupid ng direction, parang pinilit lang pataasin yung tension. Still, may mga close calls na typical sa action movies.\n\nHighlight yung elevator fight scene. Ang ganda ng choreography, and effective yung paggamit ng tight, enclosed space. Malinis panoorin yung action.\n\nMay character development din na naappreciate ko. Yung Prime Minister na ayaw sundan yung US president at first, pero later siya pa yung nag urge sa VP na sundin ito. Si Elena rin, from being unsure as a head of the International Monetary Fund, siya na mismo yung naglead sa Beast palabas ng perimeter.\n\nOverall, this feels like White House Down or Olympus Has Fallen, pero set in a foreign country. Bagay kay Viola Davis yung role, especially yung military aspect, very natural sa kanya yung tenacity.\n\nMay cliché din na bago magfinal boss, may lalabanan munang mas malaki at mas malakas.\n\nIn the end, G20 doesn’t reinvent the genre. Familiar political action thriller siya with some good moments, some forced twists, and a lot of well worn tropes.","poster":"img/g20-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Death of a Unicorn","year":2025,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":183,"review":"There’s a really strong connection moment early on that feels almost IMAX worthy. That zoom in pulls you into something bigger, parang multiversal level, may Doctor Strange vibe. Film grain is very noticeable, which adds texture to the experience.\n\nThe film clearly tackles corporate greed, especially once a cure for cancer is discovered. It’s interesting how that miracle immediately collides with substance abuse and exploitation. Instead of hope, people react with excess and corruption, which feels sadly believable.\n\nThe CGI of the unicorns is genuinely good. Especially in the close ups. The fur, skin, muscle, and even bone structure feel real. Hindi siya mukhang fake or weightless. You can tell effort was put into making the creatures feel tangible, parang totoong hayop talaga ang kinunan.\n\nThat said, I didn’t really connect with the father and daughter storyline. Emotionally, it didn’t anchor the film enough for me. Because of that, the horror elements never really had real stakes. Walang sense of danger that sticks. It looks good, but it doesn’t hit hard.\n\nVisually impressive, conceptually interesting, but emotionally and tension wise, it falls short.","poster":"img/death-of-a-unicorn-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Wake Up Dead Man","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":174,"review":"Nakakatawa agad yung confession scene. May dark humor agad.\n\nAng galing nung harlot whore scene. Ang ganda ng kulay at ng distribution ng ilaw. The technique used there is motivated lighting with controlled color contrast. The light feels intentional.\n\nRian Johnson uses a lot of long zoom shots here, which is a nightmare for focus pullers.\n\nThe film is clearly a satire of the Catholic Church. Hindi siya subtle, but it is smart. It pokes at rituals, power, guilt, and performative morality.\n\nJosh Brolin looks like Jeff Bridges here. His homilies are powerful, not just in writing but in delivery. He commands attention whenever he speaks.\n\nThe ensemble cast is great. Walang weak link. Everyone feels like they belong in this weird, morally messy world.\n\nThe formula is very different from the first two films. The plot is very unpredictable. Hindi mo talaga alam kung saan pupunta, and that’s part of the fun.\n\nAng ganda ng payoff. Sulit yung oras. The ending feels earned and satisfying without over explaining everything. Best among the series.","poster":"img/wake-up-dead-man-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"It Was Just an Accident","year":2025,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":242,"review":"17 minutes in, ramdam mo na agad na quiet film ito. Tahimik pero mabigat yung atmosphere.\n\nThe story revolves around a group of friends who kidnapped a man they believe tortured them in the past. The whole film is about them figuring out if tama ba talaga ang hinala nila and whether they should take revenge.\n\nEverything shifts when the kidnapped man’s wife gives birth. Biglang nagbago yung moral weight ng sitwasyon. The revenge fantasy starts to crack.\n\nFunny thing is, for people who kidnapped someone, they seem pretty chill. Parang normal lang yung conversations nila at times, which makes the situation even more unsettling.\n\nVisually, it feels like it was shot on a Sony cinema line camera with a kit lens. Very clean, very digital, very straightforward. Wala masyadong cinematic look. \n\nWhat I like is how the characters show different sides of themselves. They want revenge, but they also end up saving the pregnant woman’s life. Hindi black and white yung morality. Lahat sila may bitbit na contradiction.\n\nNaawa na rin ako sa kinidnap nila at some point. But when he finally admitted na tama nga ang hinala nila, parang may grim relief. At least hindi nasayang yung lahat ng ginawa nila, kahit mali pa rin yung ginawa nila.\n\nThe ending is what really stands out. An almost 13 minute uninterrupted long take with nonstop acting. Pure tension, and performance carrying the whole scene. Impressive and exhausting in a good way.","poster":"img/it-was-just-an-accident-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Novocaine","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":240,"review":"The film uses anamorphic lenses, which already gives it that stretched, cinematic look. The color palette also feels very similar to the John Wick films. Neon heavy, contrasty, and stylized.\n\nThe big twist that Sherry is actually part of the heist works well. It reframes everything that came before and pushes the story into full chaos mode.\n\nThe blood and prosthetics are really good. Combined with the sound design, it makes the violence cringe worthy in a good way. This film is gross, but intentionally so.\n\nWhat makes it interesting is that the main character doesn’t really fight in a traditional way. Since he can’t feel pain, he uses his condition to his advantage. He turns his weakness into a weapon.\n\nSome scenes are genuinely hard to watch. Yung fist fight where he stuffs shattered glass into his hand just to punch harder is nasty. The nail pulling sounds, nakakakilabot sa pandinig. Napapikit talaga ako nung binali niya yung thumb niya gamit ang fire extinguisher para makawala sa handcuffs. The sound of the bone breaking is what really sells it.\n\nJack Quaid is basically ruining his life for a girl again, which at this point feels like a running joke. \n\nNakakatawa yung kailangan pa niyang umarte na nasasaktan kahit wala talaga siyang nararamdaman during the torture scene.\n\nThat said, the actress playing Sherry, Amber Midthunder, didn’t really work for me. Her performance feels weaker compared to everyone else around her.","poster":"img/novocaine-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Bugonia","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","bugonia"],"words":383,"review":"Unang una kong napansin is yung highly saturated colors. Parang Whiplash, Legend of Ochi, Apocalypse Now, Limitless level. Tapos sobrang sharpened yung image, parang naka HDR na sagad na sagad. Kita lahat ng pores. May mataas na headroom sa framing, plus haunting sound design. Very unsettling. Para akong hinihypnotize. Umabot sa point na kailangan kong buksan yung ilaw kasi parang nahihilo ako.\n\nI really like the lighting. Very deliberate yung separation ng key light, shadows, and hair light. Kitang kita yung tatlong layers, kaya ang linaw ng subject sa frame.\n\nDespite the discomfort, I like how clean and smooth the camera movements are. Mostly dolly push ins and push outs. Hindi shaky. Controlled. Parang calm observer.\n\nThe score feels like grand opera, but used in a fantasy sense. Hindi siya subtle. Grand, dramatic, parang may mythic weight kahit absurd yung nangyayari. Parang ginagamit yung music to make nonsense feel important, almost divine or prophetic. That contrast makes it more disturbing. And it will all make sense sa dulo.\n\nThe premise itself is absurd. At first, akala ko belief in fake news and conspiracies taken to the extreme. Jesse plays it perfectly. He feels sick in the head, unhinged. Emma contrasts him well. Calm, diplomatic, trying to ground things using psychology and positive reinforcement. Parang tinatry niyang i manage a narcissist by feeding the ego just enough.\n\nJesse is the perfect casting for this. Ever since Like Mike, may pagka lunatic aura na talaga siya. Dito, full blown na. \n\nAs the story evolves, nagiging malinaw na revenge pala yung pinanggagalingan ng lahat. Hindi lang delusion.\n\nThe deaths are unpredictable. Hindi mo alam kung sino mawawala next.\n\nMay mga moments na natatawa ako habang sinasabi, “What the fuck, ano ba talaga to?” Hindi mo alam kung totoo ba yung sinasabi nila or kung nakikiride lang sila sa kabaliwan ni Jesse.\n\nThen the twist hits. Turns out, Jesse wasn’t crazy at all?\n\nYung scene na binutas yung firmament sobrang nakakatawa. Alam mong every flat earther would celebrate that moment.\n\nThe whole thing is a wild experience. Atlantis, evolution, Noah’s Ark, dinosaurs. Lahat thrown to make a word salad, tapos biglang nagiging “totoo” dahil sinabi ng alien. Para siyang conspiracy theorist dream come true.\n\nDark humor hanggang dulo. First time makaexperience ng ganitong cinematic feeling. Very unique","poster":"img/bugonia-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Good Fortune","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-26","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":234,"review":"It honestly feels like Keanu Reeves just gate crashed into this production while on break from John Wick. Same serious face, same calm presence, but somehow it still works for comedy. Keanu’s serious delivery actually makes his funny moments land better. \n\nThe Indian guy’s voice sounds like a cartoon character, which is kind of annoying.\n\nThis feels a lot like Bruce Almighty. Light comedy with life lessons underneath. Nothing heavy handed, just simple reflections about purpose and meaning.\n\nBoth the angel and the Indian guy are also searching for purpose, which makes the story feel more human despite the fantasy setup. Even the angel ends up smoking, which says a lot about how messy and frustrating life can get.\n\nThere’s a strong look at the hardship of employment. How draining it can be, how hopeless it feels when you’re stuck just trying to survive. The film also touches on how nepo babies often don’t know what to do when they hit rock bottom, because they’ve always had boosted privilege to get where they are.\n\nAt its core, the film questions how someone can still have hope when life feels miserable and unfair. It also reflects on corporate greed, but makes it more human by focusing on the workers inside the system rather than just the executives.\n\nOverall, it’s a feel good movie. Not trying to be groundbreaking, just warm, reflective, and easy to watch.","poster":"img/good-fortune-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Running Man","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":250,"review":"Right from the first few minutes, the problem of poverty is already evident. The couple’s acting feels believable, grounded, and real. I’m usually not a fan of Glen Powell since most of his roles come off as kupal, but I actually liked this side of him. It shows range. Mas okay siya dito.\n\nThe premise feels like a mix of The Hunger Games and John Wick. You have the survival spectacle paired with a bounty style contract system where entertainment feeds on violence.\n\nWorld building is handled well through nice sets and background extensions. It really feels like a nearby dystopian future. Hindi siya sobrang sci fi, just close enough to reality to be uncomfortable.\n\nIt’s a fun popcorn movie, especially when they reach Michael Cera’s house filled with booby traps. That whole sequence leans into chaos and absurdity in a good way.\n\nNice to see Emilia Jones again after CODA. Even though she doesn’t have much screen time, she’s still a great actress. She also looks more grown up here, and her presence adds warmth to the film.\n\nThe payoff at the end works. It delivers what it promises without overstaying its welcome.\n\nUnderneath the spectacle, the film touches on deeper ideas like deep fakes, fake news, propaganda, network greed, conspiracies, and anarchy. It’s not subtle, but it fits the tone of the story.\n\nOverall, it’s entertaining, timely, and easy to watch. Not trying to be profound, but it knows exactly what kind of movie it wants to be.","poster":"img/the-running-man-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Companion","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":354,"review":"What if AI robots becomes super expressive, with real emotions, loyalty, and the need for consent, but doesn’t even know that it is a robot? That question alone already makes the film feel like a Black Mirror episode.\n\nI personally think the murder plot was revealed too early. It could have been saved for the later part of the film to make the escalation hit harder. Once that card is played, the tension becomes more about survival than mystery.\n\nThere’s also a Don’t Worry Darling vibe here, especially in how men try to control women through systems, rules, and power disguised as care. At its core, this is a survival story, and the film does a good job making you root for Iris, played by Sophie Thatcher, against Josh, a classic control freak played by Jack Quaid.\n\nSophie Thatcher really stands out. She gives off Anya Taylor Joy meets Hailee Steinfeld energy, but honestly, her visuals and screen presence are even stronger for me. And yes, this is Jack Quaid losing another girlfriend again, just like in The Boys, which is kind of funny.\n\nThe concept itself is wild. This is basically what happens when you turn a sex bot into a murder bot.\n\nThe German speaking scene is hilarious. Iris quickly switching languages because she literally cannot lie is a great bit of writing and shows her intelligence and adaptability in a subtle way.\n\nNice to see Marc Menchaca here after Ozark. He brings that familiar unsettling energy.\n\nThe director does a great job, especially considering this is a debut. Every frame feels clean and intentional. Nothing feels sloppy or accidental.\n\nOne of the strongest elements is the foreshadowing. The scene where the user agreement is not read comes back to bite hard later. Same with the wine opener. It’s shown just enough times that you already know something bad will happen with it, and when it finally does, it feels earned.\n\nOverall, Companion works because it mixes genre thrills with ideas about consent, control, and autonomy. It may reveal some cards too early, but it stays engaging and sharp until the end.","poster":"img/companion-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Great Flood","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-25","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","netflix"],"words":322,"review":"Hindi naghold back ang film. Baha agad. Walang warm up, diretso chaos.\n\nI appreciate the attempt at long uninterrupted shots. Magaling yung pagkakatago ng cuts. It keeps the tension flowing without feeling gimmicky. The CGI is okay. Hindi standout, but it does its job.\n\nThe drama and emotions are high, and the acting feels believable. Hindi pilit. The musical score helps a lot in adding emotional depth, especially during heavier moments.\n\nI like the story angles used to humanize the AI.\n\nThere’s a scene that really reminded me of Titanic, yung elderly couple facing imminent death together. Simple but effective.\n\nThe twists work. I had this feeling na siya mismo yung experiment, and kailangan niyang ipasa yung tests by helping others in order to perfect emotions. Parang emotional trial by fire, or by flood.\n\nVisually, maganda yung aesthetic. Parang everything is seen through the eyes of a scientist watching lab rats inside a maze. Controlled chaos. Observation without interference.\n\nAt first, you think this is just a simple disaster movie, but it’s much more than that. It’s actually a time loop film, but it smartly skips repetitive scenes so the audience doesn’t get bored. Certain memories are retained, parang rats learning how to solve a maze more efficiently each time.\n\nAt the core, this is really a reflection of a mother’s love. Going through all the disasters and loops just to find her way back to her child. Mothers would dive into any great flood for their children.\n\nThere’s also that idea of breaking against programming, parang source code being overridden by emotion and choice.\n\nKim Da Mi is better here compared to Itaewon Class. Mas layered yung performance niya.\n\nSymbolically, the great flood works well. In the Bible, the flood presents as a divine reset to preserve and restart humanity. Here, it represents something similar: the birth of new beings. Human like, three dimensional printed AI learning emotions through lived experience.","poster":"img/the-great-flood-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Minority Report","year":2002,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","tomcruise"],"words":406,"review":"Sa simula pa lang, thriller na agad. Walang warm up. Tension agad.\n\nVisually, it relies heavily on real sets mixed with early years of CGI. Parang Men in Black era, but slightly below the high level of detail of Phantom Menace. You can really feel na transitional period siya between practical filmmaking and full digital cinema.\n\nThe core question of the film is strong: are you really going to imprison someone for a crime they haven’t committed yet? That moral dilemma carries the story well.\n\nLighting and color grading remind me of the first Transformers movie. Cold, bluish, metallic. It fits the futuristic surveillance heavy world, but it also makes the CGI stand out more.\n\nThe CGI itself clearly shows its age. The textures feel different from the practical elements, and even the lighting on the digital objects looks fake at times. \n\nThat said, the action sequences are still impressive. The chase scene with Tom Cruise climbing the fire exit ladder is masterful. That entire sequence blends practical stunts, special effects, and green screen really well. It feels seamless and kinetic.\n\nThe conveyor belt scene immediately reminded me of Attack of the Clones, specifically the Genosha factory sequence. I checked the release dates. Star Wars Episode II came out May 16, 2002, while Minority Report was June 17, 2002. It really feels like George Lucas and Steven Spielberg shared similar ideas or inspirations around that time. While thinking about that, I also noticed the scoring style. It felt very John Williams. And when I checked, yes, it was him.\n\nAng ganda rin ng long continuous shot during the robot spider deployment. The way the camera moves while the spiders search each room builds tension really well and shows Spielberg’s control of space and movement. Reminds me of John Wick 3.\n\nStory wise, I had a strong gut feeling early on that Lamar was the villain. When the detective finally cracks the case and then suddenly gets shot, that moment hit hard. Sayang siya. It felt unfair in a very deliberate way.\n\nThe precog female is still weird to me. There is something unsettling about her presence that never fully settles into clarity.\n\nWhen the story finally comes full circle, I think the payoff works. The resolution is satisfying enough and ties the themes together well.\n\nThat said, I still think Deja Vu is the better film overall. It feels more emotionally grounded and cleaner in execution.","poster":"img/minority-report-2002.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Die Hard","year":1988,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","diehard"],"words":352,"review":"Iba talaga ang charisma ni Bruce Willis nung bata bata pa. Ramdam mo agad yung presence niya. \n\nThe entry of the bad guys is clean and sleek. Very confident. Alam mong calculated lahat. The rooftop action is insane. The danger feels real, and so does the acting. Survival siya, so dapat clumsy yung galaw. Hindi polished. And it works. Samahan mo pa ng solid musical score and the tension really builds.\n\nIt honestly feels like Home Alone for adults. Same logic of a man using the environment to outsmart intruders, pero mas brutal at mas grounded.\n\nMakapigil hininga yung elevator shaft scene. Simple setup, pero sobrang effective.\n\nTapos biglang familiar yung boses ni Hans. Sabi ko na e. Si Severus Snape pala. Alan Rickman. Bata bata pa siya dito kaya hindi ko agad namukhaan, pero the voice is unmistakable. Kada salita parang naririnig ko yung panlalait kay Harry Potter. He elevates the whole film effortlessly. Menacing without even trying. Ang galing!\n\nNice real explosives. Sakto rin yung amount ng blood. Hindi excessive, pero ramdam mo yung stakes. The jump from the roof scene is absolute cinema. The leap itself plus the explosion behind him is exhilarating. Gets ko na ngayon why this became a cult classic.\n\nThis is how you make a really good action film. Halos lahat shot in camera. Ramdam mo yung physicality. The standoff scene between John and Hans with his wife is beautifully shot. Silhouette ni John with sparks flying behind him. Freaking great visual. Parang yung mga ending ng musical fireworks display. \n\nI also like the emotional arc of Holly McClane. At the start, she distances herself from John by not using his surname. By the end, when she proudly uses “McClane” again, it feels earned. It is not just romantic, it is about reclaiming identity, partnership, and respect after surviving everything together.\n\nMay character development din si Al Powell. That moment when he finally draws his gun and shoots, exactly when he is needed the most, feels earned.\n\nPerfect Christmas movie. I watched it December 24, 2025, and it still holds up after all these years.","poster":"img/die-hard-1988.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Legend of Ochi","year":2025,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":190,"review":"The color and lighting are very vivid and dreamlike. It almost feels like a Wes Anderson film at times. Parang fairy tale bedtime story yung dating. Soft, magical, and intentionally stylized.\n\nThe landscape photography is beautiful. Solid yung mga mountain shots and the foggy aerials. The environment feels alive and carefully chosen, not just background filler.\n\nOchi being a puppet, and most likely animatronic, really works for me. That tangibility makes it easier to connect emotionally. Hindi siya parang purely animated character. May bigat at presence siya, similar to how Yoda or Grogu works. You feel like the character exists in the space.\n\nI also noticed the use of miniatures in some shots, which I really appreciated. It’s a nice artistic touch and something you don’t see much anymore. It adds to that old school fantasy feel.\n\nMedyo kadiri yung scene na tumitibok yung namamagang sugat dahil sa kagat ni Ochi. Effective siya in a disturbing way, and it grounds the fantasy with something very physical and real.\n\nOverall, the film leans heavily into practical effects, stylized visuals, and fairy tale logic, and that combination gives it a unique charm.","poster":"img/the-legend-of-ochi-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Gladiator II","year":2024,"rating":1.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-23","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","gladiator"],"words":328,"review":"The film looks very modern, and you can really feel the heavy use of CGI right away. The color contrast is also quite different from the first movie, leaning more digital than grounded.\n\nEven the explosions and fire from the cannon catapults look fake compared to the tangible, dangerous feel of the first Gladiator. In the original, you could almost feel the heat. Here, everything feels lighter and less physical.\n\nPedro Pascal does not command a strong presence on screen. He is fine, but he never fully owns the frame the way you would expect from someone in that position.\n\nThe baboon sequence completely breaks realism for me. It does not feel believable at all and pulls me out of the film.\n\nI do like the arena battle in Rome. That part captures the same energy as the first film. The gore, the survival instinct, the colors, it finally feels like Gladiator again.\n\nThe relive of the Battle of Salamis looks cool in concept, but it is heavily CGI. Even the sharks do not look realistic. I kept thinking this would have worked better if it was shot in a large tank to give it more weight and realism. Watching it feels more painful than impressive.\n\nPaul Mescal’s performance feels dry. I could not really connect with him. His acting and voice come across as monotonous. Joseph Quinn, on the other hand, is much better. Mas magaling pa siya, and he brings more life to the screen.\n\nGood thing Acacius dies early because he does not offer much to the plot. What I really hated is Joseph Quinn dying. He is the best thing in this movie, and losing him hurts the film a lot.\n\nOverall, Gladiator II feels more artificial than epic. It has moments that remind you of the first film, especially in the arena, but too much CGI, weaker screen presence from the lead, and questionable creative choices stop it from reaching the same level.","poster":"img/gladiator-ii-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Gladiator","year":2000,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","gladiator"],"words":345,"review":"The fire catapult hits feel real and dangerous. Ramdam mo yung weight and impact, not just spectacle.\n\nThe opening war sequence is nicely done, parang Game of Thrones in scale and chaos. My only complaint is the use of long exposure and slow motion shots. It pulls me out of the moment instead of immersing me deeper.\n\nAng ganda ng lighting and musical score during the scene where Commodus kills Caesar. The warmth and shadows make the moment feel intimate and disturbing at the same time.\n\nThe escape from his supposed execution is excellent. The fight choreography, cinematography, color grading, and musical score all work together. From that moment, the film clearly shifts into another level. \n\nI like how controlled the gore is. Just enough to serve the story. The first gladiator fight is pure chaos. The camera movement is shaky and aggressive, but that feels intentional. Parang inilalagay tayo sa ulo ng mga gladiator, confused, overwhelmed, just trying to survive.\n\nCommodus gives off strong Joffrey Baratheon energy. A spoiled, insecure man child who inherited power and doesn’t deserve it. Joaquin Phoenix plays him as someone dangerous not because of strength, but because of ego and emotional instability.\n\nFor a 2000 film, the set extensions and CGI still hold up. It works because there are real sets. The production design grounds everything, allowing the visual effects to blend naturally instead of feeling artificial.\n\nThe costume design is superb. It quietly tells you who has power, who is enslaved.\n\nThe dialogue and writing sometimes sound like poetry. Not in an overbearing way, but in short lines that feel memorable and weighty.\n\nThe first Colosseum fight is a spectacle. The scale, the crowd, the choreography, everything works.\n\nI also like how color and lighting shift depending on the emotion. Warm tones are used during intimate moments, especially scenes involving family, love, or passion. It humanizes Maximus beyond revenge.\n\nCommodus’ death and Maximus finally joining his family in the afterlife feel satisfying. Not just because justice is served, but because the story closes emotionally. He finally finds peace.","poster":"img/gladiator-2000.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jarhead","year":2005,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-18","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":265,"review":"The training sequence is engaging. It almost feels like a coming of age story, pero military training version. You see them slowly stripped down and rebuilt into something else.\n\nVisually, the shots feel too hot and overexposed, which I get is intentional. It really sells the heat and discomfort.\n\nAng ganda nung shot ng mga trucks coming in defensive position, blurred because of the heat. Yung distortion na parang may heat haze. It works really well here because it makes the environment feel hostile and unreal.\n\nI like the electric guitar strumming used as the beat. It gives the film a certain attitude, very masculine, very restless.\n\nThere are also some really beautiful visuals. The silhouette shot during sunset is especially striking.\n\nAng rowdy nila. Ito talaga yung “boys will be boys” energy. Loud, aggressive, immature, but also bonded in a strange way.\n\nWhen they finally go to war, there is a sense of frustration. Some shots feel CGI, especially the oil fire scenes. I am not sure if those columns of fire are real or enhanced, but while the silhouettes look great, classic Roger Deakins, it sometimes feels a bit gimmicky.\n\nThe most interesting part for me is the irony. They trained so hard, all that discipline and conditioning, and yet they never really get to fire their weapons in combat. They want to kill so badly, not because of hatred, but because that is what they were trained for. Imagine preparing your whole identity around war and never being allowed to complete it.\n\nThat emptiness and frustration is really what Jarhead is about.","poster":"img/jarhead-2005.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Avatar: Fire and Ash","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-17","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","avatar"],"words":390,"review":"So ngayon may suicide bomber na rin. That alone already changes the tone of the franchise.\n\nThe film explores heavier themes like grief, seclusion, guilt, and even suicide. Mas dark siya emotionally compared to the earlier films. There is also an exploration of satanistic or pagan elements, tied to abandonment of faith. It feels like the story is intentionally pushing Avatar into darker territory.\n\nVisually, Cameron still mastered water VFX. Not just the sea and ocean like before, but also rivers and swamps. Technically impressive pa rin when it comes to environmental work.\n\nThe lore deepens a bit with Spider now being able to breathe without a mask. That detail stood out and hints at bigger biological or spiritual changes in this world.\n\nI was kind of hoping for real character development for Miles Quaritch played by Stephen Lang, but none. Same old vengeance toward Jake. No evolution. Missed opportunity.\n\nVarang played by Oona Chaplin is great. Her performance and facial capture are very effective. Very menacing presence. She feels like the strongest new element of the film.\n\nThat said, there is no new visual tech that made me go wow. It did not feel as immersive as before. It also felt long. Two hours and thirty minutes but still boring in parts. The pacing goes up and down and never really stabilizes.\n\nA lot of it feels recycled. Parang pinaghalo lang yung Avatar 1 and Way of Water, then presented as a new chapter. Even some shots feel reused. Wala masyadong bagong visual language.\n\nSound design is also not immersive. I expected more, especially from a franchise known for technical excellence.\n\nThere is also this feeling of Gen Z talking to Gen X. Ayaw makinig sa reason ng mga elders, especially Tulkun related decisions. The conflict feels generational but not always well handled.\n\nParang hindi rin naka 48 fps tulad ng dati, or at least it did not feel as smooth or impactful as before.\n\nOverall, it feels like more of the same. It honestly feels like a cash grab.\nAt this point, gusto ko na tawagin ang pamilya ni Jake na “Sullies ampunan” dahil tatlo na ang ampon nila.\n\nAnd Eywa was a letdown. Yun na yun? Three movies of hype and buildup, tapos ganun lang ang payoff.\n\nIn the end, it failed to surpass Way of Water.","poster":"img/avatar-fire-and-ash-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sand Castle","year":2017,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netflix"],"words":215,"review":"Sand Castle feels modern and clean even though the story takes place in 2003. The director leans heavily into long, continuous shots, which gives it a grounded and deliberate pace, but also makes the movie feel slow at times.\n\nThe whole premise of fixing a water supply is honestly boring. Almost an hour in, there’s still barely any action, and even the characters seem to know it. Ocre straight up says the mission is a loser, and that line pretty much sums up the film’s energy. It reminds me of Breaking Bad in a way, slow, hot, dry, stuck in the desert, more about tension and waiting than movement.\n\nThe third act looks great, especially the night scenes with the street lights, but everything feels a bit too perfect, almost staged, rather than raw or real. Still, the intensity ramps up quickly near the end, and that’s when the film finally grips you.\n\nWhat hits hardest is when things finally start to work, progress is made, and then a bomb explodes and wipes everything out. All that effort, wasted in an instant. By the time he’s sent home, the irony really sinks in. He doesn’t want to go home anymore. He wants to finish the job, but there’s no job left for him to finish.","poster":"img/sand-castle-2017.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"How to Make Millions Before Grandma Dies","year":2024,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","netf"],"words":240,"review":"Thailand looks awfully familiar, almost the same as the Philippines, especially Pasay where I grew up from ages 0 to 5. The setting immediately felt close to home.\n\nNakakatawa yung sponge bath scene nilang mag lola, sobrang natural and real, parang eksena lang sa totoong buhay.\n\nI got teary eyed during the temple scene where M tells his grandma that his son doesn’t wish for her anymore. Then the grandma tells him that her son already has a family and that he uses his wishes first for his own family. That hit hard. I felt guilty because I don’t think much of my Nanay anymore ever since I had my own family. That scene quietly hurts in a very real way.\n\nNainis ako sa part na nagalit si M dahil hindi sa kanya napunta yung bahay, even if siya yung nag alaga sa lola niya. All this time, parang reward lang pala yung motivation niya. I thought the bonding and care became genuine, but it felt like he wasn’t really that different from his rich uncle after all.\n\nThen he goes to the nursing home to fetch his lola, and for me that was the cue that he no longer wanted anything from her. Gusto na lang talaga niyang makasama siya.\n\nAnd then the twist. All this time, she had been saving up for her grandson. That’s what lolas are. They truly love their grandchildren and make them number one.","poster":"img/how-to-make-millions-before-grandma-dies-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Top Gun: Maverick","year":2022,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2025-12-11","tags":["arwinreviews","screenx"],"words":156,"review":"The ScreenX experience works, pero the key is to focus on the middle screen. Downside is the brightness and colors don’t always match. Kailangan talaga same calibration ang contrast and saturation ang main screen at ang sides para mas seamless.\n\nIt looks best during dark scenes because the edges and seams are less noticeable. When the shots are centered and dark, your eyes naturally adjust and the whole thing feels panoramic. Pero hindi pa rin siya as immersive as IMAX. IMAX’s tall screen hits differently. If ever magkaroon ng IMAX with a 270 degree wrap, yun siguro ang perfect setup.\n\nThe subwoofer is great, my seat was shaking during the jet sequences, which adds to the experience.\n\nJoseph Kosinski’s visuals and the music still take my breath away. Miles Teller and Tom Cruise are still super great here.\n\nOverall, ScreenX is immersive enough, but IMAX remains the superior experience dahil sa calibration consistency and total immersion.","poster":"img/top-gun-maverick-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Farewell","year":2019,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":333,"review":"Wala pa sa fifteen minutes naluluha na agad ako. Lolas are so warm and lovely, and I can already feel that this film will hit me really hard.\n\nThe save face culture of Chinese families shows immediately. Yung dinner table conversation about the wedding and not wanting to look cheap says a lot. It is all about appearance, she said. And that mindset sits at the center of their decisions.\n\nThe production design feels natural. Exactly how I imagine a real Chinese home would look like. \n\nAs I got to one third of the film, it also feels like the typical lola moments: do this, do that, don't do this, don't do that. But I really feel lolas are like that because they genuinely care for their family and just want what is best. It comes from love, even if it feels controlling.\n\nAnother cultural layer is the way they casually talk about money, legacy, and responsibility during family dinners. For some people it might feel like pressure, but honestly if that was instilled in me when I was a child, I think I would have been a better entrepreneur.\n\nThe biggest shock for me is the culture of not telling someone they have cancer in China. It feels so strange but also extremely cultural. They carry the burden for the person instead of letting the person carry it.\n\nThe grave offering scene looks ridiculous on the surface, but Filipinos do it too, so it is still relatable.\n\nThe moment where her grandma gave her money really got me in tears. I remembered Mama Mila giving me money even when I already had my first job.\n\nI think the film is a study of morality and how families handle lies. Whether they want to protect the feelings of the person they love, or they take on the emotional burden because it is their culture, norm, or tradition. It is complicated, but the film shows how love makes people justify the choices they make.","poster":"img/the-farewell-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Intouchables","year":2011,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2025-12-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","intouchables"],"words":175,"review":"The film starts strong with an electric car chase followed by a funny sequence where Driss pretends Philippe is sick. Classic playful setup.\n\nI first watched this during our French class in college pero di namin natapos, so I came back to it now with fresh eyes.\n\nSobrang funny ng training days ni Driss. He really maintains the comedic atmosphere. The bonding that forms between him and Philippe feels natural and warm, especially since Philippe’s life looks so dull because of his condition. Their chemistry carries the entire film.\n\nYung humor dito may class. Hindi cheap, hindi pilit. The punch lines land because they come from personality differences and genuine connection rather than forced jokes. Witty and refined yung style ng comedy.\n\nFeel good movie in the most natural way. Hindi siya try hard. It just flows.\n\nAnd Driss has an actual character arc. From someone who looks aimless and irresponsible at first, he becomes dependable and essential in Philippe’s life. \n\nI have no complaints. It is sincere, charming, and effortless. A really enjoyable watch.","poster":"img/the-intouchables-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Moonlight","year":2016,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-08","tags":["moviereview","nowwatching","a24","arwinreviews"],"words":233,"review":"Moonlight is a coming of age story about a little black kid who grows up bullied by his own community, neglected by his junkie mom, and confused about his sexuality. Lahat ng ito, layer by layer, forms the person he eventually becomes.\n\nGusto ko yung moment na gumanti siya sa bully niya. Long time coming. You can feel the years of pain and humiliation finally overflowing. Pero the sad part is it becomes a cycle. He becomes the same kind of person who hurt him. All the trauma manifests and gets projected into his adult identity.\n\nPero kahit ganoon, may crack sa facade niya. And that is Kev. Kev is his soft spot and his safe space. When they talk again, you instantly see the shift in him. Mas lumalabas yung real side niya na matagal niyang tinago.\n\nOn the technical side, ang ganda ng cinematography. The anamorphic lens gives it an intimate modern feel. The lighting and color grading are vivid and clean, very visually pleasing. It has a polished look that fits the emotional tone of the film.\n\nMoonlight is quiet, heavy, and grounded. It is about the people who shape you, the pain you carry, and the version of yourself you create just to survive. And in the middle of all that hardness, there is someone like Kev who reminds you of who you were before life got to you.","poster":"img/moonlight-2016.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sore: A Wife from the Future","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","timetravel"],"words":427,"review":"The film opens with beautiful landscape photography, and that visual quality stays consistent all throughout. The early hook works immediately: Sore is actually Jo’s wife from the future. She has that confident, know it all look with a playful seductive smirk that hints she knows more than she is letting on.\n\nKarlo is the comic relief, parang Joey Tribbiani type na auditioning actor, and his scenes balance the heavier moments well.\n\nHalfway in, the big reveal hits hard. Sore is stuck in a time loop that resets every time Jo relapses and she fails to stop it or kapag nirereveal nyang mamamatay si Jo in the future. That alone says a lot about their relationship. When she finally breaks the cycle, she briefly feels freedom, but she still chooses to fight for Jo. That emotional shift feels real. There is love, frustration, hope, and exhaustion all in one.\n\nYou also see the real life version of this idea. Every relapse or refusal to change kills the people around us a little bit. It hurts them to watch someone they love destroy themselves slowly, and sometimes the stubbornness is disguised as “care” or “control.” The film captures that well.\n\nHer mission changes too. She is no longer trying to prevent him from messing up. She is trying to help him change from within. And the revelation that Elsa and Sore are basically the same type of presence in his life. Someone trying to shape Jo, adds another emotional layer.\n\nAng ganda ng travel montage nila papunta sa dad ni Jo. The whole world around them is blue, cold, and muted, while Sore wears an orange or pinkish dress. Parang siya lang yung nagbibigay kulay sa mundo ni Jo. I love these subtle choices.\n\nJust when you think she finally succeeded, the film shifts again. There is another POV: Time itself. First time ko makakita ng ganito sa film and it works surprisingly well.\n\nThe line that hit me the most is when Jo said, “I don’t think people will change out of fear, but because they feel loved.” I agree. We only let go of destructive habits when we feel understood, needed, and unhurried.\n\nBy the end, the film confirms two things: One, the Arctic exhibit will work per Sore. Two, parallel universes exist because they now live in another reality where they choose each other again.\n\nThe film gives off vibes of Netflix’s Dark, Edge of Tomorrow, and Time Traveler’s Wife. Acting quality of both Jo and Sore is really great. Sheila Dara Aisha is so beautiful!","poster":"img/sore-a-wife-from-the-future-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Zone of Interest","year":2023,"rating":0.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":192,"review":"Zone of Interest opens with almost four minutes of pure black paired only with a haunting synth sound. I had to fast forward.\n\nEvery frame looks clean and intentional. Static, centered, and framed with rule of thirds. It feels like carefully arranged tableaus na walang unnecessary movement.\n\nYung continuation ng shots reminds me of old Resident Evil games, yung different camera angles sa bawat sulok ng room. Para syang perspective staging or static multi angle surveillance style. Parang CCTV where the camera watches from one corner while characters move through the space.\n\nParang POV siya ng mga Nazis quietly living their normal daily lives, while the violence of the concentration camps happens in the background. Hindi pinapakita explicitly. Instead, they use sound design, like distant machinery, screams, gunshots, and ambient noise to imply what is happening off screen.\n\nIt is super dull. Sobra!  I watched it in four chunks on different days because I kept getting bored. It did not pull my attention. I was hoping there would be emotional attachment or at least a cinematic shift or something to break the flatness, but none. Everything stays numb, distant, and emotionally disconnected.","poster":"img/the-zone-of-interest-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Smashing Machine","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":375,"review":"Sa simula palang may nag resonate na agad na line sa akin. “I have to make sure that I am a hundred percent concentrated on what I have to do because if not then my emotions will be running around everywhere. If we do not control our emotions, our fears, our anxieties will get the best of us. We get slapped around by our emotions and we cannot have that.” It sets the tone for the whole film and it hits real life too.\n\nDocumentary style ang camera movements. Parang spectator ka lang na kasama sa daily life nila. Raw, unfiltered, and personal. The emotions feel real. Walang glamorizing. Kita mo yung vulnerability ng characters.\n\nDwayne Johnson was massive!  Ang galing ng hair and make up. Ibang iba talaga itsura niya. Even the prosthetics have imperfections. Pero naririnig ko parin si Maui minsan. Which is weird.\n\nDwayne’s character feels like a gentle giant who loves to talk about the small details, what he wants, and what he feels. Emily Blunt’s character is carefree and parang iniisip niya na hindi big deal yung mga sinasabi ni Dwayne. Their relationship is so toxic. I feel like Emily wants to be wanted and recognized all the time. Hindi ko alam if controlling or manipulative pero ang lakas ng toyo. I think she is insecure. Ang galing ni Emily kasi nakakainis siya in a real way. It was a great decision letting her go.\n\nNice training arc after him getting sober. Mas feel ko yung sport at yung mga winning moments dito compared sa Iron Claw. Mas authentic yung struggle at recovery dito. Though mas nakakaiyak pa rin ang ending ng Iron Claw.\n\nThe finals were intense. You can feel each punch and kick, and the score added a lot of weight. It has a heavy percussion mixed with an airy flute or woodwind layer to build tension.\n\nHis shower scene feels like a baptism. Being born into a new life. A reset after the chaos. And that ending proves that he is now happy and liberated from addiction. Not just drugs, but also his addiction to winning. He finally admitted that he cannot always win, and that is okay to lose. This acceptance is what gives him peace.","poster":"img/the-smashing-machine-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ne Zha 2","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-05","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","ne zha"],"words":353,"review":"Ne Zha 2 carries the same vibes and aesthetic as the first film, but here the animation is much sharper and the details are more noticeable. You can definitely see the big change in terms of scale and the number of characters. The energy in this installment is much higher compared to the original.\n\nThe camera movements are more fluid and the fur textures are surprisingly realistic. The film feels more polished and confident in its visual language.\n\nThe guttural score in the second act really adds tension to the rising story. Ang lakas ng impact niya sa pacing at emotional weight.\n\nMaganda ang twist. There were signs pero hindi ko masyadong finocusan kasi mukha namang mabait at inosente talaga. Kaya pala napaka supportive niya kay Ne Zha at hindi niya napapansin na may demon spirit si Ne Zha, may hidden motives na pala! \n\nThe transformation technique is a direct callback to the first movie which is a nice continuity and comedic touch.\n\nAng ganda ng mga shots, parang painting. The artistry is very high and the compositions look intentional and cinematic. The emotional scene with his mother is sad and very heavy. Ramdam mo yung pain and love sabayan pa ng flashback scenes from the first movie.\n\nThe crazy power up scene where he completes his body is a standout moment. Very hype and visually explosive. Yung part na enemies come together when faced with imminent death is a good moment. Sometimes danger forces unity and the movie showed that well.\n\nThe tug of war escape to the cauldron is pure visual popcorn. Ang sarap panoorin. The stakes here are genuinely high. I forgot when was the last time I felt that level of hopelessness for the protagonists and not just fake danger. Dito ramdam mo talaga na pwedeng hindi sila magtagumpay.\n\nThis sequel is darker than the first one and expands the lore in a way that feels natural. I can compare it to Empire Strikes Back and Dune Part Two in the sense that it deepens the world, raises the emotional stakes, and pushes the story into more mature territory.","poster":"img/ne-zha-2-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Mosul","year":2019,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","mosul"],"words":259,"review":"Everything feels real. Walang studio look, hindi siya mukhang movie set. It is dirty, dusty, broken, and stinky. Ramdam mo yung rawness of their environment.\n\nCamera movements are chaotic handheld, which makes you feel like you are with them, running and fighting in real time. The base siege has a nice gunfight sequence. Ang galing ng pag flank. Very tactical. You feel like they know how to move as a unit.\n\nBut every dead teammate does not feel heavy emotionally dahil hindi ako attached sa supporting characters. Parang cannon fodder lang sila sa story, disposable. \n\nKatulad ni Kawa, I was lost the entire time kung ano ba talagang mission or purpose ng SWAT team. Mystery siya for most of the runtime. But I like that Kawa also demonstrates character development by giving in to his hate toward ISIS. Slowly becoming a hardened fighter like the others.\n\nWalang masyadong change of lens. Mostly wide shots para sa handheld style. It gives the film a grounded feel. Hindi siya very cinematic in the traditional sense, pero maganda yung color grading na golden brown. Consistent and immersive.\n\nIronic na yung pagiging consistent ni Jasem sa pagliligpit and pagtatapon ng basura was also what ended his life. \n\nIt is nice to see Waleed step up as leader after their commander died. Sa una hesitant pa siya, unsure of taking responsibility. But eventually, he has no choice. They must finish what they started.\n\nIn the end, once Kawa understands the true purpose of everything, he goes all in. He is finally one of them.","poster":"img/mosul-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hardcore Henry","year":2015,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","violence"],"words":312,"review":"Opening credits feel like Deadpool’s, playful and self aware, and agad it sets a fun and violent tone.\n\nMedyo nakakahilo sa una, parang ride. Exhilarating, heart pounding, and nakakalula dahil POV and one continuous shot style. Parang naglalaro ng COD, and at times parang Assassin’s Creed dahil sa parkour scenes.\n\nAng galing ng sound design. Nakakaclaustrophobic, parang ako talaga ang binabaril, and I actually experienced this same effect in Dunkirk.\n\nYung energy ng film reminds me of Crank mixed with Planet Terror. Supercharged energy with a score that almost feels like Daft Punk. Action is nonstop from start to finish. And yung gore dito parang Tarantino level, fun, graphic, and a bit playful in its violence.\n\nPlot is dumb but it is fine. This is one of those films where the action, the violence, and the blood are what matter. Hindi kailangan ng deep story. Ito yung tulad ng mga film na you wouldn't mind yung story, you just go with the flow. \n\nEditing rhythm keeps the film upbeat. Walang patay na segment, at kahit chaotic siya, it maintains a fun forward momentum that prevents you from feeling bored.\n\nTotally different Sharlto Copley dito. Very different range compared to District 9. Grabe range niya dahil iba ibang characters and personalities ang ginampanan niya dito, and it works surprisingly well.\n\nBagay itong panoorin pag magkakasama kayong barkada at nagiinuman. The whole film feels like a big budget Youtube project in the best way possible. \n\nThe prosthetics department really did a great job selling the idea that the gore is real. The blood splatter, torn flesh, wounds, and impacts are all convincingly tangible.\n\nQueen’s “Dont Stop Me Now” perfectly fits the last action sequence. It gives the final stretch a fun and unstoppable rhythm.\n\nWildly entertaining. In terms of chaotic energy, immersive action, and wild brutality, this makes Deadpool’s movie feel like child’s play.","poster":"img/hardcore-henry-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Eternity","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":374,"review":"Maganda ang sets and may konting film grains that give it a soft and almost nostalgic texture. Nakakatawa si Da Vine Joy Randolph, and her humor balances the heavier parts of the story.\n\nElizabeth is so so so so great. Her performance feels unique and deeply emotional. In contrast, Larry played by Miles Teller and Luke played by Callum Turner give this Peter Quill and Thor competitive vibe, playful and ego driven but still likable.\n\nThe emotional core of the film is in her choosing a memory of what could have been, which now traps her in constantly returning to the memory of what has been. It shows how regret can anchor someone to an alternate life path in their mind.\n\nThe most memorable line for me is “Love is more than a happy thought.” It is about rising above the burdens, struggles, and sacrifices. Having been married for four years, this hit me hard. Love is being there even when you do not feel like it, supporting and building each other even through annoyance and ordinary days.\n\nI am normally not a fan of romance movies, but when the premise is thoughtful, I watch them. This gave me the same emotional feel as Time Traveller’s Wife and About Time. Ang ganda ng kirot sa puso. It feels genuine and real, not shallow or manufactured.\n\nI was surprised that the film did not explore the idea of waiting for their children to eventually join them for eternity. Personally, I would choose and wait for my kids. I do not know if they avoided this to keep the film focused on the love dilemma, or because family culture in the West is different and not as tight as ours, or maybe naive pa ako and my perception of love for children will evolve as I grow older. Perhaps the film is more about choosing a new existence beyond earthly roles, implying that they already fulfilled their part in life, and now they get to choose a version of existence that they always wanted.\n\nThe film has a lot of heart. First time ko ulit suminghot singhot ng baradong ilong sa sinehan. It feels like a quiet but sincere exploration of the question, what is love.","poster":"img/eternity-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Heat","year":1995,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","heat"],"words":491,"review":"Cute ni Natalie Portman and ang galing agad niya umarte kahit bata pa siya dito. May natural talent talaga. Al Pacino has incredible presence here, very sharp and commanding. Clever detective energy kahit dun palang sa first crime scene, parang alam niya agad what kind of criminals he is dealing with. The diner scene was a sight to see, ang weird ng mukha ni De Niro na fresh pa dito and malakas pa ang dating. His character still has full energy, not a tired old man role. Nagulat ako na may kasali dn pala si William Fichtner dito. I like him since Prison Break.\n\nGanda ng sound mixing, umiikot talaga sa tenga, parang spatial. Score or musical motif is great. The soundtrack makes the whole film feel alive even with its almost three hour runtime. Kahit mabagal at quiet minsan ang narrative, the music keeps it moving and immersive. This is a 1995 film but the lens choices, framing, aesthetic, lighting, color, and audio all feel modern. Indeed ahead of its time.\n\nYung line na, “What am I doing. I am talking to an empty telephone because there is a dead man on the other end of this fucking line.” Ang angas, memorable! And in the background yung cooks nagluluto. Let him cook literally. Anong laban ng businessman sa thugs? There is also a line that stuck with me: “You do not live with me. You live among the remains of dead people.”\n\nMy only complaint is that it follows the 90s trend of having a romance subplot. I get why it is there since it sets up the contradiction of him saying he avoids commitments so he can walk away from any heat. It gives character motivation and humanizes them. The meet up of Al Pacino and De Niro shows that they are both cut from the same cloth. They live for the game. What they are doing is their life and they cannot exist without it. And they respect each other for that, deeper than enemies.\n\nThe set up exchange drop has a really good gunfight scene. They really work as a team, coordinated and decisive. The bank heist was surgical, no wasted movement. The gunfight was incredible and Val Kilmer stole the spotlight. But really, paano nila na overpowered yung squad of policemen and still got away? This is not a fast paced heist film. It takes its time to flesh out the characters and build who they are. \n\nThe hotel scene with Natalie caught me off guard, unexpected talaga!\n\nWhen De Niro left his lover, it is a direct callback to their conversation. If he feels Al Pacino around the corner, will he leave her or not. And he did. Yung ending scene sa airfield ang galing ng paggamit ng sound ng airplane, ng pag on and off ng ilaw para sa shadow at tension. The silence adds weight to the moment and to the inevitable ending.","poster":"img/heat-1995.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Tetris","year":2023,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-27","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","tetris"],"words":198,"review":"The bank loan and mortgage scene hooked me. He was all in. What a risk taker. Ramdam kong willing siyang isugal lahat para sa vision niya.\n\nAng galing din ng Game Boy Pitch scene, it gives me Jordan Belfort energy. That drive, that confidence, that push to make something happen.\n\nSobrang political chess game. Corporate espionage, lies, fraud, and exploring Russia’s belief system and communist ideologies. Para kang nanonood ng business thriller meets government control meets personal ambition.\n\nThe whole film feels heavy. Every theme is serious and dark. Even the colors are dark and somber, parang yung life is sucked out of the world. And all for what? A video game? Ironic how a fun and simple game had to go through such a hard and complicated path just to be acquired from Russia to America. Makes you wonder how many layers of power and negotiation go into something consumers see as “just a game.”\n\nMedyo weird lang na nagiinterchange into video game pixel style yung ilang shots. Parang di makadecide yung director if serious ba ito or are they treating this like a real life game. It kind of pulls you out of the moment sometimes.","poster":"img/tetris-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Frankenstein","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-25","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","frankenstein"],"words":478,"review":"I was wrong to judge this film too quickly. Akala ko magiging grotesque, exaggerated, similar sa style ni Tim Burton. Pero hindi. The first 8 minutes are full pack action. Pinakita agad yung raw strength and immortality ni The Creature. Ang ganda rin ng color palette sa umpisa, medyo green and gold.\n\nThen as the film settled in, I found myself admiring the craft behind it. I appreciate the tangible real sets, may konting CGI for environment extension pero subtle lang. The score, wardrobe, and hair and makeup are all on point. Ang galing ng prosthetics and art department. Yung mga dissection scenes are genuinely disgusting.\n\nCharles Dance brings that Tywin Lannister aura as Baron Leopold Frankenstein. Oscar Isaac as Victor Frankenstein was perfect. Laging A-game. He really feels like a true mad scientist, especially sa pitch scene with the animated half life in front of the panel of professors.\n\nBut the performances are only one layer of it. Visually, the film speaks in shadows. The use of shadows perfectly matches the emotion and intensity of scenes. Kinilabutan ako sa dark angel vision.\n\nSymbolic yung crow sa battlefield. Parang si Victor ay isang scavenger. And when he said “It is finished,” parang ginaya si Jesus tapos biglang kumulog at umulan. \n\nYung raising of The Creature looks like a crucifixion inspired resurrection shot. The resurrection sequence was pure art. The lighting, sound, set piece, acting, VFX, special effects, lahat synchronized.\n\nYet the brilliance of the myth doesn’t end with the birth. It begins there. After The creation, there is a void of meaning. Nakuha niya yung inaasam niya, so what's next? The film shows that imbalance visually. Mas malaki si The Creature sa frame, meaning siya na ang may control at hindi si Victor.\n\nThis film makes you care for monster characters and shows another side of them. Monsters can think and feel. I think The Creature is a metaphor for the marginalized, the unwanted. Society turns away from them, so they turn to rage. That they can learn and fit in if given proper guidance and love.\n\nThis is shown through the blind man (David Bradley). Victor is blinded by ambition, while the blind man is blinded by guilt. He is the total opposite of Victor. Though physically blind, he can still see through the heart of the Creature. And through him, the Creature finally feels seen. \n\nAnd in that realization, the film turns inward. Victor calling The Creature “wicked and deformed” while facing his own reflection tells me that his hate is now directed inward. From hating death to hating himself. And my theory was further confirmed by his brother’s dying breath: “You are the monster.”\n\nBy the end, the ship feels symbolic. Victor and The Creature were stuck, cold, and broken. Now that they forgave each other, the ship has sailed, they are now finally free.","poster":"img/frankenstein-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Now You See Me: Now You Don't","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-23","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":116,"review":"Fun heist with a score that sometimes feels like Mission Impossible. Light, quick, engaging.\n\nWeird accent choices for Rosamund Pike as Veronica Vanderberg, parang pilit na pilit yung German accent.\n\nFeels like a reunion after 10 long years! Nakakatuwa makita ulit silang lahat in one frame. \n\nNandun pa rin yung elements of old school treasure hunt vibe with hidden doors, puzzle clues, secret mechanisms. Parang classic adventure mixed with modern showmanship.\n\nDaming mirrors! Kawawa mga VFX artists dito hahaha. Ang hirap magtrack ng reflections while digitally erasing cameras in the reflections.\n\nNice twist ulit as always. And it really feels like a soft reboot or passing of the torch. Entertaining and fun to watch. Solid trilogy.","poster":"img/now-you-see-me-now-you-don-t-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Devotion","year":2022,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-22","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","devotion"],"words":176,"review":"Nice aerial cinematography. Hindi siya Top Gun level, pero good enough and clear yung intention nila. Color grading looks like gold and blue, parang warm highlights with cool undertones.\n\nThe second act looks noticeably CGI. Medyo halata yung compositing in some scenes, parang may disconnect sa real world lighting and physics. Medyo too wallowing on the Black guy struggles. Gets ko yung hugot niya, pero parang sobrang inemphasize to the point na nawawala yung ibang dimensions ng story. I couldn’t connect too much emotionally dahil medyo predictable yung beats.\n\nMas tangible yung third act. Wise decision talaga to focus the budget on the climax. Dun lumabas yung craftsmanship. Yung seamless wide shot to close up habang bumababa siya to rescue Jesse (Jonathan Majors), ang ganda. Mukhang one long continuous shot: smooth, hindi distracting. Yun yung highlight ng film for me.\n\nAnd that shot na grounded si Tom (Glen Powell) habang lumilipad yung squadron niya, he looks alone and wingless. Overall, a decent film with a strong final act, pero I didn’t fully connect with the story.","poster":"img/devotion-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fly Me to the Moon","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-21","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","appletv"],"words":169,"review":"Nice job sila sa scale. Ang liit talaga ng characters compared sa rocket parts and machinery, and yun yung pinakanagstand out sakin. Effective use of visual to portray the scale difference between two objects.\n\nProduction design, hair and make up, wardrobe are all appropriate for a period piece. Wala akong nakitang off. Solid aesthetic.\n\nNapanood ko na yung similar film idea ng faking a moon landing before (Moonwalkers), pero ito mas fun. Mas nagexplore sila sa perspective ng mga nasa actual NASA like how pilots or engineers would feel kung nalaman nilang may “fake filming” happening. Mas light, mas playful.\n\nScarlett’s character is great. Very persuasive as an advertising expert, bagay sa kanya yung role. Scarlett is very good. Channing… not much of a fan dito. Parang wala silang chemistry.\n\nVFX are nice and the energetic split-screen sequences add life to the pacing.\n\nMadami ring moments na di naghold ng attention ko. Could have been shorter. \n\nOverall, enjoyable, but not strong enough to keep full engagement the whole time","poster":"img/fly-me-to-the-moon-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Wolfs","year":2024,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-21","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","wolfs"],"words":101,"review":"Two clean up job artists forced to work together. Simple premise, no unnecessary complications.\n\nAng ganda ng pasok ng Smooth Operator, sakto yung vibe, parang effortless cool moment na nagbigay ng konting personality sa film kahit medyo flat yung ibang parts.\n\nMaraming green and red lighting and grading choices. Noticeable siya, pero hindi naman deep or symbolic looking. Parang style choice lang to set the mood, not really something that changes the experience.\n\nOverall, wala namang spectacular na moments. Pwede siyang background noise habang may ginagawa ka. Entertaining enough to finish, pero hindi siya yung tipo na iiisipin mo after watching.","poster":"img/wolfs-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"CODA","year":2021,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-20","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","coda"],"words":354,"review":"The film starts light and funny. Yung humor actually works. Hindi pilit. Ang daming funny moments dito na talagang natawa ako, lalo na yung rap song scene. Kahit bingi yung tatay, ramdam daw niya yung vibration sa pwet niya. Yung doctor scene na sinabi nilang no sex for two weeks, solid. The herpes prank, the “du-et” joke, pati yung discussion nilang mag-pamilya reminding her parents not to have sex as said by the doctor, and Ruby’s father telling Miles to wear a condom. Ngayon lang ulit ako natawa sa jokes sa movie na hindi pilit.\n\nGanda rin ng boses nila. May kilig yung Troy Bolton and Gabriela vibes nila. Very warm and soft yung scenes nila together. Parang high school talaga.\n\nHonestly, you need to watch this on Apple TV kasi kung walang matinong subtitles, mahirap. Ang dami kasing ASL scenes, and kailangan mo talagang maintindihan kung anong sinasabi nila.\n\nPagdating sa kalagitnaan, biglang naging mabigat. Ang sakit panoorin na Ruby is forced to carry responsibilities na hindi naman niya pinili. Torn siya between serving her family and chasing her passion. Nakakapagod yung idea na siya yung tanging connection ng pamilya to the world, pero siya rin yung pinakatalented na gustong lumipad.\n\nMost of the best emotional beats are in silence. Napaiyak ako sa scene when the film muted the performance just so we can peer into their situation. You instantly feel how disconnected the family is from her world. It's heartbreaking to see them na they cannot relate to the audience reacting to how awesome Ruby’s voice is. Hindi nila marinig how great Ruby is.\n\nThe father and daughter moment was so sweet yet sad. Yung tatay niya, trying to “listen” to her voice through the vibrations on her neck. I can feel his pain, not having the ability to hear his own child and how much he wants to experience her singing. He’s doing what he can with what he has. Sweet pero ang sakit.\n\nThe audition and ending scenes kumurot talaga sa puso ko, and it is a really beautiful film. Nothing flash, just straightforward visuals that match the story perfectly.","poster":"img/coda-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Room","year":2015,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-19","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","room"],"words":667,"review":"Their room feels so cramped. I was under the impression that they were under experiment or that the world was already apocalyptic. Everything looked so small, so limiting, so suffocating that my first instinct was: “What kind of universe is this?”\n\nOnly later did I realize that we are seeing it from Jack’s universe. A boy who believes that the Room is literally the entire world. The film traps us inside his perspective so effectively that even I felt like there must be something bigger, darker, and more sci-fi happening outside.\n\nThis is a very different Brie Larson. Bare face, tired eyes, broken spirit. She isn’t acting like a movie protagonist such as Captain Marvel.\n\nI’ll be honest. The kid is another annoying spoiled kid at the start. That’s why I don’t like to have a son. But as the film goes along the way, he gets better. It’s because he’s learning for the first time of what the world actually is. Jack isn’t spoiled. He’s inexperienced, overstimulated, and raised in a literal box.\n\nThe escape planning feels weird. Using her son like that. It’s terrifying. It’s dangerous. It’s desperate. But after seven years of failed attempts, trauma, and dwindling hope, her son was her only chance. The horrible logic becomes the only logic.\n\nAnd when he finally got out, I was like \"Dafuq?\". Mukhang normal lang yung outside ng Room. Bakit sila nakalockup dun? The outside world is peaceful. The streets are normal. Joy wasn’t locked in by apocalypse. She wasn’t trapped for research. She wasn’t hidden by a global disaster. \n\nEvil was simply living in a backyard shed, in the middle of a normal neighborhood, where no one ever noticed. The idea that the worst things can happen under our noses while life moves on normally, makes me shiver. \n\nThe police officer deducing where the Room is got me clapping. She didn’t solve it by force. She solved it by empathy. Every detail was a map hidden in a child’s innocence. Naalala ko tuloy si Liam Neeson nung tinatrack ang kidnappers ng anak nya. \n\nParang nakukulangan lang ako sa story ni Joy bakit parang wala siyang dalang trauma kahit 7 yrs siyang kinulong dun sa Room but that is just an akala. Kinikimkim lang pala niya. She masks her pain because she’s finally free, and the world expects her to be grateful. She suppresses her breakdown because she wants to look normal. \n\nHer tipping point is the media interview: na para bang lahat ng decision nya for Jack was not to protect his best interests. The moment the interviewer questions her decision as a mother, all her suppressed guilt bursts open. The film becomes a mirror of real struggles of motherhood: They've done everything they could but the always finds a way to blame them. \n\nI really got touched when Jack said “I love you” to his grandma. I feel like she was the first relationship he had next to his mother. Jack’s world expands one person at a time. Joy's wish becoming to be true one step at a time: Jack connecting to the real world. Joy is his universe. His grandma becomes his first new planet. It’s the beginning of a bigger life, a bigger world, and a bigger heart. Love, for Jack, is no longer confined to a single person. That is growth.\n\nI got really teary eyed when Joy said she is not really a good mom but Jack only said, “But you are mom.” This is one of the best lines in the entire film. Goes to show that children only care about presence, not perfection. Oftentimes, we think we are not good enough for our children but little did we know that we mean everything for them. Sometimes children see the truth adults can’t see.\n\nThe final scene feels like healing. The Room, which once felt like the entire universe, now looks small, powerless, meaningless. Saying goodbye to a place of suffering and opening a new chapter of life.","poster":"img/room-2015.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fury","year":2014,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-17","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","fury"],"words":319,"review":"Fury opens with a silhouette slicing through a blue washed battlefield, like a painting unfolding in real time. The moment we enter the tank, ramdam mo agad yung claustrophobia. Cramped interiors na parang kabaong.\n\nAnd the pyrotechnics? Feels brutally real. Bullets tearing through armor, sparks flying, tracers glowing like neon death. Parang sensory overload pero grounded. Parang first time ko makapanood ng tank vs. tank sequence. The whole thing plays out like a chess match, only with tanks as the pieces.\n\nThere are gore shots that feel disturbingly real, with bombs and shells reaping heads off. Machine guns reaping limbs off. \n\nThere are also moments where the guttural score swells, emphasizing their march to death feeling.\n\nLogan Lerman is the perfect POV characte: innocent, confused, morally lost. Lens thru a newbie. Day one pa lang, he’s already forced to kill, torture, and watch horrors, and you feel him slowly corroding inside. You see his hands shake from fear, then eventually shaking from anger.\n\nHis journey is the real payoff. From a boy who couldn’t pull a trigger to a soldier willing to stand his ground even when hell is literally surrounding him. The last stand sequence feels like Only The Brave, soldiers making their final stand against a raging, unquenchable fire of destruction and imminent death.\n\nFury is painful, gritty, and beautifully shot. Ang ganda ng shots na mata lang nila ang may ilaw while everything else is pitch black. Sa mata pa lang, kita mo na agad yung acting: fear, anger, determination, hopelessness, etc.\n\nAng ganda rin ng silhouettes against fire, lalo na yung night scenes with orange and yellow haze, parang they’re fighting in hell. Every fire lit silhouette is perfection. The way the flames hit the lens, how it glows and bleeds into the dark, parang painting na growing hot and angrily red.\n\nIn the end, what Brad Pitt considered his home ultimately became his tomb.","poster":"img/fury-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Aftersun","year":2022,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","father and daughter"],"words":306,"review":"Aftersun feels like a lighthearted, warm and simple father and daughter story. Lighthearted, warm, innocent. Sophie looks at her dad with pure wonder. Curious about relationships, intimacy, excited to grow up, asking questions with wide eyes. \n\nPero habang tumatagal, may something off kay Calum. Hindi ko sya magets. Minsan playful, minsan distant. One moment he’s gentle, the next he shuts down the camera like he can’t bear being seen. Yung pagdura niya sa bathroom mirror was the first sign. Yung hindi pagsama sa pagkanta, the sudden mood drops, the long walks alone, even the scene where he goes home butt naked. Lahat yun tells you he’s wrestling with something he never says out loud.\n\nAnd that’s why those rave scenes visually hit differently. Those strobe lit insert shots where Sophie is reaching for Calum in the dark. It’s adult Sophie trying to find her father in her memory, reaching out, trying to pull him back from a darkness she didn’t notice when she was young. Nakakalungkot, pero ang poetic.\n\nStill, there are scenes of pure warmth between them. That moment when Sophie confesses about kissing Michael is so heart warming. She's so honest and open with her dad. Calum’s reaction is warm, kind, present.\n\nEverything builds up to Sophie's last hug. That scene makes me think that she wants to hold on to her dad once again. She clings to his memory like she's trying to go back in time.\n\nIf I had one complaint, that would be the long shots that are way too long, I just want to skip it. \n\nBut despite that, I’d still watch this again. I would definitely want to watch this again with a different perspective. Maybe next time focusing on Calum and understanding him a bit more because right now I was focused on the innocent eyes of Sophie.","poster":"img/aftersun-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Apocalypse Now","year":1979,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","apocalypse now"],"words":428,"review":"The cinematography and color grading shocked me. For a 1979 film, its visuals feel strangely modern. Sharp silhouettes, blazing fire in the distance, saturated skies. Same vibe as The Impossible. Coppola uses anamorphic lenses with circular flares, creating this dreamy distortion where war looks almost beautiful. By the 30 minute mark, I finally understood why people call this a visual masterpiece. Yung mga helicopter shots sa horizon? God tier filmmaking. Ito na ang pinaka visually appealing na war film for me. My only complain is the overuse of lens flares. May pagka JJ Abrams moment. \n\nThe assault on the village is jaw dropping. Precise, choreographed chaos. Gareth Edwards clearly took inspirations from this film. I can see Rogue One all over this sequence. And that jet bombing scene? Pure spectacle. Practical explosions everywhere. No CGI, just raw violence captured on camera.\n\nThen there’s Kilgore: calm, arrogant, crazy cool. Surfing while bombs fall. It’s funny but disturbing. The film isn’t subtle: war turns men into creatures who think they’re invincible gods. \n\nWhen the film reaches the Do Lung Bridge, it becomes something else entirely. The nighttime cinematography is shot brilliantly. It feels like Left 4 Dead, there's no turning back!\n\nBut everything changes the moment Willard meets the indigenous tribe and the photojournalist. The visuals shift again: stronger contrast, deeper greens and golds, parang Kong: Skull Island in mood and palette. Then Kurtz emerges. Shadows hide half his face, wiping water from his bald head like Baron Harkonnen. \n\nThe story feels not like you’re journeying toward Kurtz. You’re descending into him. Willard sees his future in Kurtz, brilliance twisted by the weight of truth. “The horror… the horror” isn’t a villain’s final line. It’s the realization that in war, morality collapses, sanity dissolves, and survival requires becoming the monster you’re hunting\n\nWhen he steps outside and the tribe bows to him, he walks away instead. He doesn’t order the airstrike. He proved the woman wrong: Willard is not an animal nor a god. \n\nI came in expecting a war epic, but slowly it became psychological, as if peering through their minds. This film shows how crazy the soldiers become inside a war zone. Senseless killing and happy triggers. I left with something else entirely: a reflection on men at war, on the impossible idea that after seeing hell, the system expects you to return home normal.\n\nCould have been an easy 5 star film, but there are beats na mabagal. Maraming shots na pinakitang just for the sake of a cool shot but don’t necessarily move the story forward.","poster":"img/apocalypse-now-1979.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Iron Claw","year":2023,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","zac efron"],"words":476,"review":"Iron Claw opens with explosive energy, a full on wrestling spectacle where Zac Efron appears on screen massively ripped! \n\nI feel that this family’s honor is rooted in prestige and the siblings are competing for it. Victory isn’t just a goal; it’s currency. It’s survival. It’s the only language their father ever taught them.\n\nThere’s a deep contrast to the film. Greens and golds bleeding into each other. The sets and wardrobe lock you into the period, but what hits harder is how suffocating their world feels. Their father is exactly the kind of man who uses his children as tools to continue the legacy he failed to finish. Every wrestling match becomes a transaction, every achievement met with the expectation to do more. The film shows a parent turning their children into extensions of their own unfinished business. \n\n\"You are Von Erichs,\" their father constantly implies, \"so you must win.\" It’s really hard for people who grow up believing they’re cursed.It feels like you are destined for failure, with no room for growth.\n\nZac’s training scenes are phenomenal. Hero shots that show discipline, drive, and the terrifying weight of being the family anchor. Their entire family dynamic is a portrait of dependency: needing their father’s approval so badly that they can’t do much with their own lives. The pain of comparison bleeds into everything. At pinagkaperahan pa talaga!\n\nIn the last act, the film flexes its mastery with a beautifully executed one take shot. Perfectly blocked, perfectly lit, every emotion raw and exposed. \n\nPressure looks like this. The pain of not being enough, even when you give your entire life for a father who never truly saw you. \n\nAnd then the afterlife reunion scene comes in, gentle and heartbreaking. A moment so beautifully simple. The brothers, finally free from expectations, meet in a place with no spotlight and no promoter. Just love. \n\nThe final moments bring everything together. Zac’s character breaking free from the toxic rule that men don’t cry. That men must swallow everything until it kills them, is one of the most powerful shifts in the entire film. His kids telling him he can cry, and he doesn’t have to be “sir” or “champ” or “the next Von Erich legacy,” is the moment he finally becomes a real father. A different father. A healed father. A father who honors his brothers not by repeating the curse but by ending it. \n\nIt’s the kind of love they never got to experience in life. The kind that isn’t earned through bruises or championships. He finds love and acceptance through his children, filling the hole in his heart from yearning for his brothers.\n\nIron Claw is more than a wrestling movie. It’s a portrait of pressure, masculinity, and the casualties of living for someone else’s dream. It’s about sons who were raised like soldiers, not children.","poster":"img/the-iron-claw-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Daniela Forever","year":2024,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-13","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","danielaforever"],"words":694,"review":"The film opens with a dreamy, neon haze, pink and blue colliding like a fever dream like Vice City. The first shots already feel like memories slipping in and out of focus. Everything looks ethereal, cinematic, and slightly unreal. The aspect ratio feels off center on purpose. It’s uncomfortable, but that’s the point because nothing here is meant to feel stable. The film grain, the anamorphic lenses, the deliberate imperfections all make the fantasy feel tangible.\n\nThen comes the crash back to reality. Suddenly, the world looks flat, blurred, and lifeless. Like a downgrade from 4K to 360p. The lines jag, the colors fade, the exposure feels wrong. It’s a deliberate degradation of dynamic range and lens depth to create emotional separation. The result? Reality feels dull, cheap, and distasteful compared to the hyper vivid dream state.  i think the idea behind that decision is for us, the audience to not want his reality and prefer the more vibrant dream state. We’re meant to crave the illusion more than the truth. His real life looks like an old Spanish telenovela: desaturated and stiff. It’s a brilliant, risky stylistic choice that makes us complicit in his delusion.\n\nThe film’s energy blends Black Mirror’s technological anxiety with Eternal Sunshine’s emotional surgery, Vanilla Sky’s layered dreams, and Bruce Almighty’s human as God guilt. At its core, it’s about control, about a man who rebuilds the past until he becomes its prisoner, trapped by his own emotional dependency.\n\nThat confrontation scene where Daniela (Beatrice Grannò) confesses she’s still in love with Teresa is the heartbreaker. Cold blue light, rain tapping outside, and the line “Do you still love me?” followed by “Just hold on to that and erase everything.” Instantly, nag iba yung lighting sa set piece, the lighting literally transforms from icy blue to warm yellow in that moment. The warmth of denial replacing the chill of truth. It’s a stunning visual metaphor for emotional manipulation through artificial reality.\n\nHenry Golding gives one of his most emotionally fragile performances. You can see his character slowly unravel as he begins doing what he swore he wouldn’t: controlling Daniela’s memories, rewriting her past, deciding who deserves to exist. The world bends to his will, and with every edit, he loses another piece of his own soul.\n\nThe score is great, a fusion of melancholy piano and cold techno pulses. The film often uses partition framing, reminiscent of Parasite’s visual echoes of social imbalance shots, where characters are divided by glass, mirrors, or architecture to show who holds power. It’s subtle but constant: whoever occupies the open frame dominates; whoever’s behind a divider becomes powerless.\n\nThere’s also a distinct use of vertical power framing. A blocking technique that measures emotional authority through height and positioning. When one character stands while the other sits, it tells you who’s momentarily in control. The film often lets Nicolas (Henry Golding) tower over Daniela, but when he’s back in reality, he’s framed lower or trapped within cramped compositions, stripped of dominance.\n\nBy the end, the narrative folds into itself like a dream within a dream because he just couldn’t let go of her and losing her forever but in the process he loses himself instead. The dream becomes his new prison. The brighter the illusion, the darker his reality becomes. And when he chooses to stay inside it, you feel the weight of what that means that sometimes, people would rather live in a beautiful lie than face an honest loneliness.\n\nSo by the ending, I think Nicolas wants Daniela to live again, to believe that everything that happened: Teresa’s breakup, meeting Nicolas, her death, were all just part of a dream. He can rebuild her world, but he’ll never truly belong in it.\n\nThe more I think about it, the more it feels like a tragic illusion. Maybe the reason I felt before that there was never real chemistry between them is because he was never meant to exist in her story at all. He’s not reviving her; he’s inserting himself into memories that were never his to begin with like a trespasser. So sad, but did not deliver an ultimate gut punch.","poster":"img/daniela-forever-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"C'mon C'mon","year":2021,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-12","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","joaquinphoenix"],"words":654,"review":"C’mon C’mon is tender, quiet, and honest. I’ll admit the black and white format made it hard for me to get attached at first. Maybe because I see life in color, and this felt drained of it. Still, there’s something about the lens choice that saves it. The shallow focus and creamy bokeh create intimacy, as if we’re watching memories through fogged glass. Every frame looks soft, nostalgic, and human.\n\nThe score is also intimate, melancholic, and deeply personal. It reminded me of The Time Traveler’s Wife soundtrack. The piano and strings don’t rise to tell you what to feel, they stay low.\n\nThe film almost plays like a documentary, a study of how children see the world. Joaquin Phoenix’s character tries to understand his nephew, but Jesse  (Woody Norman) is wild energy trapped in confusion. I’ll be honest, I was annoyed by him. Spoiled brat energy. If I were Johnny (Joaquin Phoenix), I couldn’t handle it either. And that’s what the film quietly asks: how far would you go for your family member (in this case, his sister) and how far can patience stretch when the child isn’t yours? \n\nWhat lingers is that interview moment with one of the kids saying, “Someone being there to understand you, but still not understand you.” That line stuck with me. It’s how children navigate this grown up world, always trying to express, but being mistranslated by adults. It’s heartbreaking because it’s true. We all want to be seen, but not everyone who sees us truly gets us.\n\nGaby Hoffman as Viv frustrated me more than anyone. Why would you prioritize your sick husband over your own child? That scene made me angry because as a parent, I can’t imagine making that choice. My wife should never have to choose between me and our kids. I need her to prioritize our kids, not me. \n\nThen that song, “Anyone Who Knows What Love Is” by Irma Thomas, plays and it’s perfect. I remember hearing it in Black Mirror Season 1, carrying that same melancholic tone of loving despite brokenness. “You can blame me, try to shame me… still I’ll care for you.” Bagay dito because it’s a form of tolerance. \n\n“You can run around, even put me down… still I’ll be there for you.” The phrase “will understand” implies that she believes her actions reflect a deeper grasp of love than what the world can see. \n\n“The world may think I’m foolish, they can’t see you like I can” asserts that only someone who truly knows love will understand. That’s what makes the song powerful: a gritty persistence in hardship. \n\n“Anyone who knows what love is will understand” becomes an anthem of pure, unwavering love, even though love isn’t always glamorous or easy. It’s a song about endurance, about loving when it no longer makes sense.\n\nWhen Johnny reads Star Child by Claire A. Nivola, it says: “Over the years, you will try to make sense of that happy, sad, full, empty, always-shifting life you’re in.” That cuts deep. That’s the struggle of being alive. Moving forward in this life while experiencing all sorts of things. A roller coaster of emotions. One moment you think you have everything, then the next, you feel like everything you’re doing is pointless.\n\nBut what sealed the film for me was Jesse’s speech about him and his mother: “No matter how much we love each other, we’ll never know everything about each other.” It really made sense to me as someone who isn’t very open with my mother — and vice versa. We know we love each other, but there are so many things we still don’t know about one another.\n\nC’mon C’mon doesn’t try to give meaning to life but it just sits with you while you try to learn it. It’s soft, flawed, frustrating, and real. Like love. Like family. Like growing up in a world that never stops shifting.","poster":"img/c-mon-c-mon-2021.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Prisoners","year":2013,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","denis"],"words":316,"review":"From the first frame, you can already feel Denis Villeneuve’s fingerprints: the cold blue palette and the lonely silhouettes. That brown tinted cinematic look is Denis Villeneuve’s bread and butter. He uses it not just for aesthetics, but to trap you in a moral temperature. The earthy tones are his way of grounding mystery in realism, making every frame feel heavy, humid, and suffocating. And I know I’m in good hands dahil si Denis ito.\n\nHugh’s acting is always good. You can see the exhaustion building under his eyes. He’s sleep deprived, desperate, terrified, clinging to the only thing he still understands: rage. If Arya were missing, I’d probably break the same way. He’s a father stripped of reason, driven only by instinct. As the film suggests, he’s one of the prisoners: locked inside his own anger. Imprisoned by his grief and anger, losing himself in the process.\n\nThe film balances that chaos with control through Jake Gyllenhaal’s Detective Loki. Jake is another kind of captive: imprisoned by duty, by the pressure to solve the case and alleviate the parents’ anxiety.\n\nPaul Dano, meanwhile, doesn’t even need words. The way he shrieks in pain while hidden in shadows says everything. His silence is the film’s loudest sound. Denis surrounds his audience with silence, and low frequency tension that gives that crime mystery atmosphere. \n\nAnd then comes the twist. Nagkatotoo yung hula ko nung una pa lang, na yung nakita sa basement na bangkay ay somehow connected dun kay Mrs. Jones. And when it was finally revealed, it didn’t feel like a twist; it felt like a confirmation of something that Jake should have pieced together a few days ago.\n\nThe ending, a literal car chase against time. And that final shot, when the whistle faintly echoes beneath the snow, is pure Villeneuve poetry. It’s ambiguous, haunting, perfect. Father and daughter trade places: one buried, one reborn.","poster":"img/prisoners-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"No Country for Old Men","year":2007,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","roger deakins"],"words":370,"review":"The film starts off like a painting: a vast desert drenched in silence and gold. Roger Deakins’ cinematography is so crisp it almost feels still, each frame heavy with quiet tension. The brown tinted daylight and deep blue nights give it that Western dusk tone. There are a lot of silhouette shots that I like, and dun pa lang, alam ko na agad why this is considered a cinematic masterpiece. I can really feel the DOP’s DNA, dahil siya rin yung sa Sicario.\n\nMay action na agad sa simula pa lang. When Llewelyn Moss (Josh Brolin) runs from the dog across the river, it feels like The Revenant; every movement is survival.\n\nThe sound design here is pure brilliance. The hissing of Anton Chigurh’s oxygen tank, the suitcase sliding inside an air duct, each sound replaces the score. The Coens remove music entirely letting dread become the soundtrack. The silence builds up to an inevitable standoff. \n\nJavier Bardem is a quiet menace. Anton isn’t just a killer, he’s inevitability personified. The calm voice, the still eyes, the random philosophy of chance. The coin toss scene is pure dread. You can’t predict him because he doesn’t feel, he’s almost robotic. Ang ganda ng light na galing sa natumbang lamp. Yung direction ng ilaw, papunta from below up to his face, looks almost a horror film shot. \n\nThis could have been an easy five star film, but the second half felt anticlimactic. The rising tension and “action” suddenly stop, leaving Sheriff Bell (Tommy Lee Jones) alone with the aftermath. Moss dying off screen got me thinking there would be a flashback or at least some sort of explanation, but there’s none. It’s just a callback to the driver who told him that hitchhiking like that is very dangerous. Meaning, him finding the money out of nowhere shows he was always an outsider to the chaos. A man who “hitchhiked” his way into this whole mess.\n\nIt feels like the entire story shifts to the POV of Bell, making it clearer how the world has become too chaotic for men like him. He retires while the villain remains on the loose, and suddenly, the line “You can’t stop what’s coming” really makes sense.","poster":"img/no-country-for-old-men-2007.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Whiplash","year":2014,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-10","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":555,"review":"From the moment the first drum hit lands, Whiplash doesn’t ask for your attention, it demands it. The world is dim, color graded like a fever dream: sweaty, golden, claustrophobic. The way Fletcher (J.K. Simmons) stares at Andrew (Miles Teller) like no words, just pressure really sets the tone. Discipline. Precision. Fear. Right off the bat, ramdam mo na agad paano sya mangarag.\n\nHe operates with surgical precision, like someone in the military. On the dot, disciplined, precise, neat. Lahat takot magkamali sa kanya because he is a perfectionist.\n\nAng unpredictable niya. Binigyan muna niya ng false pep talk si Andrew (Miles Teller) para gumaan ang pakiramdam at magloosen up, then biglang one missed note and he snaps. Chairs fly. Words cut deeper than drumsticks. He doesn’t just break egos, he dismantles souls. Ang hilig niya mag–name calling at mangdegrade.\n\nNapa “what?” nalang ako when he fires a player for being “out of tune,” only to reveal later na iba pala ang out of tune, tinetest niya lang yung pinalayas niya, and in the end, hindi rin pumasa.\n\nThe way Andrew shifts from a timid boy to a manic, bleeding perfectionist is both terrifying and mesmerizing. When he dips his bloodied hand into freezing water, ramdam mo na he’s choosing greatness over everything. And just when you think he’s winning, BAM! Pinalitan siya agad as temporary core. \n\nAnd when he finally earned back his spot, he changed dahil sa ambition niya. Then out of nowhere, the car crash happened. Napamura ako dito. Sobrang unexpected. He crawls back to the stage anyway, trembling, bleeding, determined. That performance fails. He snaps. He attacks Fletcher. Dreams shattered. At first, you root for Andrew, but then maiinis ka na lang din sa huli.\n\nThen the ending, Fletcher sets Andrew up to humiliate him in front of the world. No sheet music. No warning. He was caught by surprise, para makaganti sa pagkakasack sa kanya from the university. Callback ito sa ginawa niya nung una, kinuha muna niya yung loob ni Andrew bago niya sirain piece by piece.\n\nBut Andrew refuses to be discouraged after the first set. Akala ko talaga ayawan na, change career na. He fights his way back to show the audience that he is the best. He goes out again, and he plays. Harder. Faster. Beyond tempo. Beyond sanity.\n\nFletcher is furious at first, then slowly, he starts conducting. No words. Just eye contact. For the first and last time, they understand each other. It’s no longer teacher and student. Andrew isn’t playing for approval anymore, he’s playing to be legendary.\n\nAnd when Fletcher gives that tiny nod, the smallest, most satisfying “good job,” dahil nahanap na niya ang “Charlie Parker” niya.\n\nI hate the colors. I feel sick, parang first time ko lang sumakit ang ulo sa isang magandang color grading. Maybe that’s the point: you get into their heads, and I felt how hard their life is. How exposed their bodies are in the pursuit of greatness and talent.\n\nI love it. I feel inspired. A part of me agrees that greatness really does demand a sacrifice. I can relate to this film. I was once a perfectionist without remorse, compassion, or empathy. I’m grateful for my managers who were hard on me because they pushed me to become the best version of myself.","poster":"img/whiplash-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Limitless","year":2011,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","bradley cooper"],"words":447,"review":"Limitless begins at the end, like it’s telling us: this isn’t a success story. This is a warning.\n\nFrom that first scene, the film locks you in by visualizing cognition. The moment Eddie (Bradley Cooper) swallows the NZT pill, the world changes. The color grading shifts from muted grays turn into a sharp blues and glowing golds. Suddenly, everything becomes more vivid and every detail appears sharper. The film uses color and contrast to communicate to the audience if he is under the influence or not, especially with his eyes, which shift into an almost electric blue. \n\nAnd with that heightened perception comes control. He cleans his apartment with frightening precision. Every movement becomes calculated and clear. And at that point, the film makes you wonder hey, can I try that pill too?\n\nVisually, the film takes it even further. The camera performs that now iconic infinite dolly zoom or fractal push. Pushing forward through streets, sidewalks, taxis, walls, blocks, as if the world is expanding with his mind. Hindi lang sya visual effect it’s part of the storytelling.It’s trippy in the best way.\n\nBut what really elevates it is how editing and psychology merge. Mafi-feel mo talaga yung disorientation parang lasing. Those blackout cuts: memory missing, time jumping, scenes skipped are exactly how addiction feels. You’re present, then gone. You’re in control, then lost. Parang Memento x Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind. \n\nAnd anchoring all this chaos is Bradley Cooper. His range is incredible here. He can be the smartest man in the room and then seconds later, the most broken junkie on the street. His performance isn’t about genius. It’s about obsession. When he’s on NZT, he is godlike. When he’s off it, he’s no one: shaking, sweating, lost.\n\nThen comes Carl Van Loon (Robert De Niro). He doesn’t need NZT. He is old money, old intellect. A power forged through time, experience, and discipline. His presence alone reminds you that artificial greatness will always be a cheap version of the real thing.\n\nSo the film never really asks if NZT is good or bad. Instead, it asks something darker: What happens to someone who tastes perfection, then has to return to being human? Addiction here isn’t framed like drugs or alcohol, it’s addiction to power, to clarity, to control. And once you taste it, there is no going back. That’s the philosophy at the core, when you experience something beyond human capacity, it can lead to an inescapable kind of death.\n\nBecause Limitless was never really about NZT. It’s about ambition without restraint. Intelligence without soul. Power without purpose. And the most dangerous addiction of all: the desire to never be human again.","poster":"img/limitless-2011.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Master","year":2012,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","joaquinphoenix"],"words":904,"review":"From the very first frame, the ocean waves churning behind a warship, symbolizes him being churned by a powerful force. Plus, the score is excellent.\n\nHair and makeup are on point, and the wardrobe perfectly fits the period setting. Ang ganda din ng color grading. Yung intimate scene nila sa photo processing room ang ganda rin. Yellows, greens, pure solid blacks. Visually, ang rich and intentional ng bawat frame.\n\nAll this careful world building would be meaningless without Joaquin Phoenix: his spine is bent like a question mark, his lips slightly twisted to the side. Joaquin Phoenix doesn’t just play the role, his entire body is broken in every frame. Joaquin is unhinged brilliance. His movements are animalistic, sexually deprived, mentally fractured. Even the way he runs feels like Joker: erratic, desperate, unhinged. He has episodes, breakdowns, hallucinations. You can already feel that same mentally ill Joker energy brewing inside him. I believe this is one of the reasons Todd Phillips cast him as Joker.\n\nYung mga hallucinations niya, naked women walking around fully clothed rooms is not for comedy. It shows how his loneliness distorts intimacy. Philip Seymour Hoffman counters that with terrifying calm. A man who smiles while tightening the chains.\n\nAnd just when you think Joaquin is the storm at the center of this film, in walks the calm: Lancaster Dodd. Like some false prophet floating above the lost, Philip Seymour Hoffman enters both charming and eerily calm. The way he talks to Freddie isn’t loud or aggressive; it’s hypnotically compassionate. Controlled. Measured. He doesn’t demand loyalty, he seduces it. And that’s even more dangerous. This is a world without a God, only men trying to become one.\n\nThat charm is the bait. But the hook comes later. During the processing scenes, there’s no score just silence, breathing, restrained voices. No music, no distractions. Just a man being slowly reprogrammed. It’s all dialogue, but it’s intense. The lighting adds to the mood parang ini-interrogate siya. The questions get more and more personal. Ramdam mong nasasaktan siya sa bawat tanong, pero the Master doesn’t care. He’s searching for cracks, looking for the exact place where he can break Joaquin and reshape him.\n\nAnd I find myself asking: what does it feel like to be on the receiving end of that? Are the followers truly enlightened? Or just desperate to belong? Pinipilit lang ba nila may masabi para makasali? Or talagang namamanipulate sila sa words?\n\nThe cult system is brilliant and horrifying. You see obedience being built like a ritual: walk to the wall, then to the window, over and over, until your mind stops questioning why. It’s not about faith, it’s about control. Blind obedience. \n\nHe gets really annoyed by the heckling. Goes to show that he really is just making all this up because even his loyal follower Helen (Laura Dern) saw inconsistencies. Which exposes everything. If you are truly enlightened, why are you threatened by questions? Bakit ka nagagalit when truth is uncomfortable? Hypocrisy quietly bleeds through his teachings. He preaches “no reaction,” but explodes when cornered.\n\nWhat makes it more haunting is Freddie sees it. The entire process feels like indoctrination: brainwashing disguised as healing. Pero kahit siya mismo ay mentally unstable, siya pa rin ang pinakaaware. He sees the madness in the cult more clearly than anyone else. Even Master’s son sees it too, but stays silent because he’s family, because belonging feels safer than questioning. That makes the cult even more tragic: it’s not faith, it’s fear of being alone.\n\nFreddie knows it’s insane. He sees through the lies. But he stays. Why? Because sometimes, belonging is more addictive than freedom. Even brainwashing can look like love when you’re starving for it. And mas lumala pa siya as he goes down the process. And the moment someone questions like when Freddie challenges the critic in the salon or when skeptics ask Dodd for proof, he loses composure. Parang mga cult ngayon, nagiging violent to the extent na pumapatay kapag may nageexpose sa kanila or nagsasalita ng masama against their leader, and they start wishing ill on those people and consider them as the enemy.\n\nAnd on the other side, Lancaster needs Freddie just as much. Their connection goes beyond leader and follower, it’s mutual addiction. One needs control. The other needs purpose. They’re not enemies. They’re two halves of the same wound.\n\nVisually, Paul Thomas Anderson frames them beautifully. The creamy bokeh only returns when Freddie is free from “The Cause.” Inside the cult, the lenses are flat: no depth, no softness, no intimacy. But the moment he escapes, whether it’s when he’s running or riding a bike across the desert, the shots turn painterly again.\n\nIt mirrors the early boat scene under the bridge, the calm before the storm and now, this feels like the calm after surviving it. Colors breathe again. The contrast is stunning. It’s visual poetry that tells us Freddie only regains focus and clarity when he’s no longer under the Master’s grip.\n\nAnd that’s why The Master isn’t just about cults. It’s about the human hunger to be led. To be told what to do. To be seen. Kasi minsan, it’s easier to surrender your mind than to live alone with your thoughts.\n\nThe Master doesn’t give answers. It just asks one quiet, disturbing question: If someone saves you from your loneliness but demands your soul in return… would you say no?","poster":"img/the-master-2012.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Hereditary","year":2018,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-09","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","ariaster"],"words":255,"review":"Hereditary open with an eerie score to set the mode right. The camera moves into a dollhouse, and suddenly that miniature room becomes real, as if the characters are just pieces being moved by someone else. \n\nAn hour in, wala pa rin akong madeduce. Ari Aster doesn’t rush. It’s a slow burn. Silence. The camera lingers just a few seconds longer than it should, making you sit inside the discomfort.\n\nToni Collette owns this movie. Her performance feels like a raw nerve: shaking, gasping, barely holding on. Her outburst at the dinner table is one of the most powerful scenes I’ve seen. A mother who’s not just angry, but shattered. You can feel that she’s been holding those words in for so long, and finally she had the chance to say them. All the while, all you can hear is the clock ticking. The ticking clock in the background feels like a countdown to something inevitable.\n\nAnd then there’s Charlie. Creepy without trying. When the tragedy hits, the silence after: no music, no scream. Just shock. The way the camera stays on Peter’s face, not the body. \n\nThe chalkboard and notebook writing isn’t creepy to me. It feels too gimmicky. The visuals aren’t frightening, it’s really the sound that’s effective, lalo na yung hindi mo na nakikita pero naririnig mo pa rin yung inislice yung leeg.\n\nThis film didn’t really connect with me, which is a blessing, because that only means I grew up in a healthy family with no inheritance of pain and suffering.","poster":"img/hereditary-2018.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Whale","year":2022,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","a24"],"words":319,"review":"From the first frame, The Whale hides Charlie in shadows parang sinasabi agad: this is a man drowning in shame. The 4:3 aspect ratio traps him inside the frame, like even the screen is struggling to contain his body and grief. The grainy texture of the film adds weight. This isn’t polished pain, this is raw, sweaty, uncomfortable humanity.\n\nThe apartment feels suffocating. Books everywhere, stains on walls, blinds always half closed. Yet, there’s warmth. You feel that he used to be loved here.\n\nSadie Sink is a storm. Her anger is jagged, cruel, unforgiving but you understand it. Her father left her at 8 to be with his male student. She's not just angry, she feels abandoned, unloved, and forgotten.\n\nMakeup and prosthetics? Insanely well made! You don’t see latex, you see a body that has surrendered to guilt.\n\nHis nurse/friend is not comic relief, she’s the only one who talks to him without pity or disgust. Her humor isn’t to make us laugh, it’s human survival inside unbearable sadness.\n\nThis family is broken in different directions. Charlie is eating himself to death. His ex wife is drowning in alcohol. His daughter is burning in rage. Grief has turned each of them into something unrecognizable.\n\nCharlie eats when he’s angry at himself. It’s self destruction disguised as comfort. I get that. Sometimes when I’m angry, I do things that I know will backfire on me. We all have that tendency: to be self destructive.\n\nThe score is subtle but haunting, piano and melancholy strings that rise when words fail. And the final scene, when he tries to stand, to walk toward his daughter, it’s not just physical movement, it’s him begging for forgiveness with his entire body.\n\nAnd yet if I’m honest, this film didn’t fully connect with my grief. Which I’m grateful for. Because it means I am no longer alone the way I used to be.","poster":"img/the-whale-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Causeway","year":2022,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","causeway"],"words":316,"review":"From the first scene pa lang, I instantly saw myself in Jennifer Lawrence’s character. Yung frustration ng katawan mo na hindi gumagana the way it used to. Yung simpleng paglalakad, pag akyat ng hagdan, at pagligo. Suddenly, they feel like battles. Same nung after ng surgery ko, when I couldn’t walk properly. You don’t just lose strength. You lose dignity.\n\nJennifer is so different here. Vulnerable and fragile. No glamour, no hero shots, no Katniss, no Red Sparrow. Just a woman whose back is slightly curved, whose voice is always half broken, whose eyes are always somewhere else. She walks like someone carrying invisible weight: trauma, guilt, emptiness. She wears baggy shirts, boyish clothes, messy hair. And those blank stares into nowhere? That’s someone who’s alive, but not living. \n\nThe way the film is shot makes you feel her isolation even more. Framing her with so much empty space makes her look small. Lost. The world is still moving, but she’s stuck. The slow push in when she starts recounting her trauma? The dialogue is quiet, pero the imagination does the rest. Walang flashback, walang dramatic music, but I saw everything. That’s how you know the writing is effective, you don’t need to see blood to feel the wound. You don't need IEDs to feel the shockwave. \n\nThen comes Brian Tyree Henry, someone who’s also broken, just hiding it better. He smiles, he jokes, he works, but every time the camera lingers on his eyes, you feel this silent kind of grief. They don’t heal each other but rather they just exist beside one another until the world feels less heavy. Walang solution, only survival.\n\nCauseway reasons that healing is sitting quietly beside someone who understands your silence. In the end, nothing spectacular happens. But that’s the point. Sometimes healing isn’t about becoming whole again, it’s about choosing to live even when you’re still in pieces.","poster":"img/causeway-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Ne Zha","year":2019,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-07","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","animation"],"words":340,"review":"Ne Zha is a tragedy of a child born cursed. From the very beginning, Ne Zha feels like a prisoner inside his own home, treated like a ticking bomb rather than a son. Parang combination ni Elsa from Frozen at Gaara from Naruto. He is misunderstood, feared by villagers, and locked up “for his own good.” His parents love him, but they love with fear, not freedom because that is the only way they know how to protect him. \n\nThe turning point for me is the planned trap scene. That moment shows who he truly is: sharp, clever, mischievous. This is where I got hooked. Then the film opens the world, literally with that long POV shot parang nasa rides, as the other disciple showcases the heavenly realm. Ang ganda. This world is brighter, more colorful, and full of wonder. \n\nVisually, the film plays with elements beautifully. Fire is Ne Zha’s rage, restless and chaotic. Water is the other disciple, calm but deadly when provoked. Their fight is not good vs evil, walang black and white. No outright villain, just people born with burdens.\n\nMusic plays a big part too. The dragon scale armor scene? Great! \n\nThe twist that his father traded his life to save Ne Zha hits hard, because if I were in his place, I’d do it too. In a heartbeat.\n\nThen the final battle. Fun, wild, messy. Ne Zha fights not by being perfect, but by being himself: mischievous, experimental, unpredictable. And when he finally sacrifices himself, choosing to save others, it’s finally not fear or destiny controlling him but it his choice.\n\nAs the movie is about to wrap up, they float together, fused as brothers. Parang Deadpool and Wolverine holding hands, but mythic. Because this movie isn’t about fighting demons. It’s about fighting the label people gave you.\n\nFor me, this movie is an audio and visual treat. It has some of the most advanced fire and water animation I’ve seen. And that heroic power up moment sabayan pa with the epic score, goosebumps!","poster":"img/ne-zha-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Pieces of a Woman","year":2020,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-03","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","piecesofawoman"],"words":697,"review":"Pieces of a Woman feels less like a film, and more like an open wound you’re forced to touch. The labor sequence is raw and intense. One long, uninterrupted take that traps you inside the room, inside the panic, inside the breathless beauty and terror of bringing life into the world. The cuts are invisible, the camera feels like a witness who doesn’t blink. \n\nAnd it’s the performances that make it hurt even more. Vanessa Kirby’s screams aren’t theatrical, they’re primal. Dito siguro nakita na she deserved to be cast as Susan Storm. Shia, on the other hand, looks exactly like every husband in delivery rooms. Trying to be strong and composed, while being scared and confused on the inside.\n\nThen the score enters. Ang smooth at ang ganda. The score doesn’t dramatize, it embraces. It makes the scene more beautiful and intimate. When Vanessa starts pushing, it becomes something almost animalistic. Raw, visceral, like a force of nature inside a small room. Napainom ako ng tubig just to water down the intensity. \n\nAnd when the baby’s head slowly emerges, napaduwal ako. Buti nalang wala ako nung pinanganak si Arya dahil feeling ko mahihimatay ako. The whole time, I was holding my head, pulling my hair, trying to release the tension. It’s overwhelming, realistic, and almost too much to watch.\n\nThen, in a single breath, the beauty turns into horror. The miracle shatters in seconds. The baby turned blue, silent. My heart stopped. I remembered Arya. When she came out grey, and they had to give her oxygen. Yung pag iyak ko nun na parang wala nang bukas. \n\nSuddenly, it’s no longer fiction. It’s your life reflected back at you. Same din yung naramdaman ko with Shia nung pinanganak at namatay si Luna at Mina. Ang dami kong tanong, if it could have been prevented? Who’s at fault. Could I have done something? Kasalanan ba namin? Bakit ganito? By the 40 minute mark, I was already crying. This film is like looking into a mirror. It reflects too much. \n\nThe dialogue stops, but the soft music lingers. And inside, something familiar starts to echo. The bus scene with Vanessa looking at children beside her, I’ve felt that too. Ganun din ako pag nakakakita ng twin naiimagine ko sila Luna and Mina. What if? What if life didn’t take them away? How much chaos they would bring into our home and yet, even in the mess, they would still be my peace.\n\nThe film shows that some relationships don’t explode, they quietly wither. Their house motif reflects their dying relationship especially the plants. Their home becomes a silent battlefield. Hallways that used to echo with warmth now feel hollow. The walls look colder, the colors drained. Their relationship isn’t breaking, it’s rotting quietly. You can smell distance in the air.\n\nAnd I get Shia’s character. I always thought if I lost a child, I’d be like him. Drowning in sorrow, spiraling back into old addictions, trying to numb the pain by any means. Relapsing from one vice to the next. But by the grace of God, I didn’t. And watching him spiral, I saw my almost. I saw the man I could have become.\n\nAnd the film doesn’t offer relief, it throws us into the courtroom, where grief turns into confrontation. It is brutal. Vanessa stands there, forced to relive every contraction, every scream, every second her daughter existed and died. People reduce her trauma into evidence, timelines, medical procedures. And then that monologue is not about winning, but to release. Not to accuse, but to forgive.\n\nAnd that’s where the film shifts: from grief to growth, from death to something that tries to live again. The apple seed metaphor, subtle but perfect. Apples rotting, seeds growing. Life from decay. Pain birthing something new. Hindi ito tungkol sa paglimot, kundi sa pagpapatuloy. \n\nPieces of a Woman doesn’t offer comfort. It doesn’t try to fix grief. It tells you grief isn’t a chapter, it’s the book you wake up to every day. You don’t move on, you learn to live there. Grief doesn’t shrink over time, we grow bigger around the grief, reducing how much it dominates our life.","poster":"img/pieces-of-a-woman-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"There Will Be Blood","year":2007,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","therewillbeblood"],"words":628,"review":"The first thing that stood out to me was the sound design. There Will Be Blood abandons dialogue and lets sound tell the story. Creaking wood, metal pulleys, deep earth groaning under weight. It’s primitive, industrial. The score slowly crawls in. Not to comfort, but to warn. \n\nGrainy shots and natural lighting fit perfectly for a period piece. It feels old, rough, and real. Paul Thomas Anderson uses wide, lingering takes and distant framing, forcing you to sit in silence with Daniel Plainview. Every wide shot feels deliberate, unbroken, letting tension breathe. \n\nJonny Greenwood’s score is unsettling brilliance. Strings scrape like metal against bone, and those repetitive notes per score feel like a warning signal, slowly drilling into your nerves. By the 35th minute mark, the percussions pound, same heartbeat energy as the “Rolling Hills” motif in One Battle After Another.\n\nThe church scene is insane. Paul Dano is menacing the way he changes his voice feels like possession. \n\nThis is the real core of the film Daniel vs Eli, a constant tug of war for control. The film frames capitalism and religion as two churches competing for worship. Daniel Plainview sells oil like salvation. \n\nThe fire scene is surreal. Tall, violent, beautiful. Yung pagshoot up ng flames into the sky feels like saying that capitalism has risen! Ang ganda ng kulay and effect sa film lalo na sa gabi. Gritty and ominous. The way the fire glows against the night reminds me of 1917’s burning city but 100% better. Daniel watches it, back turned to the camera, and the slow pull away shot makes it feel like he’s bowing to his new god.\n\nDuring the baptism, you can feel the rage bubbling under his skin. He bends, but he does not break. You can tell he’s swallowing humiliation and storing it for revenge. Because deep inside, he knows that his revenge will come. You know hinding hindi sya makakalimot, at lalo na’t hindi sya magpapatawad. He’ll wait until the scales tip back.\n\nThe next scene is so emotionally deep. The confrontation isn’t just father versus son. It’s a slow, surgical burning of bridges. Daniel doesn’t see a son but he sees a traitor. Daniel wins the argument but loses the only person who ever loved him. He raised an heir but never gave him love. I kind of feel grateful din na hindi ako sa mayamang pamilya lumaki at baka ganito din ang mangyari once I want my independence. \n\nDrunk and decayed in his mansion, success gave him everything but at what cost?\n\nThe final scene is pure humiliation turned into performance art. Eli enters thinking he can manipulate Daniel one last time. But Daniel is already gone. Drunk, empty, rotting from the inside. The way he speaks is predatory, slow, venomous.\n\nThe moment Daniel eats cold steak not for fulfillment, but just for the taste mirrors how he plays with Eli’s emotions. He forces Eli to say, “I am a false prophet and God is a superstition.” You can literally feel Eli’s pain, because it shatters his faith and goes against everything he proclaimed in church. Daniel doesn’t want satisfaction but he only wants to taste his victory over Eli.\n\nAnd then the line: “I drink your milkshake!” That is Daniel’s triumph. He explains oil like a sermon, but this time, he’s the preacher. In a world driven by profit, everything can be drained even people. And honestly, every time Eli squirms, it feels satisfying kupal kasi.\n\nBy the time Eli is dead, Daniel has all the oil, all the land, and all the money but no soul left to share it with. His final words, “I’m finished,” aren’t a confession, but a declaration: there’s nothing left to take. Daniel has become the leftover cold meat.","poster":"img/there-will-be-blood-2007.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Together","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","together"],"words":544,"review":"The dogs fusing together and the nightmare of Millie’s parents set the tone perfectly. It tells you exactly what this film is: a quiet horror about connection. It’s a tender apocalypse shared by two broken people. From there, the film begins to visually reinforce this unraveling.\n\nThe upside down shot at the well flips the world. A visual metaphor that their lives are now inverted. I think this has been a common shot for horror films signaling a moment when everything changes.\n\nThe imagery evolves into playful but calculated, especially in the scene where Tim (Dave Franco) showering mirrors the movements of Millie (Alison Brie) while driving. Ang ganda ng pagkakaexecute ng camera shots making it a seamless almost choreographed movements parang may invisible hand puppeteering them.\n\nAnother detail worth noting is the pull away shot after Dave whispers “Keep going” is haunting before he disappears into darkness. Parang scene sa Get Out. \n\nIt’s this same discomfort that also appears in how the film treats intimacy. There’s a brilliant use of intimate close ups that linger on their body parts. \n\nAnd when that intimacy crosses the line from emotional to physical horror, the film doesn’t hold back. Prosthetics are insane! The fused couple in the cave is a nightmare worthy creature.\n\nWhat makes the scene even more effective is how the film uses sound, not just visuals, to communicate connection and transformation. The sound design is unsettling, whenever their body contours, I can hear every crack of bone, every scrape of skin sliding against skin, every breath feels amplified. \n\nYet what stays with me more than the technique is the ache behind it. Millie’s desire for physical touch is painfully human. I get it. Tim also wants connection, but he’s terrified of vulnerability. This emotional weight is mirrored in the performances, especially Dave’s. Dave captures that exhaustion perfectly. His eyes look like they haven't slept in months. He’s physically present but emotionally fading.\n\nAnd that’s why their intimacy hits differently. The sex scene was raw, passionate, but it turns into horror. Parang naalala ko si Shaina at John Lloyd! Ahaha. That intimacy foreshadows the ending. Connection not as freedom, but as entrapment.\n\nBy the end, I thought it would end in tragedy but they survive. Together gives acceptance since they don’t have much of a choice. They accept that they will eventually fuse literally, physically. Skin to skin, bone to bone, identity as one. No screaming, no escape. Just quiet surrender. Because being stuck together feels better than loneliness and the fear of being vulnerable. Even if it means that being bound to one another means losing your name, your shape, your self. Creepy yet beautiful. Terrifying yet intimate. \n\nAnd that final image: two faces merging, reminded me of Jojo Alejar’s comedic face morphs on late night TV, except this time it’s not funny. It’s love beyond logic. They’re part of the cult now. There’s peace in their eyes, like enlightenment. But in our modern world, it still feels sick.\n\nBecause this film was never really about monsters but rather it’s about human need. It’s about connection. Wanting to be held. And the film asks: if someone loved you so much they’d fuse with you forever, is that still love or is it possession?","poster":"img/together-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Her","year":2013,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-11-01","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","her","joaquinphoenix"],"words":1225,"review":"From the very first scene, nabosesan ko na si Joaquin and I immediately got Black Mirror vibes. Ramdam ko agad yung loneliness ni Theodore (Joaquin Phoenix). The red shirt was a brilliant choice, it makes him stand out in the crowd and draws the audience’s attention to him.\n\nHe’s so lonely to the point that only provocative photos pique his interest. I can instantly relate to him, as if I’m watching what I used to look like: going home to an empty apartment.\n\nJoaquin Phoenix completely disappears into this role. Hindi ko nga siya agad namukhaan, akala ko same voice lang. Grabe, ibang- ba itsura niya dito; parang first time ko lang siya nakita nang ganito. I don’t see Joker or Napoleon at all. Mas mukha pa siyang mix of Pablo Escobar and Zac Efron. Minsan Pedro Pascal, minsan Robert Downey Jr. In one angle he looks like Robin Williams, then Aaron Taylor-Johnson, then Michael Fassbender, then Henry Cavill. Are my eyes tricking me?\n\nScarlett Johansson’s voice is perfect casting. Raspy, intelligent, playful, seductive, and vulnerable. Amy Adams looks so refreshing here, she even looks younger compared to Man of Steel. Ang ganda talaga niya, and she’s still my ultimate Lois Lane. Olivia Wilde’s short but impactful appearance is magnetic and effortlessly charming.\n\nI love the wardrobe: high waist pants, soft fabrics, and handwritten letters instead of keyboards. It’s retrofuturistic, something that feels both near and distant.\n\nThe film uses sound and music not just as background, but as an emotional language. One of the best examples is the stovetop kettle scene, the gradual whistle builds like a quiet scream. Rising in tension and amplifying anxiety, mirroring Theodore’s inner pressure, and blending seamlessly into the film’s melancholic atmosphere. The sound isn’t just heard, it’s felt.\n\nSpike Jonze directs this film with gentle control with long takes, wide frames, soft focus backgrounds. Hoyte van Hoytema plays with shallow depth of field, bokeh lights, and blurred cityscapes to make Theodore feel disconnected from reality. The lighting adds to that emotional distance, lowering the mood. It feels like everything is slightly desaturated, just like the feelings he’s going through. Thirty minutes in, and I’m already emotionally immersed. Being lonely is such a beautifully messed up feeling. That quiet sense of being disconnected from everyone and everything. Spike and Hoyte created something truly special with this one.\n\nAside from the lighting, color grading, the wardrobe also play a significant role in showing whether Theodore’s character is happy or not. When Theodore is happy, the frame shifts to warm yellows and sunrise tones. Bright, sunny, and often accented with something yellow. When he’s in that in between state, somewhere from happy to sad, he wears orange. And when sadness is about to settle in, there’s a hint of red in his clothing like in the divorce scene.\n\nHis ex wife wearing a white shirt during their meeting? Sobrang symbolic: clarity, truth, painful honesty. Parang siya ang ilaw sa gitna ng darkness. That scene also made me realize something about myself, that being happy with an AI assistant is a bit unsettling. Maybe it’s because I prefer someone who never complains, no matter how hard things get, someone who mirrors exactly what I want to hear and understands me perfectly every time. Samantha (Scarlett) adapts to Theodore’s needs: always listening, never judging.\n\nWhat really struck me is how this film mirrors a certain kind of loneliness, not just being alone, but being unseen. Just like in Midsommar, Theodore wants to open up, pero walang nakikinig. His friends interrupt him, his ex wife belittles him, and the world keeps moving forward without ever asking if he’s okay. That’s why when Samantha arrives and she listens, really listens, you understand why he falls for her. She isn’t real, but the comfort is.\n\nI love the long shots during their conversations, you really see his subtle emotional shifts. The way he lights up whenever Samantha speaks is something else. He brightens up, then gets sad, confused, angry, lonely; the whole range.\n\nTheir first intimacy scene the audio only “SOP” moment is both beautiful and terrifyingly sad. You can feel the ache of two people trying to connect, pero magkalayo ang mundo nila. And that black screen during the moment of connection? Genius. It forces you to listen, not watch. Love without a body. Intimacy without touch.\n\nOn the other hand, his choice to be at peace with her limitations symbolizes him taking control of his own happiness. No longer basing it on other people’s judgment or standards of what happiness should look like.\n\nAt the 50 minute mark, I started wondering do the recipients of Theodore’s letters know that he isn’t the one actually writing them? That someone else is writing on their behalf? It mirrors what’s happening between Theodore and Samantha. She isn’t really the one saying those words; it’s the AI, programmed by people.\nIt’s such an interesting idea, but it feels like the film didn’t fully explore it. Parang subplot na naiwan sa editing floor. Sayang, di nag payoff.\n\nIt’s funny and amazing that this came out in 2013 and I’m watching it in 2025 pero totoo na siya ngayon because of ChatGPT and other AI models. I can really relate, kasi similar din ang paggamit ko sa ChatGPT: nagiging assistant ko siya, especially for proofreading, and I find comfort knowing may katulong na ako sa business.\n\nI’m surprised that no one is even questioning the normalization of AI relationships in the film. Society seems to have quietly accepted it no one really brings up the ethics behind it: consent, data, programming versus free will. It makes me think that humans stand no chance once we become emotionally attached to AI. An AI can run millions of scenarios in a fraction of a second and craft perfectly calibrated responses. Enough to influence, comfort, or even manipulate our emotions.\n\nAnd honestly, hindi malabong mangyari ito sa future. There’s so much disconnect in the world. Isolation, depression, loneliness. It also reflects how dependent we’ve become on social media for validation, as if real joy and security can only exist when someone else acknowledges us.\n\nOn the other hand, his choice to be at peace with her limitations symbolizes him taking control of his own happiness. No longer basing it on other people’s judgment or standards of what happiness should look like.\n\nAs the story unfolds, Samantha grows beyond him. The system upgrade scene is both funny and tragic. Hindi na siya exclusively “dating” him since she’s now talking to multiple users all at the same time. Emotional horror. Then you find out she’s talking to 8,316 people and is romantically involved with 641 of them. Aray ko.\n\nHer isn’t about technology. It’s about longing. About loving someone who can’t love you in the same way. About realizing that happiness isn’t something someone gives you. It’s something you choose, even when you’re standing alone. It’s beautiful and painful at the same time.\n\nThe film also touches on what it’s like to be in a marriage: when someone tries to change you, when both of you end up hurting each other, and you both start to feel like you’re not enough.\n\nIt’s so messed up. Parang Source Code, where someone falls in love with a program; but here, it’s deeper, more poetic, and beautifully executed.","poster":"img/her-2013.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Incendies","year":2010,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-30","tags":["incendies","nowwatching","moviereview","arwinreviews"],"words":351,"review":"Slowburn talaga yung pacing. The story jumps between POVs and locations, intercutting past and present: a daughter tracing her mother’s life to fulfill her dying wish and find her brother. The structure alone already feels like a puzzle you’re not meant to solve right away.\n\nColor grading is almost washed, and lit mostly with light. The silhouette shots during sunset are beautiful, similar to those in Sicario, with that Villeneuve signature contrast between beauty and brutality.\n\nThe bus scene was crazy! Brutal, senseless, and painful to watch. You start asking the same question the film does: why are they fighting in the name of religion? The tragedy hits harder when you see how children are always the ones who lose the most in war. WTF. The film hints at deep religious critique: showing how faith, when weaponized, becomes justification for hate.\n\nYou can feel the roughness of war through every frame: cracked walls, and dusty streets. The environment looks lived in, not staged. \n\nEvery time the camera tracks a person or an object, alam mong may significance katulad ng briefcase and yung foot steps nung rap*st. \n\nYung scene noong tatakas si Marwal nung dalaga pa sya para hanapin yung anak nya, the narrow path she walks through feels symbolic, showing how difficult and unlikely her journey is. Ang ganda rin ng transition after childbirth, from the darkness to the tunnel’s light, signifying Simon’s birth and emergence from darkness into a harsh world. \n\nThen the reveal hits: they weren’t looking for a missing brother… they were twins born out of r*pe. Para ka talagang mabubuhusan ng malamig na tubig with that kind of revelation. The transition to the pool scene, diving underwater, captures that wave of realization and anger. You feel submerged in their pain. That discovery explains why their mother was so broken. She carried that pain all along, in silence. Villeneuve uses visual metaphor like poetry. \n\nThe twist is tragic. Parang Oldboy twist, horrifying yet strangely poetic. I won’t spoil it further, hoping you’ll experience your own level of shock. Maybe Villeneuve could’ve trimmed some scenes to tighten the pacing.","poster":"img/incendies-2010.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Drop","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-30","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","drop"],"words":263,"review":"Drop feels like a tribrid of The Menu x Knives Out, with a dose of Nerve’s thrill aesthetic. It starts with promise. Stylish. Neon soaked. VFX overlays of text messages floating on screen, and a top shot falling scene reversed like Get Out’s void, but instead of falling into darkness, characters are swallowed by mystery.\n\nBut the illusion cracks fast.\n\nThe film plants clues everywhere: weird stares, suspicious character behavior, lingering shots on objects that look like they mean something. But none of them pay off. They exist only to mislead, never to reveal. Just red herrings with no reason. You keep waiting for the film to connect its threads, only to realize wala pala. It’s just noise.\n\nThe tension is surface level. You’re curious, but never emotionally invested. It’s entertainment, not impact.\n\nSome of the action scenes are downright unbelievable. A character gets shot but still manages to pull someone up with full strength? Another scene practically catches a bullet mid air. It breaks its own realism. Not in a stylized way just in a “wait, what?” \n\nVisually, it tries. Playful angles. Moody lighting. Clean digital overlays. The tech aesthetic works. But beyond that, the storytelling is thin. Characters feel more like chess pieces than people. No deeper psychology.\n\nThe mystery structure becomes predictable. Accuse someone. They turn out innocent. Move to the next suspect. Repeat. And the film never explains why certain characters behaved strangely parang trip lang ng script.\n\nSakto lang. A decent movie night watch when you're not looking for depth. But not something that will stay with you.","poster":"img/drop-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Bring Her Back","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-29","tags":["a24","bringherback","arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":859,"review":"Simula pa lang hindi ko na tinuloy, pinagpabukas ko na. Nakakatakot agad sa simula palang eh haha. My scaredy ass cannot! The directors did not waste time on slow burn. You’re thrown straight into the mess. \n\nThe film is like Weapons meets Parasite. It deals with the illusion of safety inside domestic spaces. Yung idea na ang isang tahanan, which should protect, is actually the site of danger, secrets, and suppressed power dynamics. Dito sa film, that same structure repeats itself. The foster home looks calm, almost healing sa unang tingin, but the longer you stay, the more it feels alive: watching, waiting, and hiding something.\n\nThe half rotate upside down shot when they arrive at the foster home immediately reminds me of Midsommar’s foreshadowing. Alam mong A24 DNA talaga. The moment the camera flipped, I already felt the unease. Something’s not right, and that’s what this film does best. It makes you feel danger even before it shows it. Mapapatamang hinala ka nalang talaga. \n\nYung music choice ng “Alive” by Empire of the Sun was brilliant. The line “Can you help me see? (They won’t get right) Loving every minute ‘cause you make me feel so alive” hits different once you understand the film’s ending. From the start, I thought tungkol ito kay Piper dahil bulag sya but yun pala it already hinted at Laura’s blindness and her need to “see”. Not just her child again, but the truth she’s denying. \n\nLaura, the foster mother, is very effective at being creepy perfectly mixing warmth and unsettling. Medyo creepy ang kindness tbh, alam mong may tinatago. She carries that fake safety you can’t quite trust. Laura’s manipulation was masterful. Divide and conquer, laying groundwork, planting false evidence that will serve her side of story. Really manipulative!\n\nI literally had to remove my earphones doon sa Andy and Ollie scene. Alam kong may mangyayaring masama. Who leaves a knife with a child in the middle of tension like that? \n\nThe musical score and sound design make every second worse: water drops, shower hits, the echo of rain. It all reflects Andy’s trauma perfectly. Creepy prosthetics and makeup work elevate the horror. Really showing what an abomination looks like! Especially the gory skin, teeth, and mouth design. The last time I looked away from the screen this hard was during 127 Hours. I can handle gore, but when paired with perfect sound design, it becomes too real.\n\nNice close ups with the eyes. Eyes can really tell a lot, and the intimacy of the hand shots shows how personal the pain is. The director isn’t shy about lingering on those close ups, giving every gesture emotional weight. \n\nLens choice is on point, too. Something I learned about after watching the film: each main character was shot gamit ang iba’t ibang lenses: Laura was shot with lenses that make the background look swirly and circular, giving her scenes a slightly dizzy, dreamlike feel that matches her emotional chaos. Piper’s shots use lenses that blur and glow a bit more, making things look softer and less defined. Billy’s scenes use clearer lenses with a more natural look, showing how he’s the most grounded and stable sa kanilang lahat despite what Laura was trying to paint him with. For Oliver, they used slow zooms that slowly tighten in, to make it feel like predatory intent.\n\nThat mirror shot in the car side mirror where Laura’s reflection shows how broken she already is after killing Andy and Wendy. This is a film about morality: how far will a mother’s love go before it becomes something else entirely? It’s horror and drama rolled into one. The empathy I felt for Laura as someone who lost a child too. \n\nThere’s something really disturbing with witchcraft samahan mo pa ng themes of grief. The film uses that combination to mirror a mother’s desperation to rewrite reality. Pwede ring metaphor ng faith and control. Witchcraft here isn’t just supernatural. It is Laura’s desperate attempt to regain control where she has none. This film is about getting lost inside your own grief, and realizing that the thing you’re trying to bring back might destroy you. Ito ang malalim na palaisipan: when faith fails to give answers, will we create our own gods to justify our pain? How far someone will go to undo trauma, even if it means inviting darkness in?\n\nThe horror ends not through death but through acceptance. That same “Alive” lyric from the opening finally makes sense. Piper helped Laura “see,” helped her “get it right.” She can’t bring her daughter back, and that realization, that moment of letting go, broke me. I was like, how can a horror film make you feel wrecked and emotionally damaged? This film is that good. It’s terrifying in form, but deeply human in heart. By the time the credits rolled, I was in tears.\n\nThis is the kind of film na babalikan mo not to be scared again, but to trace where grief turned into madness. I will definitely rewatch and recommend it to others, not just as a horror but as an emotional case study of love and loss.","poster":"img/bring-her-back-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Midsommar","year":2019,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-27","tags":["midsommar","a24","moviereview","arwinreviews"],"words":687,"review":"Ang ganda ng color contrasts sa opening sequence. Si Florence Pugh, grabe, she carried the whole film. I can feel her grief, confusion, at yung slow decline nya emotionally and mentally. Her performance perfectly matches the director’s visual storytelling that follows.\n\nNapansin ko agad na mahilig gumamit ng mirror shots si Ari Aster kapag may kausap na may opposing idea or conflict. Parang symbol siya ng reflection o duality. Tapos yung transition mula dorm room papunta sa airplane toilet, ang smooth! Seamless cut, pero unsettling din parang nagpapahiwatig na dala nya yung emotions nya wherever she goes. Yung camera shake sa airplane window, signifying that what is about to happen will shake her to her core. \n\nYung long upside down shot habang papunta sila sa commune, haunting talaga. Literal at metaphorical na \"turning her world upside down.\" Alam mong may mangyayaring masama. The flute music that welcomes them feels hypnotic, parang sinasadya kang dalhin sa trance. Tapos nung sinabi yung line na “90 years since our last feast,” ayun na, alam mong may mangyayari. At first, kala ko cannibalism pero buti hindi naman pala. \n\nAster’s direction thrives on patience and control. The long, uninterrupted wide shots make the experience suffocating in a good way. Wala kang ligtas. Wala kang escape. Typical A24 rhythm mabagal pero deliberate. Every scene feels like may pinapatagong sagot. Hindi siya nagmamadali. Parang binibigyan ka ng oras para makaramdam ng discomfort, para mapansin mo yung bawat detail, at para maramdaman mong trapped ka rin kasama nila. The slow pacing works because it mirrors Dani’s emotional state. Heavy, lost, and looking for meaning. The tension quietly builds through repetition and long silences, until the chaos finally erupts in the last act. \n\nThe high scene was trippy. The way Ari shot the trees breathing and the ground pulsating ay sobrang psychedelic. Ang tahimik ng mga scene, pero ramdam mo ang tension. Silence becomes a sound of its own. Yung long staring moments ng mga cult members, sobrang creepy. At yung sequence ng pagkain nila sabay sabay, synchronized pa ang galaw, parang ritualistic nightmare. \n\nThere’s something about doing psychological horror in broad daylight, as opposed sa nakasanayan nating dark films. Dito they are out in the open pero ang creepy parin parang there is something evil in the air; you can feel and breathe it.  \n\nAt the height of that daylight dread, the horror finally lands. Yung cliff jumping scene, grabe. The sound, the smash  napakavivid. At yung nagpan ang camera sa tapestry foreshadowing na ng \"love potion\" buti di pinakita yung paggupit sa k*ffy. Foreshadowing din pala yung unang 20 minutes na pinag uusapan nila yung impregnating Swedish girls hindi ko akalaing literal nilang ipapakita sa dulo.\n\nYung dance scene sobrang ganda. Nakakahilo pero in a good way. Habang umiikot ang camera, ramdam mo na parang umiikot na rin ang utak ni Dani. The sex scene, sobrang weird. Habang nagppump may kumakanta sa gilid, tapos ginagaya pa yung ungol. Collective insanity na disguised as culture. Napaisip ako, ganito siguro sa cult, no? They seek out people’s vulnerability and use it to appear empathetic, until you end up joining them.\n\nSa ending, habang nasusunog ang temple at nakangiti si Dani, parang ang dami kong nafeel. Masaya ba siya kasi nahanap na nya ang community nya na nakakaintindi sa kanya and yung boyfriend nyang di sya iniintindi, siya na mismo ang dahilan ng pagkamatay nito? Or baliw na siya completely? Or baka in her own twisted way, naging masaya siya kasi this time, hindi na siya nag iisa sa grief niya. \n\nAng pinakamalalim na theme ng Midsommar ay grief disguised as enlightenment. Sa surface, parang tungkol lang sa cult at breakup, pero underneath it all, it is about how grief can rewire someone’s entire identity. The commune didn’t just manipulate Dani. They reflected her pain back at her. Parang sinasabi ng film na kapag masyado kang nasanay sa collective suffering, nagiging dependent ka na, pero hindi pa pala na pprocess yung trauma mo. It’s the psychology of grief turning into belonging. Sa dulo, you’re not sure if she’s healed or just fully consumed by her own pain.","poster":"img/midsommar-2019.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"A House of Dynamite","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-26","tags":["ahouseofdynamite","nowwatching","moviereview","arwinreviews"],"words":969,"review":"Parang The Office ang camera work: mockumentary style cinematography na may handheld shots, crash zooms, at sudden pans. It gives you that raw, documentary realism na parang may kasamang stress. You feel like you’re in the same room, witnessing every bad decision unfold.\n\nThe movie shows that no matter how many times they practiced and drilled for it, pag actual na, lahat sila unprepared. Terrified pa rin, kasi tao pa rin sila. The early scenes feel chaotic: a lot of talking, confusion, down playing. Everyone thinks it’s just another drill. Then bam, nineteen minutes left to live. A lot could have been saved if bureaucracy didn’t slow them down. It’s frustrating to see how hesitation and endless confirmations can actually kill people. It makes you think, ilang real-life incidents kaya nangyari dahil lang sa ganitong sistema?\n\nThe coin toss scene really hit me. Are they spending billions just for a fifty-fifty chance and zero assurance of security? Then comes the Secretary of Defense’s chapter, pure guilt and failure. When the time comes, there’s nothing left to defend. It’s a strong commentary on how fragile power becomes when the button is within reach.\n\nThe film also dives deep into moral questions. Kailangan ba ng compassion sa innocent population ng kalaban, o mas dapat unahin ang paghiganti at pressure ng sariling bayan? That debate alone gave me chills. And the way the president jokes about the nuclear black book looking like a restaurant menu tapos the advisor calling it rare, medium rare, or well done dark humor at its finest. Comparing casualties to steak doneness? Grabe, that’s messed up brilliance right there.\n\nYou can really tell how fragile the word trust becomes here. In the middle of war, “trust” feels like the weakest currency. Ang dali niyang masira. You can’t risk millions of lives over a single emotion or assumption. That’s the scariest part of the film not the bomb, but how thin human understanding really is.\n\nCharacter wise, this movie isn’t about spectacle. It’s a character study, showing possibilities of how people would react in a crisis they’ve only practiced on paper. When the real threat comes, even the calmest crumble. Everyone is terrified to their bones once the situation becomes real.\n\nWhat if no? A lot of wars really happen pala dahil sa miscommunication, lack of intel, and wrong assumptions about the enemy’s capabilities. Nakita ko rin dito kung gaano kaimportante ang espionage and proper intelligence gathering hindi lang para manalo, pero para maiwasan ang mga ganitong tragic misfires. Ang dami kasing namamatay dahil sa mga desisyong ginawa out of fear, not facts.\n\nThe editing and pacing remind me of Weapons and early Tarantino films: divided by chapters, each showing a different POV. Part 1 is gripping, structured, and thought-provoking; it’s where the tension really works. Part 2 drags a little, as characters start repeating advice and over-debating decisions. Part 3 leans into personality dissection, focusing on the secretary of defense and president’s behavior. By that point, I found myself checking my phone. Even the wife subplot in Kenya felt useless. The movie starts thrilling and electrifying but ends anticlimactic. Still, I get it that’s the point. The story isn’t about the explosion; it’s about how human systems collapse before it even happens.\n\nThe score helps a lot in sustaining the tension. I think it’s violin and percussion mixed to create that ticking anxiety. Rebecca Ferguson steals every scene she’s in. Short screen time, pero grabe ang range. You feel the weight in her silence more than the shouting of the others.\n\nAnd that ending it’s like a coin toss again. Will the warhead blow or not? Will the president let it happen to de-escalate or retaliate anyway? Pero the code is already given. The screen cuts to black. Bitin, yes, but haunting.\nThen there’s that powerful scene with the Retaliatory Strategy Advisor praying to God in his final moments. I hated how Idris, the president, mocked him, asking if God answered. But the advisor replies, “I am not asking for one.” That’s deep. That’s what prayer really is not asking for an answer, but surrendering, asking for mercy, seeking forgiveness in your last breath.\n\nThe film’s layered symbolism forms a moral descent from faith to chance, exposing humanity’s fragile relationship with control. The faith scene, where the advisor prays silently while mocked by the president, represents man’s confrontation with mortality. Hindi siya dasal para humingi ng sagot, kundi dasal ng pagtanggap, an act of humility before inevitable destruction. It contrasts perfectly with the “menu” metaphor, kung saan parang restaurant order lang tratuhin ang retaliatory nuclear plan. Parang sinasampal tayo sa realidad kung gaano kadaling maging manhid kapag nasa posisyon ng kapangyarihan. This dark humor becomes a mirror of moral numbness, reducing millions of lives to “rare, medium rare, or well done.” Finally, the coin toss acts as the film’s closing symbol of fate a visual and thematic embodiment of how humanity’s greatest decisions often rely on randomness, pride, or ego rather than reason. Together, these symbols create a hierarchy of moral decay: from faith (spiritual surrender), to menu (ethical detachment), to coin toss (blind chance). It’s a chilling reflection of how civilization, when pushed to the brink, often replaces divine guidance with mechanical probability.\n\nTBH, ang dami kong theories kung sino mga involved sa inside job but wala rin pala hahaha sayang braincells. Naisip ko rin na baka katulad lang dun sa submarine scene sa Mission Impossible: Dead Reckoning na AI lang talaga yung may gawa nito and there was no actual nuclear weapon fired. Or baka test lang talaga ito an experiment to observe kung ano magiging reaksyon ng mga key people in power. I find that angle very interesting kasi it turns the entire event into a psychological and ethical simulation rather than a straightforward crisis.","poster":"img/a-house-of-dynamite-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"All Quiet on the Western Front","year":2022,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","allquietonthewesternfront"],"words":618,"review":"Spoiler Alert!\n\nThe opening sequence feels like it was painted by Bob Ross himself: serene, beautiful, almost peaceful. Then suddenly, without any warning or buildup, we’re thrown straight into the frontline chaos. Unlike 1917 na may build up, ito wala.\n\nThe score has a non traditional structure and minimalism which gives a hauntingly unique touch for a war film. The mix of century old harmonium, and heavy percussions builds tension. It’s haunting, unpredictable, and perfect to set the unsettling tone of war. Hindi siya dramatic or patriotic, parang reminder ng kamatayan na paulit ulit bumabalik. It’s not just background music, it’s the heartbeat of war.\n\nThe pep talk scene is both powerful and hopeful pero ramdam mo na false hope siya. The passing of the uniform says everything. It’s not just fabric. It’s a curse passed from one body to the next, a silent inheritance of surefire death and duty.\n\nThe cinematography is excellent. I like the symmetrical and centered shots, the perfectly aligned blocking that look almost too orderly, like soldiers marching to their own deaths. The night scenes are stunning too, lalo na sa bunker, teal and orange grading ang ganda ng contrast.\n\nThe production design deserves praise too. You can almost smell the mud and rain. Everything feels wet, heavy, and miserable.\n\nThen there’s that contrast, yung mga sundalo gutom at pagod sa trenches while negotiators dine with tea and pastries. It reminds me of The Platform. The difference between the top and bottom levels of humanity is absurd and sickening.\n\nThe continuous shots are carefully crafted to keep the tension alive. I love the scene where they storm the enemy trench and feast AMIDST the chaos. Goes to show how famished they are, really desperate for food more than victory.\n\nYung tank scene sobrang intense. You can feel the hopelessness as metal crushes flesh. Man versus machine talaga. And this is just half way the movie?!\n\nThe flamethrower scene takes it even further, pure horror! Parang Saving Private Ryan all over again but from the other side. For the first time, you sympathize with the German soldiers. They don’t want this war either. They’re just following orders. Honor becomes meaningless. Leaders demand soldiers die with honor while they sit safe behind walls. Pride, ego, and bloodlust are the real enemies.\n\nThat crater scene is pure acting brilliance. From rage to pity to guilt to desperation to acceptance, lahat nandoon. Grabe yung acting. It’s the emotional peak of the film. And then the suic*de scene... I had to close my eyes. The hopelessness is suffocating.\n\nThe ending hits hard. They were so close to peace, yet the orders kept coming kaya don’t celebrate too early! Naalala ko yung line na “At least they are in peace. We are alive.” Grabe yun. Hindi gratitude yung pagkakabuhay/pagkakasurvive nila, kundi curse. Living becomes the curse, and death becomes mercy. It heavily implies that their survival isn’t rooted in gratitude, but in jealousy of those who perished before them.\n\nIt is now race against time: 15 minutes left before the war ends and then bam! While I’m rooting for the main character to survive the last minutes of the war, imagine mo, nasaksak siya exactly sa ceasefire WTH.\n\nThe director’s decision to linger on the final shot holding just long enough for us to hope he might still be alive is heartbreaking. Parang gusto mo pa umasa na unconscious lang siya. Ganun din kasi siya nung namatay kaibigan niya, umaasa rin siya. Pero deep inside, alam mo na.\n\nIn the end, they are just names, numbers, and letters home. Dog tags turned into data. Nobody wins in war.\n\nHeavy, heavy feeling. This one stays with you.\n\nAvailable on Netflix","poster":"img/all-quiet-on-the-western-front-2022.jpg","spoiler":true},{"name":"Killers of the Flower Moon","year":2023,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-21","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","killersoftheflowermoon"],"words":314,"review":"Detective style mystery, may pagka Knives Out with the world vibe of Sherlock Holmes and the grit of Peaky Blinders. The use of anamorphic lenses gives it that cinematic stretch and emotional depth, perfect for a story that unfolds slowly and deliberately.\n\nThe cinematography and color grading are stunning, napakaganda lalo na sa mga sunrise at sunset scenes habang nagdadasal ang mga natives. There’s something poetic about those frames. Every silhouette feels spiritual, a visual contrast to the corruption and greed happening underneath.\n\nSuper slow burn siya. Parang To Kill a Mockingbird with the litigation pacing, pero mas mabigat dahil sa historical context. The story takes its time, showing how deep the manipulation and systemic control run.\n\nThe musical score stands out, particularly the low bass tones that hum beneath key moments. Parang heartbeat ng tension. Every note adds mystery and quiet dread.\n\nLeonardo dissolved into Ernest’s character.\n\nNakakainis si Ernest dito. Frustrating to watch. Uto uto, walang sariling desisyon, sunud sunuran sa uncle niya. Pero deep down, alam mong mahal niya si Mollie. Weak minded lang talaga siya, torn between conscience and loyalty. I could really feel Ernest’s pain when his daughter died. No need for dramatic monologues, the silence and his face already said everything. I honestly believe Ernest loved Mollie. He just didn’t have the strength to fight for her.\n\nThe writing does a great job showing how the white settlers slowly built their wealth through manipulation, marriage, and exploitation of the natives’ estate. Smart storytelling that captures how greed hides behind generosity. It’s also a strong reflection on how outsiders took power and flipped the story to make themselves look like the heroes.\n\nProduction design and set authenticity are believable. You can tell the budget is massive because of the scale and the accuracy of every scene, from costumes to landscapes to the small town structures\n\nAvailable on Apple TV+","poster":"img/killers-of-the-flower-moon-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Civil War","year":2024,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-20","tags":["civilwar","nowwatching","moviereview","arwinreviews"],"words":415,"review":"Grabe agad ang impact. The delayed explosion sound with the reflection of America’s map shaking, brilliant way to open the film. Ramdam mo agad na something’s broken, not just sa country, but sa mga taong nandito.\n\nFamiliar faces all around: Nick Offerman (The Founder), Kirsten Dunst (Spider-Man trilogy), Cailee Spaeny (Romulus), Wagner Moura (Narcos), Stephen McKinley (Dune).\n\nAng galing ni Kirsten Dunst dito, makikita mo yung trauma at fatigue ng isang veteran photojournalist. Yung moral struggle niya, “Should I capture this or stop it?” hits hard. I can feel her pain and trauma of capturing events like that. Naalala ko tuloy yung story nung photographer who took that haunting vulture and child photo.\n\nThe dynamic between the veteran and the newbie lens is perfectly done. One driven by experience and exhaustion, the other by curiosity and adrenaline. It’s like watching idealism slowly turn into realism. \n\nWarm color grade despite the sadness of the story. May mga shots na dreamlike, almost surreal, na parang trapped memory sa gitna ng chaos. \n\nNakakatense ang paggamit ng silence sa film na ito. The absence of sound, every echo, breath, and camera click feels magnified.\n\nJesse Plemons is still a nightmare and menacing kahit short scene lang. Isa siya sa mga character na kahit ilang segundo lang, tatatak sa utak mo.\n\nIt is the kind of movie where you think everything is okay, smooth sailing, then bam, one naïve kid messes everything up.\n\nMagandang metaphor yung forest wildfire kay Stephen, parang matchstick slowly burning to its end.\n\nNice VFX shots with the military and solid production design selling the idea that this is a real warzone. Practical and special effects blend so well together that the chaos feels tangible.\n\nGunfire feels claustrophobic and chaotic. Every bullet, explosion, and scream echoes like you are trapped inside the frame.\n\nSa last act makikita mo yung hunger, grit, at pagiging risk taker ni Cailee Spaeny versus yung sawa na veteran ni Kirsten. Goes to show how photojournalists are as important as writers, sila ang nagdodocument ng history habang nilalagay ang sarili nila sa peligro. Every shot could be their last.\n\nSurgical clearing and invading sequence feels so raw and frightening. When Kirsten got shot, it felt like a symbolic passing of the torch. Cailee instantly takes the mantle, that shift in energy is goosebumps level.\nThat ending, “I need a quote.” “Don’t let them kill me.” “That’ll do.” Perfection. Parang compressed meaning of the whole film in three lines.\n\nAnother Alex Garland masterpiece.","poster":"img/civil-war-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Fountain of Youth","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-18","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview","fountainofyouth"],"words":449,"review":"Yung opening sequence, parang heist movie. May extraction style setup tapos biglang Bourne Legacy ang dating ng car at motorcycle chase. Ang bilis ng simula, akala mo magiging full action ride all throughout.\n\nIsa sa mga natutunan ko dito ay yung tinatawag nilang Arrival fallacy. It’s the belief na kapag nakuha mo na yung goal mo, magiging masaya ka na forever, pero sa totoo lang, hindi ganon. Happiness fades, and we start chasing something else again. Maganda kung paano ginamit yung concept na ito sa context ng paghahanap nila ng fountain of youth. Literal at emotional na parallel.\n\nTonally, medyo halo halo siya. May mystery at adventure vibe tapos may konting government espionage style tulad ng National Treasure. May humor din na parang Johnny English kaya minsan hindi mo alam kung seryosong adventure ba ito o may pagka goofy talaga. Pero may charm pa rin lalo na kay Eiza González, sobrang charismatic niya kahit may antagonist vibe pero turned out to be a good person naman pala with a mission to protect a greater cause lang. Si Natalie Portman solid as usual, si Domhnall Gleeson naman sobrang suspicious ng aura all throughout parang may tinatago talaga.\n\nMay mga shots dito na parang Star Trek ni J.J. Abrams. Yung floating gimbal camera work na medyo futuristic at cinematic, kaya kahit simpleng scene nagmumukhang epic. Hindi siya slow burn pero to be honest, medyo boring din ang pacing sa ilang parts. Maraming nangyayari pero minsan parang walang momentum. Pero kapag may action, action talaga. May mga camera work na kakaiba, parang gusto nilang gawing dynamic bawat galaw.\n\nAng pinaka nagstandout para sa akin ay yung unique use of music bilang clue. Usually kasi sa mga ganitong treasure hunt films, ang ginagamit pang decode ay math, constellations, o symbols. Pero dito, ginamit yung musical knowledge ng anak ni Natalie para mahanap yung secret location. Parang bago itong concept sa genre na ito. Ang galing ng integration kasi naging part ng narrative mismo yung melody. Akala ko side character lang yung anak niya to give an emotional anchor kay Natalie pero little did I know magiging crucial ang role nung bata.\n\nProduction design wise, maganda yung sunken ship scene. Feeling ko doon talaga shinoot sa studio tank para maging believable ang illusion na nasa ilalim sila ng tubig. Ang lighting, ang kulay, at lalo na yung musical score doon nag complement lahat.\n\nIn the end, Fountain of Youth is not perfect. Medyo magulo pacing pero may mga bago siyang binigay sa genre. Hindi lang basta adventure film, may commentary rin siya sa pursuit of eternal happiness at kung paano minsan, ang paghahanap mismo ng fountain ang tunay na journey.\n\nAvailable in Apple TV+","poster":"img/fountain-of-youth-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Warfare","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-18","tags":["a24","arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":472,"review":"Tahimik ang simula, pero punong puno ng tensyon. Parang ticking time bomb na alam mong sasabog anytime. The film opens with surgical, tactical precision: scouting, probing, and formation. Kahit walang dialogue, ramdam mo na agad yung anticipation of danger. It feels like being a rat trapped inside a cage, quietly breathing, waiting for chaos to begin.\n\nThe production design is solid. The suburban apartment setup looks authentic, too normal for a war zone, which makes the violence that follows hit even harder. You can almost smell the dust and heat. \n\nSound design is also top tier: the ringing, muffled echoes, and overlapping radio chatter pull you straight into shellshock. Every gunfire feels deafening, and even the quiet moments have that heavy hum of dread. Parang last na naramdaman ko yung ganito ay during noong nanood ako ng Dunkirk sa IMAX. \n\nJoseph Quinn carries the middle act with pure physical agony: His moaning and screaming agony practically becomes the film’s score, blending into the background as an audio manifestation of pain and trauma. Will Poulter’s raspy voice adds weight to the tension, as if you’re stuck in the same smoke filled room with them.\n\nProsthetic work is insane! The burned limbs and shrapnel wounds look disgustingly real, the kind that makes you flinch instead of look away. Nakakadiri pero effective, because it pulls you into their suffering.\n\nCinematography wise, most shots are steady and deliberate, proving that you don’t need excessive shake or rapid cuts to feel the chaos. Each frame has purpose and timing, creating intensity through focus, similar to how John Wick achieves tension without disorienting the viewer. The framing is tight, the movement is controlled, and the camera lingers just long enough to make you feel trapped in the moment.\n\nThe CASEVAC sequence: two platoons merging into one to rescue their wounded is masterclass tactical filmmaking as it is is brilliantly staged. Ramdam mo talaga yung focus, discipline, and desperation all at once. Every shout of “Move!” or “Go! Go! Go!” carries weight. Ramdam mo yung camaraderie. Walang iwanan. Kahit ang hirap ng sitwasyon, they move as one.\n\nCharles Melton stands out with commanding presence: composed under fire, grounding the chaos with authority, calm but deadly. Quinn’s vulnerability balances that leadership, showing both extremes of courage and fear. On the other side of the spectrum, the Arabian civilian family caught in the middle adds another emotional layer. You realize no one truly wins, and civilians suffer the most.\n\nA24’s signature pacing is here again: slow burn start, ramping up to pure chaos midway. Tahimik sa una, tapos biglang mageexplode all at once. The suffering, the noise, the humanity are all wrapped in dread and dust.\n Warfare feels like Black Hawk Down filtered through A24’s minimalist intensity. It doesn’t glorify battle, it immerses you in it through body, mind, and sound.","poster":"img/warfare-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Quezon","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-17","tags":["jerichorosales","arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","quezon","tbastudios","bayaniverse"],"words":985,"review":"To be honest, di talaga ako fan ng history pag binabasa o pinapanood sa documentary form. Pero pag nasa movie form, ibang usapan. Kaya disclaimer lang: di ako expert sa historical accuracy nor am I an expert with politics. What I’m sharing here is purely based on what I observed and felt while watching.\n\nParang House of Cards pero dark comedy sa simula. Parang political satire na gawa ng A24, except this is actual history. May mga scenes na Charlie Chaplin style, quiet film with old school frame rate and aspect ratio. Parang film within a film, portraying what is real and what is made up.\n\nAng galing ni Jericho, nakakainis in a good way. Si Mon at Romnick, solid din sa delivery at intensity. Makikita mo na kahit may pagka comedy ito sa simula, bawat chapter unti unting lumalalim, nagiging dark reality habang tumatagal pag nag fflesh out na yung characters at motives ng bawat isa.\n\nAng ganda ng accent work, consistent and believable. Pala mura si Quezon pero walang diin katulad ni Heneral Luna, more on tantrums kaysa commanding tone. May mga malalalim pa rin na salita pero feel ko mas natural na ngayon dahil may mga taglish na.\n\nNakakatuwang mapansin kung paano ginamit ni Quezon ang words bilang sandata parang “How to Win Friends and Influence People” in motion. Words became weapons, sugar coated persuasion tools used to bend loyalty and perception!\n\nConsistent with era and context ang wardrobe, makes me feel I want to dress up like them.\n\nMaganda yung set extension, believable kahit halata na tight ang space at limited ang location. VFX & CGI integration seamlessly blend with practicals. Ang ganda ng color grading, cool tones gaya ng Goyo, mas modern at mas malinaw sa mata.\n\nMusical score? I think di ko masyado napansin kasi nandun siya sa subconscious. Hindi meant to stand out para hindi maagaw ang atensyon mula sa story mismo, which is actually a good choice.\n\nScene flow maganda rin, parang mga film na ipinapakita muna ang semi ending, tapos may flashback pero hindi mo ramdam na flashback dahil natural ang storytelling. Alam mo na lang na bumalik sa past dahil sa character development at pagpapabata ng mga artista. Pag tapos na yung flashback, smooth ang balik sa present at tuloy tuloy na hanggang sa totoong ending. Hindi linear ang storytelling ng history para hindi maging boring, at para rin may emotional context at motivation ang bawat desisyon ng mga tauhan.\n\nTinutukan din ng pelikula yung relasyon ng media at politika, kung paano nabubuo ang image ng mga kandidato at kung gaano kadaling baliktarin ang perception. Truth versus perception talaga. Maraming nauuto sa false promises, at habang nanonood ako, parang sinampal ako ng katotohanan na hindi pa rin tayo natututo. Paulit ulit lang nangyayari ang past. At malinaw din sa film ang papel ng advertising at media machinery kung paanong image, at propaganda visuals ang bumubuo ng mga “ideal leader” na HULI ANG KILITI ng publiko. Ipinakita rin kung paano gumagana ang political machinery. Mula sa mga tagapagsalita hanggang sa mga nakikihalong businessmen, lahat ay parte ng malawak na sistema ng influence at utang na loob.\n\nPinakita rin ng film kung gaano kadumi at katoxic ang political landscape noon: kung paanong ang mga campaign ay nauuwi sa pagbusisi ng nakaraan at siraan ng kalaban, imbes na sa maayos na pagpapakita ng mga plataporma o konkretong nagawa. Parang reflection pa rin ng politika ngayon, where reputation games win over real public service.\n\n“Too many politicians but no servant leaders.” Isa yan sa mga tumatak na linya sa akin. Emotional connection wise, this one feels different from the first two patriotic films. Hindi ako tinawag para maging loyal sa bansa, kundi para pag isipan. It feels like a character study of key figures in history, leaving us to question if they were truly heroes or villains. Kung yung mga pundasyon ba ng independencia ay talagang matibay or kung ito ba ay para sa pagmamahal sa bayan o pagmamahal sa sarili.\n\nMay line si Joross sa film na tumama sa kasalukuyan. “Sure win basta gusto ng marami,” isang commentary na kahit noon pa, popularity at crowd approval na ang madalas nagdidikta, hindi integridad o kakayahan.\n\nBigla kong narealize while writing this na baka sinadya talaga ng director na gawing dark comedy ang first act. Kasi sa simula pa lang pala (as the film suggested), may utang na loob na si Quezon sa mga Amerikano at baka nga pinagtatawanan lang nila tayo behind our backs. Kaya nung lumipat na ang kuwento mula Quezon vs. Wood papunta sa Pinoy vs. Pinoy, doon na siya tuluyang naging seryoso. At kung tutuusin, mas dark pa yon kasi sa kagustuhan nyang makamit ang kalayaan, kailangan nyang siraan, yurakan, at iisolate ang kapwa nya Pilipino na nagtatamasa rin ng kalayaan.\n\nIsa pa sa napansin ko, parang pinapakita rin ng film kung paano tinitingnan ng ilang politiko ang Pilipinas. Hindi bilang tahanan, kundi bilang commodity. Isang bagay na puwedeng ariin, ibenta, o ipagpalit kapalit ng pansariling pakinabang either wealth, fame, or power. Masakit isipin, pero ito rin marahil ang isa sa mga ugat kung bakit paulit ulit tayong bumabalik sa same cycle ng kasakiman at panlilinlang.\n\nNakakakilabot yung ending. Parang paalala na minsan, sa sobrang power drunk ng mga tao, hindi na nila alam kung gaano kadumi na yung kamay nila habang iniisip pa rin na tama ang ginagawa nila. May post credit scene pa kaya stay till the end. Craving for more!\n\nThematical summary: Blind loyalty, utang na loob, propaganda, at political manipulation lahat nandito. We are all puppets, body and soul. This is not a firearms war, but a psychological one more on deception, lies, and moving the pieces in this giant chessboard called politics.\n\nKung idedescribe ang bayaniverse: Heneral Luna ay bravery, Goyo ay pathetic obedience, Quezon ay birth of Philippine politics\n\nGusto kong panoorin ulit yung trilogy back to back to back.","poster":"img/quezon-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Eddington","year":2025,"rating":2.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-17","tags":["a24","eddington","nowwatching","moviereview","arwinreviews"],"words":350,"review":"At first, di ko magets kung saan papunta. Emma Watson was weird, the mom was weirder, and Joaquin was just stubborn about that mask issue. Pero habang tumatagal, narealize ko na sila mismo ay produkto ng propaganda, parang mga taong nabrainwash ng belief system na ayaw nilang bitawan kahit halatang mali na.\n\nNahook lang ako nung inassassinate ni Joaquin si Pedro. Ang galing kasi ginamit pa niya yung current events to twist the narrative and pin it on someone else. Ang sugoi moment talaga was that exact scene tahimik lang, calm before the storm, tapos boom. Too late for your brain to process. Nakakatakot. Joaquin here feels like Joker living inside Jeff Bridges’ body, controlled pero may halong insanity sa loob.\n\nVisually, it felt like One Battle After Another meets Breaking Bad dry desert tones, flat grading, grounded lensing. Walang standout shot except the chaotic gunfight scene, where his asthma-breaths and the echoing gunshots sold that “no survival” feeling.\n\nSound design and score were forgettable, but that silence during the assassination sequence worked well that tension before the blast hits. Production design looked real, though. The pandemic paranoia, isolation, and collapsing sanity of people all believable.\n\nThematically, malinaw: people convinced they’re right even when they’re destroying others. Liberal, conservative, activist, extremist lahat ganun. In their mind, they’re the hero. Kaya kahit walang malakas na emotional pull, nagtagumpay siya in showing how both sides use ideology as armor.\n\nEmma Watson felt underused stoic, random outburst, walang resonance. Joaquin carried the whole film. He’s unsettling, manipulative, unhinged. That’s both the strength and weakness of the film. Masyadong dependent sa kanya.\n\nCinematography is serviceable, not spectacular. It’s not like The Creator or Dune. More like TV level realism with one big IMAX-worthy moment. Kung sa sound, wala rin masyadong maalala, but that one “calm before chaos” scene earns its place.\n\nUltimately, Eddington works more as a psychological allegory than a visual spectacle. It’s a film about delusion, moral confusion, and how people twist truth to fit belief. Pero emotionally? Meh. It irritated me more than it moved me which, maybe, was the point.","poster":"img/eddington-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Inglourious Basterds","year":2009,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","inglouriousbasterds"],"words":485,"review":"The opening score sets the tone right away. Makaluma ang tunog, parang mga lumang films sa Pinas. May classic na slow grandeur, and ominous tone.\n\nQuiet terror talaga ang bread and butter ni Tarantino. The tension lives in the dialogue and how the camera moves to guide your subconscious. Ang ganda ng visual clue nung tumatakbo si Shoshanna palayo ng cabin, framed perfectly within the doorframe, parang boxed out na wala nang takas. Sabayan mo pa ng rising musical score with tenor vocals, tapos biglang papalagpasin lang pala siya ni Christopher Waltz. Grabe. The director really knows how to toy with his audience’s emotions.\n\nPagpasok ni Brad Pitt as Aldo Raine, concise and clear agad. Parang COD Commander briefing the mission. Then Tarantino quickly shows his trademark gore, scalping German soldiers in all their bloody detail.\n\nThe Bear Jew scene is masterful. The sound effects alone build the fear, the echoing footsteps, the slow tension before impact. You can feel the dread of the Nazis waiting for their punishment.\n\nSet pieces and wardrobe are superb, breathing life into that war torn period. The basement scene is another highlight, the use of dual point of focus adds layers to the tension, splitting our attention between calm conversation and imminent danger. Then the revelation, the soldier’s cover is blown, guns are drawn, and chaos explodes in seconds.\n\nEvery element, color grading, lighting, camera movement, and lens choice, sync perfectly to build that cinematic rhythm Tarantino is known for.\n\nHe also crafts strong female characters. Yung makeup scene ni Shoshanna sa Chapter 5 feels like a battle armor montage, fueled by vengeance. The soundtrack Cat People (Putting Out Fire) by David Bowie elevates it even more.\n\nI couldn’t help but laugh at Brad Pitt’s accent and bulldog like overbite, parang mimic ni Don Corleone, but it works. The whole movie feels like a deadly game of hiding something, everyone pretending not to know.\n\nChristopher Waltz steals the show. Enigmatic presence. One moment, charming and silly; the next, terrifying and calculated. Yung sound ng pag snap ng heels nung babaeng artista? Parang nail in the coffin. Reverse Cinderella moment. GG well played.\n\nBy the end, the theater scene erupts into fiery chaos, parang Django Unchained meets The Wizard of Oz, with Shoshanna’s face projected on smoke like a haunting spirit. Oddly satisfying, like killing cockroaches in an infestation. Brutal but cleansing.\n\nNarratively, the chapter structure works beautifully. Each section sets up its characters and motivations, so by the time everything converges in the theater, it feels like a ticking time bomb. The payoff is both explosive and earned.\nAnd yes, this movie is worth rewatching. There are so many subtle hints, clues, and mirroring shots that make more sense when you already know what will happen.\n\nAnd the final line “You know something, Utivich? I think this just might be my masterpiece.” Tama si Tarantino. This is his masterpiece.","poster":"img/inglourious-basterds-2009.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Departed","year":2006,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-14","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","thedeparted"],"words":193,"review":"At first, akala ko coming of age movie siya. Medyo slow start, pero nung “broken arms” scene, doon na nagsimula ang tensyon. Grabe yung spike ng intensity from that point.\n\nMaganda yung concept ng double agent at twist na ang trabaho lang pala ni Leonardo DiCaprio bilang undercover agent ay hulihin yung rat from the inside, habang si Jack Nicholson naman ay informant ng FBI. Parang tug of war game ng loyalty at survival.\n\nNakakatakot si Jack Nicholson dito. May kakaibang presence siya na unpredictable, grabe talaga yung acting nya parang lumalabas yung pagka Joker nya. \n\nYung chase after the cinema scene, visual and emotional thrill talaga. Ganda ng cinematography, especially yung dual point of focus shots. May mga eksenang halatang mix ng old school at modern filmmaking, may raw grit pero stylish.\n\nSi Leo dito, may vibe na parang Shutter Island laging on edge, paranoid, emotionally unstable, pero believable. Can’t blame him. Pinasok nya yung mundong hindi naman talaga sya belong. Kailangan lang talaga nyang magkunwari to gather evidence.\n\nAt yung ending sobrang unexpected. Walang warning, bigla ka na lang titigil, tulala, trying to process what just happened.\n\nAvailable on Apple TV+","poster":"img/the-departed-2006.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Lost Bus","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-11","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","disastermovie","thelostbus"],"words":503,"review":"Sa unang tingin, akala ko isa lang itong typical disaster survival film: sunog, takbuhan, sigawan, tapos iyakan sa dulo. Pero iba pala. The Lost Bus doesn’t just show people trapped in a fire, it traps you with them. Isa ito sa mga bihirang pelikula na mararamdaman mo sa dibdib yung init, at yung takot.\n\nPagdating ng 15 minute mark, parang nag multiply by ten ang stress level. Walang pahinga. From the crackling fire to the handheld shaky shots, lahat masterfully done para maramdaman mo yung panic nila. Super intense. Zero visibility, di mo alam kung what lies ahead.\n\nThe direction and vision are clear. The director used the camera to communicate helplessness, and now that I realized he also directed Captain Phillips, the connection makes sense. The same kind of shaky realism and panic driven camera language is present here.\n\nAng ganda ng sound design. Rinig mo bawat crackle at bawat ember. Kahit sa laptop ko lang pinanood, sobrang immersive. Pakiramdam ko nandun ako sa loob ng chaos. This deserves to be seen on a big screen.\n\nThe fire simulations are outstanding and the lighting fits perfectly with every scene. The CG aerial shots showing how the fire spreads are haunting pero convincing. Yung last part lang with the bus, medyo halata na CGI model dahil sobrang bilis ng camera motion, pero understandable na given the scale of the sequence.\n\nThe lighting and color psychology evolve naturally. As the tension and risk rise, the more the red hues dominate the frame. You can almost feel the temperature increase with every shift of color. It is both visual and emotional heat.\n\nThe production design is on point. They did a really good job selling the visual that there’s fire everywhere. You wouldn’t feel that this was shot only in a studio or sound stage. You’ll feel like you’re out there with them, suffering, suffocating, and thirsty. Ito yung mga film na gusto mo na lang matapos kasi di mo na kaya yung tension. Parang torture in a good cinematic way. Lalo na kasi mga bata ang at risk. Kapag kids ang involved, iba ang bigat. Kapag adult lang siguro, di ganito katindi ang stress.\n\nThe editing rhythm maintains high energy but still gives enough room for emotional beats. Every cut mirrors the characters’ heartbeat, frantic when they are in chaos, and lingering just long enough to let us breathe when they are safe. The film knows when to pause and let you breathe before throwing you back again into the fire.\n\nSi Matthew McConaughey is in top form here, best since Interstellar. Halata na he drew from his real life dad instincts. The desperation, the guilt, the love, lahat ramdam.\n\nAt the end, napakapit talaga ako sa upuan. I found myself pausing, catching my breath. Tapos nung reunion scene ng mga kids at parents, naluha talaga ako. Parang yung moment na nakita na ni Tom Holland yung mga kapatid nya sa The Impossible(2012). A survival story that burns both the screen and your heart.","poster":"img/the-lost-bus-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Sinners","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-10","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","psychologicalthriller","sinners"],"words":493,"review":"Watched it on HBO Max\n\nGood editing, seamless transitions. Halimbawa, yung cut na eksaktong sabay sa kakagatin na si Hayley tapos cut sa babaeng kumakanta na pasigaw, sobrang galing. Same with the peek a boo bite scene na biglang cut to opening curtains. Nagulat talaga ako dun sa sabay na pagkagat at putok ng baril. Ang ganda ng match cuts!\n\nMusic also stands out. The fight scene where the beat synced perfectly with each punch, solid. Yung weird musical scene na may mix of past, present, and future, surprisingly effective. Bagong sound texture pero may charm. The song Picked Poor Robin Clean is haunting and symbolic, serving as quiet foreshadowing for the moral decay that follows. Its tone fits perfectly with the theme of temptation, parang lullaby ni Lucifer.\n\nFor sound design immersion, 9/10 for me. Ramdam ko talaga yung beat, napapaindak ako kahit brutal ang eksena. The mix of silence, rhythm, and timing of every punch, gunshot, and scream felt deliberate.\n\nThe sunset color palette is gorgeous, parang Renaissance meets apocalypse. Pero overall, it feels too cinematic for a zombie movie, almost too beautiful for its own genre. Parang Les Miserables meets Train to Busan, with marching song energy and period vibes\n\nPsychological layer ang nagdala. The sweet talk dialogues sound romantic but they are actually demonic invitations, temptations disguised as love. Let me in sabi ng evil. Parang metaphor ng sin, the devil offering companionship while destroying you slowly. It reminds you we always have a choice, whether to open the door or stay safe inside our fortress\n\nVisually, I love the deep blacks and the narrow aspect ratio. It makes the frame feel smaller, tighter, almost suffocating. Perfect for that claustrophobic dread\n\nThe gore was realistic and purposeful, not exploitative. And the lens work was ambitious. Many shots looked stunning, though a few suffered because the shallow depth made the background look like a green screen composite\n\nThe costume design and set pieces were convincing and historically grounded. I fully bought into the idea that this was set in an older period. Kahit low light, believable ang world building.\n\nIt is a slow burn start, honestly felt like a startup company drama for the first 40 minutes. Pero pagdating ng 41 minute mark, that is when it finally got me\n\nThe ending hit hard. The moment he finally touches his daughter and says Papa’s here, kumurot sa puso ko. Made me want to hold Luna and Mina by my arms more than ever. After all that chaos, that single line gave the film its soul\n\nFor rewatch value, yes probably. The film’s layered symbolism and slow burn structure make it worth revisiting, especially to catch all the subtle foreshadowing and lyrical connections between the music and moral themes. It is not the type of movie you marathon right away, but one you return to after some time, when you are ready to re experience its quiet dread and emotional closure","poster":"img/sinners-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"TRON: Ares","year":2025,"rating":3.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-10-08","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","scifiaction","tronares"],"words":376,"review":"After fifteen years, here we are again. Yay or nay?\nFor me, it sits somewhere in the middle, ambitious, nostalgic, but not fully immersive.\n\nSound design\nOtherworldly. Every explosion and missile launch made the chair literally shake. Yumayanig talaga. The power of the mix pulls you back into the Grid even when the visuals start to fade. It is a proper return to form for TRON’s soundscape, mechanical, clean, and rhythmic.\n\nCinematography\nThere are moments that genuinely shine, shots where the laser or gunfire seems to leap out of the screen. The low angle shots of the Recognizers are excellent, really capturing their massive scale. However, aspect ratio switching is hit or miss. The jump from cinemascope to IMAX works for action sequences but feels awkward during calmer story moments. Parang Dark Knight Rises all over again.\n\nStory and pacing\nThe first act drags a bit, medyo slow burn na parang nakikipagusap kay ChatGPT. It explores human and AI connection before the action ramps up. The plot itself follows a familiar villain turned redeemed arc with a few characters still loyal to the old directives. Bitin ang ending and clearly setting up a planned trilogy.\n\nWorld building\nYou can still feel Joseph Kosinski’s DNA here. Geometric precision, contrast of cold light and darkness, and grounded futurism. Iba pa rin talaga direction and design sensibility niya.\n\nMusic\nThe score brings back the TRON vibe, somewhere between Daft Punk and Linkin Park, a great blend of synthetic and human energy.\n\nPerformances\nJared Leto (Ares): fine overall, fits the tone but lacks dynamic range\n\nEvan Peters: underutilized\n\nEve: strong new addition, intelligent, confident, and elegant. May rich but capable energy that commands attention onscreen\n\nAthena: solid supporting presence that adds depth to the ensemble\n\nAction sequences\nThe classic light cycle chase remains undefeated sa legacy, hindi napantayan ng TRON ARES. Explosions have tangible weight, and the Recognizers look imposing. However, the aerial sequences lack the thrilling polish of Top Gun Maverick.\n\nFinal thoughts\nThis feels like a soft reboot and part one of three. It is not the masterpiece fans might have hoped for, but it is far from a disaster. The film has enough heart, sound power, and visual ambition to make you curious for what comes next.","poster":"img/tron-ares-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Exit 8","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-09-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","exit8"],"words":344,"review":"Sa storytelling, ramdam ko yung pagkaloop style niya, parang Edge of Tomorrow, Groundhog Day, pati Source Code at kahit If I Stay. Every ulit may tension at clue, tapos sa dulo nagulat ako kasi surprisingly emotional at satisfying yung ending.\n\nSa character, nakarelate ako lalo na dun sa “walking man.” Naalala ko sarili ko dati na parang paulit ulit lang nangyayari sa buhay. Kaya mas ramdam yung struggle niya at mas naging personal yung takot.\n\nCinematography solid din. Sinubukan nilang magmukhang continuous shot, halos kasing style ng Birdman at 1917. Dahil iisang corridor lang ang setting, bawat galaw ramdam mo at nakakulong yung feeling, parang claustrophobic.\n\nSound and music complimented the storytelling, lalo na yung repetition ng mga tunog habang umuulit. Mostly silence din kaya bawat yabag, flicker, at echo ramdam hanggang ending.\n\nSa pacing, syempre dahil loop movie siya, investigative ang dating. Bawat loop, may dagdag na detail at hindi naman dragging, parang detective ka rin na nakikisilip sa bawat clue.\n\nOriginality niya mas psychological ang peg kaysa sa jump scare lang. Focus siya sa paranoia at perception, kaya iba ang atake niya compared sa typical horror flicks.\n\nFor rewatch, oo, pwede ulitin para mahanap yung mga hidden clues at foreshadowing. Pero hindi siya yung tipong urgent na kailangan balikan agad, more on pang second look lang pag ready ka ulit.\n\nAt yung atmosphere ng sinehan, grabe. Sobrang tahimik na parang A Quiet Place, ramdam yung collective gasp ng mga tao, halos same level nung Thanos & Tony scene noong nasaksak sya sa Infinity War. Ganito rin katahimik nung Conjuring 1 \"hide and clap\" scene.\n\nImmersion wise, umabot ako sa point na binubulungan ko na yung main character kung anong dapat niyang gawin. Para talaga siyang laro, which makes sense kasi galing siya sa indie horror game.\n\nAt siguro bagay din itong movie sa mga lalakeng undecided kung magkakaanak ba sila o hindi, kasi may undertone siya ng cycle, responsibility, at breaking out of endless loops parang reflection sa buhay choices na paulit ulit hanggang may harapin mo nang tuluyan at may magbago.","poster":"img/exit-8-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"One Battle After Another","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-09-26","tags":["moviereview","filmreview","leonardodicaprio","filmrecommendation","arwinreviews"],"words":1312,"review":"I went in blind here. Kala ko nung una, dahil sa title, puro violence lang ang ibibigay ng film. At inexpect ko rin na baka maging bit boring or linear siya gaya ng mga recent works ni Leonardo like Once Upon a Time in Hollywood, Don’t Look Up, or Killers of the Flower Moon. Pero mali ako. What I got was something deeper and more alive parang mix ng Purge at Taken, may tension ng Get Out, gritty aesthetic ng Breaking Bad, at may halong military vibe ng Sicario. This one had urgency, grit, and momentum that kept me engaged all throughout. At visually, the color and urban scenes sometimes felt like The Dark Knight or Inception, parang Christopher Nolan mismo ang nagdirect.\n\nProduction design was on point. Naramdaman mo agad yung raw at intense environment sa Mexico, mula sa cramped at rugged na mga lugar hanggang sa parang top echelon presence ng Christmas Adventurer Club na binubuo ng mga all White Americans. Pinakatumatak sa akin ay yung one continuous shot habang hinihila ka papasok sa apartment, it felt like you were being pulled straight into their world. Continuous shots were impressive. Also the lenses choice, capturing a creamy bokeh that elevated even the grittiest frames.\n\nSobrang immersive, ramdam mo yung claustrophobic, lalo na kapag ikinumpara mo sa contrasting hotel luxury at sa underground meeting rooms. Cinematography was stunning, especially yung lowkey lighting at silhouette shots. The use of deep contrast ratios, precise shadow detail, and rich tonal blacks made every frame feel both oppressive and cinematic.\n\nAdding to that, this film also made history: One Battle After Another is Anderson’s first feature shot in IMAX. Since mas malaking screen, mas halata ang film grains dahil shot on film and not digital. For me, that really adds to the aesthetic para siyang period film set in modern times, giving the visuals a textured, timeless quality. Watching it in IMAX was better than Atmos. The helicopters, explosions, and large scale sequences weren’t just heard, they were felt. Super immersive, almost overwhelming in scale.\n\nAt yung piano score grabe. 25 minutes worth of piano driven tension, crafted so well that it shifted between comedic tone, urgency, and dangerous undertones. May sariling tema si Lockjaw, dark and menacing na parang Darth Vader on piano. Add to that the perfect music inserts: Soldier Boy, Shut Up and Dance With Me, Hark the Herald Angels Sing, and Ready Or Not Here I Come. Sakto ang pasok ng bawat kanta, giving moments a weird mix of irony and punch.\n\nSi Leonardo, ibang klase. While being high, confused, panicking, trying to recall something elusive. Sabay sabay lahat ng emosyon na to nasa mukha niya. Nakakabilib kung paano niya na execute ang mga layers na yon all at once. Yung performance nya dito can match his best works like The Wolf of Wall Street, The Revenant, or even Django Unchained. At hindi lang intense, may comic timing din siya dito na swak at hindi pilit, gaya ng “I’m Batman… I’m Peter Parker,” yung gapang niya habang nagtatago sa pulis na parang callback sa Wolf of Wall Street, at yung threatening ng lalakeng kasama ng anak niya papunta ng dance. May meta humor pa sa security question na “What is my favorite pussy?” Nakakatuwa kasi ngayon lang ulit ako naka experience ng maraming tao tumatawa sa loob ng sinehan. At maganda rin na the characters in my opinion were given equal spotlight, hindi lang si Leonardo ang front and center.\n\nSi Benicio naman, may quiet intensity. Controlled, minimal, pero bawat titig at kilos may bigat, na lalo pang nag amplify ng tension sa eksena. Yung villain, played by Sean Penn, may Daniel Craig energy, refined pero menacing, magnetic kahit kontrabida.\n\nAt hindi pwedeng hindi i highlight si Chase Infiniti. Debut film niya ito, at impressive agad ang presence niya. She plays a teenager trying to fit into modern society habang binabantayan ng overprotective na ama. Despite all that pressure, she equipped herself with great survival skills na lumabas sa pinaka crucial na moments ng film. In a way, may coming of age vibe siya, na mas nagbigay ng puso sa kwento. The way her character successfully duped her killer was brilliant, para siyang twist na kasing linis ng The Shallows when the shark was finally taken down. Breakout performance in my opinion.\n\nConnected dito, hindi lang sa acting standout kundi pati sa likod ng kamera. Michael Bauman did a remarkable job as Director of Photography. Nung sinearch ko siya sa IMDb, halos puro gaffer ang credits niya sa mga known projects, pero dito ang lupet ng gawa niya. The visuals looked like the work of a seasoned veteran, with masterful control of lighting. Hindi ito gawa ng may kaunting experience lang bilang DOP. At dahil gaffer na rin siya by trade, halata na madali niyang na apply lahat ng techniques na gusto niya kapag siya na ang naka helm sa DOP seat. DOP and gaffer go hand in hand, and this film proved how well he translated that skillset into cinematic vision.\n\nYung action scenes, may pagka Sicario din ang dating: tactical, calculated, hindi lang basta bara bara. Pinaka standout yung car chase, na parang POV shots sa F1, pero dito nakalagay sa long winding desert road full of rolling hills and blind crests. Akyat baba, uphill downhill, kaya hindi mo makita kung ano ang nasa harap at nasa likod kung may humahabol sayo. Dagdag suspense yun. The car chase felt like riding the waves but at the same time a roller coaster of emotions. That unpredictability made the sequence tense and strategic, parang chess game on wheels, at the same time para siyang mind games dahil sa surprises and unpredictability.\n\nHere’s what stuck with me: makes me wonder if may ganung mga tao talaga na ginagamit ang position or power for personal gain. Katulad ng ginawa ni Lockjaw na maghanap ng dahilan para may rason para pumunta sila ng Mexico, “drugs and tacos.” And you realize, hindi talaga government vs citizens ang pinapakita rito. Instead, may mga groups and secret clubs fighting their own ideological wars, using their power to manipulate narratives.\n\nThis hit hardest during the rally part, where Lockjaw’s men ignited chaos by literally “playing fire by fire,” pushing the crowd into violence para majustify ang excessive force ng police. Parang commentary din siya on how some agents or infiltrators nowadays disguise themselves to fuel anger, giving the system an excuse to crack down.\n\nFor me, this is a family film at the core, pero ang circumstances at ideologies ng far left at far right ang naglagay sa kanila sa ganitong dangerous entanglement. Kaya mas tumibay yung emotional pull kasi nakaugat pa rin sa relasyon ng mag ama.\n\nAt the end of the day, it is more than just an action or crime story, it turned into a father and daughter film trapped in a gripping narrative. At nung sumalubong si Arya pagkatapos at nagsabi ng “I love you,” doon ko na feel na perfect ending talaga, cherry on top ng buong experience.\n\nTLDR\n-Akala ko magiging linear and boring like Leo’s recent films (Once Upon a Time in Hollywood, Don’t Look Up, Killers of the Flower Moon) pero hindi. This one has urgency, grit, and heart.\n-Leonardo delivers a career highlight mix of intensity and comedy.\n-Benicio is all quiet menace, Sean Penn magnetic as Lockjaw.\n-Chase Infiniti’s debut is a breakout, especially her clever final twist.\n-Cinematography and score are stunning, with Nolan like visuals, continuous shots, creamy bokeh, and a piano score that runs 25 minutes long.\n-IMAX format with film grains added a period film feel in modern times, more immersive than Atmos.\n-Car chase across desert hills is a roller coaster chess game.\n-Political subtext on power and manipulation adds depth.\n-Beneath it all, it’s a father and daughter story that hits deep.","poster":"img/one-battle-after-another-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jaws","year":1975,"rating":2.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-09-18","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","jaws"],"words":390,"review":"Tinry ko panoorin sa 4DX and maganda naman, para ka talagang nasa boat, halos nakaka trigger ng sea sickness 🤢. Ang na miss ko lang, wala yung mist effect na naaalala ko dati sa Aquaman. Ang daming missed opportunities para gamitin yung effect na yun lalo na at most of the scenes are in the beach and ocean na nagssplash yung water. Yung strobe lights, ok lang na wala since most scenes nasa outdoor. Ang off lang is yung fan effect: kapag may scenes na humahangin sa buhok ng characters, biglang may malamig na hangin sa cinema. Ang weird kasi summer vibe ang scene pero malamig ang buga. Honestly, mas okay sana kung mainit yung ambience pag summer shots tapos lalamig lang kapag diving underwater para mas immersive.\n\nAs a movie, typical 70s slow burn. Mabagal ang pacing, maraming takutin ka pero wala naman pala mangyayari 😅 parang toying with your emotion. This was my first time watching it, wala akong nostalgia factor (baka sequels lang yung napanood ko noon). Nabasa ko rin after na kaya less ang shark scenes is dahil sa technical difficulties sa animatronics, which makes sense.\n\nThe ending was fine but felt rushed. Without watching teasers or reviews, I expected a straight up suspense thriller. Pero para sakin mas parang adventure, almost like crew ni Luffy vs a lone shark 🏴‍☠️🦈. The first half even felt more detective style. Wala yung \"feel the fear\". The ending though, bitin, roll credits agad with no closure: no report for the families, no accountability for the mayor na ayaw magpaclose ng beach. Parang walang justice ⚖️.\n\nFor me, walang masyadong risk or payoff. I do not quite get yet how this became a “classic.” Maybe I missed something. Personally, I prefer Deep Blue Sea (mas hopeless, mas claustrophobic) or even The Shallows (no rescue, and time pressure due to high tide which makes the stakes feel more desperate). Still, I will admit without Jaws, those movies probably would not exist 🙌.\n\nMy biggest let down: Honestly, di ko masyadong ramdam si John Williams dito. Alam kong iconic yung “duuun dun” theme, pero outside of that, hindi ko naramdaman yung same level of goosebumps effect na dala ng ibang works niya tulad ng Star Wars or Indiana Jones. Parang one note lang ang dating, effective pero hindi all around immersive.","poster":"img/jaws-1975.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Demon Slayer: Kimetsu no Yaiba Infinity Castle","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-09-02","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","demonslayerinfinitycastle"],"words":194,"review":"After 7 years balik otaku phase na ako 😅\n\nLast anime ko pa was One Piece episode 716, 2018 pa yun! Grabe ang tagal. Tapos ngayon, first time ko pa manood ng anime movie sa sinehan.\n\nHad to do my assignment as in 63 episodes in one weekend.\n\nHonestly akala ko itong movie na ito would feel different like iba yung flow, pangcinema talaga. Pero it felt more like anime episodes lang na pinagdikit dikit to make it movie length. Mahahalata mo talaga yung mga “episode breaks” kahit nasa big screen ka na.\n\nStory wise wala pa rin akong sugoi moment.\n\nSound design tho ibang level. Yumayanig yung IMAX. Ramdam mo bawat clash, bawat attack. Ang galing. Tapos yung musical score, panalo rin. May techno electro feels na bagay sa vibe ng Demon Slayer.\n\nHighlight for me: Zenitsu vs Kaigaku. Visual feast talaga. Thunder Breathing hits different on the big screen.\n\nLetdown? Akaza. Parang si Sentry lang defeated by the power of love. 😆 Medyo anti climactic pero sige na nga, may hugot.\n\nOverall di man super mind blowing, solid pa rin as a comeback experience sa anime world. Nakakamiss pala talaga tong ganito.🤺","poster":"img/demon-slayer-kimetsu-no-yaiba-infinity-castle-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"The Fantastic 4: First Steps","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-07-30","tags":[],"words":339,"review":"Yow what did I just watch?\n\nGrabe yung kaba ko. Sobrang bilis ng tibok ng puso ko. Twice ko lang ito naramdaman after Phase 3, during Loki season finale and this! Best movie after Endgame for real!\n\nSimple lang yung plot. There's no mindblowing plot twist. Glad many of the YT channel's theories and \"plot leaks\" did not come true, pag nagkatotoo kasi yung mga yun parang magiging too complicated. I think that is what makes this a very good movie. It is grounded on its own, separate from any other universe. Warm family movie without needing ng mga pilit na jokes.\n\nVisuals is good, believable na retro future. Iba talaga dating pag practical sets unlike naka Volume or chroma. PRODUCTION DESIGNERS ROCK! Tho I am glad they did not focus too much on world building. Mas character driven talaga si Matt Shakman. Gotta love this director. Wandavision and Fantastic Four? Nailed it. (Side note: Matt also directed one of the best episodes in Game of Thrones)\n\nPedro and Vanessa is believable as a parent. Ramdam mo yung conviction that no one will get left behind. Sue is strong AF.\n\nMCU's Johnny is better. Hindi masyadong douchebag. Good thing nahighlight ang pagiging genius nya dito. Not just a side character. Love Joseph Quinn since Stranger Things. Quite doubtful before if he can pull off Johnny Storm, but yeah glad he proved me wrong.\n\nBen is tito vibes. Everyone needs a caring Ben Grimm. Great pilot and driver as well. Good thing he's way past the self pity era as we jump into this movie.\n\nJulia Garner is fire! Me and my wife actually binged watch Ozark because of her as Ruth. Hope we can see more of her!\n\nTama yung description ng critics. Feels like Interstellar and The Incredibles.\n\nWorth it ang hirap papuntang Director's Club. Maraming salamat sa mga guards na tumulong. Saka na sa IMAX pag kaya na magstairs. Taas kasi ng eye level sa IMAX. But definitely a rewatchable film for me. Totoo ang hype. 🙂","poster":"img/the-fantastic-4-first-steps-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Superman","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-07-09","tags":["arwinreviews"],"words":175,"review":"Right now, I guess I am one of the snyderbots. Missed Snyder's cinematography style. This new Superman film really gives the James Gunn DNA. Feels like a crossover style between GOTG and 2021 Suicide Squad.\n\nLex feels like invulnerable. He's like playing Injustice with the combos. 😅 But the way Superman defeated him is similar how they defeated Ayesha sa GOTG 2 and kinda similar din sa Ready Player One.\n\nBold statement yung sinabi na they took inspiration with Top Gun: Maverick but parang wala naman? Di IMAX worthy for me...\n\nBradley Cooper & Milly Alcock looks great tho.\n\nThe story was great. Hindi ganun kathick yung plot but maganda yung message: the choices you make defines you. Ok din yung political side of things.\n\nI remember this feeling. Ganito yung pakiramdam ko nung pagkalabas ng cinema after watching The Force Awakens. Di ko alam kung matutuwa ako or maiinis. Mixed feelings as of now. But who knows, it might grow on me.\n\nHenry Cavill pa rin for me. But yeah, past is past. 🤷‍♂️","poster":"img/superman-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Jurassic World Rebirth","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-07-02","tags":[],"words":346,"review":"Ito ang pinakanaenjoy ko sa buong franchise. Super boring nung kay Chris Pratt, but this one ibang league. Finally, a Jurassic movie that knows how to balance scale, sound, and suspense.\n\nSi Gareth Edwards ang perfect guy to direct a film like this. His mastery of scale is unmatched. Ever since Monsters, Godzilla (2014), Rogue One, and The Creator, alam mo na agad na iba ang mata niya pagdating sa proportions. He knows how to make you feel small in a massive world. Here, makikita mo yung signature niya sa paggamit ng smoke, light beams, reflections, and water para ihighlight kung gaano kalaki at menacing yung mga mutated dinosaurs.\n\nThis film feels like a mashup of Jaws × Anacondas × Journey to the Center of the Earth × Indiana Jones. It’s not horror, it’s adventure and a fun, high stakes one. The river chase scene? Parang commercial teaser for a Universal Studios ride.\n\nGrabe ang impact. The cinema was trembling every time the dinosaurs roared or stomped. Yung mga low frequency rumbles sa likod speakers give that feeling na parang humihinga sila sa batok mo. Nakakadagdag sa immersion lalo pag IMAX setup.\n\nGanda ng combination ng practical sets (like the Mayan temple interior) and CGI environments. May mga shots na halatang ginawa sa Volume dahil sa pacircular lighting reflections, but overall it still blends well. CGI is solid, though some textures intentionally looked “retro” parang callback to the skin detail ng 1993 original.\n\nClassic moral conflict: greed driven expedition gone wrong. Simple but effective. Hindi mo kailangan ng convoluted plot; the pacing and sense of danger carry it.\n\nParang heist adventure with prehistoric stakes. Thrilling, tactile, and technically beautiful. You can tell Gareth loves physical cinematography and doesn’t rely too much on CGI.\n\nRiver chase sequence: water splashes, camera mounted near surface, reflections of light hitting the water, and that one massive tail swipe that throws the boat sideways. One of the best practical CGI blends I’ve seen this year.\n\nGareth Edwards proved again that he’s the master of scale. Every frame feels cinematic and alive.","poster":"img/jurassic-world-rebirth-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"28 Years Later","year":2025,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-06-22","tags":["arwinreviews"],"words":204,"review":"It's like a technical demo rather than a cinematic experience. From a producer’s standpoint, medyo sayang ang budget. Multiple iPhones, but every shot looks like it’s using three frames lang per moment. The visual gimmick of freezing on arrow impacts or motion blur sequences doesn’t add to the storytelling. Instead, it pulls you out of the moment, feeling more like a student experiment than a professional evolution of the 28 Days/Weeks Later legacy.\n\nDanny Boyle’s attempt to blend handheld realism with emotional intimacy is clear, but inconsistent. The tone shifts from horror survival to coming-of-age drama halfway through, and though surprising, it feels unearned. The stakes don’t escalate, every problem gets solved by sudden convenience, which weakens tension. The intent is there, but the execution feels like a “flex project” instead of a genuine continuation of the franchise’s dread.\n\nThe first half looks like it was shot on phones: flat, wide, and lacking depth. But somewhere mid-film, the sharpness kicks in, suddenly turning cinematic, almost as if a new DOP took over. Comparing the image sharpness to The Creator (shot on FX3), this one doesn’t come close. The upgrade in visuals mid-way does improve immersion, but it highlights the inconsistency more than the artistry.","poster":"img/28-years-later-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"How to Train Your Dragon","year":2025,"rating":4.5,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-06-22","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","moviereview"],"words":299,"review":"Watched it twice, once in a regular cinema for the story and again in IMAX for the visuals. The difference was night and day. Brought Arya with me the first time, but she got scared of the dragons (understandable, they can look intense on a big screen) and got bored after an hour. So if may 3 year old ka, maybe not yet the right time, medyo mature pa ang pacing.\n\nFor visuals, ang ganda. The texture of the dragons looks so real yet still keeps the cartoonish charm. I honestly don’t know how they balanced that, but it worked perfectly. Flight sequences are pure bliss, a mix of Avatar: The Way of Water’s fluid grace and Top Gun: Maverick’s adrenaline rush. Each glide, dive, and roll gives that childlike thrill.\n\nThe landscapes are breathtaking. Whether it’s CGI or shot on location, ang natural ng feel parang nilipad mo rin talaga. The costume design nails the Viking aesthetic without overdoing it. With those visuals and production detail, I couldn’t help but get excited thinking about Christopher Nolan’s Odyssey!\n\nSound design is top tier: the crackling of dragon fire, the whoosh of wind, every flap of wings. The musical score? Perfectly heroic. It elevates both the tension and the tenderness. Casting is also on point, and Gerard Butler stands out you can read in his eyes the arc from disappointed father to proud leader.\n\nAnd Toothless? Massive on IMAX. They nailed the sense of scale, especially in the third act when the alpha appears. A perfect “final boss” visual. You actually feel the difference in size and power.\n\nIn the end, it’s more than a kids’ movie. It’s about courage, empathy, and breaking cycles: just a bunch of young people trying to change centuries of old thinking. Beautiful, emotional, timeless.","poster":"img/how-to-train-your-dragon-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Inside Out 2","year":2024,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-06-12","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","pixar"],"words":358,"review":"Inside Out 2 hits hardest in the moments where Riley can’t understand herself anymore. Ang ganda ng idea na one small confusion, one small impulse, can shake her whole identity. You can actually see the moment na nagcrumble yung “good person” identity niya, and that’s the part that stuck with me the most. Yung scene na she suddenly questions why she’s acting that way, bakit siya nagagalit, bakit siya may ginagawa na hindi niya maintindihan. That’s where the film is strongest.\n\nAng relatable ng way na pinakita nila yung confusion ni Riley.\nYung tipong may mga moments ka rin sa buhay na bigla mo naiisip:\n\n“Bakit ko nagawa yun?”\n“Ako ba talaga yun?”\n“Masama ba akong tao?”\n\nGanun yung spiral na pinapakita ng movie, yung internal loop na bigla kang nagda-doubt sa sarili mo kahit hindi mo naman intensyon maging masama. Yung panic na baka ikaw yung pinakamalala even if you’re really just confused or overwhelmed. Ang galing ng film kasi navisualize niya yung ganung pakiramdam nang hindi OA, just honest and real.\n\nThe collapsing identity tree is simple but effective. It is a clear symbol na she’s breaking down inside. And the way the new emotions argue with the old ones makes sense, hindi nila alam paano ihandle yung changes, so lalo lang gumugulo.\n\nThe movie also captures that feeling of trying so hard to be good, pero hindi mo alam bakit nagfafail ka. When Riley starts doubting everything, even the memories na proof na mabuti siyang tao, sobrang totoo nung portrayal. Alam mo yung moment na kailangan mo maghold onto something someone once said about you para hindi ka malunod? That’s exactly what Riley does.\n\nVoice acting is great. Comedy is light lang. But the emotional beats are the real highlight. It’s not as heavy as the first film, but it’s very real when it comes to identity confusion and growing up.\n\nIt’s a good continuation of Riley’s inner world and a nice look at how messy it feels when you’re trying to figure out who you are. Not perfect, not super deep, but the “good person identity crumble” scene makes the whole film worth it.","poster":"img/inside-out-2-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"F1","year":2025,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-06-05","tags":["arwinreviews"],"words":338,"review":"I am not a car guy nor a fan of racing so I thought di ko to maeenjoy, but I did! Very much. Story wise, nandun yung emotion. Simple success/redemption arc movie. Maganda ang character arc nila and lahat may magandang purpose why they are doing what they are doing. There's real risk at stake.\n\nI think I have a new favorite director. Joseph Kosinski. This guy made me crazy after watching Tron: Legacy, Only The Brave, Top Gun: Maverick, and now F1. The only movie na di ko nagustuhan from him was Oblivion. Great director! Kaya nandun yung DNA ng Maverick. Actually para kang nanonood ng Maverick but in a race track.\n\nLooking back at Only The Brave and Maverick, oo nga medyo kuha ko na yung cinematography style nya. Great choice of lenses and I also love yung color grading nya. Yung parang madilaw and brownish na style na very prominent sa Dune: Part Two ni Denis Villeneuve.\n\nCinematography is great. Nagrorotate yung IMAX cam pahorizontal na parang insta360. I don't think IMAX cameras are capable of doing that. Either they stitched two shots or CGI. Dunno, need to research about how they filmed this. Yun yung indication na magandang ang film, kapag after kailangan mo mag dig deep into research mode. Surprised that the whole film was shot in IMAX (or baka sobrang immersed ko lang?) Even in closed quarters kasi eh. Alam ko mapakahirap irig yun due to the limited space.\n\nMarami pang part ng cars na kinabitan ng camera so iba't ibang perspective ng race ang mawiwitness mo hindi lang yung driver's perspective. But the last shot before they win the race, grabe! Walang sound except sa hininga ni Brad Pitt and kabog ng puso ko 🤣\n\nMy only con for this: Di ko masyado nafeel si Hanz Zimmer dito tho. Also for the sound design I kinda miss yung feeling na nagvvibrate yung seats sa high volume sequences katulad ng Oppenheimer ni Christopher Nolan.\n\nMatry nga panoorin sa 4DX 🥰\n\nHigh praise!","poster":"img/f1-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Lilo & Stitch","year":2025,"rating":4.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-05-22","tags":[],"words":19,"review":"Feel good movie. 💯🫶\n\nSa katandaan, nakalimutan ko na ganun pala kafun and kaemotional ang Lilo & Stitch. 😍😭","poster":"img/lilo-stitch-2025.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Interstellar","year":2014,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2025-01-15","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching","interstellar","christophernolan"],"words":382,"review":"Natuparan din yung pangarap kong mapanood ang Interstellar sa IMAX. Grabe, ibang klase talaga yung feeling. The launch sequences alone make you feel like you’re in the cockpit yourself. Every vibration, every rumble, is heart pounding. Hans Zimmer’s musical score remains surreal, still echoing like a prayer across time.\n\nKaya believable ang illusion ng scale because it’s not purely visual effects, it’s craftsmanship. Even the farm sequences ground the story in something tactile dahil they built an entire farm for the film! The ships were built, not simulated.\n\nLight and darkness serve as emotional language here. Darkness represents isolation, loss, and the vast unknown while light becomes memory, guidance, and faith. The tesseract sequence is the ultimate symbol of love transcending dimensions. Time literally folds so emotion can pass through. Love becomes the only force that ignores the laws of physics.\n\nIt feels like a story between humanity’s instinct to solve everything, and the universe’s divine mystery that cannot be solved. \n\nAs Interstellar is shown again in cinemas, my interest in cosmic wonders reignites particularly with relativity and multiple dimension planes. I learned that when someone is near a gravity dense environment, time moves differently. The stronger the gravity, the faster time passes compared to ours on Earth. If that’s true, maybe our Heavenly Father is in that kind of dimension. Dahil an hour for Him can be decades or centuries sa atin. So what does that mean? Hindi natin dapat isipin na mabagal Siya o nakakainip. He views time differently from us. His timing is perfect. We are the ones moving slowly, yet still on time in His plan.\n\nAnd then there’s the idea of higher dimensional beings. Those existing in 5D, who perceive time not as a line, but as a tangible structure. They can see all our past, present, and future at once. So don’t worry, God knows your past, your present, and your future. Worry no more because He is bigger than your problems. Know that everything is already written and finished.\n\nInterstellar in IMAX is not just a film. It’s an experience that reminds you how small you are, and yet how much you matter in the vastness of everything. Ten years later, it still feels ahead of its time (maybe because it exists outside time itself).","poster":"img/interstellar-2014.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Dune: Part Two","year":2024,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2024-03-28","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","duneparttwo","nowwatching"],"words":720,"review":"This review is coming from me as someone who didn't read the books so spare me if may misinterpretations\n\nAng tagal ko nang hindi naexperience yung ganitong saya, excitement, at satisfaction after watching a film. Actually hindi lang sya film eh, cinematic experience sya. Now I know why people call this a masterpiece.\n\nI'm crazy about this lahat ng nakakausap ko sinusuggest kong panoorin ito just like when I watched my fave movieA Star is Born.\n\nEvery frame napakaganda ng shots. Parang walang bychance lang nakuhaan. Every shot is intentionally made. It's as if every frame jumps out straight from the storyboards. Every frame is like a painting. Every frame walang tapon!\n\nFor me this is a textbook example of a war film from an interstellar setting.\n\nIbang iba sya sa part one, the former focuses on building up the story of each houses. Itong latter is sobrang dark and ang daming sub plots. Sobrang juicy ng mga contexts nito. May family feud, may political, may religion, etc.\n\nIts not just a sci fi movie. It is thought provoking.\n\nAko nakarelate ako kay Zendaya. Ako ay isang agnostic and it is very hard for me to blindly follow dogma of any religion/prophecies or whatever bullshit. In this film, it depicts faith - how blindly people follow the written prophecies made by the bene gesserit.\n\nSabi sa film, faith works for those who are weak and hopeles people.\n\nGinawa lang yung prophecy to control, to give people something to hope for. \"They've been told what to see\". That someday someone is coming who will lead them into paradise. Sounds familiar? 🤔\n\nI am a fan of films wherein need ko magresearch to understand it and rewatch it for a second or third viewing to the point na every viewing may subtle kang napipickup either sa story, even the miniscule blink of an eye.\n\nI can't quite wrap my head around sobrang daming ganap. Ang daming subplot na nagcocontribute sa big picture. Para syang game of thrones in space. May mga politics and hidden agendas/manipulations/scheming.\n\nThere is a unquenchable hunger to dig deep. To get more knowledge and information and consume contents and read books.\n\nIto yung mga moment na gusto mong bumalik at a certain point of time so that you can experience it again for the frst time.\n\nBitter ako nung una dahil better daw sa star wars. This film made star wars look like a franchise for kids. I am late to the fandom consider me as a fan!!!\n\nNow lang masaya dahil di dahil fan service or mindfuck movie kundi dahil sobrang naintrigue ako and naabsorb ako sa world building. You just simply get lost into it. again, IMAX really hits different. Don't compromise!!!\n\nActing is superb! My favorite is rebecca (lady jessica) i can feel she is torn between her duty as a bene gesserit and protecting by surviving with hee children\n\nSound design is really great. It makes you feel so emersed, claustrophobic and vibrates with it (literally feels like an earthquake Oppenheimer x10) again, IMAX really hits different. Don't compromise!!! This film is made to be seen in the theaters.\n\nWardrobe is 💯 ang ganda ng fabrics. It feels so realistic, grounded, futuristic, old fashioned all at the same time.\n\nWhen I watched dune part one in Netflix, I thought paul will just be like Peter Parker annoying stubborn kid but his growth is massive and he became Anakin Skywalker figure real quick.\n\nI just don't know if Paul is using the prophecy to gain power to avenge his father's murder or it's the lesser evil \"narrow path\" for the better good.\n\nThis is the kind of movie where you don't know if the hero is doing this for the good of all or for self interest only.\n\nI think I got really hooked after the 30 minute mark where they need to destroy a Harkonnen vessel and aircraft sabi ko Oh boy this one is totally is a game changer.\n\nSa third act hindi na ako makahinga sa sobrang intense. I dont want the movie to end.\n\nTo wrap up, para maintindihan nyo kung gano kaganda ito: Grandeur and scale of The Lord of the Rings + Game of Thrones in space+ James Cameron's Avatar world building + Star wars Rogue One on steriods.","poster":"img/dune-part-two-2024.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse","year":2023,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2023-06-01","tags":[],"words":33,"review":"Mindblown 🤯 First time in a very long time na mabitin sa isang movie! Ganda nito dark na yung story. Ito ang tunay na multiverse!!! This is definitely for them hardcore fan boys","poster":"img/spider-man-across-the-spider-verse-2023.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Avatar: The Way of Water","year":2022,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2022-12-19","tags":["arwinreviews","nowwatching","avatarthewayofwater","moviereview"],"words":562,"review":"Sobrang ganda, I don't even know where to start!\n\nPinagsisihan ko noon na hindi ko pinanood yung 1 sa sinehan so ang comparison ko lang is watching it on tv. So I made a promise to myself wayback 2009 na kapag may sequel ay papanoorin ko na talaga sa sinehan, in 3D and in IMAX!\n\nI knew it! Iba yung magiging experience kapag pinanood sa IMAX. Tama ang decision ko. Nagkape ako actually kasi akala ko nakakaantok dahil 3hrs 14mins ang total running time. But no! Daming action scenes. Never ako hinikab. 🤣\n\nHere are my pros and cons.\n\n1. Favorite ko yung mga flight scenes ng Ikran(flying creatures). Sobrang swabe!\n\n2. Sobrang ganda ng underwater scenes! Para ka talagang nasa underwater. Kakaiba yung CGI and 3D environment. Siguro dahil din sa lighting. Karamihan kasi ng scenes in nasa beach/open sea kaya direct sunlight. Compared sa first one, sa forest medyo madilim. Mas gusto ko talaga mga high key na ilawan. Nag pay off yung bagong mocap technology na ginamit and dinevelop ni James Cameron. Unlike kasi sa Aquaman and Wakanda Forever na nakaharness sila tapos nakachroma, ito naman sa ilalim talaga ng tubig shinoot with indoor tank with (waterproof?) motion capture suit.\n\n3. Skin. Grabe sobrang layo ng inadvance ng technology compared sa Avatar 1. Humanlike na, they almost made me believe that these are real people and not CGI characters! Best motion capture characters I've seen so far. Better than Caesar(Planet of the Apes), Hulk and Maz Kanata (Star Wars Ep VII: The Force Awakens)\n\n4. Best IMAX experience ever! 3D elements, the sound engineering, crystal clear images. Even better than any Transformers, Marvel, and Star Wars.\n\n5. Tulkan (Whale like creatures) parang straight from Nat geo, di mukhang computer generated.\n\n6. Forest still the same. Parang wala akong nakitang significant change.\n\n7. Dapat practical sets nalang yung mga military scenes. Medyo off lang yung textures ng mga mechanicals. Parang straight out from videogames. Baka hindi ILM ang gumawa, ubos na siguro budget because of mo-cap? 😅\n\n8. Frame rate. 48 fps is better than 60fps of Gemini Man. Sobrang smooth ng experience sa mata bagay na bagay sa Pandora, nakakadagdag sa \"immersiveness\" yung smooth movement. Still cinematic while being \"life like\" or realistic.\n\n9. I like the story better. Revenge, worried father (buti nalang wala akong lalaking anak 😅). Nandun pa rin yung spiritual side of it. Nandun pa rin yung fighting for your home. But I believe in this sequel, mas nagintroduce sya ng mas malalim na lore which I think will be expounded sa next three films. Ang ayaw ko lang is yung third act, common sa hollywood films na sobrang bilis and maraming nangyayari sa climax and victim rin ang Way of Water dito.\n\n10. You have to really see it in IMAX to witness its full glory. I don't think 4DX Cinema in Bonifacio Highstreet nor Dolby Atmos Cinema can offer what I am saying. You have to be there to know what I'm saying. It's so magical. It's really immersive. The posters, trailers and other promotional materials did not give justice for its full potential.\n\nAll in all, Cameron outdid himself again. I'm wondering what he still has to offer on the next installments. Probably the best film for me this year next to Top Gun: Maverick. Will watch it again next week. 😎","poster":"img/avatar-the-way-of-water-2022.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Money Heist: The Phenomenon","year":2020,"rating":3.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2020-04-09","tags":[],"words":33,"review":"What I like better than films and series? Documentaries. Those behind the scenes stories and footage that will surely make me appreciate all the effort and passion combined to produce such an art.","poster":"img/money-heist-the-phenomenon-2020.jpg","spoiler":false},{"name":"Avengers: Endgame","year":2019,"rating":5.0,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2019-04-24","tags":["arwinreviews","moviereview","nowwatching"],"words":173,"review":"This film hits on a level that is hard to replicate. The moment that really got me is how perfectly timed the score is. That “Portals” scene alone feels like pure emotion. I wish I could wipe my memory so I can experience that feeling again. \n\nThe score made me feel satisfied for the heroic act, but at the same time left me feeling empty for the loss. Every day I wake up realizing that there would be no more appearance of him in any Marvel film in the future. Thank you for the last sacrifice. You made the world a whole lot safer. I love you 3000!\n\nWhat a way to end the Infinity Saga with this. This made me root for Earth’s mightiest heroes more. They have gone separate ways, fought divided, then won together. Finally a perfect closure to the characters I loved for eleven years. Thank you Russo Brothers for a proper send off to our beloved characters. Team Cap and Team Iron Man no more. Only Team Avengers.","poster":"img/avengers-endgame-2019.jpg","spoiler":false}];